/* Academy, English edition (lessons, quizzes, book index). Created by George Barbaris */
(function(){const E={"lessons":[{"id":"c1214_vehicle","n":"4×4, AWD or SUV? Choose your vehicle wisely","src":"Overland’s Habit · Your vehicle","p":[["What makes a vehicle capable on dirt","The car is the tool that gets you out of town and to where you'll camp, walk or climb. The tougher it is, the further you go.<br>But note: <b>drive to all four wheels is not enough on its own</b>. There are fast sports cars and small city cars with 4WD that aren't built for off-road. Just as important are:<br>• the drivetrain<br>• ground clearance<br>• weight<br>• build strength"],["The labels and what they mean","• <b>4×4 / 4WD:</b> all four wheels are driven and the vehicle has real off-road ability. Applies whether the 4WD is permanent (full-time) or selectable (part-time).<br>• <b>AWD:</b> drives all wheels, but without serious off-road ability.<br>• <b>SUV, crossover:</b> categories born from the 1990s on, when families wanted cars that could leave the tarmac a little. Before that, 4×4s were mainly for the army, expeditions and farm work."],["SUV: comfort with limits","If you want trips into nature without levers and buttons, an SUV does the job. It's usually two-wheel drive, a bit higher, with a fairly strong body, and automatically sends drive to all four wheels as soon as grip is lost.<br>The limit: <b>you don't control the 4WD</b>. It engages by itself and may only work briefly, just enough to clear an obstacle. That's why many don't consider it a real 4×4."],["Before you buy: answer these","There is no “best 4×4”. Each vehicle was designed for something else: comfort, economy and easy maintenance, hard work, or image. First get clear on what you will do with it:<br>• Long or short wheelbase?<br>• Heavy or light?<br>• Petrol, diesel or LPG?<br>• SUV or selectable 4WD?<br>• With low range or without?<br>• With lockers or without?"]],"q":[{"q":"A small city car has four-wheel drive. Does that make it fit for rough dirt?","a":["Yes, 4WD is enough","Not necessarily, drivetrain, clearance, weight and strength matter too","Only if it has wide tyres"],"w":"Drive to all four wheels is only one of the criteria."},{"q":"You're driving an SUV on a muddy track. What should you keep in mind?","a":["The 4WD engages by itself and maybe only briefly","You can lock the differentials","It always has low range"],"w":"In an SUV you don't control the 4WD and it isn't permanent."},{"q":"Is a vehicle with selectable (part-time) 4WD called a 4×4?","a":["No, only full-time ones are 4×4","Only if it has lockers","Yes, both full-time and part-time are 4×4"],"w":"The term means all four wheels can be driven."}]},{"id":"c1214_fuel","n":"Which fuel suits your travels","src":"Overland’s Habit · Your vehicle","p":[["Start from how you'll use it","There's no right fuel for everyone. Ask yourself: how heavy will the vehicle be, how far from fuel stations will you go, how many kilometres do you do a year. The practical options today are three: diesel, petrol and LPG."],["Diesel: for weight, load and slow ground","<b>Why yes:</b> maximum torque comes from very low revs, so on rough ground you drive more easily and the engine works relaxed and lives longer. It burns less per 100 km, the fuel is cheaper and hard to ignite. It needs less frequent maintenance.<br><b>Why not:</b> heavier engine, more noise and vibration, more pollution, more expensive maintenance when needed. In severe cold diesel gels, add an <b>anti-gel additive</b> to the tank before you head up."],["Petrol: light and lively","<b>Why yes:</b> lighter, quieter engine with little vibration, a wide rev range and instant throttle response. It makes more horsepower from fewer cc, and the vehicle costs noticeably less to buy. Small petrol engines are efficient and reliable.<br><b>Why not:</b> burns more than diesel, the fuel is expensive, and it's highly flammable, in modern engines it's also under pressure."],["LPG: cheap litre, few stations","You don't lose power with a proper injection system. But the saving comes from the <b>lower price per litre</b>, not lower consumption, if the price or tax changes, it's gone.<br>• Clean burning, no residue in the engine<br>• Very strong tank, hard to adulterate<br>• The tank eats almost the whole boot<br>• In remote areas you rarely find a station, that's why the engine always keeps petrol too"],["When you cross water","• A petrol engine picks up moisture more easily in a river or puddle, but if it swallows water it has a better chance of surviving.<br>• In a diesel even a little water inside can destroy it for good.<br>• Modern diesels have lots of electronics and are just as vulnerable to moisture.<br>• Water in the fuel = serious damage, whichever you have."]],"q":[{"q":"You're towing a trailer and climbing slowly up a rocky track. Which diesel trait helps you most?","a":["Higher top speed","Torque from very low revs","Lighter engine weight"],"w":"With low-down torque you move calmly without straining the engine."},{"q":"A friend says LPG pays off because it burns fewer litres. What do you answer?","a":["Right, it burns much less","It only pays off on mountain trips","The saving comes from the cheap price per litre, not consumption"],"w":"If its price or tax goes up, the benefit is gone."},{"q":"You're taking a diesel to a snowy mountain in very low temperatures. What do you do first?","a":["Add an anti-gel additive to the tank","Leave the tank half full","Put a little petrol in"],"w":"Diesel gels in severe cold; the additive prevents it."}]},{"id":"c1214_weight","n":"Heavy or light 4×4: what you gain, what you pay","src":"Overland’s Habit · Your vehicle","p":[["Start from where you'll go","The same side doesn't always win. Manufacturers keep trying to make big vehicles lighter, with better materials and mechanicals. On the other hand, the organisations that run 4×4s for years in harsh conditions worldwide, humanitarian, military, commercial, don't choose light ones.<br>The general rule: <b>small</b> for weekend trips and narrow, tricky passages; <b>big</b> for long journeys and open roads."],["If you choose heavy","<b>You gain:</b><br>• Usually a more powerful engine<br>• More room for fuel, gear and passengers, a more comfortable trip<br>• More towing capacity (trailer, boat)<br>• With the right tyres, better traction on some ground<br><b>You pay:</b><br>• Higher consumption, especially uphill, that's why heavy expedition vehicles are almost always diesel<br>• Trouble in narrow Greek villages and a very hard turn-around on a narrow dirt track when you hit an impassable obstacle<br>• More tendency to roll on slopes, especially with a loaded roof<br>• Longer braking distance"],["If you choose light","<b>You gain:</b> agility and easy handling, less strain on the engine on soft, loose ground, less work when you get stuck, and cheaper, lighter recovery gear.<br><b>Watch the load:</b> a small 4×4 full of camping gear easily reaches its permitted payload limit. Then it behaves differently and dangerously, on tarmac and on dirt. Keep the gear lean or seriously consider a suspension upgrade."],["If you switch category","Don't assume you drive as before. A heavy vehicle slides more easily on snow, wet and mud, sinks in soft ground until it rests on its axles, and needs more room to stop. Relearn the vehicle on easy tracks and keep a bigger gap from the car ahead."]],"q":[{"q":"You're planning a months-long expedition with a heavy, loaded vehicle. Why will it most likely be diesel?","a":["Because it's the lightest fuel","Because weight pushes consumption up a lot and diesel is more economical","Because you can't find petrol anywhere"],"w":"A heavy vehicle needs more power; diesel delivers it on less fuel."},{"q":"In a big 4×4 you enter a narrow dirt track and hit an impassable obstacle. What's the main problem?","a":["Turning around becomes very hard","It lacks power","The gear doesn't fit"],"w":"Size is the heavy vehicle's drawback on narrow Greek roads."},{"q":"Your small 4×4 is loaded to the top and handles oddly. What do you do?","a":["Move the heavy stuff onto the roof rack to lighten the rear","Nothing, small ones are like that","Cut down the gear or consider a suspension upgrade"],"w":"Near the payload limit the vehicle becomes dangerous."}]},{"id":"c1214_tarmac","n":"The 4×4 on tarmac: drive it differently","src":"Overland’s Habit · Your vehicle","p":[["Why it behaves differently","An off-road vehicle is made for dirt; on tarmac it makes compromises. Even an SUV is heavier and taller than an ordinary car, with a high centre of gravity. That means:<br>• slower acceleration than a car with the same cc and horsepower<br>• high sensitivity to crosswinds<br>• less grip under braking and in corners<br>• if you run dirt tyres, even less grip on tarmac"],["In the rain","On wet roads, 4WD driven properly helps you keep grip when accelerating and turning. <b>Under braking, though, it hardly helps at all.</b> You stop like any other heavy car, so a longer gap and earlier braking."],["In corners","Slow down before the bend and turn the wheel smoothly, with no sudden moves. Tall 4×4s lean noticeably; it feels strange at first, in time you get used to it. Don't drive it like a low car."],["The roof rack and the load","Heavy, bulky things on the roof raise the centre of gravity further. The vehicle becomes more sensitive to crosswinds and easier to roll. Heavy items inside and low, light items on the rack."],["Quick checklist","• Bigger gap from the car ahead<br>• Earlier braking in the wet<br>• Lower speed in corners<br>• Firm hands on the wheel in crosswinds<br>• Nothing heavy on the roof<br>In a tall vehicle mistakes are harder to correct and cost more, drive with extra alertness."]],"q":[{"q":"It's raining and you must stop suddenly in your 4×4. What's true?","a":["4WD stops you faster","4WD hardly helps at all under braking","With 4WD you don't need ABS"],"w":"4WD helps with accelerating and turning, not stopping."},{"q":"Where do you put the heavy toolbox for a motorway trip?","a":["Inside the vehicle, low down","On the roof rack","It doesn't matter"],"w":"Weight up high = higher centre of gravity, crosswind trouble and rollover risk."},{"q":"How do you take a country-road bend in a tall 4×4?","a":["Go in fast and brake mid-corner","Like in a low car","Slow down before and turn smoothly"],"w":"A tall vehicle leans; it needs lower speed and gentle steering."}]},{"id":"c1214_drivetrain","n":"How drive gets to the wheels","src":"Overland’s Habit · Your vehicle","p":[["The path of torque","Torque is born in the engine and passes through a chain of parts before reaching the ground. If one link is weak, you lose power or break something.<br>• Engine → clutch → gearbox: here revs drop, torque rises and you manage it through the gears<br>• Centre unit (centre differential or transfer case)<br>• Front and rear axle → differentials → half-shafts → wheels"],["Which gearbox suits you","• <b>Manual:</b> uses less fuel and gives strong engine braking, valuable on steep descents and with a trailer or boat behind.<br>• <b>Automatic:</b> smooth, less strain on the drivetrain, pulls away well and holds momentum uphill. Downhill you'll need the brakes. In heavy use or towing it may need a transmission oil cooler.<br>• <b>Automatic with manual selection (Tiptronic-type):</b> works as an automatic, but you can also shift whenever you like, with the lever or buttons on the wheel."],["Why there's a differential","When you turn, the outer wheel traces a bigger arc than the inner one, so it must turn faster. The differential takes drive from one shaft and splits it into two, letting the wheels turn at different speeds. Without it, the tyres would scrub in every corner, the drivetrain would be strained and the vehicle would become unpredictable."],["Three kinds of 4WD","• <b>Permanent (full-time):</b> has a centre differential, so both axles always drive. Exception: some transverse-engine vehicles send drive from the front differential to the rear too, with no centre diff. They perform well but don't take very hard use; they mainly save space and cost for the manufacturer.<br>• <b>Selectable (part-time):</b> no centre differential. A <b>transfer case</b> drives two wheels (usually the rear) or, when you ask, all four.<br>• <b>SUV:</b> basically front-drive, and 4WD engages only <b>after</b> grip is lost, it reacts, it doesn't prevent. It comes from a viscous silicone coupling, a hydraulic system comparing axle speeds, or an electro-hydraulic one with electronic control. Often it reacts too late to keep you from getting stuck. If it has a lock button, use it briefly: with continuous use it overheats and fails."],["Low range: the secret gear","Many full-time and part-time vehicles have an auxiliary gearbox with two positions. In normal (high) the drive simply passes through. In <b>low range</b> revs drop and torque is multiplied:<br>• you move very slowly with full control on rough ground<br>• you go down descents without riding the brake or clutch<br>• you climb steep pitches and steps without momentum and without burning the clutch<br>• you tow a heavy trailer or boat more easily<br>The same 4×4 with low range is far more capable than one without."]],"q":[{"q":"The spec sheet says “centre differential”. What kind of 4WD does the vehicle most likely have?","a":["Selectable (part-time)","Permanent (full-time)","Rear-drive only"],"w":"The centre differential lets both axles drive all the time."},{"q":"You drive an SUV into mud. When will the 4WD work?","a":["After the front wheels have already started losing grip","Before you reach the mud","Only if you select low range"],"w":"In an SUV, 4WD is a reaction to slip, not prevention."},{"q":"Why does every axle need a differential?","a":["To raise top speed","To lock the wheels in mud","So the two wheels can turn at different speeds in a corner"],"w":"The outer wheel covers more distance in a turn."},{"q":"You must go down a steep, rocky descent. What helps you most?","a":["Low range","Fifth gear","The SUV's 4WD"],"w":"Low range gives very slow movement and control without constant braking."}]},{"id":"c1214_lockers","n":"Lockers and LSD: when to use them","src":"Overland’s Habit · Your vehicle","p":[["Why one spinning wheel stops you","An open differential always sends drive to the wheel with the least resistance. On the road that helps you. But if one wheel hits mud or lifts into the air, it takes all the drive and spins, while the other, which has grip, stays still. In mud you don't “get stuck”, you spin. The fix is to lock the differential."],["What a locker does","A locker (locking differential) ties together the two half-shafts of the same axle and forces them to turn exactly together. If one wheel spins, the other keeps pushing, so you're far more likely to find grip.<br>• Engages automatically (auto locker) or by switch (manual locker)<br>• Works with springs, electromagnetically, mechanically or with air (air locker)<br>• Usually fitted to the rear axle; at the front it raises capability further but needs know-how, which is why manufacturers rarely fit it as standard"],["Before you engage it, and after","• On flat, easy ground you don't need it<br>• <b>On ice and side slopes it is dangerous</b><br>• Prefer a locker you engage yourself, not an automatic one<br>• Use it briefly and only when needed<br>• <b>Once past the obstacle, disengage it at once</b><br>• If you lock all differentials, learn well what you're forcing the vehicle to do"],["What can go wrong","A locker stresses the whole drivetrain. Together with big wheels it's one of the most common causes of breakage. One wrong stab of throttle on a hard obstacle can snap a half-shaft and leave you in the middle of nowhere. And remember: <b>it doesn't change the vehicle's limits</b>. Suspension, engine power, clearance and side-slope tolerance stay the same. When grip is poor everywhere, don't expect miracles."],["LSD: automatic help with limits","A limited-slip differential (LSD) is essentially an automatic partial lock. It suits you if you drive fairly fast on dirt or want more safety without handling systems. For extreme situations it isn't the right choice:<br>• On a serious obstacle it needs very aggressive throttle, with no sure result<br>• On a low-grip side slope it can misread the difference between the upper and lower side and react dangerously<br>• It works only if both wheels turn a little; if one is fully buried, it won't help"]],"q":[{"q":"You're crossing a slope of icy snow. Do you engage the locker?","a":["Yes, for more grip","No, on ice and side slopes it's dangerous","Only if it's at the front"],"w":"There a locked diff can send you sideways."},{"q":"You've cleared the muddy section with the rear diff locked. What now?","a":["Leave it on for safety until evening","Speed up to clean the tyres","Disengage it at once"],"w":"Locked on good ground it strains the drivetrain for no reason."},{"q":"One rear wheel is buried and not turning at all. Will the LSD get you out?","a":["No, both wheels need to turn a little","Yes, if you floor the throttle","Yes, always"],"w":"An LSD only shares drive when both wheels have some rotation."},{"q":"You've fitted lockers front and rear. What has NOT changed?","a":["The chance of one wheel finding grip","Suspension, power, clearance and side-slope tolerance","The ability to get out of wheelspin"],"w":"A locker helps traction, not the vehicle's other limits."}]},{"id":"c35_prep","n":"Mechanical check before you set off","src":"Overland’s Habit · Your vehicle","p":[["Do it early, not the night before","In the mountains the vehicle works far harder than in town. The bearing noise, the squealing brakes, the small fault you keep putting off, it will all show up there, at the worst moment.<br>• Do repairs days ahead: after work it often needs re-tightening or adjustment once you've driven a few kilometres.<br>• Fit accessories and upgrades weeks earlier, so you can test them, sort out what comes up and learn how the vehicle now behaves.<br>• A repair far from the road always costs more, in money and effort.<br><b>Rule:</b> prefer a vehicle in perfect mechanical condition with few accessories over one full of gadgets with tired mechanicals."],["At the workshop: a full service","Ask for a proper check, not just an oil change:<br>• Steering, brakes, suspension, U-joints, ball joints, half-shafts<br>• Leaks underneath<br>• Levels: oil, coolant, brake and power-steering fluid, gear oil in all differentials, washer water, battery fluid if it's an open type<br>• Grease every grease nipple<br>• Belts: tension and wear<br>• Clutch (disc, pressure plate): it wears first in heavy off-road<br>• Spark plugs, filters, ECU diagnostics"],["Electrics","Look for chafed wires and bare ends that could touch metal or water and short out. Check the lights. If you power a winch, fridge, CB, GPS or charge devices for hours, fit a <b>second battery</b>. That way the main one stays full for starting."],["Spare wheel and recovery points","• The spare in place, in good condition and <b>the same size as the other tyres</b>, a different size strains the differentials.<br>• Strong recovery points front and rear. Some 4×4s leave the factory without a front hook, and sheet-metal tie-down eyes can tear under a hard pull.<br>• Fix: reinforced mounts from a 4×4 shop or large shackles on chassis mounts, with the pin facing away from the direction of pull."],["On the road: vibration and dust","Constant vibration loosens bolts, nuts and brackets even on the toughest vehicle, re-tighten regularly, roof rack and gear included. The best protection is gentle driving: whoever bashes the vehicle for no reason pays for it. Dust gets everywhere; clean the air filter often and replace it as the manufacturer says."]],"q":[{"q":"You have little money before the trip. What do you choose?","a":["An LED light bar and a roof tent","A full service and replacing worn parts","Bigger tyres"],"w":"Good mechanical condition matters more than accessories."},{"q":"Your spare is a smaller size than the other tyres. What's the problem?","a":["Only cosmetic","It doesn't fit the mount","It needlessly strains the differentials"],"w":"A different diameter means a different wheel speed."},{"q":"You'll run a fridge, CB and GPS for hours. What do you add?","a":["An auxiliary battery","A bigger alternator","Nothing, the main one is enough"],"w":"The second battery keeps the main one full for starting."}]},{"id":"c35_fuel","n":"How much fuel will you need","src":"Overland’s Habit · Your vehicle","p":[["Calculate before you leave","For a long route far from villages, put two numbers side by side: the route's kilometres and your tank's range. On dirt, consumption rises a lot:<br>• many kilometres in low gears<br>• turning back when an obstacle can't be passed<br>• detours and wrong turns in unknown country"],["Keep a 20% margin","The experienced hands' rule: take about <b>20% more fuel</b> than your calculation says. That's the reserve for whatever you don't foresee."],["Where you'll refuel","If the vehicle has a short range, don't assume you'll find a station. On the new sections of national roads stations are few, and many old ones have closed or been left off the route. A “fuel” sign often sends you to a nearby village, so count the detour too. Check up-to-date maps or ask people who often travel in the area."]],"q":[{"q":"Your calculation says you need 60 litres for the route. About how much do you take?","a":["60, exactly what it says","About 72, i.e. +20%","One extra jerrycan, whatever the route"],"w":"The 20% margin covers turning back, detours and low gears."},{"q":"You see a fuel station sign on the national road. What do you assume?","a":["It's right on the road, a few metres away","It's open 24 hours","It may be in a nearby village and need a detour"],"w":"Often the sign points to a station off the main road."}]},{"id":"c35_snowprep","n":"Winter vehicle preparation","src":"Overland’s Habit · Your vehicle","p":[["The right attitude","Snow is not the danger by itself. Problems come from poor preparation and overconfidence. Prepare the vehicle, be patient, respect the weather, and enjoy it."],["Fluids, fuel, battery","• <b>Coolant:</b> check the expansion tank and top up with antifreeze <b>of the same colour</b>. A different colour means different chemistry; mixing can ruin the protection.<br>• <b>Fuel:</b> leave with a full tank, because you burn more in snow.<br>• <b>Battery:</b> a cold start draws a lot of current. If it has fluid, check every cell; if it's old, replace it before you go."],["Glass and locks","• Put antifreeze in the washer reservoir. If you can't find any, blue methylated spirit with water at about 1 to 5; with a ready-made fluid, follow the ratio on the pack.<br>• To empty the reservoir, spray until it runs out. After filling, spray a little so the new mix reaches the lines and they don't freeze.<br>• Expecting frost and the windscreen is <b>dry</b>? Cover it with newspaper and throw it away in the morning along with the ice. Not on wet glass, it will stick.<br>• Lift the wipers off the glass.<br>• Spray door and fuel-cap locks beforehand with penetrating anti-rust spray, so they don't freeze and you don't snap the key."],["When you park","• <b>No handbrake:</b> if the cable freezes, the wheels stay locked until it warms up. Leave the vehicle in gear.<br>• Nose away from the wind, so snow doesn't fill the engine bay.<br>• Away from bends, climbs and descents, where someone could slide into you.<br>• Look above you for rocks, branches and landslides.<br>• Remember that in the morning the road may be ice, with no room to manoeuvre."],["Chains","Check they're in good condition and keep them where they come out easily, together with <b>work gloves</b>. If you've never fitted them, practise at home, calmly. The first time shouldn't be in the middle of a blizzard."]],"q":[{"q":"Your coolant is green and the station only has red. What do you do?","a":["Put in the red, they're all the same","Don't mix them, find the same colour","Use water instead of antifreeze"],"w":"Different colours have different chemistry and can cancel each other out."},{"q":"You're parking overnight on a snowy plateau. What about the handbrake?","a":["Pull it hard","Pull it halfway","Don't use it and leave the vehicle in gear"],"w":"A frozen cable means locked wheels in the morning."},{"q":"The windscreen is dry and you expect frost. What do you do?","a":["Cover it with newspaper","Pour water on it so ice won't form","Leave the wipers down on the glass"],"w":"In the morning the newspaper comes off with the ice; on wet glass it would stick."}]},{"id":"c1516_fuel_tanks","n":"More range: jerrycans or a second tank","src":"Overland’s Habit · Your vehicle","p":[["Why range drops","Every accessory, bigger tyres and upgrades push consumption up. The factory tank now takes you much less far. For a long trip you need more range."],["Jerrycans: easy, but risky","20-litre jerrycans are the quick fix. But know that carrying fuel in cans, inside or outside the vehicle, is <b>generally not allowed</b>, check the current rules. A full can in the mountains during the fire season is hard to justify at a check."],["The permanent fix","• <b>Bigger tank:</b> only for models with a ready-made kit.<br>• <b>Auxiliary tank:</b> for everything else. Built and fitted <b>only by a specialist</b>.<br>• If the second tank sits higher than the main one, it empties by itself through gravity. If not, it needs a pump you switch on to transfer."],["Don't fit everything","No upgrade is essential on its own. Fit them all and you'll have a heavy, slow vehicle that burns even more. Choose based on the routes you really drive."]],"q":[{"q":"You're thinking of keeping two full jerrycans on the roof rack all summer. What should you know?","a":["There's no issue at all","It's only allowed in the fire season","Carrying fuel in cans is generally not allowed"],"w":"Especially in the fire season, a full can in the mountains will put you in a tough spot."},{"q":"Your auxiliary tank was fitted lower than the main one. What do you need?","a":["A transfer pump","Nothing, it empties by gravity","A bigger filler cap"],"w":"It only empties by gravity if it sits higher than the main tank."}]},{"id":"c1214_tread","n":"Pick your tread: HT, AT, MT or winter","src":"Overland’s Habit · Tyres","p":[["Why it matters so much","The whole vehicle, the load and you stand on four patches of rubber, each roughly the size of a palm. That is the contact patch. Its size is set by diameter, width and pressure; the tread pattern does the rest. With the wrong tyres, engine, brakes and suspension can't save you. With the right ones the whole character of the car changes: grip, braking, comfort and the life of the tyre itself. That's why it is the first upgrade worth doing."],["First ask: where do I drive?","Rule: the more specialised the tyre, the better on its own ground and the worse off it. Match it to your real use, not your dreams:<br>• <b>HT</b> (Highway): 80–100% tarmac. Quiet, comfortable, happy at sustained speed. Thin sidewalls, shallow grooves, cuts easily on rock and wears fast on rough dirt. Oddly, it is one of the best in sand.<br>• <b>AT</b> (All Terrain): roughly half and half. Deeper grooves that throw out dirt, more plies, thicker tread. Today's AT tyres work fine as daily tyres. For Greek roads and mixed use, usually the most sensible choice.<br>• <b>MT</b> (Mud Terrain): 80–100% dirt. Deep, widely spaced lugs out to the shoulder so mud clears, reinforced sidewalls, works well at low pressure on rock. On tarmac: noise, fuel use, less grip, and the soft compound wears fast, especially in summer.<br>• <b>M+S / winter</b>: narrower, tightly packed blocks, good on fresh and packed snow. On hot summer tarmac it is gone very quickly. There are also special tyres for ice, sand or swamp, with very narrow use."],["What each does in the rough","• <b>Sand:</b> you want to float. The MT digs and buries itself, it is the worst there.<br>• <b>Light snow over dirt:</b> the MT digs down to the hard base and grips.<br>• <b>Deep snow:</b> the digging can leave you on your belly, careful.<br>• <b>Ice:</b> no compound is enough for a heavy 4×4. Chains, always."],["AT or MT? How to decide","Work out how much tarmac you cover to reach the mountains. If it's hundreds of km of motorway and you want speed and confidence there, accepting more slipping on dirt: <b>AT</b>. If you accept driving slower and more carefully on the road, especially in bends and rain, to be far more capable on dirt: <b>MT</b>. You'll have noise with both. Want silence? HT, and forget the hard mountains in winter. There is no perfect tyre, only a compromise."],["Making them last","• Rotate front–rear about every 10,000 km (depending on compound) for even wear.<br>• If there is a direction arrow on the sidewall, respect it.<br>• Hard compound = more km on tarmac. Soft = better grip and self-cleaning on dirt, but shorter life."]],"q":[{"q":"You're heading for dunes. Which tread will struggle most?","a":["HT","MT","AT"],"w":"The aggressive MT lugs dig into sand and the car sinks."},{"q":"You drive half tarmac, half dirt on Greek roads. What is usually the most sensible choice?","a":["M+S","HT","AT"],"w":"The AT balances tarmac and dirt with reasonable noise and toughness."},{"q":"You hit ice on good M+S winter tyres. What do you do?","a":["Fit chains","Raise the pressure","Carry on, M+S is enough"],"w":"On ice no tyre gives enough grip for a heavy 4×4."},{"q":"What keeps all four wearing evenly?","a":["Overinflating them","Rotating front–rear about every 10,000 km","Changing them only when worn out"],"w":"Rotation evens out wear, respecting the direction arrow where present."}]},{"id":"c1214_sizes","n":"Tyre size and rims: how far you can go","src":"Overland’s Habit · Tyres","p":[["Before you change size","You can't fit whatever you like. The car sets the limits: rim, wheel-arch space, suspension height, driveline strength, engine power, brakes. A bigger tyre means more weight and load on every moving part around the wheel. Stay close to the manufacturer's sizes. And note: formally the Greek Highway Code does not allow sizes other than those in the registration, check before you buy."],["Width by terrain","• <b>Shallow mud or light vehicle:</b> a narrow tyre cuts down to the firm base and grips.<br>• <b>Deep mud with a heavy vehicle:</b> a narrow tyre digs and buries itself, no.<br>• <b>Sand:</b> wide, so it sits on top without digging.<br>• <b>Rock:</b> wide, for a stable footprint and less slipping.<br>• <b>Frozen, deep snow:</b> wide with very low pressure, to stay on the surface."],["One size taller","Off-road profiles are usually 70 and up. The gentlest upgrade: same rim, profile one step up.<br><b>You gain:</b> a little ground clearance, softer passage over rough ground, rim protection from rocks and potholes.<br><b>You pay:</b> slower acceleration, maybe lower top speed, wrong speedo and odometer, heavier steering, more work for the brakes.<br>Big lifts for very large tyres rarely pay off outside competition: there will always be a taller rock or a deeper ditch."],["Make rim and tyre match","Every tyre width needs the right rim width. Outside the spec you risk the bead coming off while driving.<br>• <b>Tyre slightly narrow for the rim:</b> sidewalls nearly upright, firmer ride, but better grip and stability.<br>• <b>Wide tyre on a narrow rim:</b> bulging sidewalls, feels comfy, but you lose grip, steering wanders in bends and sidewalls cut more easily on rock.<br>Tyre makers publish rim–tyre fitment tables. Check them yourself before accepting a size change."],["Steel or alloy","• <b>Steel:</b> heavier, but takes knocks and abuse on hard dirt roads.<br>• <b>Alloy:</b> lighter, but more fragile.<br>Choose by use, not looks."]],"q":[{"q":"Heavy loaded 4×4 and deep mud. Which tyre width do you avoid?","a":["Wide","Narrow","None, it doesn't matter"],"w":"A narrow tyre digs and on a heavy vehicle buries itself; it only helps in shallow mud or on a light vehicle."},{"q":"The tyre shop suggests a wide tyre on your narrow rim. What should you expect?","a":["Better grip everywhere","No difference","Bulging sidewalls: less grip, vague steering, easy cuts"],"w":"And outside spec there is a risk of the bead coming off while driving."},{"q":"You drive very hard, rocky dirt roads. Which rim takes it best?","a":["Steel","Alloy","Chrome"],"w":"Steel is heavier but takes impacts without breaking."}]},{"id":"c1214_codes","n":"What the tyre sidewall tells you","src":"Overland’s Habit · Tyres","p":[["Why you should know it","Three minutes in front of the tyre and you can check yourself whether what you're offered fits your rim, carries the loaded car and handles the speed you drive. No need to trust anyone blindly."],["The code piece by piece","Take <b>LT 235/75 R15 104Q</b> as an example:<br>• <b>LT</b> = Light Truck. <b>P</b> = Passenger.<br>• <b>235</b> = tread width in millimetres.<br>• <b>75</b> = profile: sidewall height as a percentage of width. Here 75% of 235 ≈ 176 mm.<br>• <b>R</b> = Radial. <b>15</b> = rim diameter in inches.<br>• <b>104</b> = load index: maximum weight per tyre.<br>• <b>Q</b> = speed rating: maximum service speed."],["Don't get caught out","• Profile missing (e.g. \"P 215 R15\")? It means <b>80</b>.<br>• Profile always goes in steps of 5 (70, 75, 80…).<br>• The Radial R sits in the middle. A speed rating R, when present, is usually at the end."],["Load table (kg per tyre)","71=345 · 72=355 · 73=365 · 74=375 · 75=387 · 76=400 · 77=412 · 78=425 · 79=437 · 80=450 · 81=462 · 82=475 · 83=487 · 84=500 · 85=515 · 86=530 · 87=545 · 88=560 · 89=580 · 90=600<br>91=615 · 92=630 · 93=650 · 94=670 · 95=690 · 96=710 · 97=730 · 98=750 · 99=775 · 100=800 · 101=825 · 102=850 · 103=875 · 104=900 · 105=925 · 106=950 · 107=975 · 108=1000 · 109=1030 · 110=1060"],["Speed table (km/h)","N=140 (spares) · P=150 · Q=160 (winter, 4×4) · R=170 (4×4) · S=180 · T=190 · U=200 · H=210 · V=240 · Z=240+ · W=270 · Y=300"],["Age: the DOT stamp","Find the moulded four-digit DOT code: week + year. <b>DOT 1419</b> = week 14 of 2019. When buying a set, ask for recent production, a few months old. Rubber ages and dries out even when the tread looks fine, so don't keep old tyres to \"save money\"."]],"q":[{"q":"In \"LT 235/75 R15\", what does 75 tell you?","a":["Sidewall height as a percentage of width","Maximum speed","Rim diameter"],"w":"75% of 235 mm gives a sidewall of about 176 mm."},{"q":"The tyre shows load index 95. How much does each one carry?","a":["95 kg","950 kg","690 kg"],"w":"From the table: 95 = 690 kg per tyre."},{"q":"You see \"DOT 2305\". What does it mean?","a":["Made on 23 May","Made in week 23 of 2005","Rated up to 230 km/h"],"w":"The first two digits are the week, the last two the year."},{"q":"The sidewall reads \"P 215 R15\" with no profile. What profile is it?","a":["65","None","80"],"w":"When missing, the profile is taken as 80."}]},{"id":"c1214_retread","n":"Retreaded tyres: when they make sense","src":"Overland’s Habit · Tyres","p":[["What you are buying","A used casing with a new tread. The factory strips the old, worn rubber and bonds on new. You find them mostly in MT, less in AT and rarely in HT, because HT tyres run at high speed. Truck treads shed on the motorway make people nervous, but full retreading under strict quality control (ISO standards) produces a dependable tyre."],["For","• Softer compound, excellent grip on dirt.<br>• Cheaper than an equivalent new tyre.<br>• Often in sizes not made new.<br>• Recycling: the casing gets a second life.<br>That's why they are common in trial competitions and with hard-core off-roaders who don't care about tarmac or long life."],["Against","• Heavier than new.<br>• Weight varies tyre to tyre, even same brand and size, hard to balance.<br>• You can't know what casing you got.<br>• The soft compound wears out sooner."],["Is it right for you?","• <b>Yes</b>, if you have a light 4×4, not heavily loaded, used mainly on dirt.<br>• <b>Careful</b> with a heavy, loaded vehicle, the risk goes up.<br>• <b>Not ideal</b> if it's your everyday family car.<br>• On the road keep <b>to 100–120 km/h</b>."]],"q":[{"q":"What remains of the old tyre in a retread?","a":["The tread","The casing","Nothing"],"w":"Only the worn rubber is replaced; the casing is the same."},{"q":"You're on retreads and join the motorway. What speed do you hold?","a":["Up to 100–120 km/h","Same as new tyres","Below 60 km/h"],"w":"The upper limit keeps the risk low on tarmac."},{"q":"Why does the tyre shop struggle to balance retreads?","a":["They are very light","They have a hard compound","They vary slightly in weight and size"],"w":"Even same brand and size, they don't all come out identical."}]},{"id":"c1214_pressure","n":"Tyre pressure: how much to drop per terrain","src":"Overland’s Habit · Tyres","p":[["Starting point","The baseline is the vehicle maker's pressure, not the \"28–30 for off-road tyres\" you'll hear at the petrol station. Measure <b>with cold tyres</b> and <b>with your own gauge</b>; station gauges are rarely calibrated. Always carry an <b>electric compressor and a gauge</b>, because you adjust on the spot."],["How much to drop","Reduction from the maker's value:<br>• <b>Tarmac:</b> 0%, whatever the maker says.<br>• <b>Dirt:</b> −10%.<br>• <b>Snow:</b> −30%.<br>• <b>Mud:</b> −30%.<br>• <b>Sand:</b> −50%.<br>A percentage, not a number, because every tyre starts from a different pressure and because when you adjust on the trail the tyres are already warm."],["Once you've aired down","• The car sits lower. Over obstacles your belly is closer to the ground than you're used to.<br>• Drive slower and avoid sharp steering inputs."],["Once the soft stuff ends","Coming out of mud or sand onto hard, rocky ground? <b>Air back up straight away.</b> At very low pressure on hard ground the tyre comes off the bead easily and empties in one go."],["Why correct pressure pays","• In bends: with correct pressures you can safely go up to ~10% faster than the same car on noticeably soft tyres.<br>• Braking: pressure affects grip along the direction of travel; on loose ground lower pressure helps you stop sooner.<br>• Your wallet: correctly inflated tyres mean noticeably less fuel over the year."],["Habit","• No tyre is fully airtight; it slowly loses pressure, faster on dirt roads and high mileage.<br>• Check often.<br>• Valve caps always back on.<br>• Trust the maker's table, not the salesman's opinion."]],"q":[{"q":"You reach a sandy beach. How much do you drop from the maker's pressure?","a":["About −10%","About −50%","About −30%"],"w":"Sand −50%, mud and snow −30%, dirt −10%."},{"q":"You want to check pressures before setting off. How?","a":["At the station after 100 km","By eye","Cold tyres and your own gauge"],"w":"Warm tyres read high and station gauges are usually uncalibrated."},{"q":"You crossed mud at low pressure and there's rock ahead. What do you do?","a":["Air back up before continuing","Drop more","Carry on as you are"],"w":"Very low pressure on hard ground easily unseats the tyre."}]},{"id":"c15_intro","n":"Upgrades: a plan before the shopping","src":"Overland’s Habit · Upgrades","p":[["The trap","You start with \"just tyres\" and two years later you have a different car from the one you bought, after a lot of money and effort. Almost everything on a 4×4 can be changed. The right question isn't \"can I?\" but \"do I need it for what I do?\"."],["The usual order","• <b>Bigger tyres</b><br>• <b>Suspension lift</b>, to fit even bigger ones<br>• <b>Ring and pinion</b> with a shorter ratio, so the engine can turn the big tyres<br>• <b>Lockers</b><br>• Then the smaller items: <b>underbody armour, snorkel, lights</b>, etc."],["Before each change","• What else will it affect? Speedo, fuel use, centre of gravity, legality, roadworthiness test (KTEO).<br>• Where do I want to end up? Decide the final goal so you don't change the same part twice."]],"q":[{"q":"Where do 4×4 upgrades usually start?","a":["Lockers","Snorkel","Bigger tyres"],"w":"Then come suspension, ring and pinion and lockers."},{"q":"You fitted much bigger tyres and the car feels gutless. What fixes it?","a":["Ring and pinion with a shorter ratio","Stiffer shocks","Free-flow air filter"],"w":"Big tyres effectively lengthen the gearing; a shorter ratio restores torque."}]},{"id":"c1516_suspension","n":"Suspension: what you gain, what you risk","src":"Overland’s Habit · Upgrades","p":[["The goal","You want long suspension travel so the wheels drop deep (articulation) and stay on the ground in ditches; with a little lift you clear bigger ones too. But the car also lives on tarmac: too soft and it rolls like a boat, too stiff and it wrecks your back. You're looking for the sweet spot."],["How to do it","• <b>Ready kit for your model</b>: engineered and tested, shocks, coils or leaf springs, arms, spacers, longer brake lines, etc.<br>• <b>Home-made build</b>: if something breaks in an accident, the liability is yours. And with improvised work, forget KTEO and legality.<br>Real improvement means <b>longer coils/leaves and longer shocks</b>. A plain body lift gives little."],["How much it lifts","• <b>Solid axles</b>: 4–15 cm of lift or even more.<br>• <b>Independent suspension</b>: much less room, one reason it isn't seen as ideal for hard off-road."],["What it costs you","The higher the diffs go, the steeper the angle the shafts and half-shafts run at. CV joints work worse and break more easily off-road. Learn from reliable sources before you touch anything."],["The cheapest upgrade","<b>Wheel alignment</b>, often: modified or not, and whenever the steering pulls or you know you pushed it hard. A few minutes, little money, and it saves wear and bad handling."],["Words you'll hear","Solid axle · independent suspension · wishbones · shocks · coil springs · leaf springs · torsion bar · sway bar · Panhard rod · half-shafts · silent blocks · free-wheel hubs"]],"q":[{"q":"You want 10 cm of lift. Which suspension type takes it?","a":["Independent","Neither","Solid axles"],"w":"Solid axles accept 4–15 cm; independent much less."},{"q":"You lifted the suspension a lot. What will suffer more?","a":["The CV joints, running at a steeper angle","The windows","The radiator"],"w":"A steeper operating angle means they break more easily."},{"q":"After a hard trail the steering pulls. What do you do?","a":["Nothing, it'll settle","Wheel alignment","Change the tyres"],"w":"Alignment is cheap, quick and prevents wear."}]},{"id":"c1516_bodylift_track","n":"Body lift and wider track","src":"Overland’s Habit · Upgrades","p":[["What you're after","More <b>ground clearance</b> and a bigger <b>ramp angle</b>, to clear taller obstacles. Axle height above the ground is practically fixed; the simplest way to raise it is bigger tyres. That's where the wheel arches stop you: the gap between wheel and body."],["Body lift in practice","The body is raised off the chassis with spacers at its mounts (teflon, metal or both), and/or the arches are cut, so bigger wheels fit. It is a <b>radical job</b>: it affects structural strength and the geometry of many systems. It needs longer or new brake lines, levers, pedals, steering shaft and more. Well-known manufacturers state that such a vehicle is not meant for public roads, only for shows and competition."],["Height has a limit","A few cm on a small, light 4×4 changes little. Many cm on a tall car brings it close to the rollover point. Talk to owners of the same model with the same mod: what broke, how it drives afterwards. Often it's better to buy the car you want ready-made."],["Widening the track","When you lift, you push the wheels out in proportion, to keep the geometry and centre of gravity close to stock. It's done with <b>wheel spacers</b>, <b>rims with a different offset</b>, or both.<br>• <b>You gain:</b> stability.<br>• <b>You lose:</b> agility. It becomes more <b>understeery</b>, the turning circle grows, steering self-centres less readily and you replace <b>wheel bearings more often</b>."],["Before you decide","• Do I really need it for my routes?<br>• What else must change with it?<br>• Will it pass KTEO?<br>• What do people who've done it say?"]],"q":[{"q":"You want bigger tyres but they rub the body. What is limiting you?","a":["The battery","The wheel arches","The gearbox"],"w":"That's why the body is raised on spacers or the arches are cut."},{"q":"You widened the track. What will you notice when driving?","a":["It turns tighter","Nothing","More stable, but understeery with a bigger turning circle"],"w":"And wheel bearings wear faster."},{"q":"How do you push the wheels further out?","a":["Wheel spacers or rims with suitable offset","Stiffer shocks","Lower pressure"],"w":"Both move the wheel further from the hub."}]},{"id":"c1516_swaybar_gears_lockers","n":"Sway bar, diff gearing and lockers","src":"Overland’s Habit · Upgrades","p":[["The sway bar","It's the bar that ties the left and right suspension on the same axle. In a fast bend weight moves outwards and the inside wants to lift; the bar holds it down and keeps traction. The price: it limits wheel articulation off-road."],["Should you remove it?","Only for <b>competition or very hard off-road, and only if you know what you're doing</b>. For tarmac, daily use or a plain dirt road, no: without it a small manoeuvre or a side slope is enough to roll. Weigh what you want more, a bit more articulation or getting home."],["Big tyres, \"tall\" gearing","With much bigger wheels the engine struggles to turn them. The motorway isn't really the issue; the issue is that in a ditch low range is no longer low enough. Fix: <b>ring and pinion</b> with a shorter ratio, fewer shaft revs, more torque.<br>• Shorten the ratio by about <b>the same % the tyre diameter grew</b>, to stay close to stock.<br>• Change <b>front and rear together</b>.<br>• On some models only low range changes, with gears in the transfer case.<br>• Don't overdo it: a very short ratio multiplies driveline torque and breaks parts."],["Lockers: in what order","Usually <b>rear</b> first, then <b>front</b> if you need it. A locker loads the driveline heavily; without reinforcement expect failures, mainly <b>half-shafts</b>. Study how each system works and whether it suits you, without being carried away by salesmen or friends. (Diff types: see the lesson on how your 4×4 works.)"]],"q":[{"q":"A friend suggests removing the sway bar on your daily car. What do you say?","a":["Good idea, more articulation","No, without it the car rolls far more easily in a bend or on a slope","Only in winter"],"w":"Removal only belongs in competition or very hard off-road by experienced drivers."},{"q":"You increased tyre diameter by 10%. By roughly how much do you shorten the ratio?","a":["By half","No need","About 10%"],"w":"That keeps the overall ratio close to stock."},{"q":"You fitted lockers with no reinforcement. What is at risk first?","a":["The half-shafts","The lights","The battery"],"w":"A locker sends all the torque to the wheels and stresses the driveline."}]},{"id":"c1516_snorkel_filter","n":"Water and dust: snorkel, breathers, filter","src":"Overland’s Habit · Upgrades","p":[["The danger","The engine sucks air like a vacuum cleaner. In a deep crossing the intake mouth can suddenly end up underwater, and water in the cylinders means serious damage. In dust, the same intake swallows whatever the road throws up. A <b>snorkel</b> raises the intake high, above water and dust."],["What to protect besides the engine","The snorkel only saves the engine. These also need <b>sealing and regular checks</b>:<br>• ECU, alternator, starter<br>• distributor, spark plugs and leads<br>• winch, locker and spotlight wiring, every electrical connection<br>• GPS, CB, VHF, phones, cameras: <b>fixed and high</b> in the cabin. If you sink, water gets inside too."],["Driveline breathers","Hot diffs, gearbox and transfer cool sharply in water and create a vacuum. If the breather valves are underwater they suck in water, which mixes with the oil into a <b>white, creamy emulsion</b> that doesn't lubricate. You won't stop at once, but damage is coming.<br>• Extend the breathers up high.<br>• After every deep crossing, <b>check the oil in all diffs and the transfer case</b>."],["Kit or home-made","Prefer a <b>ready kit for your model</b>. A home-made one from metal or plastic parts is possible but needs know-how: the engine needs <b>enough air</b> and <b>clean air</b>, and both depend on pipe diameter, length and bends."],["Air filter","A free-flow (oiled) filter breathes more freely, but in the Greek summer, dust, heat and wind from late spring to early autumn, it needs very frequent cleaning and correct oiling, or it chokes the engine. Unless you have a specific reason, stay with the <b>stock paper filter</b>: it filters dust and sand better."]],"q":[{"q":"After a deep crossing you find white, creamy oil in the diff. What happened?","a":["Better cooling","Water got in through the breathers, lubrication is ruined","It's normal, it'll clear by itself"],"w":"Change the oil and extend the breathers up high."},{"q":"Where do you mount GPS, CB and VHF when crossing water?","a":["On the floor","Under the seats","Fixed and high in the cabin"],"w":"If the car sinks, water enters the cabin too."},{"q":"You drive a lot on dusty roads in summer. Which air filter do you keep?","a":["The stock paper one","A free-flow filter","No filter"],"w":"An oiled filter without very frequent care loses its benefits in dust."}]},{"id":"c1516_power","n":"Power in the 4×4: batteries, alternator, isolator","src":"Overland’s Habit · Upgrades","p":[["Do you need an upgrade?","Two small spotlights change nothing. But if you run spotlights, winch, fridge, camp lights, laptop, GPS and chargers, look at the electrical system as a whole, starting with the battery."],["Step 1: battery and alternator","• A larger-capacity (Ah) <b>deep-cycle</b> battery, which gives all its energy without being damaged.<br>• With a standard battery and alternator, after a winch pull you need about <b>2 hours</b> to recharge, a lot if you're in a tough spot, at night or in the cold. With a <b>160 A</b> alternator it drops to around <b>30 minutes</b>. It costs a few horsepower."],["Step 2: second battery + isolator","The scenario to avoid: an evening of fridge, lights and chargers, and in the morning the starter won't turn. The fix:<br>• <b>Main battery</b>: starter and vehicle lights.<br>• <b>Auxiliary (service)</b>: all other loads and the winch.<br>• <b>Isolator</b>: shares the alternator's charge between both but keeps them separate. However flat the auxiliary gets, you still start. Also sold in marine chandleries.<br>The whole install, battery, isolator, fuses, cables, by an <b>experienced auto electrician</b>. Sloppy work means short circuits and fire."],["12 or 230 V?","An inverter turns 12 V into 230 V. A 300 W unit runs a laptop, charger or TV, not a hair dryer, toaster or fan heater. Better though: <b>appliances that run directly on 12 V</b>:<br>• 230 V inside a damp car or tent can find its way to you.<br>• Conversion wastes a fair amount of energy as heat."],["Engine ECU","The ECU map can be changed too, but we don't recommend it. If you do, only at a specialist shop and aiming for <b>low-rev torque</b>, not peak horsepower up high."]],"q":[{"q":"What does the isolator do?","a":["Converts 12 V to 230 V","Charges both batteries from the alternator but keeps them separate","Waterproofs the engine"],"w":"The main battery stays full for starting, whatever you consume."},{"q":"You used the winch. With a 160 A alternator, roughly how long until the battery recovers?","a":["About 5 hours","It doesn't charge while driving","About 30 minutes"],"w":"With a standard alternator it takes about 2 hours."},{"q":"For camping, what do you prefer for your devices?","a":["12 V appliances directly","A 230 V inverter for everything","A big generator in the cabin"],"w":"Less risk from high voltage in damp conditions and less wasted energy."}]},{"id":"c1516_lights","n":"Extra lights: where to mount, when to use","src":"Overland’s Habit · Upgrades","p":[["The law first","On public roads, apart from fog lights, <b>no other spotlight is allowed</b>. If you have extras, cover them with <b>opaque covers</b> on the road."],["What they solve on dirt","• <b>Spread (wide-beam) lights</b>: in tight bends and hairpins you see the full width, obstacles and washouts the normal lights miss.<br>• <b>Swivelling work light</b>: light exactly where you dig or work the winch.<br>• <b>Rear-facing work light</b>: for reversing on rough ground, the stock reversing lamp isn't enough."],["Where to mount them","• <b>Grille/bumper</b>: easy, but they get covered by water, mud and snow and, being low, don't throw far.<br>• <b>Roof</b> (rack or bar): away from mud and water, but exposed to branches. Aimed low, they reflect off the bonnet and tire you.<br>• <b>Wings, by the windscreen</b>: clear of mud, can be turned by hand from inside (usually by the passenger), but the reflections remain.<br>• <b>Magnetic base</b>: no permanent install, put it where it suits, but not on a hard section, they come off.<br>• <b>LED bars</b>: a good, practical option."],["Moderation","Enough light to see well, not to impress. Every spotlight draws current, budget for it in your electrical system."]],"q":[{"q":"You leave the dirt for a country tarmac road. What do you do with the extra spotlights?","a":["Leave them on for safety","Cover them with opaque covers","Fit a yellow filter"],"w":"On the road only fog lights are allowed; other spotlights are covered."},{"q":"Your bumper spotlights don't help in the crossings. Why?","a":["Branches hit them","They reflect off the bonnet","Water and mud cover them and, mounted low, they don't reach far"],"w":"Their low position exposes them and limits range."},{"q":"You're reversing on a narrow, rocky track at night. What helps?","a":["A rear-facing work light","More lights on the bumper","Fog lights"],"w":"The stock reversing lamp doesn't light enough off-road."}]},{"id":"c1516_armor","n":"Armour: skid plates, bumpers, rocksliders","src":"Overland’s Habit · Upgrades","p":[["What gets hit underneath","The rock sticking up in the middle of the track usually finds: <b>sump, gearbox, transfer, diff housings, steering and fuel tank</b>, and often the exhaust and catalyst. Few 4×4s come with serious skid plates from the factory."],["Skid plates: material and price","• <b>Aluminium</b> (reinforced, aircraft grade): light, doesn't rust, takes impacts less well.<br>• <b>Stainless steel</b>: handles both impacts and corrosion, but heavy. Holes across the surface cut weight and help ventilation.<br>Watch out:<br>• The mechanical parts above must <b>cool</b>, overheating means failure and a stranded car. In extreme use a gearbox oil cooler is added, especially on automatics.<br>• You lose a little <b>ground clearance</b>, more if it sits on rubber mounts.<br>They make most sense on lifted cars in genuinely hard use."],["What's worth fitting","• <b>Steel bumper</b>: often comes with the winch. Solid base for accessories, <b>proper recovery points</b>, and no worrying about plastic. But it's for off-road: <b>it may not pass KTEO</b> without type approval, and in an accident insurance may not cover you.<br>• <b>Rocksliders</b>: side steel rails that save the sills from rocks and give a point for the Hi-Lift. Almost essential on hard routes."],["What to avoid","• <b>\"Cattle-catcher\" bullbar</b> up front: useless off-road, usually illegal, dangerous to pedestrians and, in an impact, to your own grille and bonnet.<br>• <b>Side steps</b>: they hang you up on marginal crossings and collect mud and snow."],["Rules without exception","• <b>Never a strap or wire rope</b> on a decorative bumper or sill, it will bend or tear off.<br>• Any change to dimensions or technical specs (even a winch) without approval is illegal. Ask before you do it."]],"q":[{"q":"You choose an aluminium skid plate. What do you give up?","a":["Impact resistance","Weight, it's very heavy","Rust resistance"],"w":"Aluminium is light and rust-free but more vulnerable to impacts."},{"q":"You're preparing for hard, rocky routes. Which addition is worth it?","a":["Front bullbar","Side steps","Rocksliders"],"w":"They protect the sides and support the Hi-Lift."},{"q":"You need a tow. Where do you attach the strap?","a":["The shiny bumper","Only a proper recovery point","The sill"],"w":"A decorative bumper bends or tears off under load."}]},{"id":"c1516_racks_seats_cage","n":"Load and crew protection: rack, buckets, cage","src":"Overland’s Habit · Upgrades","p":[["Roof rack","Solves space. For a 4×4 choose a heavy-duty rack (ARB style): <b>strong build, high side rails, reinforced feet, plenty of tie-down points</b>, so the load doesn't shift over bumps. Check beforehand that it takes spotlights, ropes and a Hi-Lift.<br>• Respect the <b>maximum roof load</b> of both rack and car.<br>• The dirty, greasy stuff goes up top: shovel, Hi-Lift etc., handy, and the cabin stays clean.<br>• Want something simpler? A <b>roof box</b>: keeps things dry and frees the passengers' legroom."],["Bucket seats","They hold you tight over rough ground, together with racing harnesses of <b>4 points or more</b>. You gain passive safety (crash, rollover) and active safety: you \"wear\" the car instead of hanging on the wheel and pedals. For touring, though, comfortable reclining seats are better. Without racing ambitions, keep your money."],["Internal roll cage","For competition it is the next mandatory step. For touring, weigh risk against cost: it raises safety a lot but eats space and comfort.<br>• With a cage, competition rules require a <b>helmet and 4-point harness</b>, so your head doesn't hit the tubes.<br>• It is built <b>by a professional</b> inside the body, not by the local welder. A badly made cage becomes a threat to the crew."],["External cage","For extreme conditions: protects from trees, branches, rocks and slopes, softens a rollover onto the side, and gives points to tie and secure the vehicle on a marginal crossing. But it is <b>illegal</b>, because it changes the external dimensions."]],"q":[{"q":"Where do you carry the muddy shovel and Hi-Lift on a trip?","a":["On the rear passengers' floor","On the roof rack, within the load limit","In the boot under the clothes"],"w":"They stay accessible and the cabin stays clean."},{"q":"You fitted an internal cage for competition. What else do you need?","a":["Nothing","Just a helmet","Helmet and 4-point harness"],"w":"That protects your head from the cage tubes."},{"q":"Is an external cage legal on the road?","a":["No, it changes the external dimensions","Yes, always","Yes, if painted"],"w":"Any change to external dimensions without approval is illegal."}]},{"id":"c1216_intro_limits","n":"What a 4×4 can and can't do","src":"Overland’s Habit · Off-road driving","p":[["Who needs one","You don't have to be a 4×4 fanatic. Hiking, cycling, climbing, photography, birds or just the village: in the mountains, your starting point is usually at the end of a dirt track. That's where a 4×4 is the tool that gets you there."],["Know what you drive","Not every '4×4' is the same. An SUV runs out of ability much sooner than a vehicle with <b>low range</b> and <b>lockers</b>. Pick your route based on what's in your garage, not what you wish was there."],["Where you stop","• Closed areas: they protect sensitive ecosystems and landscapes.<br>• Private land.<br>• Passages that are impassable or dangerous.<br>Limits apply to you and to the vehicle. Respect them and you grow them step by step without putting anyone at risk. Ignore them and your passengers and your wallet pay."],["Your first steps","• Start with an easy route: you test the vehicle and yourself.<br>• For your first tough sections, go with more experienced people.<br>• A difficult passage with a single vehicle: never.<br>• In the mountains we're all one team, you help and you get helped."]],"q":[{"q":"You have an SUV without low range or lockers. How do you pick a route?","a":["Same as for any 4×4","Based only on your experience","More conservatively, because it reaches its limits sooner"],"w":"Without low range and lockers, off-road ability is more limited."},{"q":"You want to try your first difficult passage. What do you do?","a":["Go alone, to learn without pressure","Go with more experienced people and a second vehicle","Go at night, when nobody's around"],"w":"Experienced help and a second vehicle get you out of trouble."}]},{"id":"c89_rules","n":"Before the dirt: law, safety, respect","src":"Overland’s Habit · Off-road driving","p":[["Skill is built in the field","You can read as much as you like, it will spare you mistakes of ignorance, but it won't teach you to drive. That only happens on the ground. The vehicle doesn't judge; it does what you ask, as far as it can. Even the smartest electronics won't save a driver who doesn't know what he's doing. Most accidents come from haste and ignorance. The antidote: <b>knowledge + experience</b>, with patience and open eyes."],["The Highway Code doesn't end with the tarmac","• <b>Seatbelts for everyone</b>, in every seat. Over the bumps an unbelted person hits the windscreen or you.<br>• <b>Zero alcohol</b>.<br>• Light food before driving. Heavy food brings drowsiness, and in the mountains drowsiness is a danger."],["Facing an obstacle","• Don't know it? <b>Get out and walk it</b> first.<br>• <b>Never turn round on a side slope.</b> If you feel the vehicle lifting: disengage the locker, don't stop at the critical point, give a little throttle instead of braking, and steer downhill.<br>• If the passage is beyond you, turn back. No shame in it, especially far from your base.<br>• Turn the music off: you want to hear the ground and the engine.<br>• Don't overtake without visibility. A forest track hides drops, rocks and oncoming vehicles, and locals usually come down the middle."],["Respect for the place and its people","• Stay on established dirt tracks. Not off them in rivers, lakes, beaches, mountains, let alone protected areas.<br>• Don't enter private land without permission.<br>• Don't drive over pastures and fields: they're someone's livelihood.<br>• Don't cut plants or branches for no reason, don't harm animals.<br>• No showing off, no noise, no rubbish. Respect residents and customs."],["Fire","Many wildfires start from a cigarette end or object thrown from a car. <b>Never a lit cigarette out of the window</b>. Put it out and keep it inside."]],"q":[{"q":"A deserted dirt track. The rear passenger unbuckles. What do you tell them?","a":["Fine, the Highway Code doesn't apply here","Buckle up again, it applies everywhere and protects you over the bumps","Only if we go above 50 km/h"],"w":"The Highway Code applies on dirt too, and an unbelted passenger is easily injured."},{"q":"You've reached a crest and want to turn round, but the ground tilts sideways. What do you do?","a":["Turn round there, carefully","Turn round with the locker engaged to be safe","Don't turn on a side slope, find level ground"],"w":"Turning on a side slope is a classic cause of rollovers."},{"q":"A field next to the track looks nice for a bit of 'play'. What's true?","a":["Stay on the established track, it's someone's pasture or field","It's allowed, there's no sign","It's allowed if it hasn't rained"],"w":"You don't leave established tracks, and fields are someone's living."}]},{"id":"c89_gen","n":"Slow and smooth: the basis of driving on dirt","src":"Overland’s Habit · Off-road driving","p":[["The right mindset","On dirt, precision and a calm mind win, not force. Snow, mud and ice aren't a reason to stay home, they're a chance to learn, as long as you know what each surface needs. Charging in with the pedal on the floor looks fun, but it ends in broken parts, especially far from base. A roadside-assistance membership that also covers mountain/forest roads is worth the money."],["Before you go in","• Engage <b>4WD before</b> you need it, not after you're stuck.<br>• Select <b>low range early</b>, and always when in doubt. If it's easier than expected, shift up or take it out. The reverse can't be fixed: in high range mid-obstacle, when the torque runs out, you've stopped.<br>• Choose your gear now. In water, mud, sand or a steep slope you don't shift, the second the clutch is down is enough to get stuck."],["On the obstacle","• Go at the <b>lowest speed that gets you through</b>. Let the vehicle crawl on engine torque, without riding the clutch.<br>• Use the <b>highest gear</b> the ground allows. How high that is, you only learn with practice.<br>• Throttle: on gently so you don't spin, off gently so the wheels don't lock.<br>• Brake: little and soft. On a loose downhill, let the engine brake, just lift your foot.<br>• Steering: you decide where each wheel goes. Don't let the vehicle choose."],["Why wheelspin is the enemy","A heavy foot means wheelspin, and wheelspin almost always means stuck. A wheel turning in the air doesn't push you forward; it digs, ruins the grip and can throw you sideways unpredictably."]],"q":[{"q":"You approach a muddy climb and aren't sure whether you'll need low range. What do you do?","a":["Go in high and switch to low if it gets hard","Go in high for more momentum","Select low range before going in"],"w":"From low you can easily shift up; from high mid-obstacle there's no going back."},{"q":"Which gear do you choose to clear an obstacle?","a":["Always first","The highest the ground allows without losing momentum","Third, for momentum"],"w":"The highest possible gear gives softer torque and less wheelspin."},{"q":"Downhill on loose dirt. How do you scrub speed?","a":["With engine braking, lifting off the throttle","By pressing hard on the brake","In neutral, to roll freely"],"w":"Engine braking keeps the wheels turning and the vehicle under control."}]},{"id":"c89_table","n":"What to engage and where: 4H, 4L and lockers","src":"Overland’s Habit · Off-road driving","p":[["How to read the lists","Each line gives, in order: <b>4WD (if selectable) · low range · centre locker · rear locker · front locker</b>.<br>'Maybe' means: you decide on the spot. Every vehicle is different, and the same ground needs different handling uphill, downhill, straight or in a turn."],["Roads and tracks","• Tarmac: NO · NO · NO · NO · NO<br>• Smooth dirt: maybe · NO · NO · NO · NO<br>• Broken dirt: YES · YES · maybe · maybe · NO<br>• Ruts: YES · maybe · maybe · maybe · NO<br>On tarmac and good dirt, 4WD and low range don't help, they wear the drivetrain and burn fuel. The only exception for a 4×4 on tarmac: when heavy rain makes it very slippery."],["Loose and slippery","• Mud: YES · maybe · YES · maybe · NO<br>• Sand: YES · YES · YES · maybe · NO<br>• Snow: YES · maybe · YES · maybe · NO<br>• Ice: YES · NO · YES · maybe · NO"],["Slopes, water, hard obstacles","• Hard obstacles: YES · YES · YES · YES · maybe<br>• Water crossing: YES · YES · YES · YES · maybe<br>• Steep climb: YES · YES · YES · maybe · maybe<br>• Steep descent: YES · YES · NO · NO · NO<br>• Side slope: YES · YES · maybe · NO · NO"],["When and how to engage","• Some engage 4WD and low range just before the obstacle. Others, in a proper 4×4, keep them in for the whole tough section (not on a beaten track) to always be ready. On many SUVs, continuous engagement, where allowed, strains the mechanicals.<br>• Lockers (other than the centre) go in <b>for one specific obstacle</b> and come out right after.<br>• <b>Front locker never in a turn</b>.<br>• Never used them? Get training at a club or school first."]],"q":[{"q":"It's raining very hard and the tarmac is extremely slippery. What's true about 4WD?","a":["Never on tarmac, under any circumstances","It's the one case where it's justified on tarmac","Add low range too, to be safe"],"w":"Otherwise, 4WD and low range on tarmac cause wear and burn more fuel."},{"q":"You're going down a steep descent. What do you do with the centre locker?","a":["Always lock it","It makes no difference","Leave it unlocked"],"w":"On a steep descent all lockers stay out; you want 4WD and low range."},{"q":"You cleared the rocky bit with the front locker in and a turn follows. What do you do?","a":["Take the front locker out before turning","Keep it in for grip","Engage the rear one too"],"w":"With the front locked the vehicle won't steer and the drivetrain is loaded."}]},{"id":"c89_seat","n":"How to sit and hold the wheel on dirt","src":"Overland’s Habit · Off-road driving","p":[["The seat isn't set just once","On tarmac you set it and forget it. On dirt you readjust it to the terrain, with two goals: <b>to see as much as possible</b> and <b>to protect your back</b>.<br>Before a steep climb, a crest or a dip, if the seat goes up, raise it all the way. Otherwise, at the moment that counts, all you'll have in front of you is sky."],["Your body","Back <b>upright, vertical to the ground</b>. Over the bumps, don't sink into the seatback; keep your torso straight and your abs lightly tensed. That way your muscles take the hits. Hunched, you load all the shaking onto a few vertebrae."],["Your hands","• Position <b>10 and 2</b>, as if the wheel were a clock, fingers on top.<br>• On rocks and rough ground, and <b>always in reverse</b>, thumbs stay <b>outside</b> the rim, not wrapped around it.<br>• When a front wheel hits something, the wheel can 'kick back' and spin sharply. It happens more with manual steering, but also with power steering. A sprained or broken thumb far from a health centre is a serious problem."],["Quick check","• Can I see the ground ahead of the bonnet?<br>• Back straight, core tight?<br>• Hands at 10 and 2, thumbs out?<br>• Every input to wheel, levers and pedals: <b>calm, smooth, precise</b>."]],"q":[{"q":"You're reversing between rocks. Where are your thumbs?","a":["Wrapped around the rim for a good grip","Outside the rim","Inside the spokes of the wheel"],"w":"If the wheel kicks back, a wrapped thumb gets injured."},{"q":"Just before a steep crest. What do you do with the seat?","a":["Lower it, to be more stable","Leave it as it is","Raise it as far as it goes, to see the ground"],"w":"Sitting low, at the crest you see only sky."},{"q":"You're being shaken on a broken dirt track. Which posture spares your back?","a":["Upright back and abs lightly tensed","Relaxed, sunk into the seat","Leaning forward over the wheel"],"w":"With a straight torso your muscles absorb the hits instead of your discs."}]},{"id":"c89_slip","n":"When the ground won't hold you: slippery, rocky, soft","src":"Overland’s Habit · Off-road driving","p":[["Recognise the slip","Slippery isn't only wet. Watch for:<br>• wet grass and dry leaves<br>• <b>pine needles</b>, among the worst<br>• ice and snow<br>• a thin layer of mud on hard ground, which acts like grease<br>• dust, especially in summer<br>On a climb or descent, control is lost very easily."],["Handling the slip","• Slowly, and even slower in turns.<br>• Moderate revs, so the engine holds you and you don't need the brake.<br>• If you can, 4WD, but <b>centre and lockers out</b>.<br>• Pick a gear so you touch the brake as little as possible.<br>Momentum helps in mud and fresh snow. On a descent, a side slope or when you need a precise turn, it ruins you."],["On rock","You'll find fixed rock on mountain passes, often with small sharp stones from rockfall and frost. You'll also find smooth, round stones and boulders in rivers.<br>• Smooth, slow, and zig-zag to avoid the tallest and sharpest ones.<br>• If you can't avoid a sharp one, take it with the <b>full tread</b>, never the sidewall, which cuts easily.<br>• In a riverbed cuts are rarer, but on smooth rock the vehicle can slip and drop suddenly, with damage underneath you can't see."],["On soft, loose ground","Here you live on momentum: lose it and you may not get it back.<br>• Low range, start gently and straight, wheels pointing where you're going.<br>• Turns as wide as possible, no brake. Let friction slow you.<br>• Inside the section: steady speed. No brake, no stopping, no gear change.<br>• Don't spin, every spin buries you deeper."]],"q":[{"q":"A forest track under pine trees, dry. How do you treat it?","a":["Like dry dirt, at normal speed","As slippery: pine needles give some of the worst grip","With a locker engaged for traction"],"w":"Pine needles are slippery even when dry."},{"q":"A sharp rock in the middle and you can't avoid it. How do you take it?","a":["With the sidewall, so it doesn't hit underneath","With the edge of the rim","With the full tread of the tyre"],"w":"The tyre sidewall is thin and tears easily."},{"q":"You're in the middle of a soft section and it's getting heavy. What do you do?","a":["Hold a steady speed with no brake, stop or gear change","Stop to see what's going on","Shift down for more power"],"w":"On soft ground, lose momentum and you're stuck."}]},{"id":"c89_sand","n":"Sand, mud, fresh snow: live on momentum","src":"Overland’s Habit · Off-road driving","p":[["Read the sand before going in","Sand is one of the hardest surfaces and one of the toughest on the vehicle, it scours everything, like sandblasting. The traps:<br>• soft, dry sand at the foot of dunes<br>• wet sand at river mouths<br>• gravel and pebbles: they look firm but give way just the same<br>• seaweed buried under the sand<br>The wet strip where the waves break holds better, a good place to regain momentum.<br>Remember: you don't drive on beaches. But winter often eats coastal and riverside tracks and forces you onto sand."],["Driving on sand","• What helps: a strong engine, a light vehicle, wide smooth tyres.<br>• Momentum is your ally, even for small rises.<br>• The <b>×5</b> rule: treat every bump as five times bigger. A 20 cm hump = a 1 metre obstacle.<br>• Build momentum for the climb and back off after the crest, the drop behind it is waiting.<br>• Need to stop? On a firm spot, on packed gravel or plants, and after finishing the manoeuvre. Pull away with no trace of wheelspin.<br>• To turn: a very wide turn, or stop on a firm spot and reverse."],["When material piles up in front","In mud or fresh snow, if you feel what you're pushing ahead is slowing you, <b>stop pushing</b> before you belly out.<br>• Reverse back along your own ruts.<br>• Forward again with a bit more momentum, <b>gently rocking the wheel left-right</b>, often that's how the tyres find grip.<br>• In deep, heavy mud or snow: shovel, or branches and stones in the worst spots."],["Downhill and breaking trail","• On a descent in mud or snow, even with gentle braking the vehicle can keep sliding, towards the ditch. Go in slowly.<br>• Breaking trail in snow or mud is hard on clutch, drivetrain and CV joints. Alone? Stop now and then to let it rest. In a group? Take turns at the front."],["Afterwards","• Wash <b>underneath too, with pressure</b>, after any sand. If it was near the sea, even more thoroughly, salt corrodes where you can't see.<br>• After snow, check the grille: if snow blocks the radiator, the engine overheats."]],"q":[{"q":"Ahead of you in the sand is a hump of about 20 cm. How do you treat it?","a":["As an obstacle of about 1 metre","As nothing, it's small","As double, i.e. 40 cm"],"w":"On sand every bump counts about five times bigger."},{"q":"In mud, the mass you're pushing ahead is stopping you. What's right?","a":["Full throttle until it goes through","Reverse along your ruts and go again with a bit of momentum, gently rocking the wheel","Switch off and wait for it to dry"],"w":"Insisting bellies you out; a fresh try with momentum often gets through."},{"q":"You're back from a snow route. What must you check?","a":["The air-conditioning pressure","The brake fluid level","Whether snow has blocked the grille and radiator"],"w":"A blocked radiator means overheating."}]},{"id":"c89_snow","n":"Snow: find out, plan, pull away, brake","src":"Overland’s Habit · Off-road driving","p":[["Find out what's really happening","• TV news often exaggerates snow, with footage from other days.<br>• The local police station isn't always objective either, it prefers you stay home.<br>• The most reliable picture comes from the local <b>KTEL</b> bus company, which must run its routes. Don't ask whether the road is open; the clerk won't want to guarantee it. Ask like a passenger: <b>'What time is the next one to [village]?'</b>. If it's closed, they'll tell you."],["Plan the day","• Not alone on an unknown, rough snowy mountain, especially on loose ground or in heavy snowfall.<br>• Prefer lower altitude and main roads. No alternative? You turn back. Recklessness isn't courage.<br>• Travel in daylight. At night on an unknown snowy road, every small problem grows.<br>• Snow changes the landscape: it covers ditches, rocks and junctions, brings branches down, erases sections of road. You get lost easily."],["Pulling away and turning","Fresh or slushy, old or powdery, snow grips roughly like wet tarmac. The real difficulty starts when it freezes.<br>• Rule for any vehicle: the <b>highest gear</b> that doesn't let the wheels spin and doesn't bog the engine.<br>• On level ground, <b>pull away in second</b>. From a stop on a climb, different logic applies.<br>• Throttle so gentle it's like stepping on an egg.<br>• Slow down <b>before</b> the turn, with at least <b>three times</b> the braking margin. No sudden steering.<br>• Are there ruts from others? Drive in them. Breaking trail yourself? Choose carefully where you place the wheels."],["Braking","• Distance to the vehicle ahead: <b>at least three times</b> normal.<br>• Prefer to scrub speed by calmly shifting down, off the throttle, and let inertia die away.<br>• Hard braking without ABS: don't stamp on the pedal. <b>Press-release</b>, so the wheels don't lock. ABS does this by itself.<br>• With little friction, the distance you need to stop grows a lot."],["A 4×4 doesn't brake better","A 4×4 pulls away, pulls and turns better in snow. But it brakes and slides the same as or worse than a small car. On the roads to ski resorts, most cars that end up off the road are 4×4s. Don't speed and don't overrate the vehicle."]],"q":[{"q":"You want to know if the snowy road to a mountain village is open. What do you do?","a":["Watch the TV news","Call the KTEL and ask when the next bus leaves","Ask on social media"],"w":"KTEL runs the routes and knows whether the road is open."},{"q":"You're stopped on a level snowy spot. Which gear do you pull away in?","a":["First, with plenty of throttle","Third","Second, with very gentle throttle"],"w":"A higher gear gives softer torque and less wheelspin."},{"q":"You're following another vehicle on a snowy road. What distance do you keep?","a":["At least three times normal","The same as on tarmac","Shorter, to drive in its ruts"],"w":"In snow the stopping distance grows a lot."},{"q":"You must brake hard in snow in a vehicle without ABS. How?","a":["Stamp the pedal all the way","Pull the handbrake","Press and release the brake repeatedly"],"w":"That way the wheels don't lock and you keep steering control."}]},{"id":"c89_chain","n":"Chains: where they help and where they don't","src":"Overland’s Habit · Off-road driving","p":[["How they work","A chain digs. That's how it finds grip, but it also deepens the ruts. The same logic applies in mud, where they also help."],["Depending on the snow","• <b>Ice:</b> here they're essential. They score the surface and give a little grip, but don't expect miracles.<br>• <b>Wet, slushy snow:</b> it gives way under the weight. Chains help mainly when it's fairly deep or sits on an old, frozen layer. If they dig down to the tarmac, the gain drops a lot.<br>• <b>Deep, cold, fresh snow:</b> it usually packs well, and with steady speed and calm driving you get through easily. Chains here may just dig you deeper. The hard parts are descents, tight turns and braking. But if there's a lot of snow and you lack experience, fit them without a second thought."],["Which wheels","On an SUV, or when driving without 4WD, chains go on <b>the driven wheels</b>. Find out beforehand whether those are the front or the rear, not in the snow."],["Limits of use","• Always slow: manufacturers usually say <b>below 40 km/h</b> (check the instructions for your own set). Faster, and tyres and suspension suffer.<br>• Back on clear tarmac? Take them off. With chains there the vehicle steers poorly and <b>brakes much worse</b>.<br>• Fitting and removing them is part of safety. If you can't be bothered, don't set off."]],"q":[{"q":"The road is a sheet of ice. What do you do about chains?","a":["Not needed, 4WD is enough","Fit them, here they're essential","Fit them only for the descent"],"w":"On ice chains score the surface; 4WD doesn't brake any better."},{"q":"With chains on, how fast do you go?","a":["Up to 80 km/h on a straight","Same as without chains","Below 40 km/h"],"w":"Faster, and tyres, suspension and the chains themselves get damaged."},{"q":"You have an SUV driving on two wheels. Where do the chains go?","a":["On the driven wheels","Always at the front","Always at the rear"],"w":"A chain only helps where the engine's power goes."}]},{"id":"c89_ice","n":"Ice on the road: tell it apart and react","src":"Overland’s Habit · Off-road driving","p":[["Two kinds, two behaviours","<b>White ice:</b> forms in very hard frost straight from packed snow, without melting first. It looks dull and its surface is rough. It may be only in the wheel ruts or across the whole road. It is slippery, but with the right kit you can get across.<br><b>Black ice:</b> forms when snow melts on a warm or sunny day and the water refreezes at night. It is clear, so you see the dark tarmac underneath and think the road is dry. It appears suddenly, in patches or everywhere. Try to avoid this one."],["White ice: how to cross it","• Ice only in the ruts and there is room? Leave them and drive on fresh, untouched snow, all wheels, or at least the two on one side. <b>Never on the cliff side.</b><br>• Whole road frozen, or you can't leave the ruts? Fit <b>chains</b>, here they make a big difference.<br>• 4×4 in low range, very slowly, in a gear high enough that power reaches the wheels softly. Gentle throttle, gentle brake, zero wheelspin."],["Black ice: how to handle it","• The warning sign: <b>the steering suddenly goes light</b>.<br>• Chains help very little here.<br>• A small patch you must cross? Shovel <b>sand or fine gravel</b> from nearby onto it.<br>• If you have to drive on it: stay alert, stay calm, no brakes. If you lose control the vehicle becomes a sledge and there is little you can do. The best defence is not to be there."],["When the slide starts","Get off the brake. No big or sudden steering inputs. A little controlled throttle so the wheels bite again.<br>And keep your eyes <b>where you want to go</b>, not on the drop. Your hands follow your eyes without you noticing, stare at the edge and that's where you'll go."],["Quick checklist","• Dull and rough = white, passable with chains<br>• Clear, 'dry-looking' tarmac = black, avoid it<br>• Warm day then freezing night = careful in the morning<br>• Shovel and chains always aboard in winter"]],"q":[{"q":"The road looks dry, but the steering suddenly went light. What do you suspect?","a":["A power-steering fault","Black ice","A deflating front tyre"],"w":"Black ice is invisible; you notice it through the loss of grip at the wheel."},{"q":"On which ice do chains help most?","a":["Rough white ice","Clear black ice","Equally on both"],"w":"The rough surface of white ice gives chains something to bite."},{"q":"The vehicle is sliding towards a cliff edge. Where do you look?","a":["At the edge, to judge the distance","In the mirror","At the spot on the road you want to reach"],"w":"Your body takes you where you look."}]},{"id":"c89_obst","n":"Rocks, logs, ruts and ditches without damage","src":"Overland’s Habit · Off-road driving","p":[["Protect the underside","Underneath you have diffs, axles, gearbox, sump and fuel tank, all vulnerable and all needed to get home. Every obstacle calls for <b>low speed and controlled power</b>. If you can't clearly see the entry and exit, get out and look.<br>Long vehicle? The rear overhang is usually bigger than the front. Come off obstacles at a shallow angle so you don't hit the back."],["Rocks and logs","• Don't let a rock or log pass between the wheels: that's where you hit or hang up. <b>Put one wheel on top of it</b> and creep forward, so the whole vehicle is lifted.<br>• Blocking the track? Try moving it by hand or with a strap. Careful, though: a heavy load can pull the vehicle in an unexpected direction.<br>• Use the shovel to build an up-ramp and a down-ramp.<br>• Thick tree: no need to cut it all. Saw through roughly <b>half the diameter</b>, place the jack right under the cut and lift. If it's dry, it often snaps by itself.<br>• Driving over logs? Assume they will roll. Wedge them along their whole length with <b>plenty of big triangular stones</b> and only move off when you're sure they won't shift."],["Deep ruts","In a deep rut the vehicle runs like a train on rails: it goes straight even with the wheel turned, and the steering takes a beating. When it suddenly finds grip, it turns sharply wherever the wheels are pointing.<br>• Ideal: <b>straddle the ruts</b>, one wheel between them, the other outside.<br>• If you can't: always keep in mind where the steering points and look further ahead.<br>• Grounding on your belly in mud or snow? For a short stretch, shovel down the centre hump or fill the ruts with stones and thick branches. If they go on for kilometres, change route."],["Mounds and ditches","• <b>Mound:</b> get out and check the far side. The risk is hanging up on your belly at the top. Go slowly and build footings with stones and branches, mainly on the way down.<br>• If you hit a high cross-obstacle with speed and the vehicle gets airborne: <b>feet off all the pedals</b>. On landing the front end takes huge forces; any extra load can break it.<br>• <b>Ditch:</b> approach it <b>at an angle</b>, so only one wheel drops in at a time. Same with town speed bumps. Fill a deep ditch with stones and branches that won't give way. If it's so big you'll be inside it, check first that you can climb out."],["Quick checklist","• Slow and controlled, always<br>• One wheel on the obstacle, never between<br>• Logs: chock before you drive on<br>• Ruts: straddle them, watch the steering<br>• Ditches: at an angle<br>• Airborne: feet off the pedals"]],"q":[{"q":"A rock sits in the middle of your line. How do you cross it?","a":["Let it pass between the wheels","Put one wheel on it and creep forward","Approach fast so you don't get stuck"],"w":"That lifts the whole vehicle and keeps the belly clear of the rock."},{"q":"In a deep rut you've turned the wheel, but the vehicle keeps going straight. What's the danger?","a":["When it finds grip it will turn sharply","The engine will stall","None, the rut guides you"],"w":"The turned steering is 'waiting'; once the wheels bite, the vehicle changes direction abruptly."},{"q":"A ditch cuts across the track. How do you approach it?","a":["Square on, both front wheels together","With speed to jump it","At an angle, so one wheel drops in at a time"],"w":"With one wheel in at a time the vehicle doesn't hang up or get twisted."},{"q":"A thick fallen trunk blocks the way. What's the smartest approach?","a":["Cut about half the diameter and jack it up under the cut","Push it with the bumper","Hit it with speed"],"w":"The jack snaps the trunk at the cut without you having to saw all the way through."}]},{"id":"c89_mud","n":"Mud, puddles and washouts: read before you enter","src":"Overland’s Habit · Off-road driving","p":[["On foot first","A mud hole can hide rocks, other obstacles and depth you wouldn't imagine. Get out and assess it up close; a mistake here may have no way back. If there's another route, it is almost always better than damage.<br>If you decide to dig footings with the shovel, make them wide, deep and firm, especially at the entry and exit. Think: if your boot sinks 10 cm, the vehicle puts many times that weight on four footprints not much bigger than a shoe."],["Prepare the vehicle on dry ground","• <b>Windows closed.</b><br>• <b>Recovery straps attached beforehand.</b> In the mud, kneeling in freezing slurry, you'll struggle even to find the recovery points, let alone work with spanners.<br>• 4×4, low range, centre diff locked and rear locker if the line is straight, in the highest gear the slope and the vehicle allow."],["Speed: never blind","<b>Never charge into a mud hole you don't know.</b> It may be far deeper than it looks.<br>If you know it and go in fast, mud will fly high. Set the wipers to fast beforehand, or you'll be blind the moment it hits the windscreen. If white steam then rises from the bonnet, it's usually just water evaporating on the hot engine."],["Washouts and edges","• Washouts form where water collects or drains: on the mountain side, the cliff side or mid-track. Drive slowly there.<br>• Under snow they're invisible, and many 4×4s have been buried in them. <b>Practise tight-space manoeuvring</b> so you can turn back.<br>• Keep away from the edges, especially with a slope, deep mud, snow, a drop, scree, loose rock or a stream, and above all after heavy rain, which softens the ground. The vehicle's weight can make it give way."],["Watch the wheels safely","With your head out of the window you judge clearances and wheel placement better. <b>But not near branches</b>, close the window properly there; a branch in the eye is a serious injury. If someone is guiding you from outside, agree the hand signals before you start."]],"q":[{"q":"Why do you attach the recovery straps before entering the mud?","a":["So you don't forget them","Because once in the mud the recovery points are hard to find","To make the vehicle heavier"],"w":"On dry ground it takes a minute; in the mud it may be impossible."},{"q":"After heavy rain the track has an edge above a stream. Where do you drive?","a":["Away from the edge, because the soft ground may give way","On the edge, it's drier there","It doesn't matter in a 4×4"],"w":"Wet ground at the edges may not hold the vehicle's weight."},{"q":"You're passing between branches and want to see where the wheels go. What do you do?","a":["Put your head out briefly","Open both windows","Keep the window shut and have someone guide you with agreed signals"],"w":"Branches can injure you badly; the spotter sees for you."}]},{"id":"c89_steep","n":"Steep climbs, descents and side slopes","src":"Overland’s Habit · Off-road driving","p":[["Before you start the climb","• <b>Walk it first.</b> From the driver's seat near the crest you see only bonnet and sky; beyond it there may be a tight bend or a drop.<br>• If it's so steep that you struggle to climb it on foot, don't attempt it in the vehicle.<br>• Plan your retreat now: if you don't make the top, or if there's no sure way on from the other side, you'll come back down the way you came, in reverse."],["Going up","• Pick the highest gear that gets you up <b>without changing</b>. Too high: not enough power. Too low: the wheels spin.<br>• A rule that usually works: <b>2nd low to climb, 1st low to descend</b>.<br>• Lockers if you have them, as long as there isn't also a strong side slope or bends.<br>• Line the vehicle up with the steepest line of the slope. Steady throttle, no clutch and no gear changes, and ease off at the crest.<br>• If you stop at the top to look, <b>chock the wheels</b> with stones or blocks."],["Going down","• 1st low and as little brake as possible: braking on a steep slope starts the slide.<br>• If you must, brake very gently and briefly each time. Sharp or constant braking swings the vehicle sideways to the slope.<br>• If the wheels lock and you slide like a sledge, <b>give a brief touch of throttle</b> so they turn again.<br>• <b>Never with the engine off</b>, you lose brake and steering assistance. <b>Never in neutral.</b>"],["If the climb fails","Come down <b>straight, in reverse</b>. Visibility is limited, the steering kicks harder and the knocks are harsher, so practise reversing beforehand. <b>Never turn around on a steep slope</b>: the rollover risk is high. If you're losing grip, use a little throttle instead of the brake."],["Side slopes","Most 4×4s tolerate about <b>30°</b> of side slope, less on loose ground, when lifted or heavily loaded. A sideslip turns into a rollover very fast, so don't try it before you have a feel for the vehicle. Hesitation here is good judgement.<br>• Avoid slippery slopes and slopes with holes or bumps; don't drive over rocks and logs.<br>• Secure the load well: as on ships, most capsizes start with <b>cargo shift</b>.<br>• Passengers on the uphill side, or better, out, walking higher up or behind.<br>• <b>No lockers</b>. The downhill wheels carry most of the weight; if they spin, things get dangerous.<br>• You can drop the pressure a little in the uphill tyres, it helps a bit, no miracles.<br>• If you're losing grip: come fully off the brake and give a little throttle."],["Turning on a slope","<b>Don't.</b> At some point you'll be sideways to the slope. Reverse out instead. If it's truly unavoidable: first reinflate any tyres you let down, then turn <b>downhill with throttle</b>. Never on unstable or icy ground."]],"q":[{"q":"Which gear do you usually pick for a steep descent?","a":["2nd low","1st low","Neutral with brakes"],"w":"1st low gives the most engine braking."},{"q":"The climb failed halfway. What do you do?","a":["Turn around on the spot","Reverse straight down, using a little throttle rather than heavy braking","Switch off and roll down on the brakes"],"w":"Turning on a slope leads to rollovers; straight reverse is the safe way."},{"q":"On a side slope, where are the passengers?","a":["On the downhill side for balance","They stay where they are","On the uphill side or, better, outside and above the vehicle"],"w":"Weight high on the slope or out of the vehicle reduces rollover risk."},{"q":"On a side slope you feel grip going. What do you do?","a":["Come fully off the brake and give a little throttle","Brake hard","Engage the lockers"],"w":"Braking makes the slide worse; a little throttle restores grip."}]},{"id":"c89_river","n":"River crossing: from one bank to the other","src":"Overland’s Habit · Off-road driving","p":[["How deep you can go","Up to axle height, roughly the middle of the rim, most 4×4s cross without special measures. Deeper, or in fast water, you must know where <b>the air intake</b> and <b>the ECU</b> (the engine's 'brain') are, so the water doesn't reach them. For your first rivers, don't go alone."],["Read the river from the bank","Check depth, current speed, the bottom and the exit on the far side. Calm water usually has a muddy bottom, fast water a stony one. In the first you risk getting stuck, in the second hitting the underside."],["Prepare before you get wet","• <b>Tow strap attached</b> at the right points, its free end secured high up, ready. In cold, murky, fast water it's almost impossible to do.<br>• <b>Switch off the headlights</b> and let them cool, so the hot glass doesn't crack in cold water (not needed with LEDs).<br>• <b>Rear locker</b> and <b>centre diff</b> locked if your line is straight.<br>• <b>1st low</b>, and no gear changes in the water.<br>• In a group, the tallest vehicle with the most experienced driver goes first."],["In the water","• Shallow but fast river: cross <b>at an angle, nose into the current</b>, so it pushes you less.<br>• Don't deliberately drive exactly with the current or exactly against it, even in shallows.<br>• Keep a steady, low speed: a bow wave forms that lowers the water level behind the bumper.<br>• If you feel power dropping and the nose sliding downstream, don't stop and don't change gear.<br>• <b>Never</b> enter a deep, fast-flowing river, it will sweep you away."],["If something goes wrong","• <b>The vehicle starts to float</b> (it can, in deep water with doors shut): forget the seats. Open the doors slightly, holding them firmly, so water enters in a controlled way, adds weight and puts you back on the bottom.<br>• <b>The engine stalls in the water</b>: don't restart. First make sure the intake is clear of the water and no water has entered the cylinders, otherwise you risk hydrolock and serious damage."]],"q":[{"q":"Before a deep crossing, which two parts of the vehicle must you locate?","a":["The air intake and the ECU","The jack and the spare","The battery and the indicators"],"w":"If water reaches them, the engine stops or gets damaged."},{"q":"The engine stalled in deep water. What do you do?","a":["Restart immediately to get out","Don't restart until you're sure no water got into the cylinders","Crank it in gear to move"],"w":"Starting with water in the cylinders can wreck the engine."},{"q":"Shallow but fast river. How do you cross?","a":["Straight with the current","Square across, as fast as possible","At an angle, nose into the current"],"w":"That way the water pushes on less of the vehicle."},{"q":"Why keep a steady, low speed in the water?","a":["To build a bow wave that lowers the level behind the bumper","To spare the gearbox","To keep the bonnet dry"],"w":"The steady wave keeps water away from the engine bay."}]},{"id":"c89_forest","n":"Forest tracks: what leaves and branches hide","src":"Overland’s Habit · Off-road driving","p":[["Under the leaves","On abandoned or minor forest tracks, a thick layer of leaves covers holes, washouts, water, boulders, branches and, worst of all, <b>cut stumps</b>, usually at the edges. A stump does more damage than a rock of the same size, because it doesn't move when you hit it; a rock usually shifts. Drive slowly and away from the edges."],["Branches and trees","• Don't drive over fallen branches: they flick up and hit the vehicle wherever they land.<br>• <b>Windows closed</b>, a branch in the face or eye is a serious injury.<br>• Watch for low branches, especially with a roof rack, and even more when it's loaded.<br>• In tight spots, judge the gap between trees carefully, especially when turning between them."],["A bush in the middle of the track","On abandoned dirt tracks, bushes often grow on the centre hump. If you have to drive over one, check what it is first: thick trunk or strong branches, leave it. Thin, springy branches, keep it <b>under the centre of the vehicle</b> and go very slowly, with no wheel on its base. The branches will bend and spring back up."],["Pine needles","The slipperiest surface in the forest: it slides like a rug even under your shoes. Avoid such stretches where you can, especially on steep slopes."]],"q":[{"q":"On an abandoned forest track, where do you usually expect cut stumps under the leaves?","a":["In the middle of the track","At the edges of the track","Only in clearings"],"w":"Stumps usually sit at the edges, where the trees were felled."},{"q":"A bush with thin branches grows in the middle of the track. How do you pass?","a":["With one wheel on its base","With speed so it breaks","Keep it under the centre of the vehicle and go very slowly"],"w":"The branches bend under the vehicle and spring back without damage."}]},{"id":"c89_alt","n":"High in the mountains: less air for engine and driver","src":"Overland’s Habit · Off-road driving","p":[["Why things change","As you climb, the air thins and every breath, yours and the engine's, carries less oxygen. An average non-turbo engine loses roughly <b>1 hp for every 100 m of altitude</b>, because the fuel-air mixture gets leaner. Turbochargers were originally developed precisely to make up for this loss."],["What to expect from the vehicle","• Less power than you're used to.<br>• Often <b>overheating</b> and <b>higher fuel consumption</b>.<br>If you're planning a hard pass up high, allow for this in advance: a climb you do easily down low may be out of reach at altitude."],["What to expect from yourself","You may feel unusual tiredness or dizziness. Don't worry; stop, calm down, and it usually passes in about a quarter of an hour. Slow down on tiring work such as digging or recovery. If the symptoms don't pass or get worse, go lower."],["In the field it all comes at once","The mountain rarely gives you one obstacle at a time: mud on a steep climb with a side slope, snow with deep ruts in a tight hairpin, puddles on grass, mounds, all on the same day. Techniques are guides, not guarantees. Only practice and experience get you safely through such combinations."]],"q":[{"q":"You're planning a hard pass at 2,000 m. What do you expect from the vehicle?","a":["More power thanks to the cold air","Less power, possible overheating and higher consumption","No difference"],"w":"Thin air leans the mixture: about 1 hp less per 100 m."},{"q":"High up, you feel dizzy while digging out the vehicle. What do you do?","a":["Stop and calm down; it usually passes in about a quarter of an hour","Dig faster to get it over with","Run to the vehicle and drive down fast"],"w":"It's a common reaction to altitude; if it persists, descend calmly."}]},{"id":"c89_tips","n":"Habits that keep you out of trouble","src":"Overland’s Habit · Off-road driving","p":[["Know your vehicle's numbers","Learn by heart its <b>length, width, height, weight, payload, ground clearance, turning circle and climbing ability</b>. That way you don't wedge between two trees, leave the roof rack on a branch or put the diff on a rock. Lie under the vehicle once and see where, and on which side, the lowest and most vulnerable points are."],["Walk first, drive second","You can't see everything from the driver's seat. Check unknown or risky sections on foot and plan <b>where each wheel will go before you commit</b>. Then drive through at a steady pace and decisively, hesitating with a wheel in the air doesn't help. Where needed, put a spotter outside to show you the line."],["Reversing over long distances","Practise driving in reverse for many metres. On a narrow track that ends in a dead end there's often no room to turn, and the only way out is back. If you also drive at night, make sure you have <b>strong reversing lights</b> or an extra rear lamp."],["Small details, big difference","• On hard sections <b>turn the music off</b>, so you hear the vehicle and the ground.<br>• When you stop, <b>don't park on dry grass</b>: the hot catalytic converter can start a fire.<br>• <b>Air conditioning</b> spares you dust and heat, but going in and out a lot wears you down. It also takes power from the engine and raises consumption, so it cuts your range."]],"q":[{"q":"You stop for lunch in a summer meadow with dry grass. Where do you park?","a":["On the tall grass for shade","On dirt or gravel, away from dry grass","Anywhere, as long as you switch off"],"w":"The catalytic converter stays hot for a long time and can ignite the grass."},{"q":"Which of your vehicle's figures are worth knowing by heart?","a":["Only its top speed","Only its colour and number plate","Dimensions, weight, ground clearance, turning circle"],"w":"They tell you whether you fit, whether you clear, and where you'll hit."},{"q":"Why turn the music off on hard sections?","a":["So you can hear the vehicle and the ground","So the battery doesn't go flat","To spare the sound system"],"w":"The vehicle's noises warn you before something breaks."}]},{"id":"c89_geom","n":"How much your 4×4 can 'fit': geometry and limits","src":"Overland’s Habit · Hard terrain & extremes","p":[["Get underneath, open the bonnet","Before a hard crossing, know what you've got. Look under the car: where the differentials sit, the sump, anything hanging low. Those are the spots you don't want a rock to meet. Then open the bonnet and find the air intake, the ECU, the ignition and the alternator. They will matter the first time you drive into water."],["The three angles, in degrees","• <b>Approach:</b> how steep a climb or ramp you can take at the front without the bumper hitting. It shrinks the lower and longer the front overhang is.<br>• <b>Departure:</b> the same for the tail, when you come down or leave an obstacle.<br>• <b>Ramp (breakover):</b> how sharp a mound you can straddle without the belly touching between the axles."],["Ground clearance","It is the tallest obstacle that passes under the vehicle, between the wheels, without a wheel driving over it and without anything hitting. It is measured at normal tyre pressure."],["Water, climbs, side slope","• <b>Wading depth (cm):</b> set mainly by the air intake and the ECU position. Practical rule: most vehicles pass comfortably up to the middle of the wheel.<br>• <b>Climb:</b> the slope you can climb starting from a standstill, with good grip.<br>• <b>Side slope:</b> how far you can lean sideways without rolling, correctly loaded."],["Before the obstacle","• Do you know the manufacturer's numbers?<br>• Do you know where the weak spot underneath is?<br>• Which angle decides this obstacle: front, rear or belly?<br>• You learn the limits in practice too, step by step and safely, not only on paper."]],"q":[{"q":"You are climbing a steep ramp and worry the front bumper will hit. Which angle matters?","a":["The ramp angle","The approach angle","The departure angle"],"w":"The approach angle concerns the front as you meet the slope."},{"q":"You cross a sharp mound and hear the belly scraping. Which limit did you exceed?","a":["The departure angle","The maximum side slope","The ramp angle"],"w":"The ramp angle shows how much mound fits between the axles."},{"q":"What do you check first to know how deep a water crossing you can handle?","a":["Where the air intake and ECU are","How wide the tyres are","The engine capacity"],"w":"If water reaches them, the engine is at risk."}]},{"id":"c89_wx","n":"Weather up high: what wind, ice and fog do to you","src":"Overland’s Habit · Hard terrain & extremes","p":[["Wind: don't underestimate it","In the mountains a strong wind is as serious as at sea, when pitching camp and at the wheel. Watch for it at high altitude, on scree and in forests with rotten or brittle trees. On a narrow track with mud, snow or ice, a gust that moves you a few centimetres is enough. Example: a shady bend next to a drop, frost, and a gust pushes the rear onto the ice. There you slow right down and keep away from the edge."],["The morning after a clear night","When a surface is colder than the air, moisture settles on it. With clear sky, no wind and temperatures below zero, that moisture turns to ice.<br>• Windscreen: scrape with something wide and turn on warm air. <b>Never water</b>, it refreezes at once.<br>• If the day was sunny on a snowy mountain and the night was clear and still, expect <b>transparent, slippery ice</b> in the shady spots."],["Driving in fog","Fog is cloud on the ground. It forms when warm, moist air cools over cold ground, and it clears when it warms up or the wind blows. Common in snow, near lakes and rivers, and early in the morning with frost.<br>• <b>Fog lights on</b>, even if you think you're alone on the mountain.<br>• <b>No high beams or spotlights</b>: they bounce back off the droplets and blind you.<br>• In very thick fog you may feel dizzy and lose your sense of balance, especially after standing still for a long time. It usually passes once you start moving again."],["Blizzard: know when to turn back","A blizzard means heavy snow with very strong wind, very low temperature and almost no visibility. In our climate snow gathers up high. Rule: <b>however high you get, somewhere above there is snow you cannot pass</b>. Don't overestimate the vehicle or yourself."]],"q":[{"q":"You wake up at camp and the windscreen is frozen. Which move is wrong?","a":["Scraping with a wide object","Pouring water from the jerrycan","Turning on warm air"],"w":"The water refreezes at once and makes it worse."},{"q":"Thick fog on a deserted mountain road. What do you do with the lights?","a":["Use only the roof spotlights","Turn them off so you don't get dazzled","Fog lights on, not high beams"],"w":"Strong light reflects off the droplets; fog lights make you visible without blinding you."},{"q":"Sunny day on the snowy mountain, then a clear, windless night. What awaits you in the morning?","a":["Slippery, transparent ice in the shade","Melted snow everywhere","Hail"],"w":"Meltwater refreezes overnight."}]},{"id":"c89_light","n":"Lightning in the mountains: where to be, what to do","src":"Overland’s Habit · Hard terrain & extremes","p":[["Why it concerns you","A flash between clouds is cloud-to-cloud discharge. A lightning strike goes from cloud to ground, with a bright flash and a bang like an explosion. People outdoors, hikers, campers, anglers, people on boats, are the ones hit most often, usually out of ignorance. Even a nearby strike can leave burns, hearing damage, arrhythmias or nerve problems. Stay calm, but take it seriously."],["Pick the right spot","• Get off peaks, ridges and open ground; away from lakes and large rivers.<br>• Above the tree line? Get down as fast as you can.<br>• Don't pitch a tent next to the tallest trees. Never under a lone tree, better to get wet. Inside a forest, stand among lower trees.<br>• Leave the motorbike or bicycle. Keep away from metal fences, poles and wind turbines."],["If it catches you outside","• Take off anything metal: keys, phone, radio, belt with a metal buckle.<br>• Crouch on your heels. <b>Don't sit on the ground, don't lie down</b>, more contact with the ground means more current through you.<br>• In a group: at least <b>5 m</b> apart from each other.<br>• Don't step into the open as soon as it eases; many strikes happen at the end of the storm."],["The car is your shelter","Lower the tall antennas and get inside. The metal body works as a Faraday cage: if it is hit, the current runs through the shell and not through you. The noise will be terrifying, but you are protected.<br><b>Getting out:</b> the tyres don't earth the vehicle, so charge may remain. Don't touch metal while already standing on the ground, <b>jump out</b> in one move."],["If someone is hit","A person who has been struck carries <b>no</b> charge; touch them at once. Check if they are breathing. If there are no signs of life, start <b>CPR</b> and call 112. Don't leave them alone until help arrives."]],"q":[{"q":"The storm catches you in an open meadow, far from the car. What position do you take?","a":["Lie face down in the grass","Crouch on your heels, without sitting down","Run to the tallest tree"],"w":"The less contact with the ground, the less current passes through the body."},{"q":"There are four of you on the mountain and lightning is striking. How do you stand?","a":["In a line, holding hands","Huddled together in one spot","Spread out, at least 5 m from each other"],"w":"That way one strike doesn't hit everyone."},{"q":"Someone has been struck and isn't breathing. What do you do?","a":["Touch them at once, start CPR and call for help","Wait for them to 'discharge' before touching","Put them in the car and do nothing else"],"w":"They carry no charge and immediate CPR can save them."},{"q":"The storm has passed and you are getting out of the vehicle. What do you watch for?","a":["Not touching metal while standing on the ground","Getting out on the driver's side","Opening all the windows first"],"w":"The vehicle may still be charged, since the tyres don't earth it."}]},{"id":"c89_terr","n":"When it rains in the mountains: rocks, collapsing tracks and sudden water","src":"Overland’s Habit · Hard terrain & extremes","p":[["The decision comes first","A mountain dirt track usually sits on loose ground, cut in a hurry and never maintained. After rain you can find anything: deep mud and standing water, fallen trees, a track cut away, swollen streams, gullies running along it or across it. If serious bad weather is coming and you are on unstable ground, at a river or high up: <b>drop the plan, get down low, don't cross rivers</b>. If in doubt, head for the quickest safe road."],["Where falling rocks come from","• <b>Rain:</b> water carries loose material down. Even once the water has cut a channel, the material may keep coming. It can't be predicted and a whole slope may go, you leave quickly and find a safe spot.<br>• <b>Frost:</b> on sunny faces meltwater gets into cracks, freezes at night, expands and splits the rock. The ice holds the pieces in place, until the sun melts it. <b>That</b> is the most dangerous time to pass underneath.<br>• If you have to pass under a bare, steep slope: turn radio and music down, stop the chat, open the window a little and listen."],["A track that gives way","Persistent rain soaks the ground unevenly, depending on what lies beneath. Soft soil on unstable subsoil sinks and takes mud and a piece of track with it.<br>• The weight of the car can make it worse. The mud may be very deep and hide rocks or gullies.<br>• Before you go in, make sure you won't end up trapped in the middle.<br>• Fallen trees, old landslides and deep gullies mean a weak area: expect more further on.<br>• In a group: long gaps, low speed, music low, and <b>no stopping on an unstable spot</b>."],["Flash flood","Rare, but treacherous. A wall of water that can reach <b>3–5 m</b> comes down very fast with mud, logs and boulders. It can arrive <b>in a stream bed that is dry right now</b>.<br>Before crossing a stream, near a dam or when it's raining higher up:<br>• Stop, turn the music off, listen upstream for a roar.<br>• Look for branches moving while there's no wind around you.<br>• Black clouds over the mountain, distant thunder, or a sudden silence with no birds? Wait, listen, and don't go in if in doubt."]],"q":[{"q":"When is it most dangerous to pass under rocks that froze overnight?","a":["At dawn, before the sun is up","When the sun warms them and the ice melts","Late in the evening"],"w":"The ice holds the broken pieces; when it melts, they fall."},{"q":"You want to cross a dry stream bed, but high above you the mountain has black clouds. What do you do?","a":["Cross quickly, since it's dry","Stop, listen for a roar and wait if in doubt","Park in the stream bed and wait"],"w":"A flash flood can arrive even in a dry stream bed."},{"q":"Three vehicles are crossing a track that has subsided. What is the big mistake?","a":["Keeping long gaps","Keeping the music low","Stopping on the unstable section"],"w":"Standing weight on soft ground can make it give way."}]},{"id":"c89_aval","n":"Avalanche: warning signs and how to react","src":"Overland’s Habit · Hard terrain & extremes","p":[["Two wrong beliefs","Many think avalanches happen only on very high mountains and with huge amounts of snow. Both are wrong. An evenly covered slope above the tree line, with a few bushes and rocks, and wet spring snow is enough. The car may be breaking trail easily, and a little further on a fresh mass can block the track or come down on whoever is passing."],["Why snow slides","By day the sun softens and melts the snow; at night it refreezes. This cycle, almost every day, builds layers that don't bond. Meltwater runs between the fresh layer and the old, frozen one and acts like oil. That's why the danger is mainly <b>during the day</b>. Often the lower layer goes and leaves the upper slab unsupported, especially on grass or smooth rock slabs."],["When and where to be suspicious","• <b>25 cm</b> of fresh snow is enough for a big avalanche.<br>• Lots of snow in a few hours = higher danger for the next <b>24 hours</b>.<br>• Most come down on slopes of <b>30°–60°</b>, not just the very steep ones.<br>• Suspect slopes: old frozen snow underneath, smooth rock, or grass with sparse bushes.<br>• Suspect weather: spring drizzle and days of slow, repeated temperature swings.<br>• Judgement comes with experience. In spring, in such places, always be suspicious."],["If it carries you away","• <b>The car does not protect you</b>: even a small avalanche can flip it or crush it.<br>• If you set it off, try to get out to the side, out of the flow.<br>• If it takes you: back to the flow, head up, mouth closed, swimming movements to stay on top.<br>• When you feel it slowing: one strong push upwards, if you know which way that is, and arms in front of your face to keep an air space.<br>• While moving, the snow is soft; once it stops, it sets like concrete."]],"q":[{"q":"25–30 cm of fresh snow fell in a few hours. What do you conclude for the next day?","a":["It's not enough for an avalanche","The danger is higher for the next 24 hours","Only slopes above 70° are at risk"],"w":"A little fresh snow is enough, and the danger rises after rapid snowfall."},{"q":"Which time of day is usually the most dangerous in spring?","a":["During the day, while the snow melts","Midnight","Just before dawn"],"w":"Meltwater lubricates the layers and they slide."},{"q":"An avalanche is carrying you and you feel it is about to stop. What do you do?","a":["Stay still so you don't sink","Keep shouting for help","Push upwards and keep an air space in front of your face"],"w":"Once it stops the snow sets hard; the air space keeps you alive."}]},{"id":"c89_xside","n":"Across a steep slope: preparation and handling","src":"Overland’s Habit · Hard terrain & extremes","p":[["Know the limits before you go in","On a side slope gravity works against you all the time. If you get onto a slope that doesn't go through, you have only one good option: reverse, slowly, in exactly the same tracks. Otherwise the vehicle rolls to the bottom or until something big stops it.<br>• With everything ideal, an average 4×4 just reaches <b>40°</b> sideways. Lifted or loaded, much less. For an average 4×4, <b>30°</b> is already the limit.<br>• You don't need an inclinometer: on a big side slope assume you are always close to rolling.<br>• Check and set <b>tyre pressures</b> beforehand. With soft, comfort suspension better not to try: a small rock or pothole starts a rocking that can end in a rollover."],["Walk it and build the tracks","• Walk the whole line. Look separately at where the upper and the lower wheels will go.<br>• <b>Upper wheels:</b> clear a strip at least <b>twice the tyre width</b>. Remove rocks, branches, anything that could lift them even for a moment. Level bumps with the shovel. If you have hands and time, dig a shallow groove for them to sit in.<br>• <b>Lower wheels:</b> same strip, focusing on holes and soft spots that will sink and increase the lean. Support with ramps or whatever you find there.<br>• The fact that the ground held you means nothing: it will take a tonne or more, at an angle. Loose material gives way easily. In mud or snow, also count how slippery it is."],["Load and people","• Empty the roof rack.<br>• Heavy items inside go on the <b>uphill side</b>, and tied down well, shifting load is one of the most common causes of rollovers.<br>• <b>All passengers out</b>, away from the downhill side. Whoever guides stands in front of and above the vehicle."],["On the crossing","• 4×4, low range, <b>1st</b>. Slowly, step by step.<br>• Few, steady steering movements. No nervous hands, no wheelspin.<br>• <b>Lockers off.</b> The wheel that climbs over a bump needs to turn a little faster. Locked, they all turn together and one spins, and on a slope wheelspin swings the tail downhill."],["If it starts to go","• <b>Never</b> try to hold a sliding or tipping vehicle with your hands. Get away.<br>• People hanging off the upper side help only for a momentary wheel lift, and only if there is no drop below. On a long, steady slope they risk being caught underneath.<br>• If the slope needs outside support, tie the vehicle beforehand with straps to fixed points above, but on a long slope there is rarely anything solid.<br>• If you slide sideways and a wheel grips again or catches on an obstacle, the vehicle swings sharply. Correct with throttle and steering, the right move is often the opposite of instinct.<br>• Bring it to a stop with gentle inputs, breathe, and decide: carry on or reverse back? There is no room for ego here."]],"q":[{"q":"You are preparing the ground for the upper wheels. How wide a strip do you clear?","a":["Equal to the tyre width","At least twice the tyre width","The whole wheelbase"],"w":"That gives you margin if the vehicle shifts a little."},{"q":"Where do you put the heavy gear inside the vehicle before the crossing?","a":["On the downhill side","On the roof rack","On the uphill side, tied down well"],"w":"It acts as a counterweight and doesn't shift."},{"q":"Why do you leave the lockers off on a severe side slope?","a":["So the wheel meeting a bump can turn faster, without wheelspin","To save fuel","To brake better"],"w":"With lockers on, a wheel spins and the tail goes downhill."},{"q":"Which side slope is already the limit for an average 4×4?","a":["About 15°","About 30°","About 50°"],"w":"40° is the absolute limit only in ideal conditions."}]},{"id":"c89_xwater","n":"A swollen river: how to read it before you go in","src":"Overland’s Habit · Hard terrain & extremes","p":[["The decision first","Fast or very deep water is dangerous for people and the car, however well it is built. Never alone, never without a second vehicle, never without experience. Turning back is not cowardice. Team, gear and experience make the difference, a strong vehicle on its own can end up 'swimming' and wedged on rocks.<br>• The current can push you off line, pin you against boulders or roll you; here low weight is a disadvantage.<br>• On such a crossing <b>seat belts stay undone</b>, so you can get out fast.<br>• You will get soaked. Cold water and exhaustion in winter put a heavy load on the heart.<br>• Water gets in everywhere. The only real fix: sealing and a snorkel with the right filter."],["Read the water","You check: bed, depth, speed and direction of current relative to entry and exit, ground and slope on both banks, water and air temperature.<br>• <b>Noisy water</b> = rocks below. You won't sink, but you slip and there are hidden steps. The vehicle with low range, lockers, height and big wheels goes first.<br>• <b>Quiet water</b> = mud below. You don't know how deep the mud is. The most powerful vehicle also goes first, or it may stay there for months.<br>• At a wide point the river is usually shallower and slower. On bends and behind big obstacles it gets deeper.<br>• Wheel tracks going in on one side and not coming out on the other: bad sign.<br>• A wide, calm river with islets is not automatically safe: the surface dries, underneath there may be trap mud or sand."],["Measure on foot, safely","The best depth gauge is a person crossing on foot. But <b>ALWAYS</b> with a strong stick and <b>tied to a long rope</b> whose other end is tied to a fixed point, not held in someone's hand. If the current takes them, nobody holds them by hand. Waders protect, but if they fall they fill with water: they must come off at once. If a person can walk it, the vehicle can usually drive it. How much depth you can take depends on vehicle, driver and conditions, there is no single number for everyone."],["One move, from entry to exit","You check entry and exit as carefully as the bed: mud, sand, rocks, gullies. In a river there is no 'try, back up, try again', nor 'charge in and see'. You plan the whole crossing in advance and do it <b>in one continuous move</b>, with a set sequence of actions.<br>• <b>A tow strap fitted to every vehicle</b> before it goes in, even if the first ones crossed easily."],["Cold water and ice","• Hot engine into icy water: light lenses crack, discs and drums warp, and the pressure difference sucks water through the breathers into diffs, gearbox and transfer case, damage you don't see at once. Let things cool for <b>30–45 minutes</b>.<br>• On ice: check the thickness strictly. Shallow water means stronger ice, but the middle of a lake is not like its edges. If you cross: <b>one vehicle at a time</b>, at fairly good, not excessive, speed.<br>• If it breaks, people must be out of the water <b>within 10 minutes</b> at most, hypothermia. The vehicle is usually recovered backwards; plan for the last vehicle too, in advance."]],"q":[{"q":"The water ahead is noisy and foaming. What bed do you expect?","a":["Bottomless mud","Rocks, with slippery cobbles and hidden steps","Smooth sand"],"w":"Babbling water is breaking over rocks."},{"q":"Someone wades in to measure the depth. How do you secure them?","a":["With a stick and a rope tied to a fixed point","With a rope you hold in your hand","Give them waders and that's enough"],"w":"If the current takes them, no hand can hold the load."},{"q":"You've been driving for hours and reach an icy river. Before going in…","a":["Go straight in while the engine is hot","Pour water on the bonnet to cool it","Wait 30–45 minutes for the systems to cool"],"w":"Sudden cold cracks, warps and sucks water into the boxes."},{"q":"The ice looks solid. How do you cross?","a":["All together in convoy, close up","One at a time, at fairly good but not excessive speed","Very slowly, with the first one towing the others"],"w":"Less load at any moment and no stopping on the ice."}]},{"id":"c89_stall","n":"Deep water: the bow wave, stalling and the key you don't turn","src":"Overland’s Habit · Hard terrain & extremes","p":[["Rule number one","If you play in deep water, sooner or later the engine may stall in it. Then: <b>you don't crank it</b>. Everything else comes after."],["How the wave helps you","When you move steadily and slowly, the vehicle pushes a wave ahead of it, like a boat's bow. The flow around it lowers the level a little behind the grille, keeps water away from the engine bay and cabin, and pushes you gently forward."],["The right speed","• <b>Too fast:</b> a big, powerful vehicle charging in raises a huge wave and drowns the engine.<br>• <b>Too slow:</b> the wave doesn't have time to form.<br>• <b>Lifting off midway:</b> the water following you catches up and gets in through every hole, even one second is enough.<br><b>The right recipe:</b> enter with controlled momentum, and in the deep part drive slowly with <b>steady</b> throttle.<br>• In a large body of water (flooded field, big pond) you are pushing tonnes of water. A tired 60–80 hp engine may not cope. If you follow a taller, stronger vehicle, the water in front of you rises once it pulls away.<br>• Fit a tow strap <b>before</b> you go in, so you don't have to find the points under water."],["Why cranking breaks engines","If you are not used to holding low revs with steady throttle, the engine stalls easily, from a handling mistake or, worst, because it swallowed water. Pistons compress air; water <b>does not compress</b>. If it gets into the cylinders, conrods and valves bend. With some luck the engine stops before the damage is done, and a hasty crank finishes it. Water gets in mainly through the air intake, more rarely through the exhaust."],["If you suspect water in the engine","1. No cranking.<br>2. Tow the vehicle out of the water.<br>3. Dry the intake thoroughly with a dry cloth.<br>4. Remove <b>all the spark plugs</b>. Then crank a few times: if there is water, it will shoot out of the holes.<br>5. Keep going until nothing comes out.<br>6. Wipe, spray plugs, leads and electrics with water-displacing spray (e.g. WD40), refit and try."],["If it starts to float","In deep water, depending on weight and how sealed the cabin is, the vehicle may lift. Usually the lighter rear lifts first, you lose grip and then control. Fix: <b>open a door</b> to let water in so it settles again, <b>not</b> on the side the current comes from, and hold it firmly or tie it. Wet carpets are a small price."]],"q":[{"q":"The engine stalls in the middle of a deep crossing. What do you do first?","a":["Crank it straight away while it's hot","Don't start it; tow the vehicle out of the water","Open the bonnet in the water"],"w":"If water got into the cylinders, cranking will bend the conrods."},{"q":"You are at the deepest point with the wave ahead of you. What must you avoid?","a":["Lifting your foot off the throttle","Holding steady revs","Keeping the wheel straight"],"w":"If you lift off, the wave following you catches up and floods the vehicle."},{"q":"Why does cranking with water in the cylinders destroy the engine?","a":["Because water shorts the starter","Because water thins the oil","Because water doesn't compress and the pistons bend conrods and valves"],"w":"The piston stops against the water and the force goes into the parts."},{"q":"The rear lifts and you feel you are floating. Which door do you open?","a":["The one on the current side","The one opposite the current, holding it firmly","None, you close the windows"],"w":"Incoming water weighs the vehicle down; on the current side the door would be torn from you."}]},{"id":"c89_line","n":"A safety wire over an obstacle: when and how","src":"Overland’s Habit · Hard terrain & extremes","p":[["Do you really need it?","You rig a safety line only when there is no other way: ice of doubtful strength, a very steep spot or a crumbling gully where the vehicle could slide sideways, or a river with a strong current. It is <b>work for the experienced</b>, learn it first alongside people who have done it. A very deep, fast river with mud or boulders on the bed is not crossable; you look for another route.<br>• It takes heavy gear, many hours and a lot of work. People and a vehicle hang on it.<br>• <b>The whole group</b> must truly agree. Nobody goes in because they were pushed."],["Three conditions, or nothing","1. Before the line is rigged, at least one person or one vehicle can get across, by any means. If not, you stop here.<br>2. The ground gives a safe entry and exit ramp.<br>3. There is one absolutely solid anchor point near the entry and one near the exit. <b>A car does not count as an anchor</b>, however heavy."],["Gear and ratings","• You need people on both banks at the same time. Anything you send across is tied to a rope first, so you can pull it back if it sinks.<br>• Know the wire rope's rating for sure (from the tag, if it's still there).<br>• <b>Pulleys, shackles and straps</b> must be rated for the load.<br>• In a river the force of the water is enormous, far more than your weight, or even the vehicle's."],["Rigging","• One <b>continuous wire rope</b>, tensioned over the obstacle, anchored to a fixed point on each side.<br>• <b>Exit lower than the entry</b>, so the wire slopes towards the exit and the pulleys roll by themselves.<br>• In a river the wire goes <b>at an angle, against the current</b>.<br>• Thread <b>two pulleys</b>. On each, fit <b>two straps of about equal length</b>: one pulley for the front of the vehicle, the other for the rear.<br>• Link the pulleys with a rope of about <b>4 m</b> (one vehicle length), with a free end that reaches the start, to pull them back after each vehicle.<br>• Every knot, shackle and pin is checked twice."],["The crossing","• Front pulley straps to the front recovery points, rear pulley to the rear ones.<br>• Fit one extra strap to a front point, with an end that reaches the far bank, for a winch pull if the vehicle can't make it on its own.<br>• In a very strong current you may need the windows open, so water flows through the cabin and pushes the vehicle less. But first <b>tie the doors shut with rope</b>."]],"q":[{"q":"You are looking for an anchor for the line on the bank. What is ruled out?","a":["A big, solid boulder","A heavy car","A thick, healthy tree"],"w":"However heavy, a car can move."},{"q":"How do you set the wire height between entry and exit?","a":["Exit higher than the entry","Exactly the same height","Exit lower, so the wire slopes that way"],"w":"That way the pulleys roll to the exit by themselves."},{"q":"No one in the group can get to the far side by any means. What do you do?","a":["Don't rig a line, look for another route","Send the strongest vehicle first","Throw the wire across and hope it catches"],"w":"Without people on both banks the line can't be rigged safely."}]},{"id":"c89_xsnow","n":"Deep snow: settings, throttle and descents","src":"Overland’s Habit · Hard terrain & extremes","p":[["Read the snow","From mid-December to April mountain tracks are usually under snow. What makes it hard is the cold, the thin air that robs the engine of power, and the depth, density and texture of the snow. The older it gets, the worse: it firms up and freezes, turns to slush, or a fresh layer sits on ice. Soft spring snow can hide black ice underneath. Depth changes every few metres.<br>• Don't use the full width of the track: ditches and rocks hide at the edges.<br>• Fallen rocks under the snow can't be seen even by the most experienced.<br>• <b>When windrows start to multiply, a little higher the track closes</b>, even if nothing else shows it. That is the time to turn back."],["Drivetrain: what to lock and when not to","In SUVs the electronics usually decide. In a proper 4×4, if you have the choice: low range, the highest gear that won't dig, centre and rear lockers engaged.<br><b>Exception:</b> on a track with <b>side slope</b> or mostly <b>icy</b>, the locker can kill you, it multiplies sideways sliding. If you don't have the experience, leave it."],["Throttle, wheelspin, counter-steer","• At some point the snow packing onto the tyres will take your steering away. That's expected.<br>• In general, gentle throttle. But there are moments when you need a hard push and wheelspin to regain control and put the vehicle on line, with <b>throttle and opposite lock</b>.<br>• <b>Controlled wheelspin</b> gives a push and cleans the treads. Uncontrolled spin digs, polishes the snow and buries you. The less you spin, the less you get stuck.<br>• A light vehicle on narrow tyres may get further than a heavy one, until its ride height stops it.<br>• If momentum won't beat the snow and you have no more power, stop throttling.<br>• This needs practice. <b>Never</b> on a public road with traffic."],["Descents, braking and turning round","• Two dangers: you don't stop where you expected because of the slipperiness, or you descend for kilometres and at the end can't climb back up the icy slope. Alone, without chains or a winch, it's a hard spot.<br>• Brakes are usually stronger at the front, so the rear wheels don't lock first. A <b>locked centre diff</b> cancels that balance and the tail can step out on a slippery descent, <b>even without braking</b>, just by shifting down.<br>• <b>No sudden braking</b>: friction melts the snow, which refreezes at once into a strip of ice under the tyres.<br>• Turning on a narrow track: shovel clear only the turning spot. A helper pushes the nose or tail sideways while you spin a little; the handbrake does the same. Practise first on a snowy open lot."],["Beached on the belly","A wrong guess of the depth or a bit of carelessness braking is enough. If there's no other vehicle to pull you: shovel and patience. Dig out the packed snow under the belly, mainly under the <b>differentials and half-shafts</b>, and get out <b>in reverse</b>, the way you came."],["Before you try this for real","On ice, on loose or steep ground, or in a river with current, use these techniques only if:<br>• you have had relevant training;<br>• an experienced instructor is with you;<br>• you already have experience;<br>• you have practised them in a controlled, safe place;<br>• a second vehicle is there for support."]],"q":[{"q":"Snowy track with side slope and ice underneath. What do you do with the lockers?","a":["Lock them all for maximum grip","Lock only the front","Leave them off"],"w":"On side slope or ice, lockers increase uncontrolled sideways sliding."},{"q":"You are going down a slippery descent with the centre diff locked. What is the danger?","a":["The tail steps out, because the front/rear braking balance is lost","The gearbox overheats","The engine stalls"],"w":"A locked centre brakes both axles equally and the tail can get away."},{"q":"You are climbing and windrows on the track are multiplying. What does it mean?","a":["The track higher up will be clear","The track will soon close","Fog is coming"],"w":"It is the most reliable sign you are nearing impassable snow."},{"q":"Why don't you brake hard in snow?","a":["Because it wears the pads","Because friction makes a strip of ice under the tyres","Because the engine stalls"],"w":"The snow melts from the heat and refreezes at once."}]},{"id":"c1011_assess","n":"Someone's stuck? Think first, then pull","src":"Overland’s Habit · Recovery & breakdowns","p":[["Stop before you grab a strap","When a vehicle stops, your first move is not to pull out the gear. It is to stand still and look. One calm idea often does more than another 50 horsepower or a winch. In the field things rarely go the way you picture them, and rushing is the surest way to make them worse. Usually you have time to study it, take it."],["What you weigh before deciding","• What exactly is the problem? What could go wrong?<br>• What do you have on your side?<br>• What state are the people in, how tired?<br>• What does the spot offer: trees, rocks, logs to anchor to?<br>• What are you carrying: winch, straps, snatch blocks, ropes, shovels?<br>• How much daylight is left?<br>• What options exist? Which is simplest, which is safest, which is fastest if time is short?"],["Discuss together, one voice leads","Objections are raised <b>before</b> anything is rigged and before cable comes off the drum. Once the plan is set, everyone works for it, including those who disagreed. One person coordinates, gives clear instructions, and everyone knows their job. Anyone anxious or panicking is kept at a distance. Improvising during the pull brings chaos and dangerous mistakes."],["Picture changed? Freeze everything","The anchor may turn out rotten, or a rock may appear under the mud. Then you don't carry on out of stubbornness. You stop, look again, and before doing anything different everyone hears about it. No quiet changes halfway through."],["Keep it simple, save your strength","The more pulleys and angles, the more points that can fail. Complex rigs are tried in practice with your group, not in a real recovery. On the mountain you choose one well-thought-out pull. And watch for <b>fatigue</b>: it clouds judgment, stirs up tempers and egos, and lies behind many accidents."],["Where good judgment comes from","Without experience and without a second vehicle, you don't attempt a hard crossing, no matter how much gear you have. Knowledge comes from reading with a critical eye. Experience comes only from being out there, testing your own limits and the vehicle's, ideally with more experienced people or in club training. Experience isn't how many years you've owned a 4×4; it's how many times you've solved a problem in the mud. Over time you also develop an instinct that tells you \"not this\"."]],"q":[{"q":"A team member has objections to the plan. When is the right moment to raise them?","a":["When the strap goes tight and you see if it works","After the recovery is over","Before anything is rigged"],"w":"After the decision everyone executes the same plan; changes during the pull cause mistakes."},{"q":"Mid-recovery you discover a rock under the mud that changes things. What do you do?","a":["Stop, reassess and tell everyone","Quickly change the angle without losing time","Pull harder to get past it"],"w":"New facts need a new assessment, and everyone must know about the change."},{"q":"You want to learn a multi-pulley rig. Where do you try it?","a":["At the next real recovery","In a practice session with your group","Alone, so you can concentrate"],"w":"Complexity raises risk a lot; learn it where a mistake costs nothing."}]},{"id":"c1011_stuck","n":"Alone and stuck: the first steps","src":"Overland’s Habit · Recovery & breakdowns","p":[["Before the hard section","See mud, snow or sand ahead? Prepare now, not once you're buried.<br>• Tyre pressure: around <b>−30%</b> for mud or snow, around <b>−50%</b> for sand. You gain grip, you lose a little ground clearance. Reinflate once you're out.<br>• <b>Low range</b> in the shortest gear, so the wheels turn without spinning.<br>• <b>Lockers</b> in, if you have them."],["While crossing","The secret is <b>steady speed</b>. Don't crawl and don't charge in spinning, both leave you there, especially if you hit a hidden hole or rock. Let momentum carry you. Inside the obstacle you don't stop, don't change gear, don't lift off and don't stab the throttle. Same revs, same pace."],["The moment you feel it bogging down","• First, lift your foot. Every spin digs a hole.<br>• If you're still moving a little, ease off the throttle and turn the wheel gently left-right, the tyres often find something to bite.<br>• If you've stopped, try reverse gently, along your own tracks.<br>• Rock back and forth calmly, gaining a little each time. A few moves may pack a ramp you can climb out on."],["Give the tyre something to grip","In front of and under the spinning wheels lay whatever you find: floor mats, branches, stones, boards, even cloths or clothes."],["With the jack","If you're held by something that can move (a rock, a log), lift the vehicle and pull it out from under. If it won't move, lift and build a ramp with stones or whatever you have to drive over it. On soft ground the jack sits on a solid board so it doesn't sink. No-exception rule: <b>never</b> go under a vehicle held only by a jack, only on solid blocks."],["When it won't come out on its own","If the chassis is sitting on mud, snow or sand and the wheels no longer bear weight, improvising is over. You need a second 4×4 to pull you or a winch."]],"q":[{"q":"There's a snowy stretch ahead. Roughly how much do you drop the pressure?","a":["Around 30%","Around 50%","You leave it alone"],"w":"About −30% for mud or snow; −50% is for sand."},{"q":"The wheels are spinning and the vehicle has stopped. What is the right next move?","a":["More throttle to dig down to hard ground","Off the throttle, then gentle reverse along your tracks","Shift to a higher gear"],"w":"Spinning buries you; you get out calmly, with gentle rocking."},{"q":"You need to clear something under the lifted vehicle. What must be true?","a":["The jack is holding firmly","Someone has a hand on the handbrake","It rests on solid blocks"],"w":"Never under a vehicle held only by the jack."}]},{"id":"c1011_tow4x4","n":"When another 4×4 pulls you out","src":"Overland’s Habit · Recovery & breakdowns","p":[["Check at home","You need <b>two solid recovery points at the front and two at the rear</b>, rings or hooks in good condition. Inspect them regularly: in every recovery they are the first to take the load."],["Rig the connection","• Pull from the centre: one strap links the right and left points (a bridle), and the tow strap connects to its middle with a shackle or pulley so they don't rub on each other.<br>• The two legs of the bridle form an angle <b>under 45°</b>. That way the load is shared and the points aren't at risk.<br>• Shackle: screw the pin in hand-tight to the end and back it off <b>half a turn</b>. The load will tighten it; if you tighten it fully, it won't undo afterwards."],["Where the tow vehicle stands","Ideally the heavier vehicle pulls the lighter one. Before it approaches mud, snow or sand, walk the ground and find how close it can get without getting stuck too. Ideally both vehicles and the strap are in a <b>straight line</b>. If that's not possible, know that once the strap goes tight the system will try to line itself up."],["When the ground has its own opinion","• On a slippery slope, the vehicle being pulled will slide downhill, toward the steepest fall line, wherever the tow vehicle pulls.<br>• If there's a drop below, secure <b>both vehicles</b> to a fixed point with a <b>second strap</b>.<br>• In deep ruts, the vehicle will follow the ruts. If they lead the wrong way, dig new ones with the shovel in the right direction."],["The pull itself","First <b>slowly take up the slack</b> until the strap is tight. Then pull steadily and smoothly. No jerks, no shoves, no back-and-forth with slack. And don't hold the clutch halfway: it burns out fast."]],"q":[{"q":"You rig a bridle on the two front points. What angle between the legs do you want?","a":["As wide as possible, over 90°","Under 45°","It doesn't matter"],"w":"A small angle shares the load properly between the two points."},{"q":"How do you close a shackle pin?","a":["Hand-tight to the end, then half a turn back","Tighten it with a wrench to be sure","Leave it half screwed in"],"w":"Under load it tightens itself; the half turn lets you undo it afterwards."},{"q":"You're recovering a vehicle on a slippery slope above a drop. What do you add?","a":["More speed in the pull","Lower tyre pressure on the tow vehicle","A second strap securing both vehicles to a fixed point"],"w":"The vehicle will slide toward the slope and may drag the tow vehicle with it."}]},{"id":"c1011_winch","n":"Working with a winch and snatch blocks","src":"Overland’s Habit · Recovery & breakdowns","p":[["When you bring it out","• There's no heavier vehicle to pull.<br>• The vehicle is stuck very deep.<br>• The tow vehicle has no firm ground to stand on.<br>• Or you simply judge that a winch is simpler and safer.<br>Respect it: it's huge force in a small box. Learn its manual and, if you can, take training. Most accidents happen when routine makes you skip the details."],["Snatch blocks: force instead of speed","Every time the cable runs through a snatch block and comes back, the pull grows. With one block you get roughly <b>double</b> the force, with two roughly <b>triple</b>. The price: the vehicle comes in slower, about half speed with one, around a third with two."],["When a straight line won't fit","Narrow track, bad ground, rocks or trees in the way? Anchor a snatch block to the side and change the direction of the pull. In these cases the block is not a luxury, it's a necessity."],["Don't kill the cable","• Wire rope pulled tight over a branch, a ring or a sharp edge, or run through a small pulley, is <b>permanently deformed</b> and its life shortens.<br>• Anything that bends it must have a diameter of at least <b>4 times</b> the cable diameter. Pulleys at least <b>6 times</b>.<br>• <b>Never</b> join two cables with clamps. At peak load they slip and the wires whip free, they can kill."],["Spooling out, spooling in, gloves","• The cable wants to twist when loaded.<br>• When spooling out, don't let the drum turn faster than you're pulling; take out only what you need.<br>• When spooling in, the cable goes on under tension. If the winch doesn't do it by itself, a helper keeps tension. Slack cable rides over its wraps and kinks.<br>• Anyone touching cable or operating the winch wears <b>work gloves</b>. Always."]],"q":[{"q":"You add a second snatch block to the rig. What changes?","a":["Force rises to about triple, speed drops","Nothing, only the angle changes","Both force and speed increase"],"w":"You gain pull and pay with speed, about a third."},{"q":"Your cable is 10 mm in diameter. What minimum pulley diameter do you want?","a":["20 mm","40 mm","60 mm"],"w":"Pulleys at least 6 times the cable diameter; other bend points at least 4."},{"q":"The cable is too short and you consider joining a second one with clamps. Right?","a":["Yes, if you use three clamps","No, at peak load they slip and the cable whips","Yes, for small loads"],"w":"The joint fails exactly when tension is at its maximum."}]},{"id":"c1011_nowinch","n":"Pulling without a winch: emergency options","src":"Overland’s Habit · Recovery & breakdowns","p":[["Read this first","When you have no winch and no second vehicle, you can improvise. But these methods are <b>the last option</b>. They put heavy loads on the tools, the vehicle and the people. They suit only a light vehicle and need full attention."],["A strap twisted with a stick","• Tie a long strap to one recovery point, run it around a tree or other fixed anchor and bring the other end back to the second point.<br>• Put a bar or a thick, long piece of wood between the two legs. Someone turns it, the strap twists and shortens bit by bit.<br>• The driver helps with minimal throttle, no wheelspin.<br>• On a heavy, loaded vehicle of several tonnes, forget it: the force is huge and the stick can spin back violently."],["The wheel becomes a drum","• On the spinning wheel you fix a \"drum\". There's a ready-made part in 4×4 shops. Otherwise put a block in the centre of the rim and on top a strong piece of wood or a long tool (big adjustable wrench, wheel brace), tied through the rim holes, with the ends sticking out.<br>• Tie a rope to an anchor <b>in line</b> with the wheel, tension it and wind the other end on the improvised drum. Think first about which way the wheel will turn.<br>• Short gear, gentle throttle, the wheel turns as slowly as possible. The rope winds up and the vehicle moves toward the anchor.<br>• Make sure the rope doesn't catch brakes, lines or other parts behind the rim."],["Hi-lift for pulling","• Open the hi-lift fully. One strap from one end goes to the vehicle, one from the other end to the anchor.<br>• Take up the slack by hand and start closing it with the handle.<br>• Use chain where you can, so you don't waste the tool's travel just tightening straps.<br>• Each cycle moves you only as far as the hi-lift's travel. For more, secure the vehicle, release, and rig again."],["The risk you take on","With the wheel-drum, the <b>half shaft</b> carries the whole pull plus the resistance of the mud, work it was never built for. It can break. If you do it, you do it knowing that. With all these methods, anyone not working stays clear of straps and ropes under tension."]],"q":[{"q":"You use the wheel as a drum. Which part of the vehicle is stressed most?","a":["The half shaft","The battery","The cooling system"],"w":"It carries the entire pull, which it wasn't designed for, and can break."},{"q":"You're pulling with a hi-lift and the first cycle is done. How do you continue?","a":["Pull the handle harder to gain more","Secure the vehicle, release and rig again","Engage reverse to help"],"w":"Each cycle gives only as much as the tool's travel."},{"q":"You have a heavily loaded vehicle of several tonnes. Does the twisted-strap method suit it?","a":["Yes, as long as two people turn the stick","Yes, with more throttle","No, the forces are huge and the stick can spin back violently"],"w":"It's an option only for light vehicles."}]},{"id":"c1011_anchor","n":"Where to tie: anchors and the buried anchor","src":"Overland’s Habit · Recovery & breakdowns","p":[["The basic rule","An anchor is anything that won't move: a tree, a post, a rock. You want the car to come, not the tree to be uprooted or the rock to shift. So you tie <b>low</b>, as close to the ground as possible. If the first anchor doesn't convince you, tie it back to a second one and share the load."],["On a tree","The bark must not be damaged, strip it and the tree dies. Use a wide protective strap (tree protector) or, if you don't have one, the car's floor mats in between. Don't loop a choking noose around the trunk; it saves you a shackle but kills the tree."],["On a rock","• Big, well set into the ground, not moving at all.<br>• Shaped so the strap can't ride up and slip off the top when it goes tight.<br>• Choose wrong and you lose the anchor at the worst moment and the cable becomes a whip.<br>• Where the strap touches a sharp edge, put cloths or mats in between. Under tonnes of load, an innocent edge cuts like a blade."],["Nothing there? Bury one (deadman)","• <b>You'll need:</b> the spare wheel (or a log), a pole or steel bar, a strap, rope, a shovel, strength and patience.<br>• <b>Where:</b> straight ahead of the stuck vehicle, where the cable reaches when almost fully unspooled. That leaves few wraps on the drum and the winch at its maximum pull.<br>• <b>How:</b> dig a hole deep and well bigger than the spare. Tie the strap tightly to it and lower it in. Drive the pole or bar in at an angle so the spare can't rotate and the strap can't slip once the pull starts.<br>• Cut a trench so the strap leaves the hole at about <b>45°</b>. Where it reaches the surface, place a round log or stone so it rolls over it instead of digging into the soil."],["Before you give the signal","On slippery or sloping ground, the vehicle won't necessarily come toward the anchor. It will head downhill or follow the ruts. Allow for it in the rigging."]],"q":[{"q":"You tie a strap to a tree for a winch pull. At what point on the trunk?","a":["As high as you can reach","At bumper height, whatever that is","As close to the ground as possible"],"w":"Low down the tree takes less leverage and doesn't get uprooted."},{"q":"On a bare beach you bury the spare wheel as an anchor. Where do you dig?","a":["Straight ahead, where almost all the cable reaches","Next to the stuck vehicle, to keep it close","Wherever the ground is softest"],"w":"With few wraps on the drum the winch pulls at maximum force."},{"q":"The strap runs over a sharp rock edge. What do you do?","a":["Nothing, the strap can take it","Put cloths or mats in between","Wet it so it slides"],"w":"Under load the edge cuts the strap."}]},{"id":"c1011_lift","n":"Lifting the vehicle and moving it a distance","src":"Overland’s Habit · Recovery & breakdowns","p":[["When you need to lift it","Rarely. When the vehicle has bellied out in sand or bog, when it dropped into a gully that looked smaller, or when a log or rock suddenly ended up underneath. You lift to remove the obstacle, fill the hole or move the vehicle itself. Usually a strong pull is easier, but it can rip whatever is under the chassis."],["With a tripod and a winch or tow vehicle","• Jack broken or nowhere to stand? Build a strong tripod or \"A-frame\" in front of or behind the vehicle, from logs or two bars.<br>• Tie to the vehicle, run the strap or cable <b>over the top of the tripod</b> and pull slowly.<br>• Put something metal where the cable bears, or it will cut the wood.<br>• Block the lifted vehicle, then lower gradually, by easing the winch or moving whichever vehicle can drive."],["With a log as a lever","• No winch: find a thick, strong, long log. The bottom end sits on a stone or block so it doesn't sink.<br>• For the front, tie it firmly to the front bumper, leaning back. Reverse the vehicle slowly (on its own or towed) and it rides up onto the lever. Do the job, then move forward under control to remove the log.<br>• For the rear, the opposite: on the rear bumper, leaning forward, and move forward.<br>• Only with a <b>steel</b> bumper (original or aftermarket). The plastic one on a modern SUV will break."],["Moving it for kilometres: protect the driveline","This isn't about a few metres of recovery. When wheels turn with the engine off, they drive the differentials and gearboxes and can cause damage that can't be fixed. On a part-time 4×4, disengage 4WD. On a full-time one, disengage the <b>centre differential</b> and, if the transfer case has a neutral position, use it. Do exactly what the manufacturer says, especially on SUVs with complex systems."],["Roadside assistance and friends","• With roadside assistance, ask for it to be <b>loaded onto a flatbed</b>, not towed. Many don't know a 4×4's quirks.<br>• Check the differentials before and right after delivery. You don't know what happens during transfers, and only this way can you claim compensation.<br>• If friends tow you: the tow vehicle takes up the strap slowly and pulls off gently. You, in the towed vehicle, keep the strap <b>always tight with light braking</b>, no jerks and no daydreaming.<br>• Towing with a strap on public roads is <b>prohibited</b> under the Greek Highway Code (ΚΟΚ). There's a fine (check the current one) and insurance won't cover you."]],"q":[{"q":"Your full-time 4×4 will be towed several kilometres. What do you disengage?","a":["Only the lockers","Nothing, as long as the gearbox is in neutral","The centre differential (and neutral on the transfer case if it has one)"],"w":"Otherwise the wheels drive the driveline with the engine off."},{"q":"Your SUV has a plastic bumper. Can you lift it with a log lever?","a":["No, the bumper will break","Yes, if you move slowly","Yes, only from the rear"],"w":"The lever loads the bumper heavily; it needs a steel one."},{"q":"A friend is towing you on a dirt road. What's your job as driver of the towed vehicle?","a":["Help with throttle","Keep the strap tight with light braking","Stay in neutral and touch nothing"],"w":"A tight strap prevents the jerks that cause damage."}]},{"id":"c1011_winchsafety","n":"Pulling safely: the rules that don't bend","src":"Overland’s Habit · Recovery & breakdowns","p":[["The right attitude","Whoever works slowly and by the rules isn't timid, they're experienced. Don't rush, stay calm and keep away anyone who makes you anxious. Give clear instructions. And don't underestimate fatigue, neither others' nor your own."],["Where no one stands","• Never step over a tight cable or strap and never stand next to it.<br>• No one behind or beside a vehicle being pulled.<br>• Everyone else stands at least as far away as the length of the tight cable. If it snaps, the ends whip back and can maim.<br>• Hang a damper on the cable or strap, or a blanket, a jacket, a mat, to cut its path if it breaks. The distance stays the same, though."],["Gear that holds","• Shackles, straps and snatch blocks need a certified working load limit of about <b>150% of gross vehicle weight</b>.<br>• You size it to the <b>heaviest</b> vehicle in the connection. A small SUV's shackle isn't enough for a heavy pickup, check the marking first.<br>• Tie to <b>both points</b> with a bridle: it needs less force and does less damage.<br>• <b>Never</b> pull from the tow ball."],["Before every winch use","• Check the cable for rust and broken strands, especially if it's not used often. If you find some, unspool the bad section, work with the rest and replace the cable once home.<br>• Don't kink it and don't run it through small pulleys.<br>• Remove all twists it picks up when unspooled. When tied to the anchor, it should lie slack with no urge to twist.<br>• No vehicle drives over the cable while it's on the ground.<br>• A last look over all of this before you press the button."],["After the job","Ideally two people unspool the whole cable and rewind it neatly, without twists and without one wrap riding over another. Lubricate it as the manufacturer says. And <b>work gloves, always</b>: broken strands can't be seen, but they cut."]],"q":[{"q":"You've put a blanket over the tight cable. How close can spectators come?","a":["As close as they like, the blanket is enough","They stay at least the length of the tight cable away","Two or three metres"],"w":"A damper reduces the whip; it doesn't cancel the distance rule."},{"q":"A light and a heavy vehicle are connected for a recovery. What do you size the shackle to?","a":["The lighter one","The average of the two","The heavier one, at about 150% of its gross weight"],"w":"The gear must withstand the biggest load in the connection."},{"q":"The pulling vehicle has no rear ring, only a tow bar with a ball. What do you do?","a":["Don't use the ball; find a proper recovery point","Loop a strap over the ball","Use the ball but pull slowly"],"w":"The ball isn't designed for that load and can become a projectile."}]},{"id":"c1011_mech_engine","n":"Engine, power, fuel: field first aid","src":"Overland’s Habit · Recovery & breakdowns","p":[["It won't start","<b>The starter doesn't turn at all:</b><br>• Check the battery terminals first, rough tracks loosen them.<br>• Flat battery: jump leads from another vehicle, with the donor's engine <b>running</b> so its battery doesn't drain too. Red on +, black on −.<br>• No donor but you have a downhill: ignition on, second gear, clutch down, push, and once rolling release the clutch.<br>• Battery and leads fine and still nothing: probably the ECU, workshop.<br><b>The starter turns but the engine won't fire:</b><br>• Do you have fuel? If so, maybe a blocked filter.<br>• Has the low-range lever been knocked into neutral by a jolt or a bad shift?<br>• Otherwise probably the fuel pump, workshop."],["It runs, but won't move","You select a gear, press the throttle and nothing. With an automatic, check the selector isn't in N. In any case check whether the transfer case (low range) lever is sitting in neutral. Put it back in the right position."],["It coughs and cuts out","• <b>Water or bad fuel</b> (from a river crossing or a dodgy station): drain the tank with a transfer pump or rubber hose, with the end held low. Watch where you dump it and no cigarettes anywhere near. Blow out the filter too.<br>• <b>Wet or muddy electrics</b>, loose or worn plug leads: spray the contacts with moisture-displacing spray and secure anything hanging with insulating tape or tie wraps. A worn wire must not touch metal.<br>• <b>Loose battery terminals</b> not making good contact.<br>• <b>Fuel pump</b>: workshop."],["The engine has overheated","• Watch the temperature gauge, but know that with a cooling fault it may read wrong too.<br>• Switch off and let it cool.<br>• <b>Never open the radiator cap hot</b>, the system is under pressure and boiling water will spray out.<br>• Wait long enough. Gloves, a cloth over the cap, open it little by little, body and face to the side. A floor mat in front of you works as a shield.<br>• Then, with the engine running, add water or <b>coolant of the same colour</b> slowly. In a sealed system, top up the overflow tank once the temperature has dropped.<br>• From then on, keep an eye on the gauge all the time."],["Warning lights and electrics","• <b>Check engine</b>: the ECU has logged a fault. A single one is no drama, but if it stays on you don't know how many have built up. With an <b>OBD reader</b> you can read the code yourself and judge how serious it is. Once home, have a workshop read and clear the memory.<br>• <b>Lights, radio or heater dead</b>: fuses first. Then the harness connectors, in the engine bay, under the console, in the driver's and passenger's footwells. Jolting works them loose."]],"q":[{"q":"Another vehicle is jump-starting you. What's right?","a":["The donor's engine stays off for safety","The donor's engine runs, red on +, black on −","It doesn't matter which colour goes where"],"w":"A donor with its engine running doesn't drain its own battery."},{"q":"The starter turns normally, the engine won't fire, and you've just come off very rough ground. What do you check early?","a":["Whether the low-range lever ended up in neutral","Tyre pressure","The radio fuses"],"w":"Jolts can move the lever; along with fuel it's one of the first checks."},{"q":"The engine has overheated badly. Which move is dangerous?","a":["Switching off and waiting","Putting on gloves before touching the cap","Opening the radiator cap straight away"],"w":"The circuit is under pressure and will spray you with boiling water."}]},{"id":"c1011_mech_chassis","n":"Steering, brakes, wheels, oil: read the signs","src":"Overland’s Habit · Recovery & breakdowns","p":[["Stop immediately","<b>Oil pressure light:</b><br>• Stop now. Low level means zero pressure, check and top up.<br>• Level correct and the light stays on: either the sensor has failed or the system is losing pressure. That also leads to overheating.<br>• If you absolutely must move, keep fairly high revs in low gears to build pressure, without overdoing it.<br><b>Brake warning light or a pedal that sometimes drops into nothing:</b><br>• Stop and top up fluid to max, not above. It's mildly corrosive. Bleed the system if you know how.<br>• If it continues, look for a leak: someone presses the pedal while you watch hoses and lines. If you find it, isolate that circuit (front or rear) with whatever you have. From then on you're braking on two wheels only.<br>• Power brakes with no fluid: you press with all your strength and get roughly half the deceleration."],["The steering is heavy or pulls","• <b>Heavy, manual steering:</b> usually lost pressure in a tyre, set it to the manufacturer and terrain. If play has increased, have the rack or steering box checked; a hard knock loosens bolts.<br>• <b>Heavy, power steering:</b> check the fluid level and top up, or at the first fuel station, it won't suffer for a short while. Level fine: worn pump or lines. Completely without assist: the belt probably broke or came off after a knock, you struggle on to the workshop.<br>• <b>Pulls to one side:</b> camber or a damaged road, uneven pressures, a knock that spoiled the alignment (no immediate danger, but get it checked with proper equipment soon), a bent rim, or bad loading that tilts the vehicle, unload and load properly from scratch."],["It shakes above a certain speed","• Balance weights fell off: not dangerous, drive at the speed that shakes least.<br>• Mud in the rims: clean inside and out. The weight upsets the balance and tires out mechanical parts. If it doesn't go away, tyre shop.<br>• Bent rim: a steel one straightens with a few sledgehammer blows; aluminium, never. If the bend is large, fit the spare, the tyre may suddenly deflate."],["You hear something you shouldn't","• <b>Metallic noise and vibration in tight turns:</b> wheel bearing, half shaft or U-joint. Replace soon, and it won't be cheap.<br>• <b>Loud squeal from the engine,</b> mostly when cold, in winter: loose or worn alternator, water pump or fan belt. Tighten or replace.<br>• <b>Like gravel inside the engine,</b> louder at some revs: loose or worn timing chain or belt. Workshop <b>urgently</b>, or valves, pistons and cylinders are at risk. Until then, drive at the revs where it's quietest.<br>• <b>Bird-like chirping from the brakes:</b> probably warped discs (common if you drove hot into water or snow). If the pads squeal, they're probably worn out, many have a wear indicator. Workshop soon."],["Clutch and gearbox","• The engine runs normally but the car moves hesitantly in gear with throttle: the clutch is slipping. Can't be fixed in the field, workshop.<br>• The lever sticks and some gears go in hard or not at all: clutch or synchromesh. Workshop urgently."]],"q":[{"q":"The oil pressure light comes on mid-route. What do you do first?","a":["Carry on to the first village","Stop immediately and check the level","Raise the revs to build pressure"],"w":"Without oil pressure the engine destroys itself quickly."},{"q":"You hear a gravel-like sound inside the engine at certain revs. What does it most likely mean?","a":["A stone in the tyre tread","Something normal with a cold engine","Loose or worn timing chain/belt, workshop urgently"],"w":"If timing is lost, damage reaches valves and pistons."},{"q":"You hit something and the aluminium rim is badly bent. What's the right move?","a":["Fit the spare","Straighten it with a sledgehammer","Drop some pressure and carry on"],"w":"Hammer only on steel rims; with a big bend the tyre can suddenly deflate."}]},{"id":"c35_kitplan","n":"What goes in the vehicle, and why","src":"Overland’s Habit · Vehicle kit","p":[["Start from the trip, not the list","Split your gear into three piles: what the <b>vehicle</b> needs, each person's <b>personal</b> kit, and the <b>shared</b> camp gear. A list helps you not forget things; it doesn't oblige you to take everything. The aim: what you will really need, and nothing that only eats space and weight."],["Ask yourself before you load","• What kind of trip is it: easy, hard, or in between?<br>• How many days, how many people, which vehicles?<br>• How difficult is the terrain and what is special about it?<br>• What weather does the season bring at that altitude?<br>• Does it need special clothes, footwear or supplies?<br>• How often will you find water, food, fuel?<br>• Do you have maps and recent information on the route?<br>• Is the first-aid kit full and the vehicle in good shape?"],["What you are loading for","You load for whatever stops you or makes driving unsafe: mud, snow, a river, a fallen rock, a landslide, a breakdown, a flat tyre. The kit must get you out of these without becoming a problem itself through sheer bulk. And the two most valuable items weigh nothing: <b>calm</b> and <b>clear thinking</b>."],["The vehicle basics (pick to suit)","• <b>Papers and money:</b> registration, licence, insurance, roadworthiness certificate, IDs, VHF licence if you have one, cash, not just cards.<br>• <b>Navigation and comms:</b> map, compass, phones, PMR/CB/VHF, spare antenna.<br>• <b>Recovery:</b> jack with base block, short and long straps, rope, shackles, stakes or ground anchor, pulley, shovel, crowbar, traction boards, winch, chains in winter.<br>• <b>Tools and spares:</b> tool set, saw, axe, oil, brake fluid, coolant, WD40, spark plugs, belts, fuses, bulbs, wire, tape, owner's manual, spare keys.<br>• <b>Power and light:</b> compressor with gauge, cigarette-socket splitter, jump leads, torches, chargers, batteries.<br>• <b>Safety and work:</b> warning triangle, fire extinguisher, first-aid kit, gloves, rubber boots, rain jacket."],["When you are the organiser","Make a plan your skills can handle, so everyone enjoys it without needless risk. Newcomers usually fall into one of two traps: they take almost nothing because they feel confident, or they fill the car out of enthusiasm and then can't find anything. Pack so nothing rattles and everything comes out fast, with the most useful items in the easiest spot."]],"q":[{"q":"How do you use a kit list properly?","a":["Always take everything on it","Read it and keep what suits this particular trip","Ignore it once you have experience"],"w":"The list is a reminder; the type of trip decides what you load."},{"q":"A newcomer fills the car to the roof 'just in case'. What is the risk?","a":["None, the more the better","He will always be missing the first-aid kit","He won't find what he needs quickly and carries useless weight"],"w":"Overloading is one of the two usual extremes; the other is taking too little."}]},{"id":"c35_radio","n":"Group comms: which radio for which job","src":"Overland’s Habit · Vehicle kit","p":[["Why a phone isn't enough","First rule: without experience, don't go to the mountains alone, find a club or a group and learn by doing. Inside a group the phone is awkward: no signal everywhere, and you won't call people one by one to say the same thing. On a radio one person talks and everyone hears, instantly and free. Keep the phone for emergencies: roadside assistance, police, fire brigade. <b>112</b> works even without a SIM card. If you go alone, carry SIMs from all <b>three networks</b>. A satellite phone works almost everywhere (not in caves or tunnels), but handset and calls are expensive."],["CB: the convoy radio","4 W power, 40 channels, and with a good antenna a few kilometres even without line of sight, that's why 4×4 clubs prefer it. Each club usually has its own channel.<br>• <b>FM</b> for short distances inside the convoy: clean sound.<br>• <b>AM</b> when someone has fallen behind: slightly longer range, more noise.<br>• No licence is needed any more; keep the purchase papers showing conformity. Amplifiers ('linears') are illegal."],["The antenna makes the difference","An antenna of at least one metre, fitted permanently by a technician, powered from the battery, and with the <b>SWR tuned</b> with a meter. Untuned, you lose range and may burn the transmitter. Higher works better, but most people mount it at the rear (bumper or spare-wheel carrier) so it doesn't hit branches. A magnetic antenna on a cigarette plug is fine for a trial, not for good, it picks up noise."],["PMR 446: the pocket radio","Cheap, small, legal without a licence. Up to 0.5 W on 446 MHz FM, so they can't talk to CB. Car to car about 500 m; in open ground with line of sight over 10 km. Only 8 channels, so near towns they get busy. American FRS units are illegal in Europe. Ideal once you're out of the car: spotting a driver on an obstacle, walking, the taverna. Choose models on AA/AAA batteries, ideally rechargeable, standard cells last 1–2 days."],["VHF/UHF: licence only","They are sold freely, but using them <b>without a licence is forbidden</b> and can lead to confiscation or even arrest. With an amateur radio licence you get more power and mountain-top repeaters for nationwide contact (144–146 MHz VHF, 430–440 MHz UHF). The licence comes through exams in radio theory and regulations at the regional authorities; for the entry class a few weeks of study is enough, ask the Radio Amateur Association of Greece. Active members of a recognised Civil Protection volunteer team can also get a limited VHF licence. <b>Marine VHF</b> is forbidden on land, not in the car."]],"q":[{"q":"Your phone has no SIM and there's an accident. What do you do?","a":["Nothing, no SIM means no calls","Call 112","Wait until you find your own network"],"w":"112 works even without a SIM card."},{"q":"The convoy is moving close together. Which CB mode do you use?","a":["AM","It doesn't matter","FM"],"w":"At short range FM gives clean sound; AM only if someone drifts far away."},{"q":"You fitted a CB antenna but didn't tune the SWR. What happens?","a":["Poor range and a risk of burning the transmitter","Nothing, it's just a bit quieter","It only works on AM"],"w":"SWR tuning protects the set and gives proper range."},{"q":"You want radios to guide a driver from outside the car. What can you use without a licence?","a":["PMR 446","Marine VHF","American FRS"],"w":"PMR 446 is licence-free in Europe; FRS and marine VHF are not."}]},{"id":"c35_radiorules","n":"Talking clearly on the radio","src":"Overland’s Habit · Vehicle kit","p":[["Before you transmit","• Listen for a moment: someone may be talking faintly and you'd cover them.<br>• Press PTT and let about <b>one second</b> pass before you speak, or your first word is lost.<br>• Know what you'll say before you press, the frequency is shared."],["While you talk","• Say first who is calling whom, e.g. 'Tail to lead, do you copy?'.<br>• Slowly, clearly, steady tone, no swearing, children or strangers may be listening.<br>• Release the button only after your last word is out.<br>• When two people are talking, wait for the one who was asked to reply, then add your comment.<br>• Save small talk for the stop."],["Voice, mic, wind","• Don't shout: the sound distorts. Normal voice, mic at <b>5–10 cm</b>.<br>• With cabin noise or at speed, turn the front of the mic towards the inside of the cab.<br>• Outside in wind, turn so the wind hits your face, not the mic."],["Settings and sharing","• Set <b>squelch</b> exactly where the noise just disappears. Higher cuts weak signals and you lose whoever drifts away.<br>• On CB at very short range, prefer FM so receivers aren't swamped by strong signals.<br>• Leave short gaps between transmissions: someone may need to break in for help.<br>• It all rests on good manners, with a little practice they become habit."]],"q":[{"q":"A friend says you always sound 'cut off' at the start. What do you fix?","a":["Speak louder","Wait about one second after pressing PTT","Hold the mic closer to your mouth"],"w":"The transmitter needs a moment to open; otherwise the first word is lost."},{"q":"You can no longer hear the last car that dropped behind. What could be wrong?","a":["Squelch set too high","Squelch set where the noise just disappears","You're talking on FM"],"w":"High squelch cuts weak, distant signals."},{"q":"Why leave short gaps between transmissions?","a":["To rest the battery","So the transmitter doesn't overheat","So someone asking for help can break in"],"w":"Channels are shared; an urgent call must find room."}]},{"id":"c35_jacks","n":"Lifting the 4×4: which jack where","src":"Overland’s Habit · Vehicle kit","p":[["Safety first","• <b>Never get under a vehicle held only by a jack</b>, no type is a safety stand.<br>• Put a thick, wide block underneath (a carpenter can cut you one or two): you gain height and the jack doesn't sink.<br>• A stone only if there's nothing else: it can sink or slip, and the car can fall."],["The factory jack and the hydraulic","The crank-handle mechanical jack that comes with the car is for a tyre change on tarmac. On soft ground, on a slope, with lifted suspension or mud tyres it often won't even reach, let alone lift a wheel out of a hole. A hydraulic jack (bottle or workshop trolley type) is quick and easy. Before buying, check <b>lifting tonnage</b> and <b>maximum height</b>, enough for a heavy, tall 4×4."],["Hi-Lift","A heavy mechanical jack with long travel: lifts even a very tall vehicle on almost any ground and slope. It also works as a makeshift hand winch, anchor point or lever, and is sometimes the only way to change a tyre on a lifted car. The price: long, heavy, awkward to carry, and it needs <b>strong lifting points</b>, steel bumpers, custom brackets or an adapter that grips the wheel."],["Inflatable (airjack)","A cushion of tough plastic: you lay it flat under the car and inflate it with a compressor or the exhaust gas, via a hose on the tailpipe. It spreads the weight over a large area, so it is <b>the best choice in mud, snow and sand</b>, with a wide safety margin. It is slow, doesn't lift very high, and dislikes sharp stones and hot exhausts, on rock it bursts. Buy a strong one that lifts your weight high enough."]],"q":[{"q":"You're stuck in deep sand and need to lift the wheel. Which jack works best?","a":["The factory crank jack","The inflatable airjack","The hydraulic bottle jack"],"w":"The airjack spreads the weight over a large area and doesn't sink."},{"q":"You bought a Hi-Lift. What does your vehicle need to use it?","a":["A 12V socket","Lowered suspension","Strong lifting points that carry the full weight"],"w":"Without proper bumpers or brackets the Hi-Lift slips or bends bodywork."},{"q":"You want to check something underneath for ten seconds while the car is on a jack. Allowed?","a":["No, never","Yes, if the ground is level","Yes, if it's hydraulic"],"w":"No jack is a safety support, however short your stay."}]},{"id":"c35_straps","n":"Towing: straps, ropes, shackles","src":"Overland’s Habit · Vehicle kit","p":[["The basic rule","Mud, snow, sand and water trap even the best 4×4, even on an easy dirt road. So a strap always lives in the car. Anything used for pulling, strap, chain, wire rope, rope, kinetic rope, must be rated for <b>at least twice your vehicle's weight</b>. The forces are huge: if something breaks, the pieces and the stretched strap fly like a projectile."],["What to take, what to skip","• Wide strap, rope or wire rope with ready-made eyes, chain with a tensioner, all work if they are seriously built.<br>• Avoid thin straps 'decorated' with plastic fittings.<br>• Check the eyes: prefer ones woven into the body of the strap. Simply stitched eyes can tear open on the first pull, even if the strap itself doesn't break.<br>• To anchor to a tree: a wide strap round the trunk, <b>never wire rope</b>. If the bark comes off, the tree dies."],["Kinetic rope: lots of force, lots of risk","The towing car moves off, the rope stretches, stores energy and 'flicks' the stuck car out. The stuck driver gives gentle throttle <b>only at the moment he feels it coming free</b>: earlier he digs in, later he misses the peak pull. It works superbly on heavy vehicles but is dangerous for beginners: it needs reinforced recovery points rated for many tonnes and strict safety rules."],["Plain ropes and their care","People with a sea background often prefer rope: for the same length and strength it is lighter, takes less space and with the right knots does many jobs. Anchor rope is cheap; pre-stretched rope costs more but doesn't twist or tangle. Keep synthetics out of the sun and wash them now and then in cold water with mild soap. When new, put an indelible mark e.g. every 5 m and measure it from time to time: if the gaps grow, the rope has stretched, start retiring it."],["Shackles","• <b>Two heavy-duty shackles per strap</b>: one on the towing car, one on the stuck car.<br>• Find them at chandlers; galvanised is enough.<br>• Screw the pin fully home, then back it off <b>half a turn</b>: under load it won't loosen, and afterwards it opens without pliers."]],"q":[{"q":"Your vehicle weighs 2.5 tonnes. What minimum rating does the strap need?","a":["2.5 tonnes","5 tonnes","1.5 tonnes"],"w":"Rule: at least twice the vehicle's weight."},{"q":"You must anchor to a tree. What goes round the trunk?","a":["A wide strap","The winch wire rope","A chain"],"w":"A wide strap doesn't strip the bark, so the tree survives."},{"q":"You've screwed the shackle pin fully home. Next step?","a":["Tighten it more with pliers","Leave it as it is","Back it off half a turn"],"w":"It won't loosen under load and opens easily afterwards."},{"q":"On a kinetic rope recovery you're in the stuck car. When do you use the throttle?","a":["Before the tow car moves","Gently, at the moment you feel it coming free","Never, stay in neutral"],"w":"Earlier you dig deeper, later you miss the peak of the pull."}]},{"id":"c35_anchor","n":"Ground anchors, pulleys and traction boards","src":"Overland’s Habit · Vehicle kit","p":[["A winch with nothing to tie to","No tree, no rock, no second car around you. If your route could take you there, get a <b>ground anchor</b> from a 4×4 shop: it has twin blades that dig into the soil and bite harder as you pull. In a pinch, big strong stakes or a spare wheel buried in the ground also work."],["What a pulley gives you","Two things: you <b>redirect the line of pull</b> wherever suits you, and you <b>multiply the force</b>. That way you can free a car stuck 'like a suction cup' in mud, or pull a vehicle heavier than your own."],["How to choose one","• Heavy duty, certified, with a stated maximum load.<br>• Rated for at least <b>twice the winch's pull</b>, like shackles, straps and chains.<br>• Large diameter, so it doesn't kink the wire rope.<br>• Groove width matching your own wire or rope.<br>• <b>Opening (snatch block)</b>: you feed the line in from the side, no free end needed."],["Traction boards and mats","Boards (traction boards, recovery tracks, sand/mud mats, PSP) are a pair of hard plastic or metal plates that go under the wheels where grip is missing. They work best <b>before you get stuck</b>: you see a section won't go, lay them down and drive over. If you've already sunk, you usually need a jack to get them under the wheel. They normally ride on the roof rack. Flexible metal or rubber mats are lighter, good for mud, sand and soft soil, not for rock, as they can't bridge gaps."]],"q":[{"q":"Your winch pulls 4 tonnes. What rating do you want on the pulley?","a":["At least 8 tonnes","4 tonnes","2 tonnes"],"w":"The pulley sees roughly double load, so it needs at least twice the winch's pull."},{"q":"The wire is already tied at both ends. What pulley lets you thread it?","a":["A fixed small-diameter one","Any, as long as it's galvanised","An opening snatch block"],"w":"A snatch block takes the line in from the side."},{"q":"You see a stretch of deep sand ahead. When do the boards come out?","a":["Only after you get stuck","Now, before you go in","Never in sand"],"w":"Boards work mainly as prevention; after you're stuck they need digging or a jack."}]},{"id":"c35_handtools","n":"Hand tools for the trail","src":"Overland’s Habit · Vehicle kit","p":[["For digging","Off-road you'll dig more often than you think. You want a strong shovel with a short handle, medium width and point, so it works in any soil, not a beach spade. If space is tight, get a military-style folding one. Check: tough material, a <b>thick pivot pin</b> and a <b>metal locking ring</b>, not plastic. Good ones also turn into a hoe."],["For rocks, ice and logs","For snowy mountains and gorges, strap a strong steel crowbar of about one metre to the rack: it breaks ice and moves stones and logs. If a fallen tree is loose, tie a strap to it and drag it aside; if it is still held by the roots, you'll have to cut it."],["Saw, and what to watch","Sawing by hand is tiring, so choose well:<br>• <b>Pruning-saw type</b>: fits between branches.<br>• <b>Bow or cord saw</b>: two-handed, for thicker wood.<br>A chainsaw or 12V electric saw cuts fast, but in some areas and seasons logging is banned and the noise attracts forest wardens. Be ready to show you're just clearing the road."],["Hatchet","Sturdy, with a short handle. It takes a little practice, but it cuts branches to get you through, and the back hammers in tent pegs."]],"q":[{"q":"You're choosing a folding shovel. What shows it's a good one?","a":["A plastic ring to save weight","A thick pivot pin and a metal locking ring","A very wide, pointed blade"],"w":"The hinge is the weak point; metal and a thick pin keep it solid."},{"q":"You cut a fallen tree on a forest road with a chainsaw. Why might you get into trouble?","a":["In some areas and seasons logging is banned","12V burns the battery","It won't cut wet wood"],"w":"The noise brings wardens and you must show you're not logging."}]},{"id":"c35_winch","n":"Winch: how strong, which one, how to keep it","src":"Overland’s Habit · Vehicle kit","p":[["What it is","A motor on the front of the car that winds steel wire rope or synthetic rope onto a drum. On 4×4s it is almost always <b>electric</b>; there are also manual, hydraulic and mechanical ones driven by the engine."],["Reading the numbers","• Pull is given in <b>pounds (lb)</b>: the load at which the motor stalls, however much current it gets. A '9' = 9,000 lb.<br>• For kilos divide by ~2.2; with 2.25 you get a round, conservative figure: 9,000 lb ≈ 4 tonnes, 6,000 lb ≈ 2.6–2.7 tonnes.<br>• The rated pull applies with <b>one layer</b> of wire on the drum. Each layer reduces it; with a full drum it drops to around <b>50%</b>.<br>• For safety we usually work from the second layer, so the wire's anchor on the drum isn't at risk."],["How strong to buy","Aim for at least <b>twice the vehicle's weight in pull</b>, because deep mud multiplies resistance. But more isn't always better: a car stuck 'like a suction cup' pulled with huge force may come out, or bend its chassis. If you have a light car and want to recover heavier ones, use a <b>pulley</b> instead of an oversized winch."],["Before you buy","• Pull and line speed, current draw.<br>• Mounting method, bumper shape and strength.<br>• What changes it needs: cutting the grille, moving lights.<br>• The Highway Code in theory doesn't allow changes to factory dimensions and length, ask a specialist."],["Other types and maintenance","The portable electric winch that hangs on the towbar is made for boats: cheap and light, but weak, slow, with a small drum that twists the wire, an emergency option, only with pulleys. Hydraulic and mechanical ones are heavy, complex and expensive; worth it only for continuous professional use. For maintenance follow the manual on lubrication, check brushes and bearings regularly, and if it has a drum brake, check the thermal cut-out and brake wear."]],"q":[{"q":"Roughly how much does a 9,000 lb winch pull?","a":["9 tonnes","2 tonnes","4 tonnes"],"w":"9,000 ÷ 2.25 ≈ 4,000 kg."},{"q":"The drum is almost full of wire. How much pull do you have compared with the rating?","a":["About half","The same","Double"],"w":"The rating applies on the first layer; each layer reduces it."},{"q":"You have a light 4×4 and often help heavier ones. Which is the better solution?","a":["The biggest winch that fits","A pulley in the line","Two straps in series"],"w":"A pulley multiplies force without an oversized winch loading the chassis."}]},{"id":"c35_rope","n":"Wire rope, synthetic rope and the hand winch","src":"Overland’s Habit · Vehicle kit","p":[["Steel wire rope: what to look for","Thin steel wires are twisted into strands around a core; more strands means more flexible. Galvanising protects it from weather but makes it a bit stiffer and slightly weaker. In practice a good choice is <b>6 or 8 strands</b>, and better still a non-rotating rope with more strand layers and a proper core."],["Caring for the wire","• Wire left wound up for years wears as much as wire that's abused. Unwind it now and then.<br>• Lubricate with <b>proper wire-rope oil</b>; slightly warmed, it penetrates to the core.<br>• <b>No grease</b>: it stays on the surface, collects dust and sand and eats the wire.<br>• No WD-40 for long-term lubrication: it's mainly a rust remover and over time can do harm.<br>• After heavy work: unwind, clean off mud and grass, lubricate."],["Synthetic rope: pros and cons","Made of Kevlar, Spectra or polyethylene (Dyneema and Plasma are brand names). <b>Pros:</b> lighter and stronger than wire, doesn't tangle, stores less energy so it isn't lethal if it snaps, and works with smaller pulleys. <b>Cons:</b> sensitive to abrasion, heat (e.g. from the drum) and sunlight, critical sections need a polyurethane sleeve. It wears without showing it and costs more."],["Hand winch","It has no drum: two jaws grip the wire and pull it as you work a lever. Choose it by the same criteria. The stronger, the heavier, get one you can carry to where it's needed. It takes some effort, but does impressive work."],["Example: road cut by subsidence","• Cross only if there's no drop-off or risk of further slides, you have daylight ahead, people, kit and rested drivers.<br>• Dig an entry and exit ramp and fill the mud with stones and branches.<br>• Send the <b>lightest, unloaded</b> vehicle first, while someone looks for an alternative.<br>• If it sticks, even a small one-tonne hand hoist can get it out, and then it pulls the rest."]],"q":[{"q":"Why not put grease on winch wire rope?","a":["It stays on the surface and collects dust and sand","It makes the wire slip on the drum","It's expensive"],"w":"Dust plus grease becomes an abrasive; proper oil gets inside the strands."},{"q":"What is the big safety gain of synthetic rope?","a":["It handles drum heat better","It never wears","It stores less energy, so it's less dangerous if it snaps"],"w":"Snapping wire whips; synthetic rope drops far more gently."},{"q":"You must cross a muddy subsidence. Which vehicle goes first?","a":["The heaviest, to open the way","The lightest, unloaded","Whichever has the newest tyres"],"w":"The light one sinks less and comes out more easily if it sticks."}]},{"id":"c35_chains","n":"Snow chains for a 4×4","src":"Overland’s Habit · Vehicle kit","p":[["Before winter comes","• Chains must be <b>for the size of the tyres you have on now</b>, if you changed tyres, the chains changed too.<br>• Do a trial fit in the yard in good weather. The first fitting shouldn't happen in the dark, in cold and wind, on a climb.<br>• Carry gloves and something to kneel on."],["Which type to buy","Classic chains have a chain tread and differ in density, extra links, pattern (ladder or diamond, the latter holds better sideways) and how they tension: rubber, rigid hoop, spring. For a heavy 4×4 on mud tyres you want <b>heavy duty, with a woven tread</b> that doesn't sink between the lugs. With snow tyres or AT you have more choice."],["After fitting","• Secure the loose end properly: if it flaps it hits the wheel arch, chips paint and can cut a brake line.<br>• <b>Stop after a few kilometres</b> and recheck tension, ties and fasteners. A chain can come off the wheel without you noticing."],["Spiders and socks: where they don't work","<b>Spider</b> chains go on fast, but on a tyre circumference of over a metre they have just four narrow grip points; under braking or a slide in a bend maybe none is touching. They suit straight roads with packed snow, not ice, mud and potholes in the mountains. Fabric <b>snow socks</b> give good grip on snowy tarmac, don't wear tyres or suspension and, if they fail, just tear. But they can't run where there's no snow, tear easily on dirt and with mud tyres, and it's not certain they'll be accepted as chains at a check. For mountains with a 4×4, chains."]],"q":[{"q":"You fitted chains and moved off. What do you do after a few kilometres?","a":["Nothing, they went on properly","Stop and recheck tension and fasteners","Speed up so they settle"],"w":"Chains settle and loosen; the check prevents damage or losing one."},{"q":"Where do spider chains make sense?","a":["On an icy forest road","In mud with potholes","On a straight road with packed snow"],"w":"With only four grip points they aren't enough for ice and rough ground."},{"q":"Heavy 4×4 on mud tyres. Which chains?","a":["Heavy duty with a woven tread","Snow socks","Spiders"],"w":"A woven tread doesn't sink between big lugs."}]},{"id":"c35_aircomp","n":"Air, tools and fluids in the vehicle","src":"Overland’s Habit · Vehicle kit","p":[["Tyre pressures: you can't do without these","Off-road you drop and raise pressures often, so you need a <b>gauge</b> to measure and a <b>compressor</b> to reinflate. Gauges come small or heavy-duty from accessory shops and good fuel stations. Compressors range from the cheap supermarket one to semi-pro units with a built-in gauge, or, at worst, a foot pump. If you have air lockers you already carry a strong pump: inflate the spare and mattresses, blow dust off seats and tent, fan a fire with damp wood."],["Tools, even if you're not a mechanic","Carry a tool set and a few basic spares even if you can't use them: a passer-by or someone in the group may know how. A loose tie-rod end, for example, can be fixed on the spot with simple spanners, as long as they're there. What exactly to carry is your mechanic's call; he knows your car's weak points."],["Fluids and the morning check","• If the car burns oil or loses coolant, carry top-up, don't hold the group up.<br>• <b>Every morning, before you set off</b>: look at the levels and top up.<br>• A small leak you spot in the car park won't strand you in the middle of nowhere."],["The right attitude","Whatever you take, something else will break. Carry the basics and let calm and ingenuity cover the rest."]],"q":[{"q":"Which habit prevents most breakdowns on a trip?","a":["Washing the car every evening","Checking levels every morning before setting off","Changing oil halfway through the route"],"w":"A look at the levels catches leaks before they become failures."},{"q":"Who do you ask what tools and spares to carry?","a":["An accessory salesman","A general forum","The mechanic who knows your car"],"w":"He knows your own vehicle's specific weak spots."}]},{"id":"c35_small","n":"Small things that save the day","src":"Overland’s Habit · Vehicle kit","p":[["Light","• Forget old torches with flat batteries; <b>LED</b> torches are small and run for hours.<br>• A <b>head torch</b> leaves both hands free.<br>• Sturdy, waterproof, with <b>spare batteries</b>.<br>• Always a <b>second torch</b>: if you have only one and it fails, you're in the dark."],["Power from the cigarette socket","A cheap socket splitter gives you several outlets for chargers, CB, GPS, cool box or compressor. <b>Careful:</b> it has a load limit, just like household ones; with big loads together it burns out."],["The odds-and-ends box","A small box that fixes small problems:<br>• Spare glasses, notepad and a <b>pencil</b>, a pen freezes in the cold and leaks in the heat.<br>• Wire, insulating tape, a bit of cable, string, cable ties in several sizes.<br>• Safety pins, needles, thread, fishing line.<br>• Two or three lighters, a small penknife besides your main knife, batteries.<br>• Sweets for low blood sugar and a few condoms, they also work as a water container."],["For the dirty jobs","Sooner or later you'll step out into dust, mud or snow to attach a strap. Have <b>work gloves</b> (cheap fabric ones with leather reinforcement are fine) and a bag of work clothes. It helps to keep a bag permanently in the car with a <b>towel, full change of clothes, wool socks, gloves and a beanie</b>, together with rubber boots and a rain jacket."]],"q":[{"q":"Why a pencil instead of a pen in the box?","a":["It's cheaper","It can be erased","A pen won't write in the cold and leaks in the heat"],"w":"A pencil writes at any temperature."},{"q":"You have a very good head torch. Do you need another?","a":["Yes, always a second one","No, if it's LED","Only in winter"],"w":"A torch that fails leaves you without light; the second is your backup."},{"q":"You plug a cool box and a compressor into the socket splitter together. What's the risk?","a":["The spare tyre deflates","The splitter burns out from overload","None"],"w":"Every splitter has a load limit; big consumers exceed it."}]},{"id":"c35_mandatory","n":"Extinguisher and triangle: working, not just present","src":"Overland’s Habit · Vehicle kit","p":[["Why it matters off-road","In most cars the extinguisher is missing, or small, unsuitable, expired or buried under the luggage. In town a fire burns one car; in a forest it can burn a whole mountain. Get a proper extinguisher and <b>practise beforehand</b>, when you see flames you won't be reading instructions."],["Which one and where","• In a <b>bracket under the driver's or passenger's seat</b>, as rallies require: you grab it as you get out.<br>• Compliant with European standards. For private cars a portable dry-powder unit is required for class A, B, C fires and electrical current up to 1000 V, minimum rating <b>8A, 34B, C</b>, secured inside the cabin, check current rules.<br>• Check the service date and pressure gauge before every big trip."],["Secure anything that moves","A metal tool that slides and touches the battery terminals throws sparks and can ignite carpets and cloth inside the car. Tie down jack, spanners and bars, and keep the extinguisher where you can reach it at once."],["Warning triangle","• Always carry it.<br>• Place it well behind the stopped vehicle, before a bend, around <b>100 m</b>, and weigh it down with stones.<br>• Choose a simple, stable one the wind won't knock over. It needn't be expensive: it may get run over or taken.<br>• If you have none, a chair or anything visible is better than nothing."]],"q":[{"q":"Where should the extinguisher be?","a":["Deep in the boot","In a bracket under the driver's or passenger's seat","On the roof rack"],"w":"There you grab it immediately as you get out."},{"q":"What minimum rating is required for a private car's extinguisher?","a":["2A, 8B","There's no specification","8A, 34B, C"],"w":"Dry powder for classes A, B, C and current up to 1000 V."},{"q":"You broke down just after a bend. What matters with the triangle?","a":["It goes before the bend and stands firmly","It's expensive and reflective","It goes right next to the car"],"w":"It must warn oncoming drivers in time and not blow over."}]},{"id":"c35_firstaid","n":"The 4×4 first-aid kit","src":"Overland’s Habit · Vehicle kit","p":[["How to put it together","Minor injuries, knocks and stings happen often in the field. The kit is like the spare wheel: you carry it even if you've never needed it. Build it with your <b>pharmacist</b>, who will tell you when and how each item is used and its contraindications, and ask every travel companion to bring <b>their own medication</b>."],["For wounds and knocks","• Cotton wool, sterile and non-sterile gauze, plasters<br>• Plain and elastic bandages, haemostatic bandage<br>• Antiseptics: alcohol, hydrogen peroxide, iodine<br>• Burn ointment, instant cold pack<br>• Orthopaedic neck collar<br>• Disposable gloves, small scissors, tweezers, thermometer"],["For pain, stomach, allergies","• The group's personal medication<br>• Painkillers, fever reducers, antispasmodics<br>• For nausea and diarrhoea<br>• Antihistamine tablets and cream, cortisone cream<br>• Adrenaline auto-injector (prescribed by a doctor)<br>• Ammonia (or stick) for stings, insect repellent, venom suction pump"],["Three items that make the difference","• <b>Neck collar:</b> a hard jump over a bank or a skidding stop can seriously injure the neck, even a vertebra. If there's severe pain or dizziness afterwards, the collar does no harm, moving without support can leave permanent damage.<br>• <b>Disposable gloves:</b> you don't know if the casualty has an infectious disease, maybe neither do they. They protect you both.<br>• <b>Adrenaline auto-injector:</b> someone may be allergic to wasps without knowing: racing pulse, breathing difficulty, swelling, an injection is needed at once. A non-medic can use an auto-injector by following the instructions on it (into the thigh, even through trousers). Get it via a doctor/pharmacist and learn how it works before you leave. Call <b>112</b> at once."]],"q":[{"q":"Nobody in the group is a doctor. Is an adrenaline auto-injector worth carrying?","a":["No, only a doctor can inject","Yes, it is made for anyone to use by following its instructions","Only in winter"],"w":"A severe allergy can appear for the first time in the field; the auto-injector is designed for non-medics."},{"q":"After a hard jolt the passenger has severe neck pain and dizziness. What do you do?","a":["Ask them to move their head to see if it hurts","Give a painkiller and carry on","Fit the collar and avoid any unnecessary movement"],"w":"Supporting the neck prevents permanent damage from a possible fracture."},{"q":"Who helps you best to stock the kit?","a":["Your pharmacist","Your mechanic","A list from the internet"],"w":"They know indications, contraindications and correct use for each medicine."}]},{"id":"c1216_plan_why","n":"Before you turn the key: why the plan matters","src":"Overland’s Habit · Trip planning","p":[["Start from the basics","You already know some things: the area or the starting point, whether you'll go alone or with others, and the dates. That's the base. Now you need to tie it into a proper plan."],["Kilometres lie","On dirt, distance tells you nothing about time. A short line on the map can eat your whole afternoon. Mountain and forest roads are often rough or unmaintained, and every spell of bad weather changes them.<br>• Washouts and landslides<br>• Mud, water-filled potholes<br>• Snow piled up by the wind<br>• Trees fallen across the road<br>Any one of these can slow you down or turn you back."],["Trust today's conditions","The map, a friend's report from last month, a comment on social media, your own memory from last year: all old news. What counts is how the road is today. So don't get attached to the final destination, unless it's the only way out. Always have a change of plan ready for every leg."],["What you gain","With good preparation you reach your goal safer, enjoy it more and put less strain on yourself and on nature. Without a plan the group runs late and takes risks for no reason. Find out beforehand whether any points are at risk from flooding, landslides or snow. And if you could end up stuck somewhere remote, carry <b>water, food and fuel</b>."],["Remember","<b>The plan doesn't take away the adventure. It's part of it.</b>"]],"q":[{"q":"A friend drove the road a month ago and says it's fine. What do you do?","a":["I treat it as definitely open","I take it as a hint, but check current conditions and keep an alternative","I cancel the trip"],"w":"Dirt roads change with every spell of bad weather; what counts is the conditions on the day."},{"q":"The route crosses a remote area. What must you carry?","a":["A powerbank","A spare pair of shoes","Water, food and fuel"],"w":"If you're stranded far from help, these keep you going until you get out."}]},{"id":"c1216_plan_goals","n":"Who's coming and what you want to do","src":"Overland’s Habit · Trip planning","p":[["Define the kind of trip","First decide the length: one day, a weekend or several days. Then the type: is there a specific pass you really want, or do you just want to explore an area? The clearer the goal, the easier the rest of the plan comes together."],["Measure people and vehicles","Look realistically at what everyone can handle, person and car. A driver with a brand-new 4×4 and no experience doesn't belong on a hard mountain destination in winter. Nor does a vehicle with no service history. Ask yourself the same question: do you know how, and are you able, to do what you're planning?"],["The pace of the weakest","The group moves as fast as its weakest member can manage.<br>• If one of the cars is less capable, the hard river crossing comes off the programme.<br>• With small children or people with health or fitness issues, the plan adapts to them.<br>• If someone is forced to drop out, some or all of you will drive extra to escort them out, and morale drops.<br>So look for company with similar vehicles, a similar driving level and the same expectations."],["Build it together","A plan takes time and experience. Get as many people involved as you can: it comes out more complete and everyone feels it's theirs."],["Not just the steering wheel","Put other things in the programme too: camping, cooking, photography, video. If the area allows, try rafting, canoe, kayak or rappel, organised or when the chance comes up."]],"q":[{"q":"In a group of four cars, one has no lockers and low clearance. The route has a deep river. What do you do?","a":["We all cross, we'll pull it out if it gets stuck","The weak car waits alone on the bank","We drop the river from the programme or find another way"],"w":"The route has to work for every vehicle; you plan around the weakest."},{"q":"Why does it cost the whole group when one member drops out halfway?","a":["Because some have to drive extra kilometres to escort them out","Because everyone has to pay their expenses","It doesn't, the rest carry on as normal"],"w":"Nobody is left alone; escorting them to the exit eats time and spirit."}]},{"id":"c1216_plan_info_fuel","n":"Fuel, local knowledge and help in the area","src":"Overland’s Habit · Trip planning","p":[["Ask those who've been","Maps and the internet help, but nothing replaces someone who knows the area. They'll tell you where to eat well, where to pitch a tent, what's worth seeing and where to be careful. A question in an online group often brings valuable answers."],["Fuel plan","In the mountains and remote areas fuel stations are few and quite a few have closed.<br>• Mark on the map the ones you find.<br>• Confirm they're operating: ask locals, the municipality or the nearest police station (the numbers are online).<br>• On a long mountain leg, make sure your range reaches the next <b>confirmed</b> station.<br>• If you have even a little doubt, carry some reserve fuel. An obstacle just before the station can send you on a long detour. For containers, check what's allowed."],["Where to find help","In Greece you're rarely truly far from a village, but don't take it lightly. Along with the fuel stations, find in the wider area:<br>• a clinic or health centre<br>• police<br>• fire service<br>• roadside assistance<br>Note where they are, how you get there and their phone numbers. Always keep 112 in mind."],["Dress for the altitude","Take clothes for the weather you'll meet up there, not the weather at home. Mountain winter is harsh, and in summer the evenings get chilly."]],"q":[{"q":"The map shows a fuel station in a mountain village on your route. What do you do?","a":["I call the municipality or the local police station to confirm it's operating","I rely on the map","I leave with half a tank, I'll fill up there"],"w":"Many mountain fuel stations have closed; confirmation from a local source is the only sure thing."},{"q":"Which list of phone numbers is most useful to have for the area?","a":["Tavernas and rooms to rent","Clinic, police, fire service, roadside assistance","Not needed, I always have signal"],"w":"If something goes wrong, you want to know right away where help is and how to call it."}]},{"id":"c1216_weather","n":"Read the weather before and during the route","src":"Overland’s Habit · Trip planning","p":[["Weather decides","Out in nature nothing affects the outing as much as the weather. Before you leave, check a detailed forecast for your area and days. If serious bad weather is coming, change the goal or the date. And always be ready for surprises."],["How much it changes with altitude","Up high you get more cold, more cloud and fog and stronger wind than down on the plain. Count on <b>about 1 °C less for every 150 metres</b> you climb. So, whatever the season, carry warm and waterproof clothing."],["What to check in the forecast","• <b>Precipitation:</b> above a certain altitude, in winter rain turns to snow. That decides whether the road goes through.<br>• <b>Temperature:</b> tells you what equipment and clothing you need.<br>• <b>Cloud and base height:</b> if the base is lower than where you're going, you'll be driving in fog.<br>• <b>Wind, strength and direction:</b> dangerous on difficult or icy ground, and when setting up camp."],["Where to get a forecast","A smartphone with internet is enough. Sites such as windy.com give a few days' forecast for almost every peak and ravine. For severe-weather warnings, also check the Hellenic National Meteorological Service (EMY)."],["Out on the route","Over time you'll learn to sense what's coming on your own. Watch for:<br>• clouds thickening or coming down<br>• a sudden drop or rise in temperature<br>• wind picking up or changing direction<br>When you see them, rethink the plan."]],"q":[{"q":"You start at 400 m with 16 °C and will camp at 1,300 m. Roughly what temperature do you expect there?","a":["About 15 °C","About 10 °C","About 0 °C"],"w":"900 m difference ÷ 150 ≈ 6 °C less, so around 10 °C."},{"q":"The forecast gives a cloud base at 1,000 m and your route crosses at 1,400 m. What does it mean?","a":["You'll surely have sunshine above the clouds","It doesn't concern you","You'll probably be driving in fog"],"w":"When you're above the cloud base, you're moving inside the cloud, i.e. in fog."},{"q":"You're going to the mountains in July. What clothes do you take?","a":["Warm and waterproof as well as summer clothes","Only summer clothes, it's July","Just a jacket for the car"],"w":"In the mountains cold and rain come in every season."}]},{"id":"c1216_terrain_snow","n":"When a mountain pass closes","src":"Overland’s Habit · Trip planning","p":[["Ground and weather together","Bad weather alone doesn't close a pass. What closes it is the weather combined with the surface (dirt, gravel, sand, rock), the gradients and the terrain. When you lay out the route, mark every point where you cross a stream or river without a bridge. There you always want a plan B."],["Snowdrifts: the spring trap","Wind piles snow into local heaps, <b>snowdrifts</b>. They can be many metres long or high, while the snow around them is just a few centimetres.<br>• You find them mostly on shaded, north-facing sides.<br>• They last even late into spring, when everything else has melted and the road up to there is dry.<br>• If one blocks the road, you turn back or take another route."],["What else winter leaves","Rain and snow leave behind landslides, subsidence, fallen rocks and deep washouts. Assume that after every spell of bad weather the road may be different."],["Pick the right season","Most of the mountain and forest network is opened and maintained <b>from May onwards</b>. It usually stays passable until late autumn. In winter many roads close again. Plan accordingly."]],"q":[{"q":"Late April, the road up to the saddle is dry. Where is snow most likely to stop you?","a":["On a shaded north-facing side","On a sunny south-facing slope","Nowhere, it's melted everywhere"],"w":"Snowdrifts last long on shaded north sides, even when everything around has melted."},{"q":"You want to find the forest roads open and maintained. When do you go?","a":["January","From May to late autumn","Any time, they're the same all year"],"w":"Maintenance happens from May on, and winter often closes them again."},{"q":"A two-metre snowdrift blocks the road. What do you do?","a":["Try it with momentum","Wait a few hours for it to melt","Turn back or take an alternative route"],"w":"You don't gamble on it and it won't melt within the day; you change route."}]},{"id":"c1216_bans","n":"Where you're not allowed to go","src":"Overland’s Habit · Trip planning","p":[["Check before you leave","If the route goes through a National Park, a NATURA area or another protected zone, find out beforehand what applies there. The rules change from area to area. In some places even simple vehicle transit isn't allowed, let alone camping or fire. There are, for example, bear areas where wheeled vehicles are banned for months in winter."],["Fire season","It runs from <b>1 May to 31 October</b>. On high-risk days and hours, local authorities can ban vehicle traffic and staying in forests, parks and sensitive areas. Before you go up, check whether a ban applies on your day.<br>• First reason: you don't want to be trapped by a fire.<br>• Second: every violation gives arguments to those who want to close the mountains to 4×4s."],["A closed barrier means stop","Going around it, opening it or breaking it isn't clever. It's illegal and irresponsible. Find another route."],["Why it concerns everyone","In some European countries traffic in the mountains has been closed almost everywhere. The more people break the rules and cause trouble, the faster barriers will go up here too. Respect for nature keeps the roads open for all of us."]],"q":[{"q":"It's 20 August, a very high fire-risk day, and your route enters a forest. What do you do?","a":["I go in, I just don't light a fire","I check whether a traffic ban applies and, if so, I respect it","I go at night when nobody sees me"],"w":"In the fire season (1/5–31/10) authorities can close forests to vehicles on high-risk days."},{"q":"You find a closed forestry barrier on the road you'd planned. What do you do?","a":["I go around it","I open it and close it behind me","I respect the ban and change route"],"w":"The barrier is a legal ban; breaking it is illegal and brings more closures."}]},{"id":"c1216_pace","n":"How much time a day on dirt takes","src":"Overland’s Habit · Trip planning","p":[["Why time is hard","On tarmac the calculation is easy. On dirt it isn't. The time for each leg depends on:<br>• the condition of the road<br>• how the group drives<br>• what the vehicles can do<br>• how often you want to stop for the scenery<br>An unknown pass or lots of photography needs more time. A known road you're just crossing, less."],["Count on 10 km/h","For a leisure trip on dirt roads, allow an <b>average speed of about 10 km/h</b>. It looks slow, but it includes stops to read the ground, photos and short visits. If the route has difficult points, add more time. For comparison, in amateur events the average reaches 30–40 km/h, and in rallies with professionals on closed stages 60–70 km/h. You're not racing."],["How many kilometres at most","For a trip you want to enjoy, keep the day's dirt to <b>60–80 km at most</b>, not counting tarmac. Adjust it to the group, the programme and the mood."],["Rhythm and breaks","Keep a steady pace. Lots of short pointless stops tire you more than they rest you.<br>• Tarmac: about a 20-minute break every 2 hours.<br>• Dirt: about 10 minutes every hour."]],"q":[{"q":"Your day has 70 km of dirt road. How many hours do you allow for the dirt alone?","a":["About 2 hours","About 7 hours","About 14 hours"],"w":"At an average of about 10 km/h, 70 km takes around 7 hours including stops."},{"q":"A member proposes 150 km of dirt in one leisure day. What do you say?","a":["It's a lot; up to 60–80 km is sensible","Too little, let's add more","Fine, if we start at 10"],"w":"For a comfortable trip, 60–80 km of dirt a day is the upper limit."},{"q":"You're driving on dirt. Which break schedule fits?","a":["No stops until the end","One long stop every 4 hours","About 10 minutes every hour"],"w":"Dirt is more tiring; a short stop every hour."}]},{"id":"c1216_daylight","n":"Reach your camp in daylight","src":"Overland’s Habit · Trip planning","p":[["The day's target","Whatever kind of trip you're on, split the kilometres so that you have <b>about 2 hours of light</b> left when you reach where you'll camp. You want time to pick a good spot and set up without stress. Fatigue, bad weather and delays eat into that margin. If you've booked a room, the problem is smaller."],["Why not at night","Off-road in the dark means you can't see the ground, you lose the track and you tire faster. It needs special equipment and experience. Avoid it."],["Sunrise – sunset times (1st | 15th of the month)","Jan 07:42–17:16 | 07:40–17:28<br>Feb 07:30–17:47 | 07:17–18:02<br>Mar 06:57–18:17 | 06:36–18:32<br>Apr 07:11–19:48 | 06:50–20:01<br>May 06:30–20:15 | 06:15–20:28<br>Jun 06:04–20:42 | 06:01–20:49<br>Jul 06:05–20:52 | 06:14–20:47<br>Aug 06:27–20:35 | 06:39–20:19<br>Sep 06:55–19:54 | 07:06–19:32<br>Oct 07:20–19:08 | 07:34–18:47<br>Nov 06:51–17:26 | 07:06–17:13<br>Dec 07:22–17:05 | 07:35–17:07"],["Correct the table","• Overcast: count on darkness <b>at least 1 hour earlier</b>.<br>• Dense, tall forest in the mountains: it gets dark noticeably earlier.<br>• After sunset there's a little light for about half an hour. Don't count on it for setting up.<br>• Clock change: on the last Sunday of March we go 1 hour forward, on the last Sunday of October 1 hour back. Watch for it if your trip falls on those days."]],"q":[{"q":"15 October, sunset is 18:47 and it's overcast. By what time do you want to reach your camp spot?","a":["Around 18:45","Around 15:45","Around 19:30"],"w":"Overcast means dark about 1 hour earlier (≈17:45) and you want 2 hours of light before that."},{"q":"Why do you want a margin of light when you arrive?","a":["To pick a spot and set up safely without driving in the dark","To take sunset photos","No need, I set up with a torch"],"w":"Night driving off-road is dangerous and a good camp spot needs light to find."}]},{"id":"c1216_group_supplies","n":"Big or small group, water and what fits in the car","src":"Overland’s Habit · Trip planning","p":[["How many cars?","Every size has a cost and a benefit.<br>• <b>Big group:</b> moves slower, is hard to coordinate, struggles to find a table and rooms for everyone.<br>• <b>But:</b> when it gets tough you have many hands and many shovels, jobs are shared and spirits rise.<br>• For the beginner it's safer: a washout is much easier to cross when others have already crossed it ahead and you follow their tracks."],["Water: never skip it","Far from villages, water and food become a matter of survival. Without water you last far less than without food. So even if you take no food, you always take water. Remember the engine's radiator may suddenly need some too.<br>• Base: <b>at least 2 litres per person per day</b>.<br>• Increase it for heat, cold and effort, and add a small reserve.<br>• Greek mountains have many springs. Mark them on the map beforehand."],["Loading by priority","Space and payload have a limit, so you make choices.<br>• Put what you want to take in order of importance and load from the top of the list down.<br>• For each \"special\" item ask: will I really have the time and the mood to use it?<br>• When you're done, go through the list again and <b>take out about 10%</b>."],["Share the equipment","On a group outing agree beforehand who brings what, so everyone isn't carrying the same things. Tow straps, chains, grills, charcoal: they only need to be in the group, not in every car."]],"q":[{"q":"Three people are going for two days in hot weather. What is the minimum water you take?","a":["6 litres in total","12 litres, because that's the minimum and heat needs a margin","Enough for coffee, we'll find springs"],"w":"3 people × 2 days × 2 litres = 12 litres is the base; with heat you need more."},{"q":"The equipment list is ready and it all fits. What's the next step?","a":["I look again and cut about 10%","I add whatever else fits","I send it to the others to bring the same"],"w":"Less weight and more space make you more flexible; something is almost always surplus."},{"q":"What is a real advantage of a big group?","a":["It moves faster","Many hands and shovels when someone gets stuck","It finds rooms easily"],"w":"A big group is slower, but when it gets tough it has more help."}]},{"id":"c1216_leader","n":"Who holds the reins in the group","src":"Overland’s Habit · Trip planning","p":[["Someone has to be in charge","Every group outing needs one person who runs the programme and solves the problems. Usually it's whoever already does the planning, the calls and the coordinating, and the group recognises them on its own. Even an average leader is better than none. Without a leader, friction is certain."],["How decisions get made","The leader decides for everyone and carries the final responsibility. On serious matters, though, it's good to gather the group first for a quick talk: each says what they want, what they fear, what they suggest. Then the leader decides.<br>• The others make concessions and follow, without constant grumbling.<br>• If the leader can't cope, someone else usually takes over naturally."],["When it's serious, the experienced","When a vehicle gets badly stuck, for example deep in sand, it's no time for a lesson. The most experienced members do the job, properly and safely."],["Everyone learns","Leading takes experience, and you only get experience by doing it.<br>• <b>Change leader every day</b>, with an experienced member quietly backing the new one.<br>• Share the jobs too: navigation, cooking, setting up. Don't overload the newest members, but don't leave them out either.<br>• Don't push someone to cook if they don't want to. And don't give the map to someone who keeps getting lost, but make sure at least one more person can navigate."],["The leader's preparation","• Before you go, practise what you haven't done: map and compass, pitching the tent, chains.<br>• Don't do everything yourself. Share it out.<br>• \"Let's go and see\" works for a relaxed drive, not for real adventure, unless the vehicle is fully equipped.<br>• When you're back, <b>do a debrief</b> and keep notes for next time."]],"q":[{"q":"The group must decide whether to carry on as the weather turns bad. How does the leader handle it properly?","a":["Decides at once without asking anyone","Holds a vote and is bound by the majority","Quickly hears everyone's wishes, fears and ideas, then decides personally"],"w":"Responsibility stays with the leader, but a short talk first gives a better decision and keeps a good atmosphere."},{"q":"On a multi-day trip you want everyone to learn to lead the group. What do you do?","a":["Change leader every day, with an experienced member quietly backing them","Always keep the same one, the best","Appoint nobody, let's all decide together"],"w":"Rotation trains everyone, and quiet backing keeps it safe."},{"q":"A car is buried deep in sand. Who takes over?","a":["The newest member, to learn","The most experienced members","Whoever is closest"],"w":"In serious situations there's no room for experiments; safety comes first."}]},{"id":"c1216_tell_someone","n":"Leave your plan with someone and carry a health card","src":"Overland’s Habit · Trip planning","p":[["Why you do it","A good car, good gear and good organisation reduce the risk, but they don't remove it. On a long or adventurous route, and especially when you're <b>alone with one vehicle</b>, someone must know where you are. If something happens to you, that person may be your only help."],["What goes in the plan","• The exact route, the timetable and when you expect to be back<br>• Make, model, colour and plate of the vehicle<br>• Who you are and everyone's mobile numbers<br>• What equipment you have, what tent and what colour<br>• Anything else that would help a search team<br>Do it every time you go off-road alone."],["Who you give it to","• <b>If you're in an off-road group:</b> a message to the group or to one member is usually enough. Many people in such groups are ready to come and get you out.<br>• <b>Otherwise:</b> to a person you trust, with a regular contact time."],["Contact time and when it means alarm","Set a time when you'll make contact and keep to it. You both need to know that in the mountains you talk when there's signal, not when you want. Agree:<br>• <b>how many hours without news mean they call for help</b><br>• whom they call first and how they escalate: 112, EMAK, fire service, army, local authority<br>Make sure they won't forget. They hold your safety in their hands."],["Health card","If you or someone in the group has a serious health issue (e.g. epilepsy, diabetes, haemophilia, allergy to a medicine or to a sting), tell someone in the group, along with the doctor's instructions. If you're alone, carry a card with your ID:<br>• name and address<br>• emergency phone numbers<br>• medicines you take and allergies<br>• doctor and their phone number<br>• blood group, if you know it"]],"q":[{"q":"You're going alone for three days in the mountains and leave the plan with a friend. What's the most important thing to agree?","a":["That they'll call you if they get worried","What time you'll eat","How many hours without news mean they call for help, and whom they call"],"w":"Without a clear limit and sequence of actions, help either never starts or starts too late."},{"q":"Which of these does the health card not need?","a":["Medicines and allergies","The car's number plate","Your doctor's phone number"],"w":"The plate goes in the route plan; the health card holds what anyone caring for you needs."},{"q":"In which case is it most critical to leave a detailed plan?","a":["When you go alone with one vehicle on a remote route","When you go with many cars on tarmac","Never, I have GPS"],"w":"Alone, there's no other vehicle to call for help for you."}]},{"id":"c1216_maps_start","n":"The map before the screen: your first steps","src":"Overland’s Habit · Navigation","p":[["Why open a map","The screen tells you where to turn. The map shows you what is around you. If you have studied it at home, you move through the mountains with confidence, as if you had been there before. If you only look at your phone, you may pass right by a spring, a gorge or a viewpoint and never know it. And planning the route is part of the trip."],["Before you press «Start» on the GPS","In Greece many villages share the same name in different prefectures. The GPS will pick one, not necessarily yours.<br>• <b>Check the prefecture</b> of the destination in the search.<br>• Glance at the map: is the route heading the right way?<br>• If the distance or time looks odd, stop and check again."],["Which map to buy","Get a detailed map from a serious Greek mapping company. Check the paper: you will open and fold it many times, often folded differently to show your area. It must take that without tearing. If you will handle it with wet or muddy hands, in the rain, or write on it, choose <b>waterproof or laminated</b>."],["How to build experience","• Start with nearby day trips. You learn your vehicle and your gear.<br>• Go back to the same routes in another season, in other weather. They will feel new.<br>• Then move to two- or three-day trips with an overnight.<br>• The long multi-day trips come last.<br>You will find route ideas on Greek 4×4 forums and websites."]],"q":[{"q":"You type a village name into the GPS. What do you check before you set off?","a":["Only whether the phone has signal","The destination's prefecture and the route on the map","Whether the voice guidance is in Greek"],"w":"Many villages share a name. If you don't check, the GPS may send you to the wrong prefecture."},{"q":"You will hold the map in the rain and write on it. Which do you choose?","a":["A photocopy","A cheap paper one","Waterproof or laminated"],"w":"Water and mud quickly ruin plain paper."}]},{"id":"c1216_scale_legend","n":"Read the scale and the legend","src":"Overland’s Habit · Navigation","p":[["Scale in plain words","The scale tells you how much the ground has been «shrunk». At 1:500,000, 1 cm on paper is 500,000 cm on the ground, i.e. <b>5 km</b>. Rule: <b>smaller second number = more detail</b>."],["Pick the scale for the job","• General road map: only the main roads. For getting to the area.<br>• <b>1:500,000</b> (1 cm = 5 km): touring map, a bit richer.<br>• <b>1:250,000</b>: by region. Shows main and many secondary dirt roads, contour lines and sights.<br>• <b>1:100,000</b> (1 cm = 1 km): the right tool for safe off-road driving.<br>• <b>1:50,000</b> (1 cm = 500 m): even more detailed, with dense contours. Usually made for specific mountains."],["Detail versus bulk","Detailed maps cover a small area. For a long crossing at 1:100,000 or 1:50,000 you will carry many sheets. It is worth it, because you get far more and more accurate information. But a map does nothing if you can't read it."],["Legend first","Contour lines are drawn the same way everywhere. The symbols for roads, settlements and trails, though, change from company to company. The legend, in a corner of the map, explains them all: tarmac, passable dirt road, trail, scenic route, distance markers, beaches, villages.<br>• <b>Every time you pick up a map from another company, read the legend again.</b>"],["Watch the colours","A dirt road, a trail and a river may have similar lines in different colours. Mix them up and you may drive into a dry riverbed instead of a road, or look for dirt and end up on the highway. Before you choose a road, check its colour in the legend."]],"q":[{"q":"On a 1:100,000 map you measure 7 cm between two villages. What is the straight-line distance?","a":["700 m","70 km","7 km"],"w":"At 1:100,000, 1 cm is 1 km, so 7 cm is 7 km."},{"q":"You are planning off-road driving in a mountain area. Which map helps you most?","a":["1:50,000","1:500,000","General road map"],"w":"The smaller the second number, the more detail and contour lines."},{"q":"You bought a map from a different company. What do you do first?","a":["Read the new map's legend","Assume the colours are the same","Look only at the village names"],"w":"Each company has its own colours and symbols."}]},{"id":"c1216_compass_orient","n":"Make the map «face» the right way","src":"Overland’s Habit · Navigation","p":[["The map convention","Almost every map has <b>North at the top</b>. South is at the bottom, East on the right, West on the left. If a map is different, it will have a north arrow somewhere obvious. Look for it before you start."],["What a compass has","A magnetic needle that points north and a dial (compass rose) with the four points: <b>N</b> North, <b>E</b> East, <b>S</b> South, <b>W</b> West. Around the edge are degrees from 0° to 360°, counted from north."],["Which compass you have","• <b>Simple:</b> the dial is fixed, only the needle turns.<br>• <b>Rotating card:</b> the whole dial turns with the needle.<br>• <b>Electronic:</b> shows the direction on a screen.<br>All three do the job. But a small compass needs no battery."],["Orienting in the field","1. Lay the map on a level surface.<br>2. Wait for the needle to settle on north.<br>3. Turn the map until its top points the same way as the needle.<br>4. Now match what you see, such as peaks, streams, ravines and villages, with what the map shows.<br>Don't rest the compass on the bonnet or next to metal. It may read wrong."],["Practice","At first it will feel hard. Do it at every stop, even when you know where you are. It quickly becomes a habit, and one day it will get you out of a tight spot."]],"q":[{"q":"You are in a clearing and want to match the peaks around you with the map. What do you do?","a":["Hold the map so the lettering reads normally","Turn the map so its top points where the needle points","Turn the map towards the sun"],"w":"When the map faces north, each feature on it lies in the same direction as the real one."},{"q":"Your compass has a fixed dial and only the needle moves. What type is it?","a":["Rotating card","Electronic","Simple"],"w":"On a simple compass the rose does not move."}]},{"id":"c1216_coordinates","n":"The «address» of every point: φ, λ and EGSA '87","src":"Overland’s Habit · Navigation","p":[["Why it matters to you","In a call for help, «just past the big plane tree, on the left» tells rescuers nothing. Two numbers tell them everything. Every point on earth has a unique pair of coordinates, the <b>position fix</b>. Because the earth is almost a sphere, the fix is given in angles, i.e. in degrees."],["The earth's grid","• <b>Parallels:</b> horizontal circles, parallel to the Equator. They give <b>latitude φ</b> (LAT).<br>• <b>Meridians:</b> lines from pole to pole, like orange segments. They give <b>longitude λ</b> (LON).<br>Zero is the Equator for φ and the Greenwich meridian (England) for λ."],["How to read the values","• <b>φ:</b> 0° at the Equator, 90° at the poles. North = N (positive), south = S (negative). So 38°N = +38° and 46°S = –46°.<br>• <b>λ:</b> 0° at Greenwich, up to 180°. East = E (positive), west = W (negative).<br>• <b>All of Greece is N and E.</b> If you see S or W in a Greek position, something is wrong."],["Degrees, minutes, seconds","It works like a clock: 1 degree = 60 minutes (′), 1 minute = 60 seconds (″). To convert decimals, multiply the decimal part by 60. E.g. 37.50° = 37° 30′ and 22.25° = 22° 15′. Your phone and GPS convert automatically. Just be careful which format you read out on the phone."],["EGSA '87: the fix in metres","Greece also uses <b>EGSA '87</b> (Greek Geodetic Reference System). It «flattens» the country onto a plane and gives the position in <b>metres, X and Y</b>, which is handy for measuring distances. Good Greek maps print both systems in the margin, EGSA '87 and degrees. Most navigation apps show whichever you choose."]],"q":[{"q":"A friend sends you a position in the Peloponnese marked W. What do you think?","a":["It is wrong, Greece is N and E everywhere","It is correct for the western Peloponnese","W means «winter»"],"w":"Greece lies north of the Equator and east of Greenwich."},{"q":"The GPS shows 21.75°. What is that in degrees and minutes?","a":["21° 75′","21° 07′","21° 45′"],"w":"0.75 × 60 = 45 minutes."},{"q":"What is the main difference between EGSA '87 and φ/λ?","a":["It gives the position in metres X, Y instead of degrees","It only applies at sea","It is not used on maps"],"w":"EGSA '87 treats the country as a plane and measures in metres."}]},{"id":"c1216_fix_on_map","n":"From the map to numbers and back","src":"Overland’s Habit · Navigation","p":[["What you need","• A map with a printed <b>grid</b>, i.e. horizontal and vertical lines of longitude and latitude. A map without a grid is no good for a position fix.<br>• <b>A ruler and a pencil</b>, because the grid is usually sparse and you will draw your own lines.<br>The denser the grid, the less work."],["Where the values are printed","• <b>Top and bottom margins:</b> longitude λ.<br>• <b>Left and right margins:</b> latitude φ.<br>Many good maps also have a second scale in metres (EGSA '87) in the margin, next to the degrees (WGS84). The method is the same for both."],["You want to say where you are","1. Mark your location on the map as precisely as you can.<br>2. From there draw a vertical line to the top or bottom margin. Read <b>λ</b>.<br>3. Draw a horizontal line to the side. Read <b>φ</b>.<br>Those two numbers are your position. Write them down before you read them out on the phone."],["You are given a position and look for the point","Do the reverse:<br>1. Find the λ value on the top or bottom margin and draw a long vertical line.<br>2. Find the φ value on the side and draw a horizontal line.<br>3. Where the lines cross is the point you are looking for, e.g. a group vehicle that got stuck."]],"q":[{"q":"You want to read the longitude λ of your point. Where do you draw the line?","a":["Horizontally, to the side","Vertically, to the top or bottom margin","Towards the legend"],"w":"λ is printed on the top and bottom margins."},{"q":"Your map has grid lines only every few centimetres. What do you need for an accurate fix?","a":["A ruler and pencil to draw lines out to the margins","Nothing, I guess","A bigger map"],"w":"With a sparse grid you draw your own lines out to the margin values."}]},{"id":"c1216_altitude_contours","n":"How high you are and what slope lies ahead","src":"Overland’s Habit · Navigation","p":[["Altitude in the mountains","Altitude is how many metres above sea level you are. Together with the position fix, it shows exactly where you are on the slope. The GPS gives it with some accuracy, but there is a second tool too."],["The altimeter and its weakness","The altimeter, analogue or digital, stand-alone or built into a watch or phone, is really a <b>barometer</b>. As you climb, pressure drops about <b>1 mb per 10 m</b>. The problem is that it doesn't know why the pressure changed. When the weather changes fast, pressure can move up to 4 mb in an hour. That means an error of about <b>40 m</b> without you having moved."],["Keep it right","• Reset it <b>about every three hours</b>.<br>• Reset it at a point of known altitude: the coast (0 m), a summit, a refuge, a village or a height printed on the map.<br>• If the altimeter «climbs» while you sit in camp, pressure is falling. Watch out for bad weather coming."],["Contour lines: the mountain in slices","Imagine cutting the mountain into horizontal slices of equal thickness. That fixed thickness is the <b>contour interval</b>. From above, each slice looks like a closed curve. Every point on the curve has the same altitude. The outer curves are the base and the smallest one in the middle is the summit."],["Read the slope before you go","• <b>Tight lines</b> = steep slope. Watch for a cliff or a hard climb.<br>• <b>Wide lines</b> = gentle, open ground.<br>• A point halfway between the 500 and 600 m lines is at about 550 m.<br>With a little practice you will tell valleys, ridges and plains apart. That way you can judge in advance whether a pass or an off-track route stands a chance."]],"q":[{"q":"You slept in the same spot and in the morning the altimeter reads 40 m higher. What most likely happened?","a":["The battery failed","The ground rose","Air pressure fell because of the weather"],"w":"The altimeter measures pressure. A weather drop of about 4 mb looks like a climb of about 40 m."},{"q":"On the map, your route crosses a spot where the contour lines almost touch. What do you expect?","a":["A very steep slope","A flat meadow","A lake"],"w":"Tight contours mean a large height difference over a short distance."},{"q":"You climb 200 m. Roughly how much does pressure drop?","a":["2 mb","20 mb","200 mb"],"w":"About 1 mb per 10 m, so 200 m ≈ 20 mb."}]},{"id":"c1216_bearing","n":"Take a bearing and give a proper fire report","src":"Overland’s Habit · Navigation","p":[["The idea","A compass doesn't only show north. It also tells you <b>at what angle</b> something you can see lies, such as a peak, a village or a column of smoke. That angle, in degrees from north, is the <b>azimuth</b> or bearing."],["How you measure it","1. Step away from the vehicle and from metal objects.<br>2. Hold the compass <b>level</b>, a little below eye height, so the needle swings freely.<br>3. Let the needle settle on north.<br>4. Face the target squarely.<br>5. Imagine a straight line from the target to the centre of the compass. Where it crosses the dial is your reading in degrees."],["If you see a fire","Call <b>199</b> (Fire Service) or <b>112</b> at once. 112 is free from a mobile, even without a SIM. Say clearly:<br>• <b>your position</b> (φ, λ)<br>• <b>your altitude</b>, e.g. 1,100 m<br>• <b>the bearing to the fire</b>, e.g. «smoke at 75°»<br>With your position and the angle, the service knows which way to look. If the fire is close to you, first get to a safe spot, then call."],["In your kit","• A small, reliable compass in the glovebox. It takes no space and needs no battery.<br>• Practise on known peaks before you need it for real."]],"q":[{"q":"You see smoke from a hilltop. Which numbers do you call?","a":["100 or 166","199 or 112","1510"],"w":"199 is the Fire Service and 112 the emergency number. Both are free."},{"q":"Which information helps the Fire Service most to locate the fire?","a":["Your position, your altitude and the bearing to the smoke","«I see smoke somewhere to the north»","Only the name of the nearest village"],"w":"From your position and the angle, the direction of the fire can be found."},{"q":"Where and how do you hold the compass to take a bearing?","a":["On the bonnet, engine running","Upright in front of your face","Level, a little below eye height, away from metal"],"w":"The needle needs to be level and free from the influence of metal."}]},{"id":"c1216_navigator","n":"The navigator's role in the vehicle","src":"Overland’s Habit · Navigation","p":[["Who does the job","Someone must always know where you are, where you came from and where you are going. The driver can do it alone, but it is far better if the <b>co-driver</b> takes it on as navigator. The driver watches the road and the navigator watches the map. In a convoy, the navigator is usually the co-driver of the <b>lead vehicle</b>, who coordinates with the group leader. The word came from the sea, but the job is the same."],["How you track your position","1. Start from a point you are sure of, e.g. the departure village. Put your finger there on the map.<br>2. As you move, slide your finger along the route.<br>3. Keep looking outside and comparing with the map.<br>4. At every landmark, such as a junction, bridge, chapel or stream, find it on the map and correct your finger's position.<br>That way you catch a wrong turn at the next landmark, not an hour later."],["When the navigator can't cope","Signs they are lost: they are surprised by every stream or village, or can't say where you are now. This is dangerous, especially <b>at night or in fog</b>. Stop, work out the position together and, if possible, hand the map to someone else."],["Map and GPS together","You no longer need stars and a sextant. A GPS gives you two numbers and you find them on the map. But the GPS doesn't replace the navigator. The person who reads the map is the one who understands what the numbers mean."]],"q":[{"q":"Four vehicles are travelling together. Who usually navigates?","a":["The co-driver of the lead vehicle, in coordination with the leader","The driver of the last vehicle","Whoever has the newest phone"],"w":"The lead vehicle opens the way, so that is where the navigator is needed."},{"q":"You cross a bridge. What does the navigator do?","a":["Nothing, carries on","Finds it on the map and corrects their finger's position","Switches off the GPS"],"w":"Each landmark confirms or corrects your position on the map."}]},{"id":"c1216_gps","n":"What the GPS does for you and what it doesn't","src":"Overland’s Habit · Navigation","p":[["The rule first","<b>Electronics help, they are not enough. Always carry a paper map and know how to read it.</b> A flat battery, a fault or poor signal can leave you without a screen at the worst moment."],["What it is, in two lines","GPS (Global Positioning System) began as a US military system and today everyone has it. There are other satellite systems: Glonass (Russia), Beidou (China), Galileo (Europe). The correct general name is <b>GNSS</b>. A modern phone uses them together and reaches about <b>3 m</b> accuracy."],["Help the reception","The antenna needs open sky. The signal is spoiled by:<br>• buildings, mountains and deep ravines<br>• dense trees<br>• heavy rain or snow<br>• the vehicle's metal roof and <b>metallised tint</b> on the windows<br>The more satellites it «sees», the more accurate the position. In the vehicle, the answer is an <b>external antenna on the roof</b>."],["What you can ask of it","The GPS finds four things: φ, λ, altitude and time. From these it gives:<br>• speed: current, average, maximum, minimum<br>• direction of travel, like a compass<br>• precise distance travelled<br>• time from point to point<br>• distance and bearing to a known point<br>• your position on a digital map, with picture and voice guidance<br>• recording of route and points"],["Track, waypoints and your own judgement","• <b>Track:</b> while running, the GPS logs your position every few seconds and builds a continuous line. It is your way back, even in the dark. Keep it on all the time.<br>• <b>Waypoints:</b> points you save yourself, such as a spring, a junction or a camp spot.<br>You can move both to a computer and share them. But <b>the GPS can't see</b> a cliff, a landslide or a closed road. You judge those."]],"q":[{"q":"The phone on the dashboard keeps losing signal and the car has metallised tint. What helps?","a":["Turning up the brightness","An external antenna on the roof","Closing the windows"],"w":"The metal roof and the film block the signal. The external antenna sees the sky."},{"q":"Night has fallen and you want to go back the same way. What helps you most?","a":["The track you recorded","A waypoint at the destination","The maximum speed"],"w":"The track is the line where you have been, so it is your way back."},{"q":"The GPS shows a road ahead. Can you be sure it is passable?","a":["Yes, since it shows it","Yes, if the signal is good","No, you judge the obstacles and road condition yourself"],"w":"The GPS records and displays, it can't see what lies ahead of you."}]},{"id":"c1216_digital_sat","n":"Digital map and satellite imagery: using them right","src":"Overland’s Habit · Navigation","p":[["The set-up","A map file on its own shows you nothing. If you go digital-only, you need:<br>• a phone, tablet or laptop<br>• a navigation app<br>• <b>steady power from the vehicle</b>, because if the device dies you have no map<br>• perhaps an external GPS receiver<br>Expect more cost, cables and mounts."],["How you judge a map","The same applies to paper and digital. Ask three things:<br>• <b>Is it complete?</b> Does it have the small dirt roads, or only a few?<br>• <b>Is it accurate?</b> Is the road drawn where it really runs?<br>• <b>Is it up to date?</b> When was it last checked for changes?<br>On the third, digital wins: it updates easily over the internet, free or for a small subscription."],["What a good app gives you","• Measures distances and plots a route by criteria you set.<br>• Shows altitude at mountain passes and where the fuel stations are.<br>• You can load other people's data, such as roads, springs, beaches or campsites, even if they are not on the map.<br>• You print sections or the whole route for the navigator."],["Study from above before you leave","Apps like <b>Google Earth</b> give you free, fairly recent satellite images. You can see the terrain and vegetation of the area, even in 3D. You will see whether the road runs through forest or over a bare ridge, and where the clearings for camping are. The more you use it, the more you learn to see things in the image that no map shows."],["The limit of the image","<b>The view from above doesn't tell you whether the road is passable.</b> A dirt road may look clear and be cut by a stream impassable for a 4×4, or have been washed out by rain after the image was taken. Use it to plan. You make the decision on the spot."]],"q":[{"q":"A map has every dirt road, but some are drawn 200 m away from where they really are. Which criterion fails?","a":["Completeness","Accuracy","How up to date it is"],"w":"Accuracy means the road is drawn where it really runs."},{"q":"On Google Earth the road looks clear all the way to the summit. What does that mean?","a":["That it is definitely passable","That it is tarmac","Nothing certain, a stream or damage may cut it"],"w":"The image doesn't show water depth, damage after it was taken or exact gradients."},{"q":"You go with only a tablet for a map. What must you have?","a":["Steady power from the vehicle","A bigger screen","Nothing extra"],"w":"Without power the device dies and you are left with no map."}]},{"id":"c1216_drformat","n":"A route plan in 5 steps","src":"Overland’s Habit · Navigation","p":[["Where you want to go","Write down the final destination and the points you definitely want to see: sights, viewpoints, springs, gorges. Talk to people who have been there recently. The less you leave to chance, the better."],["Which way you will go","Join the points with roads that suit the group, i.e. what it wants and what it can handle. Choose roads for the view, for the places they pass or for the driving challenge. Plot the whole route, start to finish, on a map and in Google Earth.<br>• Wherever there may be an obstacle, such as a river crossing or a snowy road, <b>have an alternative ready and checked</b>."],["How many kilometres a day","Split the route into days. Aim for about <b>60–80 km of dirt a day</b>. More, and everyone gets tired, especially the passengers, and the trip becomes a chore. Less, and you are left feeling something was missing.<br>• Work out how many km you get from one tank.<br>• Mark where you will refuel, even if it means a detour."],["Where each day ends","Every day ends at a <b>sure place to sleep</b>: a guesthouse, rented rooms or a campsite.<br>• For rooms: phone and book in advance.<br>• For camping: make sure you arrive with <b>at least two hours of daylight</b>, to choose a spot and set up."],["One folder for everything","Put all the material in one place, digital or on paper:<br>• maps and tracks<br>• sights and stopping points<br>• accommodation and fuel stations<br>• useful phone numbers<br>Before you leave, load the tracks and waypoints into the navigation app you will use."]],"q":[{"q":"Your route has a river crossing in the middle. What do you do when planning?","a":["Cancel the trip","Plot and check an alternative route","Nothing, I'll see on the spot"],"w":"If the crossing is impassable, the alternative is already ready."},{"q":"The group wants 150 km of dirt in one day. What do you say?","a":["Too much, everyone will be tired and the fun will go","Too little, let's add more","Distance doesn't matter"],"w":"A good pace is about 60–80 km of dirt a day."},{"q":"You will camp tonight. When should you reach the spot?","a":["Whenever, I have a torch","Just as the sun sets","With at least two hours of daylight left"],"w":"You need time in daylight to find a spot and set up safely."}]},{"id":"c57_layers","n":"Layers: warm without sweating","src":"Overland’s Habit · Clothing & personal kit","p":[["Work out the cold before you leave","The higher you go, the cooler it gets: count on roughly <b>2°C less per 150 m of altitude</b>. Up high you also get more cloud and stronger wind. If the route crosses mountains, warm clothes go in the car <b>even in August</b>."],["Wind drops the temperature","In wind the body loses heat much faster. What it roughly feels like:<br>• 0°C and 10 km/h → −4°C<br>• 0°C and 20 km/h → −10°C<br>• 0°C and 40 km/h → −16°C<br>• −8°C and 30 km/h → −25°C"],["How to build your layers","A thick jumper has two settings: it roasts you, or you take it off and freeze while sweaty. Wear <b>several thin pieces</b>:<br>• Next to skin: base layer, not cotton<br>• Middle: fleece for warmth<br>• Outside: windproof or waterproof shell<br>Too warm? Take one off. Cold? Add one. Two thin technical pieces do more than one heavy one."],["The day changes, you adapt","Cool morning, hot midday, damp dusk, cold night. Sitting in camp you'll need everything from a T-shirt to a jacket. Walking uphill you <b>sweat on the way up, chill on the way down</b>, and may get wet. Adjust all the time, don't wait."],["Quality and space","Buy technical clothing from an outdoor brand, not a fashion label. A cheap market-stall fleece won't keep you warm. Keep <b>one full warm change</b> in case you get soaked, but watch the volume: the boot has to fit everything else too. Fleece is flammable, keep it away from fires and stoves."],["Quick check","• Mountains on the plan? Warm clothes in<br>• Wind forecast? Dress for the feel, not the thermometer<br>• Three layers, not one heavy piece<br>• One dry change in a bag"]],"q":[{"q":"You climb 600 m from the village to the plateau. Roughly how much colder should you expect?","a":["About 8°C","About 1°C","No difference"],"w":"At ~2°C per 150 m, 600 m gives about 8°C less."},{"q":"The thermometer reads 0°C and the wind is 40 km/h. What do you dress for?","a":["For 0°C","For about −4°C","For about −16°C"],"w":"Wind steals heat; it feels like about −16°C."},{"q":"You're sweating on a climb in fleece and a windproof. What do you do?","a":["Nothing, you'll cool down later","Take one layer off before you're soaked","Strip to the base layer and put it all back on at the top without stopping"],"w":"Layers are there so you adjust early; sweat chills you afterwards."}]},{"id":"c57_basemid","n":"What touches your body: base layers, fleece, socks","src":"Overland’s Habit · Clothing & personal kit","p":[["Leave at home","• <b>Cotton vest</b>: soaks up sweat, dries slowly, chills you and smells<br>• <b>Nylon socks</b> and cheap synthetics: rub, tear, hold no warmth<br>• <b>Old pure-wool socks</b>: shrink in hot water or a dryer"],["First layer: base layer","Wear a <b>base layer</b>, short or long sleeve; in cold weather add <b>base-layer leggings</b>. It insulates and pulls moisture away from the skin. Materials: polyester, <b>merino</b> (fine, warm, pricey wool) or a blend. They dry fast. Look for <b>antibacterial treatment</b> so it doesn't smell after days. Summer: a thin light-coloured base layer also blocks the sun."],["Second layer: fleece","Synthetic, light, doesn't go mouldy, doesn't hold smells, warm and tough. Thick <b>high loft</b> is the warmest; it's mostly used in jackets and liners, and as a top it only suits very low temperatures. In the shop look for a <b>dense, even weave</b> on both sides."],["Two weaknesses of fleece","• <b>It doesn't stop wind</b>: when it blows, wear a windproof or jacket over it<br>• <b>It burns easily</b>: not right next to a fire, fireplace or stove. Pull your sleeves back when adding wood and watch for sparks and cigarettes, the surface melts"],["Socks that work","The good combination: <b>thin base-layer sock inside + wool hiking or ski sock</b> over it. Choose anatomical ones that don't wrinkle, dry fast and have antibacterial treatment."]],"q":[{"q":"You're packing for three days in the mountains. What goes next to your skin?","a":["Cotton T-shirt","Synthetic or merino base layer","Thin jumper"],"w":"A base layer moves sweat away; wet cotton chills you."},{"q":"You're wearing only fleece and a strong wind picks up. What's missing?","a":["A windproof on top","A second fleece","Nothing, fleece blocks wind"],"w":"Fleece insulates but wind goes through; it needs a shell."},{"q":"Evening at the campfire in fleece. What's right?","a":["Sit as close as you can","Doesn't matter, it's synthetic","Keep your distance and watch for sparks"],"w":"Fleece is flammable and melts with heat."}]},{"id":"c57_boots","n":"What's on your feet: driving, mud, water","src":"Overland’s Habit · Clothing & personal kit","p":[["First ask: what will you be doing?","If you're staying behind the wheel and don't plan to get out in the mud, wear something <b>low, soft, warm, with a thin sole</b>, e.g. a quality leather trainer or après-ski boot. You feel the pedals. If you'll be getting out to rig straps and dig, wear a <b>high, waterproof, semi-stiff boot</b> and keep wellies in the boot."],["By terrain","• <b>Mud</b>: high waterproof boot; in heavy mud wellies<br>• <b>Snow</b>: high waterproof boot, maybe gaiters<br>• <b>River</b>: wellies or high waterproof boot<br>• <b>Driving only</b>: low, soft, warm"],["The mountain boot","Your main shoe outside: high and waterproof, so you can step in mud and on river stones without getting wet or slipping. <b>Gore-Tex</b> is light, tough and breathable. A stiff sole supports the ankle but <b>tires you at the wheel and dulls the feel</b> of brake and throttle. Look for the middle ground."],["Wellies","Lining of fleece or synthetic fur (cheap fur smells). For regular use buy good ones; for emergencies cheap ones do. Get them <b>1–2 sizes larger</b>: you'll pull them off in a hurry, in a tight space, muddy, with wet socks. After each use <b>dry the lining</b>; if it won't come out, pull it so it turns inside out."],["Buying and testing","• Wear <b>both</b> with a thick sock<br>• Walk around the shop for <b>at least 15 minutes</b><br>• If it pinches now, it will pinch forever, mountain boots hardly stretch<br>• Don't listen to a salesperson who doesn't know off-road use<br>• Wear new ones at home for several days<br>• On the trip take <b>an old, proven pair</b> as well"]],"q":[{"q":"Today you'll only drive, without getting out. What do you wear?","a":["Heavy stiff boot","Low soft shoe with a thin sole","Wellies"],"w":"A thin sole gives feel on the pedals."},{"q":"You try a boot and it pinches a toe a little. What do you do?","a":["Buy it, it will stretch","Buy it half a size smaller","Try another, this one won't soften"],"w":"Mountain boots barely stretch; the pressure stays."},{"q":"Why do wellies need a larger size?","a":["So they come off fast even when muddy and with wet socks","So they don't slip","So they fit over the boot"],"w":"A tight welly with mud and damp sticks to the foot."}]},{"id":"c57_shell","n":"The outer layer: waterproofs, down, gloves, hat","src":"Overland’s Habit · Clothing & personal kit","p":[["What to check in a waterproof","Jacket, trousers and windproof must be waterproof <b>with sealed seams</b>: turn it inside out, the seams have tape bonded over them. You also want <b>breathability</b>. A <b>Gore-Tex</b>-type membrane has tiny pores (up to ~1.3 million per cm²): sweat vapour gets out, raindrops don't get in. A bin bag with holes will do for a tyre change, but you'll be soaked in sweat inside."],["The trousers","Choose <b>earth colours</b> so mud and dust don't show. <b>Side zips on the legs</b> let you take them off without removing muddy boots or wellies, and open for ventilation when you're working hard."],["When it drops below zero","Jeans and cotton hold moisture, useless. Sitting at the fire you roast in front and freeze behind, and technical layers aren't enough. That's when you need a <b>down jacket</b> over fleece and base layer, or a short ski jacket. Fill: down, feather or synthetic. Quality = <b>fill power</b>, usually 500–800; the higher, the better. It loses insulation if it gets wet, stays compressed, or is washed often."],["Hands","Fingers get cold first, and you need them for work. Only a glove with a membrane is waterproof; it also needs insulation that doesn't make you clumsy. Untreated leather lets water in. Practical fix: a <b>thin cheap fleece glove inside</b> your leather work gloves, layers for the hands too."],["Head and ears","• Summer: hat, cap or bandana for sun and sweat, especially if you have little hair<br>• Winter: beanie or wool headband<br>• <b>Ears</b> freeze first: headband or ear warmers, also under a beanie<br>• Rule: put the warm layer on <b>before</b> you get cold, e.g. as soon as it gets dark"]],"q":[{"q":"In the shop, how do you know a jacket won't leak at the seams?","a":["From the price","Look inside for tape bonded over the seams","From the colour"],"w":"Sealed seams are visible on the inside."},{"q":"Two down jackets, 550 and 750 fill power. Which insulates better?","a":["The 550","They're the same","The 750"],"w":"Higher fill power means better down and insulation."},{"q":"It's snowing and you're working in leather work gloves. How do you keep hands warm?","a":["A thin fleece glove inside","Take the gloves off for better feel","Wet the gloves so they stiffen"],"w":"A thin inner layer adds warmth without losing grip."}]},{"id":"c57_coldrule","n":"Cold: two rules, emergency fixes and the car bag","src":"Overland’s Habit · Clothing & personal kit","p":[["Rule 1: put on whatever you have","Hands cold and all you have is a beanie? Put it on. Neck cold and you only have gloves? Wear them. The body controls heat <b>centrally</b>, with sensors everywhere. The fewer spots 'shouting' cold, the less heat you lose overall, and the more is left for where it hurts, e.g. feet in wet shoes."],["Rule 2: before, not after","In the mountains as at sea: <b>dress before you get cold</b>. Once the body is chilled it takes a long time to recover, however much you put on. The right moment is <b>as soon as it gets dark</b>, before the damp gets into your bones."],["If it catches you unprepared","• <b>Black bin bag</b> with holes for head and arms: blocks wind and rain<br>• <b>Newspapers</b> as insulation: over your vest, under the outer layer, kept dry<br>• Don't sit still while wet, change or get in the car"],["How much clothing to take","On your first trips don't skimp on clothes, mountains, snow and damp don't forgive, and the car does the carrying. Think about days, fatigue and whether you can wash and dry. Rule of thumb: <b>one change of base layers per 2–3 days</b>; the rest depending on swims and evenings out."],["Always stays in the car","A small bag that never comes out:<br>• Towel and <b>a full change of clothes</b><br>• Wool socks, gloves, beanie<br>• <b>Work gloves</b> for straps and shackles in the mud"]],"q":[{"q":"Your feet are cold and all you have is a beanie in your pocket. Does wearing it help?","a":["Yes, you cut total heat loss and the body sends warmth where it's needed","No, the head has nothing to do with the feet","Only if you put it on your feet"],"w":"Heat control is whole-body; every part you cover helps all of it."},{"q":"You're in camp and the sun has just set. You're not cold yet. What do you do?","a":["Wait until you start shivering","Put the warm layer on now","Light a fire and stay in a T-shirt"],"w":"Dress before you get cold; afterwards it takes time to warm up again."},{"q":"Five days with no washing machine. How many changes of base layers?","a":["One for the whole trip","Five, one a day no matter what","About two, one per 2–3 days"],"w":"The practical rule is one change per 2–3 days."}]},{"id":"c35_personal","n":"Pockets and bag: what you carry on you","src":"Overland’s Habit · Clothing & personal kit","p":[["Always on you","• <b>Pocket knife or multitool</b> (some have a small saw, spoon, opener)<br>• <b>Small torch</b><br>• <b>Phone in a waterproof case</b><br>• A <b>collapsible water container</b> up to 1 L"],["Knife and torch","The knife is a matter of taste; whatever you choose, keep it <b>clean and sharp</b>. Mini torches the size of a lighter are bright, but they're expensive, easy to lose and need <b>special lithium batteries</b> you can't find everywhere. If you take one, carry a spare battery."],["Water for emergencies","A folded water container takes no space and gets you out of trouble when you lose or empty your bottle. A known survival trick: a <b>condom</b> carries up to about a litre fairly safely."],["The clothes bag","With little experience, take <b>more, not less</b>, the car does the carrying. Rule of thumb: <b>one change of base layers per 2–3 days</b>, plus one full warm change in case you get soaked.<br>• Base layers short & long sleeve, base-layer leggings<br>• T-shirt, trousers, thin fleece, tracksuit<br>• Underwear, socks, beanie, gloves<br>• Jacket, waterproof, gaiters<br>• Trainers, mountain boots, wellies<br><b>Summer, also:</b> shorts, swimsuit, sarong, flip-flops, hat, towel"]],"q":[{"q":"You've lost your water bottle far from the car. What saves you?","a":["A collapsible water container in your pocket","The torch","Nothing, you turn back"],"w":"A folded container takes no space and carries up to ~1 L."},{"q":"What's the downside of a lighter-sized mini torch?","a":["It isn't bright enough","It's easy to lose and needs special batteries","It's very heavy"],"w":"It's bright, but lithium batteries aren't available everywhere."}]},{"id":"c57_campstart","n":"Think first, then shop: the law, your needs, the cold","src":"Overland’s Habit · Camp life","p":[["Start from what you want","A weekend near home and two weeks at the far end of Greece need almost <b>the same gear</b>: tent, mattress, light, gas stove, cookware. The difference is you. Ask yourself:<br>• How “wild” do I want to live?<br>• How much space does the vehicle have and how much weight can it carry?<br>• Which home comforts can I leave behind without missing them?"],["What the law says","In Greece wild camping is <b>generally not allowed</b>. You can legally pitch a tent at organised sites, municipal or private, under their terms. What is usually tolerated is <b>spending the night from dusk to dawn</b> and moving on. In some protected areas even that is not allowed, ask before you go. There is a fine; check the current amount."],["The mountain night cold","Don't be fooled by the month. In the mountains nights get freezing not only in winter but in April, October, even May–June. Temperature drops about <b>2°C for every 300 m</b> of altitude, and after sunset it falls sharply. Two summer sleeping bags and a couple of blankets won't save you. Your big opponent is <b>damp</b>: it makes the cold bite into you. You'll manage without a chair; with the wrong sleeping bag, you won't."],["How to shop properly","Decide what you'll do, read up, ask, and then buy. For <b>winter camping</b> you need gear made for cold and damp, not whatever is on offer. Here, cheap means paying twice."],["Checklist","• Tent, tarps, ropes, pegs, mallet<br>• Mattress, insulating mat, sleeping bag, fleece liner<br>• Blankets, pillow, sheet–pillowcase<br>• Candle lantern, lighting<br>• Towel, shower, shovel<br>• Chairs, table, cookware<br>• Washing line, clothes pegs<br>The list is complete, pick from it, you don't take everything."]],"q":[{"q":"You want to spend a night outdoors, outside an organised site. What is usually tolerated?","a":["Pitching a tent for a week wherever you like","Staying from dusk to dawn, unless the area is protected and forbids it","Nothing, not even one night, ever"],"w":"Camping needs an organised site; a single overnight is usually tolerated, with exceptions."},{"q":"You go to the mountains in May with a summer sleeping bag. What's the right conclusion?","a":["You'll probably be cold, mountain nights freeze and damp makes it worse","It's May, there's no problem","Bringing a chair and table is enough"],"w":"Temperature falls with altitude and after sunset, and damp makes the cold worse."},{"q":"You're preparing for winter camping. What do you buy?","a":["The cheapest you can find, in double quantity","Your summer gear plus one extra blanket","Quality gear made for cold and damp"],"w":"Cold and damp don't forgive makeshift solutions."}]},{"id":"c57_tents","n":"Choose and pitch a tent that holds","src":"Overland’s Habit · Camp life","p":[["First decision: which season","• <b>Summer (1-season)</b>: usually single-skin, big openings, very cheap. For the holidays, then it's thrown away.<br>• <b>3-season</b>: all year except harsh winter. Double-skin, with a light inner and lots of mesh; the flysheet isn't always fully waterproof.<br>• <b>4-season</b>: fully waterproof, breathable flysheet, more poles for wind, openings you control, strong floor. It costs €300–400 and up, but lasts for years."],["Second decision: shape and height","• <b>Dome (igloo)</b>: first choice for the mountains. Two or more poles crossing over the whole body; each extra pole means a bit more work to pitch, but it stands up better to snow and side wind.<br>• <b>Tunnel</b>: almost vertical walls, so you can stand almost everywhere. That's why expeditions use them as kitchens and bases.<br>• <b>Ridge tent with poles</b>: simple, but weak in rain and especially wind; rare today.<br>Remember: the tent is heated by <b>your body</b>. A tall four-person tent means comfort but cold; a low one, around 1.10 m, with company inside, keeps the warmth."],["What to check in the shop","• Good <b>ventilation inside</b>: mesh inner, breathable flysheet, zip or velcro vents.<br>• <b>Porch</b>: a covered space at the entrance for wet clothes and boots, gold in winter.<br>• Strong materials.<br>There are also pop-up tents that open by themselves with flexible poles, and tents that open like an umbrella, cheap and quick, not for rough weather."],["On site: pitching","• The flysheet should be <b>taut over the frame</b>, with no sagging; that way it doesn't flap in the wind.<br>• The flysheet <b>must not touch the inner</b>, where it touches, rain or night condensation gets in.<br>• <b>Tarp underneath</b>: keeps out ground damp and the tent packs away clean. A second one makes a windbreak or shelter.<br>• If the tent isn't rainproof, <b>don't throw plastic sheeting over it</b>: it stops it breathing and fills it with damp. Rig a <b>sloping taut tarp</b> above it on poles or branches.<br>• A 3-season tent becomes more winter-ready if you sew windproof covers over the mesh panels."],["What you carry with it","• Plenty of rope: for a shelter, for extra guylines in wind, for everything<br>• A light mallet, rubber or wooden (or the back of an axe)<br>• A few spare pegs<br>• Two tarps"],["Its pros and cons","<b>Pros</b>: cheap accommodation, space, contact with nature, good protection from cold and rain, provided the tent is right and well pitched. <b>Cons</b>: effort to pitch and pack, practically no protection from theft or attack, little protection if you bought poor quality, and it needs a suitable spot and ground."]],"q":[{"q":"You're going on a winter weekend in the mountains with possible snow. Which tent is the best choice?","a":["A 4-season dome (igloo) with crossing poles","A single-skin summer tent with big openings","A ridge tent with two poles"],"w":"Crossing poles give strength against snow and side wind."},{"q":"In the morning you find the inner wet in one spot. What's the most likely cause?","a":["You had a vent open","The flysheet was touching the inner there","The tent was pitched too tight"],"w":"Where the two fabrics touch, water finds its way inside."},{"q":"It starts raining and your tent isn't waterproof. What do you do?","a":["Throw plastic sheeting straight over it","Close all openings and wait","Rig a taut sloping tarp above it"],"w":"Plastic on the tent stops it breathing and brings damp inside."},{"q":"Why is a low tent worth it when it's cold?","a":["It pitches faster","It's always cheaper","Your body heats it more easily"],"w":"Less air volume means your body heat is enough to warm it."}]},{"id":"c57_rooftent","n":"Sleeping on or inside the vehicle","src":"Overland’s Habit · Camp life","p":[["Three ways without a classic tent","• <b>Rooftop tent</b>: a bed on the roof rack.<br>• <b>Inside the vehicle</b>: you make a bed in the load bed or the rear.<br>• <b>Bivouac</b>: the climbers' term for a night outdoors without shelter, often in bad weather. It's also the name of the small insulated bag-tent, a sleeping bag with a “roof”. An emergency option, with no comfort at all."],["Rooftop tent: what you gain, what you pay","<b>You gain</b>: no pitching or striking, bedding stays in place, a ready soft mattress, space inside the vehicle, height away from insects and snakes. The ground doesn't matter, rocks or mud, same thing. You only need to <b>level the vehicle</b>, by eye or with blocks and a spirit level.<br><b>You pay</b>: expensive and heavy. It <b>raises the centre of gravity a lot</b>, increases consumption, worsens handling on tarmac at speed and on dirt, and snags on branches. You can't stand up to dress, and a night toilet trip means climbing down a ladder. Single-skin, it isn't ideal for winter and strong wind."],["If you buy a rooftop tent","• Two types: <b>opens like a book</b> (hard base, fabric dome) or <b>a box that lifts straight up</b> with a mechanism and fabric on all four sides. Mechanism and price differ.<br>• Open and close every candidate in the shop.<br>• Check the lid locks firmly and the latches are solid.<br>• <b>Closed, it must not stick out beyond the roof outline</b>, otherwise it's a serious hazard on the road."],["Sleeping inside: what you gain, what you pay","The vehicle is a metal box: it keeps out wind and rain, not biting cold or summer heat. It must be long and wide enough to lie down comfortably.<br><b>You gain</b>: ready in seconds, protection in any weather, you can sleep almost anywhere with a small level spot, discreetly, even where a tent isn't allowed.<br><b>You pay</b>: without upgrades, only moderate protection from cold, damp and heat; cramped for two with bags, dressing and muddy shoes; every time you move you pack up and remake everything."],["Build your bed","• The usual solution: a <b>wooden platform</b> with storage underneath and a flat sleeping surface on top, so you don't unload in the snow to lie down.<br>• Then sort out the mattress, warmth in winter, air in summer, where you dress in the rain, where the muddy stuff goes.<br>• <b>Ventilation in rain</b>: <b>wind deflectors</b> on the front windows, so they can stay slightly open without letting water in.<br>• <b>Insects</b>: removable window screens or simple netting hung inside the doors; whatever gets in stays trapped and bites during the day too.<br>• <b>Darkness</b>: dark curtains or the silver sunshades sold at petrol stations. Thick dense fabric also cuts condensation on the glass.<br>You'll find plenty of conversion ideas, from SUV to VAN, online."]],"q":[{"q":"You're considering a rooftop tent. What changes in driving?","a":["Cornering grip improves","The centre of gravity and fuel consumption go up","Ground clearance increases"],"w":"Weight up high means a less stable vehicle and more fuel."},{"q":"In the shop, which rooftop tent do you reject straight away?","a":["One that, closed, sticks out beyond the roof outline","One that opens like a book","One with an aluminium ladder"],"w":"Anything protruding from the roof is a hazard on the road."},{"q":"You're sleeping inside the vehicle and it's raining. How do you ventilate?","a":["Leave the engine running with the heater on","Open the tailgate a little","Open the front windows slightly, fitted with wind deflectors"],"w":"Deflectors let air in without the rain."}]},{"id":"c57_heat","n":"Warmth at night: safe options and traps","src":"Overland’s Habit · Camp life","p":[["The first rule","• <b>Don't sleep with the engine running</b> for the heater. At best you wake up out of fuel.<br>• In a tent or vehicle <b>almost everything catches fire easily</b>: sleeping bags, clothes, fabric. Treat every heat source as dangerous."],["What works","• <b>Parking heater (liquid fuel)</b>: many diesel 4×4s have one or can take one. A small burner uses a few grams of fuel per hour from the tank and a fan sends the heat to the cabin and/or the engine for pre-heating. It uses little fuel but <b>a fair amount of power</b>, so you need a <b>second battery</b>. Fitting is done <b>by a specialist</b>, along with the wiring. Home-made builds: only if you really know what you're doing.<br>• <b>12 V electric blanket</b>: laid on the mattress, under a sheet or sleeping bag. It draws about <b>6 A</b>, so a strong battery lasts around <b>7 hours</b>. For the whole night you need a second battery. There are also 12 V heated seat covers with a timer.<br>• <b>Candle lantern</b>: an enclosed lantern hung from the roof; it doesn't tip over, smoke or drip. It doesn't heat much, it <b>cuts damp</b> and the chill. Use only its proper candles; ordinary ones melt and drip."],["Under strict conditions: gas heater","Only a small <b>catalytic one with no open flame</b>, from a camping shop. A patio heater is out of the question. Before lighting it:<br>• Read its instructions.<br>• Leave <b>an opening for fresh air</b>, e.g. a zip half open.<br>• Put it on a <b>stable spot</b> where it can't tip over.<br>• Nothing flammable next to it, no clothes, no sleeping bag.<br>• <b>Turn it off before you sleep or leave</b>.<br>Improvised candles (tea lights, oil lamps in a lantern) mean <b>an exposed flame</b> next to fabric, better not."],["What doesn't work for the night","• <b>12 V fan heaters from the lighter socket</b>: up to ~100 W because of the fuse, they blow lukewarm air.<br>• <b>Bigger fan heaters</b> wired straight to the battery with a 30 A fuse, ~300 W: with the engine off they flatten the battery in about <b>2 hours</b>.<br>• <b>12 V infrared heater</b> (~400 W, 35 A): same problem.<br>These are meant to help the cabin <b>while the engine runs</b>, not for sleeping."],["Power from the sun","Solar panels (folding or flexible, from marine shops and online) charge the vehicle battery and small devices: phone, GPS, laptop, camera, torch batteries. But be realistic: in winter on a cloudy mountain or in dense forest there's little sun. For a proper battery charge they need <b>a full day in the sun</b>."]],"q":[{"q":"You want a gas heater inside the tent. Which option is acceptable, with care?","a":["A patio heater, in the corner","A small flameless catalytic one, with an air opening, off before sleep","A camping burner lit on the floor"],"w":"Only flameless catalytic, ventilated, stable and off when you sleep."},{"q":"You have one strong battery and a 12 V electric blanket (about 6 A). What do you expect?","a":["It will last the whole weekend","It will be flat in half an hour","It will last about 7 hours, not necessarily the whole night"],"w":"For a full night without worrying about starting, you need a second battery."},{"q":"A 300 W fan heater on the battery with the engine off. What's the problem?","a":["It flattens the battery in about 2 hours","It doesn't heat at all","It's illegal"],"w":"12 V fan and infrared heaters are for use with the engine running."},{"q":"What does a parking heater need to run properly all night?","a":["One extra jerrycan of fuel and nothing else","A second battery and fitting by a specialist","Windows open all night"],"w":"The fuel it burns is small; the power it draws isn't."}]},{"id":"c57_sleep","n":"Warm sleep: from the ground to the sleeping bag","src":"Overland’s Habit · Camp life","p":[["How warmth works","The sleeping bag doesn't warm you. What warms you is <b>the air trapped around you</b>, the bag just holds it. Two consequences:<br>• Anything that <b>compresses the fill</b> (heavy blankets on top, thick clothes inside) pushes the air out and makes you colder.<br>• <b>You lose more heat downwards</b> than upwards: the ground draws heat all night."],["First the sleeping mat","• <b>Ruled out</b>: the inflatable beach mattress. The air inside stays cold and passes the ground's chill up to you.<br>• <b>Cheap and good</b>: a foil blanket (insulating mat).<br>• <b>If you feel the cold easily</b>: one or two wool blankets <b>underneath</b>, or even a rug or a flokati, if you have space.<br>• <b>Best balance of comfort and warmth</b>: a mountaineering <b>self-inflating mat</b>. At home store it <b>inflated</b> (in the loft, under a bed), not squashed in its bag, so its foam keeps its spring."],["Then the sleeping bag","For the mountains outside summer, a proper sleeping bag <b>can't be replaced</b>: not by two cheap ones inside each other, not by blankets, clothes, candles or a liner. A sleepless night ruins the next day, and in the mountains that's a safety issue.<br>• For winter choose a <b>mummy</b> shape: narrow at the feet, wider at the shoulders, with a closing hood. A rectangular bag is comfy, but not for the mountains.<br>• In a good bag, thermals and a well-closed top are enough.<br>• Synthetic or down fill: with a vehicle, weight and bulk matter little; the criterion is warmth."],["Read the label","• <b>Comfort</b>: the lowest temperature for comfortable sleep, in thermals with the bag closed.<br>• <b>Extreme</b>: the lowest temperature at which it protects you from frostbite, wearing all your clothes, survival, not sleep.<br>• <b>Tolerance</b>: the range in which you can sleep in it.<br>For a Greek mountain in winter aim for about <b>−15°C comfort</b>, depending on how much you feel the cold. Before sunrise, in a shady snowy spot, it drops even lower. In summer the same bag will cook you."],["In the shop","• Buy from a <b>mountain-gear shop</b>.<br>• <b>Spread it on the floor</b> before you pay.<br>• If it doesn't loft, or it's flattened or misshapen from packaging, ask for another.<br>• Don't believe that a thin, “empty” sleeping bag will keep you warm, whoever tells you so."]],"q":[{"q":"You're sleeping on cold ground. Which mat will chill you the most?","a":["An inflatable beach mattress","A wool blanket underneath","A self-inflating mountaineering mat"],"w":"The air in an inflatable doesn't warm up and passes on the ground's chill."},{"q":"You're cold in your sleeping bag and think of piling three heavy blankets on top. What will happen?","a":["You'll definitely be much warmer","They'll compress the fill and push out the air that keeps you warm","Nothing will change"],"w":"Insulation is air; whatever compresses it reduces it. Put the blankets underneath instead."},{"q":"The label says Comfort −5°C and Extreme −20°C. What cold do you count on for comfortable sleep?","a":["−20°C","−5°C","Any temperature in between"],"w":"Extreme is a survival limit; for sleep, Comfort is what counts."},{"q":"You're back from a trip. How do you store the self-inflating mat?","a":["Tightly rolled with a strap","Permanently in the boot","Inflated, not squashed in its bag"],"w":"That way the foam inside keeps its shape and spring."}]},{"id":"c57_bagcare","n":"Sleeping bag: down or synthetic, and how to keep it alive","src":"Overland’s Habit · Camp life","p":[["Choose the fill","• <b>Down</b>: warm, light, packs small and lofts back up. It warms because it holds air between its clusters. The weak point: <b>when wet, it clumps</b> and loses almost all its insulation.<br>• <b>Synthetic</b>: <b>cheaper</b> and <b>copes better with damp</b>. The fill is like a sheet of fibres and doesn't slide down with gravity. But where it's constantly pressed it loses loft fairly fast and makes cold spots.<br>If you expect a lot of damp, synthetic is safer; if you keep it dry, down is warmer."],["If you buy down","• Look first at <b>fill power</b>: how much air the fill holds. The higher, the warmer, and the pricier.<br>• Down-to-feather ratio: around <b>30–70%</b> is medium quality, <b>80–95%</b> very good.<br>• The bird matters too: <b>goose</b> is considered the best.<br>• You want <b>baffles</b>: without them, every time you turn the down slides off your shoulders and hips and leaves cold spots. More baffles, less shifting, a little more weight, with a vehicle you don't care."],["On the trip","• Down bag: <b>keep it away from rain and water</b>.<br>• For really biting cold add a <b>fleece liner</b> inside. It gives a bit of extra warmth but <b>doesn't replace</b> a proper sleeping bag."],["Washing","• <b>Synthetic</b>: zip closed, turned inside out, washing machine on the delicates programme, <b>no spin</b>.<br>• <b>Down</b>: do what the label says. If it says nothing, avoid the machine and especially spinning, use a special cleaner from a mountain shop and <b>no tumble dryer</b>. Never together with the trip clothes.<br>Treat it like a good woollen."],["Back home","• <b>Shake it out</b> so the fill lofts again.<br>• <b>Air it for at least 24 hours</b>.<br>• Store it <b>open, not in the compression sack</b>: under the bed wrapped in an old sheet, or on a trouser hanger in the loft.<br>• If it hangs, move the point where the hanger presses now and then, so you don't get a permanent cold strip."]],"q":[{"q":"Your down sleeping bag got soaked in the rain. What do you expect tonight?","a":["It will be as warm as ever","The fill will clump and lose much of its insulation","It will be warmer because of the damp"],"w":"Down insulates with the air it holds; wet, it loses it."},{"q":"You're comparing two down bags. Which spec tells you most about warmth?","a":["The colour of the fabric","The size of the stuff sack","Fill power and an 80–95% down ratio"],"w":"High fill power means the fill holds more air."},{"q":"You're back from a weekend. What do you do with the sleeping bag?","a":["Shake it, air it for 24 hours and store it open","Leave it in the compression sack until next time","Wash it with the clothes and put it in the dryer"],"w":"Permanent compression and damp ruin the fill."}]},{"id":"c57_wash","n":"Washing and toilet away from facilities","src":"Overland’s Habit · Camp life","p":[["A shower with little water","Dust from the dirt track, sweat, salt from the sea, in summer you'll want to wash every day. Two simple solutions:<br>• <b>Garden pressure sprayer</b>: a container with a <b>hand pump</b>. You pump it up, press the trigger and the air pushes the water out. The pressure makes it feel like plenty of water: with a little practice, <b>5 litres are enough for a short shower for two</b>. Not enough for very long hair.<br>• <b>Solar shower bag</b>: leave it on the roof or hang it from a branch; a dark bag in the sun gives lukewarm to hot water. Drawbacks: it often drips, can't go inside the vehicle full and suffers on the roof rack when full. A variation: a shower head permanently fitted to a jerrycan strapped to the rack."],["A tank in the vehicle","• <b>Simple</b>: a ~40 L plastic jerrycan secured inside and a small submersible pump on the lighter socket, you don't lift 40 kg to rinse off.<br>• <b>Permanent</b> (for a big vehicle, as on a boat or caravan): a wooden box on the floor with a flexible marine tank or a plastic tank held by straps, and an electric pump that starts by itself when you open the tap, with a switch at the battery.<br>• Think in advance <b>how you'll fill it</b>: from a tap with a hose and funnel is easy; from a river needs another solution.<br>• Take a <b>long hose</b>, it also does for a quick wash of the vehicle."],["Mind the pump and the hot water","• With an electric pump, <b>switch it off before you set off</b>: the bumps open the tap and soak everything.<br>• Hot water from the engine, with a heat exchanger in the cooling circuit, is possible, but only if a <b>mechanic</b> designs it (pipe sizes, capacity). Otherwise you risk overheating the engine or scalding yourself."],["A bathroom off the tailgate","The <b>rear door</b> and a tarp give you a cubicle. If the door opens upwards it's a roof; if it opens sideways it's a wall to tie the awning to. The same space becomes a kitchen in wind or rain, storage for bulky things at night, a spot to change or eat in a downpour. You need a tarp with eyelets, rope, poles, pegs, or a ready-made commercial solution."],["Toilet without a trace","• <b>Shovel</b>: the basic solution. Bury everything, paper too.<br>• <b>No wet wipes</b>: they don't break down and stay on the spot for years.<br>• A <b>folding seat</b> if squatting doesn't work for you, you still need the shovel.<br>• <b>Special bags</b> for men and women, for emergencies inside a tent or vehicle; armies and expeditions use them, they're not cheap.<br>• <b>Chemical toilet</b>: no shovel or bags, but it needs special fluids and is emptied <b>only at designated points</b>, e.g. at a campsite. Never on the ground.<br>And don't bring the whole house with you: a few comforts fewer make the experience more real."]],"q":[{"q":"You have a garden pressure sprayer. How much water do you allow for a short shower for two?","a":["Half a litre","About 5 litres","At least 40 litres"],"w":"The pressure makes a little water go a long way."},{"q":"Before heading onto a dirt track, why do you switch off the electric water pump?","a":["Because the bumps may open the tap and flood the vehicle","So the water doesn't heat up","So the pump doesn't clog with dust"],"w":"With the pump on standby, the tap only has to open slightly to empty the tank inside the vehicle."},{"q":"What do you do with toilet paper and wet wipes in nature?","a":["Leave them, the rain will dissolve them","Burn them on the spot","Bury the paper with the shovel; never leave wet wipes"],"w":"Wet wipes don't break down and stay for years."},{"q":"The chemical toilet is full. Where do you empty it?","a":["In a pit away from water","Only at a designated point, e.g. a campsite","In a flowing stream"],"w":"Chemical fluids never go into the ground or water."}]},{"id":"c57_living","n":"The camp lounge: seating, shade, light","src":"Overland’s Habit · Camp life","p":[["Where you sit","Chairs and a table aren't compulsory. But if you have space, you soon won't do without them.<br>• <b>Chair</b>: the classic 4×4 one is the <b>folding chair that closes like an umbrella</b>, with a cup holder and pockets, comfortable and small when closed.<br>• <b>Table</b>: the classic ones with legs folding underneath need flat space on the boot floor. Handier are the <b>aluminium roll-up tables</b> or the suitcase tables with stools.<br>• For relaxing, one or two <b>hammocks</b>.<br>Choose based on your space and needs."],["Shade and roof","• A <b>beach umbrella or a tarp</b> over where you sit: shade in summer, protection from drizzle in winter.<br>• <b>Rack-mounted awning</b> with a folding aluminium frame: opens quickly and is very practical. The cost: <b>higher consumption</b>, noise on the road and permanent weight up high."],["Choose your light","• <b>Gas (cartridge)</b>: strong white light, simple. But the glass and mantle <b>don't survive the bumps</b>.<br>• <b>Paraffin (hurricane)</b>: warm light, nice atmosphere. But little light, open flame, smoke, smell, and fuel that can spill in the vehicle.<br>• <b>Electric LED table lamp</b>: strong and completely safe; with rechargeable batteries you carry no spares. Get good-quality <b>LED</b>, it uses far less.<br>• <b>From the vehicle battery</b>: lots of light without batteries, but the vehicle isn't always nearby, and light + fridge + music drain the battery faster than you think.<br>• <b>Candle lantern</b>: atmosphere, and with citronella it keeps insects away. But it gives little light, blows out in the wind, and the candles melt in the hot vehicle.<br>• <b>Solar lights</b>: charge by day, light for hours at night, if there's sun. In cloud or forest they fall short."],["A set that works","There's no perfect light; combine:<br>• <b>A head torch each</b>, light where you look and hands free<br>• One <b>strong LED table lamp</b> with rechargeable batteries for kitchen and lounge<br>• If you have a second battery: an <b>LED floodlight with a long cable and magnetic base</b>"]],"q":[{"q":"You're cooking in the dark and want light and both hands free. What do you use?","a":["A paraffin lamp on the table","A candle lantern","A head torch"],"w":"It lights where you look without you holding it."},{"q":"Why is a gas lamp not ideal for dirt-track trips?","a":["Its glass and mantle break with the bumps","It gives very little light","It needs 12 V power"],"w":"Its fragile parts don't survive being carried on dirt roads."},{"q":"You're considering a permanent awning on the rack. What should you know?","a":["It takes half an hour to put up","It raises consumption and noise and adds weight up high","It doesn't give enough shade"],"w":"It's practical, but you carry it every kilometre."}]},{"id":"c57_kitchen","n":"Outdoor kitchen: packing and a stove that won't let you down","src":"Overland’s Habit · Camp life","p":[["Why cook","It takes organisation and a bit of effort, but a hot meal shared by the group at the end of the day changes the whole trip. All it takes: having brought the utensils and ingredients."],["What you take","• Stove and <b>the right fuel</b>, firelighters, grill, lighters<br>• Pots, plates, cutlery, cups<br>• Multitool, opener<br>• Water container, wash bowl, sponge, washing-up liquid<br>• Kitchen roll, rubbish bags, tablecloth, kitchen gloves<br>• Cool box and ice packs"],["Pack it in a box","Put all the kitchen gear in one <b>plastic box</b> of the right size:<br>• You find everything in one place, coffee, gas, mugs together, and nothing breaks.<br>• Bottles stay <b>upright</b>, so fewer leaks.<br>• The rigid sides wedge in well and don't move around.<br>• If oil, wine, sugar or rice spills, only the box gets dirty.<br>On long trips also lay a <b>thick plastic sheet</b> cut to the size of the boot. Mud, oil, fuel, antifreeze, sooner or later something spills, and carpet is hard to clean. At home you take it out, rinse it and store it."],["Gas stove: cold and altitude","• Up high there's less oxygen: the flame <b>weakens</b> and burns <b>more gas</b>.<br>• The small <b>butane</b> cartridge stops working near 0°C.<br>• <b>Below zero butane freezes</b>: you definitely need <b>propane</b>.<br>• Prefer a larger <b>butane–propane</b> cylinder with a big burner. A strong stove can be turned down; a weak one won't even boil water for tea."],["Liquid-fuel stove","Burners that run on paraffin or petrol, fuels you find everywhere. They're very powerful and <b>work below zero</b>. Petrol needs care, because it's dangerous. With a vehicle you have space, so you don't need the expensive lightweight mountaineering stoves, unless you'll also climb on foot."]],"q":[{"q":"Winter night in the mountains, −5°C. Which stove will work reliably?","a":["A small butane-only cartridge","A propane or liquid-fuel stove","Any, the cold makes no difference"],"w":"Below zero butane freezes; propane and liquid fuels work."},{"q":"You go up to high altitude. What changes when cooking on gas?","a":["The flame weakens and the cylinder runs out faster","The flame gets stronger","Nothing"],"w":"Less oxygen means weaker combustion and higher consumption."},{"q":"Why is a plastic box worth it for kitchen gear?","a":["It keeps food cold without a cool box","It weighs less than a bag","Everything in one place, bottles upright, spills stay inside"],"w":"Order, fewer breakages and a clean boot."}]},{"id":"c57_load","n":"Load it right: weight, positions, securing","src":"Overland’s Habit · Camp life","p":[["Before loading: less stuff, fewer people","• On your first off-road outings keep the load small. <b>Weight</b> tires the engine, wears shocks and suspension faster on dirt, and <b>greatly raises the chance of getting stuck</b> in mud or snow.<br>• Ideal crew: <b>two people</b>, driver and co-driver.<br>• A good co-driver stays calm, doesn't get carsick easily, gives clear directions (left–right without hesitating), reads a map, puts up with hardship and is interested in nature and weather.<br>• If you have no company, a <b>4×4 club</b> gives you companionship, help, advice and training."],["What goes where","• <b>Heavy goes low</b>, that's the basic rule.<br>• <b>Recovery gear</b> (heavy, bulky, always on board) in its own box, <b>firmly secured</b> and easy to reach from the boot.<br>• On the <b>roof rack or roof box</b> only <b>light and bulky</b> items. Weight up high raises the centre of gravity and the risk of rollover on extreme slopes or tight bends at speed.<br>• Extra <b>fuel</b> only in a dedicated <b>external jerrycan holder</b>.<br>• A roof box also raises consumption and noise, changes the left–right weight balance and can bump you up a toll category."],["Organise in bags and boxes","Use plastic boxes or wood/metal boxes made to fit the vehicle, and separate:<br>• Personal items in <b>small soft bags</b><br>• Shoes separately<br>• Blankets, sheets, sleeping bags separately<br>• Tent, mats, tarps separately<br>• Kitchen gear separately<br>That way you get a jumper without unloading the whole vehicle in the rain."],["Tie it all down","The load will go over rocks, rivers and steep slopes, anything not tied down will move.<br>• <b>Don't stack above the seat backs</b>: you lose rear visibility when reversing, and in hard braking or on a descent things fall on you.<br>• Hold it with an <b>elastic cargo net</b>, like the motorcycle ones, less rattling on dirt.<br>• Cover it with a <b>dark cover</b>: it keeps the dust off and cuts reflections in the windows when manoeuvring."],["What you keep, what you leave","• <b>Never leave the recovery gear behind</b> to fit other things. A loaded 4×4 gets stuck more easily, without straps and tools it won't get unstuck.<br>• Leave the show items: flared arches, excessive lights, side steps, big bumpers that change the dimensions. They don't help in the mountains. For any modification, ask experienced people first.<br>• <b>Trailer box: no</b> for Greek off-road. Narrow tracks and village lanes. If you meet an obstacle and must turn back, you unhitch and turn the vehicle and the loaded trailer separately, often by hand. Plus consumption, instability and noise."]],"q":[{"q":"You have a heavy toolbox and a light bag of sleeping bags. Where does each go?","a":["The box on the rack, the sleeping bags in the boot","The box low inside, the sleeping bags on the rack","Both on the rack to save space"],"w":"Heavy low, light and bulky up high, for a low centre of gravity."},{"q":"Not everything fits. What do you never leave behind?","a":["The chairs","The second set of lights","The basic recovery gear"],"w":"A loaded 4×4 gets stuck more easily; without straps it won't get out."},{"q":"Why don't you stack things higher than the seat backs?","a":["They block visibility and fall on you in hard braking","It wears the upholstery","It increases consumption"],"w":"Safety: rear visibility and nothing to hit you."},{"q":"Why doesn't a trailer box suit Greek mountains?","a":["It's banned on dirt roads","It doesn't hold enough","On a narrow road, to turn back you must unhitch it and turn it, often by hand"],"w":"In tight spots turning round becomes very hard, plus instability and consumption."}]},{"id":"c1011_campsite","n":"Where to set up for the night","src":"Overland’s Habit · Camp life","p":[["Legally: you overnight, you don't camp","In Greece wild camping is <b>prohibited</b>. It is only allowed in organised sites, municipal or private. Simply <b>overnighting</b>, from sunset to sunrise, is allowed. In some areas even that is banned, find out what applies before you leave.<br>• Stay away from inhabited and busy spots.<br>• Not in National Parks or protected areas.<br>• The bans are serious, not a formality."],["If you sleep in the vehicle","With a rooftop tent or sleeping inside the 4×4 you have many more options: the edge of a dirt track, a clearing, a car park. The rule: stay <b>discreet</b>. Don't spread out, don't disturb, don't draw attention."],["Search while there's light","Allow about <b>2 hours of light</b> before sunset to find a spot and set up without stress. Darkness, fatigue, bad weather and an unexpected delay push you into bad decisions."],["Cold, altitude, valleys","• Winter on high, bare ground without gear for severe cold: <b>no</b>. Temperatures drop to life-threatening levels.<br>• Go lower, to a level, sheltered spot, e.g. at the edge of a forest, with room for the tent and your kit.<br>• Cold air is heavier and collects at the bottom of deep valleys. Damp and ice form there, and dawn bites.<br>• If you stay in a valley, count on the freezing draught flowing down, or move a little higher."],["Spot check before you unload","• Not easily seen from the road or farmhouses.<br>• At the forest edge you can see around you.<br>• Trees overhead cut cold and night damp (heavy all year, except summer).<br>• Leaves on the ground hide twigs and pine cones, clear them.<br>• Look for glass, wire, thorns, ants, sharp stones.<br>• Look up: wasp nests, bees, dry branches that could fall.<br>• In rain: not on loose soil or where you see signs of running water.<br>• Never under a lone tall tree, it draws lightning.<br>• Ideally: slightly higher than the surroundings, but sheltered from the wind."]],"q":[{"q":"You reach the area half an hour before dark. What's the problem?","a":["None, a torch is enough","You have far less than the ~2 hours of light you need to choose well","That it will be hot"],"w":"With little light and fatigue you choose in a hurry and often wrongly."},{"q":"Winter night: where will you get the most ice and damp?","a":["At the bottom of a deep valley","On a gentle slope at a forest edge","In a clearing a little higher up"],"w":"Cold air flows down and pools in low ground."},{"q":"A storm is coming. Which spot do you avoid at all costs?","a":["A dense stand of low trees","An open spot away from tall objects","Under a lone tall tree"],"w":"An isolated tall tree is a target for lightning."}]},{"id":"c1011_nocamp","n":"Water next to the tent: why not","src":"Overland’s Habit · Camp life","p":[["The riverbank trap","A spot by a river looks perfect. In practice, and especially in winter, it brings you more problems than it solves:<br>• Damp and a freezing air current that follows the riverbed, the cold gets heavy.<br>• The noise of the water masks anything that would warn you: rising water, an animal, a person.<br>• Summer: shade from plane trees, but also mosquitoes and clouds of insects."],["A dry bed doesn't mean safe","A flash flood is rare, but it kills. In the mountains a calm stream becomes a torrent within minutes and the level rises metres in a short time. On the plains too, an old dry riverbed can fill in seconds from rain in the hills or a dam release, without a drop falling where you are."],["If you must stay close","• Find the marks of the <b>highest water level</b> on the banks (debris, branches, a line in the soil) and pitch well above them.<br>• Stay alert even in a dry bed.<br>• If it's raining higher up, you leave, you don't wait to see."],["Don't blindly trust a tip","An \"amazing spot\" someone recommended may be fine for a summer picnic and useless for a winter night: exposed, sloping, frozen by the river. Always ask which season and what needs they mean, and keep an alternative."],["List: you don't sleep here","• Bare peaks battered by wind.<br>• Bottoms of deep valleys (damp, ice).<br>• Plateaus with soggy ground.<br>• In or next to riverbeds, especially in rain.<br>• Under tall trees in a storm.<br>• On heavy animal tracks."]],"q":[{"q":"The sky above you is clear, but you see black clouds over the mountains higher up. You're in a dry riverbed. What do you do?","a":["Move higher, out of the riverbed, at once","Stay, since it isn't raining here","Dig a trench around the tent"],"w":"Water from rain higher up arrives in minutes, without warning."},{"q":"Why is the sound of the river a drawback at night?","a":["It stops you reading","It scares animals away","It hides danger sounds, like rising water or someone approaching"],"w":"You lose your hearing as an alarm system."},{"q":"Someone suggests \"the best spot by the river\" for a winter trip. What do you do?","a":["Go without a second thought","Ask which season and needs they mean, and keep an alternative","Go only if it's a weekend"],"w":"A summer swimming spot rarely works for a night in the cold."}]},{"id":"c1011_scout","n":"Small camp tricks","src":"Overland’s Habit · Camp life","p":[["At night: warm sleep","In winter it isn't the air that chills you most. It's the damp, frozen ground underneath. So insulation goes <b>downwards</b>: mat, thick fluffy blankets on top of it, and then the sleeping bag."],["At the fire: pots and tinder","• Before putting a pot on the fire, rub its outside with green soap. Afterwards the soot rinses off easily.<br>• Have pots just for the fire, in their own bag that doesn't dirty anything? Leave the black: a black surface takes heat faster, so less wood and time.<br>• Fire won't catch? Look on pine trees for lumps of <b>dried resin</b>. It burns strongly, like a liquid firelighter, without the dangers."],["Cooking without pans","• Egg: pierce the shell slightly with something sharp and rest it at the edge of the embers.<br>• Stone as a pan: flat, smooth (e.g. slate), clean, heated in the fire, the heat sterilises it. Good for eggs, bacon, sausages. <b>Careful:</b> a stone with cavities or moisture inside can burst violently. Choose a dry, solid one, not from a river.<br>• Orange as a pot: cut off the top, empty the inside without piercing the peel, and heat a little water in it on the embers."],["In the morning: packing up","• Shake and clear grass and leaves off the tent floor and sleeping bag before folding them, otherwise they go mouldy and rot.<br>• Tent wet or just damp? As soon as you're home, pitch it or spread it out to dry completely. Otherwise it's ruined."]],"q":[{"q":"You're cold at night although the sleeping bag is good. Where do you add the extra blanket?","a":["On top, over the sleeping bag","Around your head","On the mat, under the sleeping bag"],"w":"Most of the cold comes from the frozen ground."},{"q":"You want to cook on a stone. Which one do you pick?","a":["A flat, dry, solid stone with no cavities","A wet stone from the river","Whichever is closest to the fire"],"w":"Water trapped inside the stone boils and makes it burst."},{"q":"You got home with a damp tent. What do you do?","a":["Store it as it is until the next trip","Open it up straight away to dry completely","Put it in the sun inside its bag"],"w":"Moisture in the bag brings mould and ruins the fabric."}]},{"id":"c1011_animals","n":"Animals around camp","src":"Overland’s Habit · Camp life","p":[["What you need to know","A wild animal almost always wants to avoid you. It attacks when it feels trapped or threatened, when it protects young, or when you get between a pair. Sick or injured animals are unpredictable and may turn aggressive."],["Before you pitch","• Not on animal trails or next to places where they drink.<br>• Leave springs free, with space around them.<br>• No tent on an ant nest or near wasps and bees.<br>• In bear country: kitchen at least <b>30 m</b> from the tents. Food hung at least <b>30 m</b> away, <b>3 m</b> off the ground and <b>4 m</b> from trunks.<br>• Once you've eaten, food, pots and rubbish go into the vehicles. That way no night visitors and no bags torn open by martens and jackals."],["On the move","• Make a little noise as you walk, so they hear you before they see you.<br>• Don't tease, chase or feed.<br>• Lift stones from the far side before putting a hand underneath.<br>• Shake out clothes and shoes before putting them on.<br>• High hiking boots: most snake bites are low.<br>• If you find a young animal alone, don't touch it, your scent can make the parents abandon it."],["If you come face to face with a bear","Bears in Greece rarely attack, unless very hungry or feeling threatened.<br>• Don't block its escape route.<br>• Stay <b>calm and still</b>. Panic shows.<br>• When it loses interest, move away slowly, with slow movements, facing it. <b>Don't run.</b><br>• If it looks aggressive, something bothers it, most likely you're blocking its way.<br>• Last resort: climb a tree without thorns, prepared to stay there for hours."],["The rest, one by one","• <b>Wolves:</b> rare, protected, avoid people. Real danger only in an extreme case, e.g. badly injured and immobile.<br>• <b>Foxes:</b> many have learned there's easy food near people. However tame it looks, it's wild: shoo it with noise, don't approach or stroke it. If cornered, it bites.<br>• <b>Deer, roe deer, wild goats:</b> males, especially in the rut, can become dangerous. Move away discreetly, without turning your back.<br>• <b>Wild boar:</b> numerous now. They don't come for food, but if you're in the herd's path they may knock you down. Respect the tusks and mothers with young."],["Snakes, stings, rabies","• <b>Snakes:</b> rarely a problem. Calm, slow movements; if needed, push it gently away with a stick. You don't kill it, all species are protected. In Greece only <b>vipers</b> are venomous. They swim, too.<br>• <b>Sting:</b> first aid at once; if there are signs of allergy, 112.<br>• <b>Rabies:</b> cases have been found in wild animals in Northern Greece, so vaccine baits are dropped from the air every year. If you see such a bait, neither you nor your dog touches it.<br>• Sick or injured animal: notify the competent bodies."]],"q":[{"q":"A bear appears 20 metres from you. What's the right reaction?","a":["Run as fast as you can to the car","Stay calm, and when it loses interest move away slowly without turning your back","Throw it food to keep it busy"],"w":"Running can trigger a chase; calm lowers the tension."},{"q":"Camp in bear country. Where does the food go for the night?","a":["Hung at least 30 m away, 3 m high, 4 m from trunks, or locked in the car","Inside the tent, in a closed bag","Next to the fire"],"w":"Smell draws animals; you want it far from where you sleep."},{"q":"You see a snake on the path. Which is true?","a":["Kill it, it might be venomous","Stay calm and let it go; only vipers are venomous and all are protected","Throw stones at it so it leaves"],"w":"Snakes rarely strike if you leave them alone."},{"q":"Your dog is sniffing a rabies bait on the ground. What do you do?","a":["Let it eat it, it protects the dog","Pick it up and throw it in the bin","Lead the dog away and nobody touches it"],"w":"The baits are for wild animals and must be left alone."}]},{"id":"c1011_injured","n":"First care for an injured wild animal","src":"Overland’s Habit · Camp life","p":[["First call","Before anything else, find a <b>wildlife rehabilitation centre</b> online and call them. They'll tell you what this particular species needs and you'll arrange together how it gets to them. There are no general instructions that fit every animal."],["Prepare the box","• Bird or small animal: a cardboard box to its size, with <b>15–20 small holes</b> in the sides. Enough air, little light.<br>• Fox cub, larger animal or raptor with hooked talons: one or two fruit crates tied firmly together, lined with cardboard so it can't see out.<br>• Lay a plastic bag on the bottom and plenty of newspaper on top. The animal makes a nest and the car stays clean."],["Catching it","• Throw a large towel or small blanket over it, hold it gently and put it in the box.<br>• Sharp beak or talons: gloves, and watch the feet.<br>• Then: a quiet, warm spot, not in the sun, away from children and pets.<br>• <b>No stroking</b>, no sudden movements, no loud voices. Stress kills."],["Water yes, food no","Feeding takes know-how and can wait. What matters from the start is <b>hydration</b>.<br>• <b>Birds:</b> in a glass of water dissolve <b>1 teaspoon of sugar</b> and a pinch of salt (about <b>1/6 of a teaspoon</b>). Better with two people: towel over the head so it can't see, hold the body with the wings folded. With a needle-less syringe or dropper, a few drops into the mouth, and wait for it to swallow. If it's very small or won't open its beak, drip onto the edges. A little water is enough, back in the box and quiet.<br>• <b>Other animals:</b> a small dish of clean water inside the box."]],"q":[{"q":"You found an injured blackbird. What's the right box for transport?","a":["An open basket, so it can see out","A cardboard box with 15–20 small holes, paper and a bag on the bottom","A closed plastic bag"],"w":"Little light and enough air keep the bird calm and safe."},{"q":"What do you give a weak bird until the centre takes it?","a":["A few drops of water with 1 teaspoon of sugar and a pinch of salt per glass","Breadcrumbs and milk","As much water as possible, forced"],"w":"Hydration comes first; leave feeding to the specialists."},{"q":"The animal is in the box. What helps it most?","a":["Letting the children stroke it to calm it","Taking it out often to check on it","Quiet, warmth without sun, and a call to a rehabilitation centre"],"w":"Stress from people is one of the biggest dangers for a wild animal."}]},{"id":"c1011_fire","n":"Campfire: from the first match to the ashes","src":"Overland’s Habit · Camp life","p":[["If it isn't allowed, you don't light it","• <b>Fire season, 1 May – 30 October:</b> no fire at all in the countryside, let alone in a forest.<br>• Drought and strong wind, whatever the season: don't light. You won't control it.<br>• A small fire is enough for a deadly disaster, Mati proved it.<br>• Rule: the fire is always as small as you need, never bigger."],["Build the fire pit","• Choose a spot with natural wind shelter that throws the heat back towards you.<br>• A ring of stones or a small pit.<br>• A windbreak or the vehicle behind the fire, at a safe distance.<br>• Away from tents and anything that catches easily.<br>• On dry ground, clear a wide zone all around."],["Lighting and feeding","• <b>Never petrol.</b> If you lack experience, bring ready firelighter cubes or lamp oil.<br>• Easy option: <b>shoe polish</b> on cotton wool or toilet paper. It catches at once and, closed in its tin, doesn't make your kit smell.<br>• Wood: dry dead branches from the trees, they snap with a dry crack. What you find on the ground holds much more moisture.<br>• The colder it is, the stronger the wind or the sharper the frost, the <b>smaller</b> you start and the slower you grow it.<br>• Once it's going well, it can take damp wood. Put logs nearby, at a safe distance, to dry."],["Keep the balance","Fire lives on three things: <b>air, heat, fuel</b>. Upset one and it drops.<br>• Too much wood at once = you smother it.<br>• Wet wood or a heavy pot = they lower the temperature.<br>• Spread too wide = it loses heat and dies.<br>• Burning nicely? Leave it alone."],["Watch","• <b>Never</b> left alone, not even when it's raining.<br>• A log throwing sparks: out at once.<br>• Follow every spark that flies off and make sure it's out."],["Putting it out","• For sleep or departure: cover the whole pit with stones and plenty of soil. In the morning the embers will still be warm and it will relight easily.<br>• In strong wind: lots of water on the soil, stir, and check with your hand from a distance that no heat is coming off.<br>• In an emergency shelter: the fire goes <b>outside the entrance</b>, with a windbreak behind it. While it burns, the door <b>never closes fully</b>, smoke and carbon monoxide knock you out in your sleep and you don't wake up."]],"q":[{"q":"It's 15 October and the weather is cool. Can you light a fire in the countryside?","a":["No, we're still in the fire season (1/5–30/10)","Yes, October is outside the season","Yes, if it's small"],"w":"The fire season runs from 1 May to 30 October, whatever the weather."},{"q":"The fire won't catch and it's windy. What do you do?","a":["Pour on a little petrol","Start smaller, with firelighter and thin dry twigs, and build up slowly","Pile on lots of wood so it takes"],"w":"Petrol explodes and too much wood smothers the fire."},{"q":"It's raining and you're in an emergency shelter with a fire at the entrance. What about the door?","a":["Seal it tight to keep the heat in","It doesn't matter","It always stays partly open while the fire burns"],"w":"Smoke, fumes and carbon monoxide in a closed space kill in your sleep."}]},{"id":"c1011_cook","n":"Outdoor kitchen: plan and fire","src":"Overland’s Habit · Camp life","p":[["At home, before you leave","Answer these first:<br>• How many people, how many days?<br>• When will you next find a shop?<br>• What do you buy and how will you keep it fresh?<br>• Do you have a fridge? How much does it hold?<br>• At what altitude and on what (fire, gas stove) will you cook?<br>Goal: less weight, less rubbish, fewer pots and less time."],["What you load, what you leave","• Yes: ready meals in tins, pre-cooked portions, small packs that finish in one meal and need no fridge.<br>• Remove cardboard boxes and outer wrappers at home, don't carry them.<br>• No: anything that smells strongly (e.g. fish), anything that breaks and spills (raw eggs), anything that spoils without a fridge (anthotyro, fresh milk).<br>• Pasta: <b>orzo</b> or thick spaghetti instead of penne, same portion, half the volume."],["On the grill and in the embers","Potatoes in the embers and vegetables on the grill are food for everyone, vegetarians included. Keep room for a <b>grill</b>. A <b>fish grill</b> that closes like a sandwich lets you turn everything in one go, without roasting your hands and face. With legs it stands on its own, but takes space in the load."],["Pot over the fire","• It takes practice, and the pots go black.<br>• The pot sits on a <b>trivet</b> (tripod) or on two bricks or flat stones.<br>• Don't cook on the main fire. Rake a few coals to the side with the shovel and make a <b>second, small fire</b> just for cooking. That way you control the heat without disturbing the big one.<br>• Feed it with very thin sticks or brushwood gathered beforehand: steady heat, less soot."],["To warm up","• <b>Alcohol</b> for warmth or <b>coffee</b> to recover in the cold: no. They can upset the body's temperature regulation.<br>• Yes: hot tea with plenty of sugar and lemon, or hot soup.<br>• A freshly cooked dish in the mountains is doable, even on a gas stove, and it lifts the whole group's mood."]],"q":[{"q":"You want to simmer food while the big fire is burning hard. How do you do it?","a":["Put the pot in the middle of the fire","Pour water on the fire to bring it down","Rake a few coals aside and make a small cooking fire"],"w":"A separate cooking fire gives you control without spoiling the main fire."},{"q":"You're frozen after hours outside. What do you drink?","a":["Hot tea with sugar and lemon, or soup","A tsipouro","A double espresso"],"w":"Hot, sweet drinks help; alcohol and caffeine can hinder thermoregulation."},{"q":"Space in the load is tight. Which pasta do you take?","a":["Penne","Orzo","Farfalle"],"w":"Small, compact pasta fits in much less volume."}]},{"id":"c1011_food","n":"Food that keeps without a fridge","src":"Overland’s Habit · Camp life","p":[["The basic rule","The less fridge you have, the more carefully you shop. The times below apply <b>outside summer</b>, in the heat they shrink a lot. Whatever you open, you finish."],["Safe, no worries","• Rice, pasta, instant mash, tins: no problem. Even an opened packet of pasta keeps.<br>• An opened tin with leftovers: no, it goes.<br>• Coffee, sugar, oil, powders and liquids: in containers with an <b>airtight lid you have tested</b>, or you'll find them spilled.<br>• Salt, pepper, oregano, spices: in small plastic boxes with lids (e.g. chewing-gum tubs).<br>• <b>Long-life</b> milk in small cartons; for coffee, single evaporated-milk portions. Cream also long-life."],["Careful, a few days","• Hard yellow cheeses (kefalotyri, graviera, parmesan): <b>3–4 days</b>. Their enemy is air, wrap them tightly in cling film. If they start to \"sweat\" and turn glossy at the edges, fry and eat them.<br>• <b>Feta in brine:</b> 3–4 days depending on the heat. Once opened, you finish it all. Skip other white cheeses.<br>• Full-fat yoghurt: up to 2 days, never in summer. If you really need it (e.g. with antibiotics), keep space for it in the cool box.<br>• Fruit: large, thick-skinned (apples, oranges), not too ripe. No strawberries, kiwis, ripe bananas.<br>• Vegetables: large and firm, not leafy. For the grill: oyster mushrooms, courgettes, aubergines, onions. For the embers: potatoes, onions, chestnuts.<br>• Wash and dry fresh produce well at home, put it in a large plastic box and fill the gaps with paper as it empties, over the days it bruises more easily."],["Meat: caution","• Without a reliable fridge: <b>no</b> mince, chicken or fish.<br>• For <b>1–2 days</b> at most: freeze it in the butcher's paper and put it in an insulated bag, or wrap it in many layers of foil and then newspaper.<br>• Alternatively: traditional smoked and cured meats that need no fridge, mainly pork (syglino, apaki, smoked chop, village sausage). Pick the driest one, with the least liquid."],["The emergency bag","A small bag of fast energy is equipment, not a snack. After fatigue and cold, e.g. after digging in snow, it brings you round at once:<br>• Energy bars, nuts.<br>• Dried fruit: figs, apricots, prunes, dates, pineapple.<br>• Chocolate, sesame bar, sweets, figs with walnut.<br>• If you have nothing else, a spoonful of sugar."]],"q":[{"q":"Three days in November with no fridge. Which cheese do you take?","a":["Anthotyro","A piece of graviera tightly wrapped in cling film","Cream cheese"],"w":"Hard yellow cheeses keep 3–4 days outside summer, if air can't get at them."},{"q":"You want to grill chops on day two and have no fridge. What do you do?","a":["Keep them in a bag inside the car","Put them in the river water","Freeze them at home, in the butcher's paper, inside an insulated bag"],"w":"Frozen and insulated, meat lasts 1–2 days at most."},{"q":"You opened the feta at the first meal. Which is true?","a":["You finish it at that meal","It easily lasts to the end of the week","Covering it with tap water is enough"],"w":"Opened feta without a fridge spoils fast."}]},{"id":"c1011_dishes","n":"Washing up without polluting the water","src":"Overland’s Habit · Camp life","p":[["The routine","• Fill a <b>basin</b> with water and take it away from springs, lakes, rivers and the sea.<br>• Wash inside the basin, never directly in the trough, on the bank or in the surf.<br>• A little soap is enough."],["Which soap","Look on the label for <b>biodegradable</b> and <b>neutral pH</b> (about 7). It's in every supermarket and washes even with seawater."],["For your hands","Spring and stream water is ice-cold and within minutes you can't feel your fingers. A pair of heavy rubber kitchen gloves solves it, they take almost no space."]],"q":[{"q":"There's a crystal-clear spring next to camp. Where do you wash the pots?","a":["In a basin, well away from the spring","In the trough, since the water is running","In the stream flowing out of the spring"],"w":"Even a little soap pollutes water that people and animals drink."},{"q":"What does the right detergent's label say?","a":["pH about 2, for grease","Biodegradable, neutral pH about 7","pH about 12, for tough stains"],"w":"Neutral and biodegradable means less harm to nature."}]},{"id":"c1011_respect","n":"Leave without a trace","src":"Overland’s Habit · Camp life","p":[["Why it matters","In a few decades our rubbish has multiplied and changed: plastics and metals that last decades or centuries, and toxics like batteries, asbestos, medicines, pesticides. Whatever is left in a gully or thrown from a window ends up in the soil, the rivers, the sea. A broken piece of glass can act like a lens and start a fire; a discarded battery pollutes."],["Before you leave home","• Unpack the food: less rubbish in the mountains.<br>• Simple menu, fewer packages.<br>• Rubbish bags and a small shovel in the car."],["At camp","• Whatever rubbish you make goes in a bag and <b>leaves with you</b>.<br>• Organic and human waste: in a hole you dig, away from water and spots the rain washes over, away from trails and places where others pitch or walk. Cover with soil and disguise with natural material.<br>• Toilet paper sparingly, plain white. Used paper is buried in the hole or goes in the bag.<br>• Washing always away from springs, lakes, rivers, sea, with a little biodegradable, neutral-pH soap.<br>• Stones, plants, whatever you found in nature stays there. No pointless cutting of branches and flowers, no pointless killing of animals and insects."],["Animals and people","• Wild animals: watch from afar, don't approach, <b>never feed</b>. Pets under control. Extra care around nests, mating, young.<br>• Other visitors: came for quiet like you. Keep music and voices down.<br>• Herds and riders have <b>priority</b> on dirt tracks and trails. Stop, wait, no horn and no sudden movements, otherwise you scatter the animals and the shepherd has every reason to be angry.<br>• Shepherds' gates and fences: go through, but leave them <b>exactly as you found them</b>, closed or open."],["Final check","• Pick up whatever others left or the wind brought too. It's not shameful, it's responsibility.<br>• Look at your bag: all that would be in nature now.<br>• Leave the spot clean and inviting for whoever comes next.<br>• Same logic as the international Leave No Trace: what counts is what you take away, not what you brought."]],"q":[{"q":"You open a closed shepherd's gate to get through with the 4×4. Then?","a":["Leave it open for whoever comes behind you","Close it again, exactly as you found it","Leave it half open"],"w":"The gate's position keeps the animals where the shepherd wants them."},{"q":"A herd is coming towards you on the dirt track. What do you do?","a":["Honk so they make way","Drive slowly through them","Stop and wait quietly for them to pass"],"w":"Herds have priority and noise scatters them."},{"q":"You see a broken bottle in the forest. Why pick it up, even though it isn't yours?","a":["Because it can focus the sun like a lens and start a fire","Because it sells for recycling","No need, it isn't yours"],"w":"Glass is a hazard both for injury and for wildfire."}]},{"id":"c1516_convoy","n":"Convoy: moving as a group without losing anyone","src":"Overland’s Habit · Group, kids & pets","p":[["The order is sacred","The lead vehicle sets the route and everyone follows. You don't change position or stop without saying so: for an unplanned stop, <b>tell the lead on the radio first</b>. After each stop you take <b>the same position</b> again, that way you can count everyone at a glance."],["At turns: look back, not ahead","At every change of direction you indicate and <b>wait to see that the car behind has seen it</b> and indicated too. If they've fallen behind, you stop at the junction until they appear. Don't worry about the car in front: they'll wait for you. Result: nobody gets lost and not everyone has to stop at every junction."],["Spacing by road","• <b>Highway</b>: about <b>100 km/h</b> with <b>~50 m</b> spacing, closer is dangerous, further breaks the group up<br>• <b>Dirt road</b>: <b>10–20 m</b><br>• <b>Dust, gravel, mud, slime, snow, loose ground</b>: <b>three times</b> that"],["Overtaking","Not everyone into the outside lane together. <b>One at a time</b>: close up behind the slow vehicle, indicate, check for yourself, pass and drop back into your position. Then the next one goes."],["At an obstacle","Cross <b>one vehicle at a time</b>. The next one moves only when the previous one is completely clear of the obstacle and has said so on the radio or by signal."]],"q":[{"q":"The car behind isn't in your mirror and you reach a fork. What do you do?","a":["Turn, they'll find you on GPS","Stop at the fork until you see them indicating","Drive back to look for them"],"w":"Everyone looks after the car behind; that keeps the group together."},{"q":"On the highway, roughly what speed and spacing does the convoy keep?","a":["100 km/h and ~50 m","130 km/h and ~10 m","80 km/h and ~200 m"],"w":"That keeps both safety and cohesion."},{"q":"Dirt road with a lot of dust. What spacing do you keep?","a":["5 m so you can see their lights","10–20 m as always","About three times the 10–20 m"],"w":"Dust and flying stones need a much bigger gap."}]},{"id":"c1516_family","n":"Find company: the 4×4 community","src":"Overland’s Habit · Group, kids & pets","p":[["Don't go alone","Off-road, a single vehicle is a vehicle with no help. Getting stuck, a breakdown or an injury need a second car and a second pair of hands. <b>Off-road isn't safe alone</b>, and it's less fun too."],["Where to find a group","The Greek 4×4 community lives online: once on forums, today in <b>Viber, WhatsApp, Messenger and Facebook</b> groups. One message is enough to find travel companions, even if you've never met. A group gives you:<br>• Information on routes and road conditions<br>• Organised trips<br>• Fast communication with many people at once<br>• Photos and a record of past trips"],["The good companion","• Sees the 4×4 as a way to reach nature, not an end in itself<br>• Makes the <b>necessary</b> upgrades (suspension, tyres, skid plates) and keeps servicing on time<br>• Listens to advice and respects the leader<br>• Gets dirty so the group doesn't stall"],["What ruins a trip","• An unserviced vehicle that breaks down halfway<br>• Needless risk, e.g. crossing a swollen river<br>• Moaning at the first difficulty<br>• Mocking the beginner, newcomers need encouragement and guidance, even if they came with the wrong vehicle or clothes"],["Your place in the group","Everyone brings their own character. Choose to be the one who helps, follows the rules and makes the group safer."]],"q":[{"q":"You want to try a tough dirt road but have no company. What's the right move?","a":["Go alone, you'll be careful","Look for companions in a 4×4 group before you go","Go alone but with a full tank"],"w":"Alone you have nobody to pull you out or call for help."},{"q":"A beginner came on the trip with unsuitable tyres. What do you do?","a":["Mock them so they learn","Tell them to go home","Help and guide them"],"w":"Encouragement makes good companions; mockery drives them away."}]},{"id":"c57_motion","n":"Car sickness on the road: prevention and handling","src":"Overland’s Habit · Group, kids & pets","p":[["Why you get sick","The brain learns you're moving from your eyes, muscles, joints, skin and above all the <b>balance organ in the inner ear</b>. When you read in the back, the ear says 'we're moving' and the eyes, fixed on the page, say 'we're still'. The signals clash, and nausea comes. It hits all ages, more often children and people not used to long drives."],["Before you set off","• Ask the pharmacist for a <b>motion-sickness tablet</b>, most cause some drowsiness; there are also wristbands, patches, chewing gum<br>• No fatty, spicy or strong-smelling food before and during the drive<br>• With children, try what suits them: some want a light snack, others an empty stomach<br>• Few perfumes, sunscreens and cosmetics in the cabin"],["Where and how to sit","Whoever gets sick sits <b>facing forward</b> and, if possible, in front, looking <b>far out at the horizon</b>. Small children always in the back, in their seat: put it <b>in the middle</b> of the rear bench and high enough that they can see out."],["On the road","• Fresh air: good ventilation or a window slightly open<br>• No smoking in the car<br>• <b>No reading</b> maps, books or screens while moving<br>• If two people feel sick, <b>they shouldn't look at each other</b><br>• The driver makes <b>regular stops</b>"],["The first signs","Dizziness, yawning, mild headache, or suddenly cold sweat, queasiness, vomiting. If a child is sweating, yawning a lot or restless, <b>stop right away</b>, before it builds. Symptoms can last hours or even days afterwards."]],"q":[{"q":"Your friend in the back feels sick while watching a video on their phone. What's the cause?","a":["The eyes say 'still', the inner ear says 'moving'","Only the screen brightness","The tyre pressure"],"w":"The clash between eye and balance signals causes nausea."},{"q":"Where do you put the child seat for a child who gets car sick?","a":["In front, to see the road","In the middle of the rear bench, high enough to see out","Rear right, low so they sleep"],"w":"They stay in the back for safety but can see the motion outside."},{"q":"The child in the back keeps yawning and sweating. What do you do?","a":["Keep going, not far now","Give them something to read to distract them","Stop now for fresh air"],"w":"These are early signs; stopping before nausea peaks helps a lot."}]},{"id":"c57_kids","n":"Off-road with children: rules, mood and the child seat","src":"Overland’s Habit · Group, kids & pets","p":[["Before you leave","• Tell the children <b>the plan</b> and what you expect from them, the trip is also a chance to talk<br>• Pack <b>plenty of dry changes of clothes and shoes</b>: water and mud pull them like a magnet<br>• Snacks within reach and <b>plenty of water</b><br>• Travel games for the hours on the highway"],["Two or three 'don'ts', not twenty","Lots of 'don'ts' wear everyone out and don't help. Decide what really matters for safety, keep <b>two or three basic rules</b>, and everything else is allowed. Let the child explore nature and test their limits."],["On the dirt","Off-road, children usually <b>calm down</b> and cooperate more than on tarmac: they sense something special is happening. If something goes wrong, <b>stay calm, don't frighten them</b>. Make regular stops."],["Child seat: no saving money here","It makes no sense to spend on modifications and skimp on the seat. Buy a <b>branded seat of the right category</b> for weight and age. Read the instructions for both the seat and the car (often on the sun visor or door). <b>An airbag can kill a small child</b>, follow the seating instructions."],["In the shop and after","• Ask to <b>try it in your own car</b>; if they refuse, go elsewhere<br>• Sit the child in it: side and head support for their height and weight<br>• Straps that don't bother them, a <b>buckle they can't open easily</b> themselves<br>• Uncomfortable seat = moaning all day<br>• <b>Check often</b> that it stays tight, especially after a dirt road"]],"q":[{"q":"The salesperson won't let you try the child seat in your car. What do you do?","a":["Buy it, they all fit","Look in another shop","Buy a cheaper one and forget about it"],"w":"Without a test you don't know if it fits properly in your vehicle."},{"q":"After two hours of dirt road, what do you check on the child seat?","a":["That it's still tight and correctly secured","The fabric colour","Nothing, it was fitted right once"],"w":"Vibration loosens the fitting; check regularly."},{"q":"How do you set rules for a child in nature?","a":["No rules","The more 'don'ts' the safer","A few clear safety rules, the rest free"],"w":"A few rules get followed; many get ignored."}]},{"id":"c57_dog","n":"Travelling with a dog: inside the car, not hanging off the back","src":"Overland’s Habit · Group, kids & pets","p":[["Before the first trip","• <b>Training</b>: a dog that listens can move off the lead, in an unfamiliar place it will watch you so it doesn't get lost. Exception: an untrained male that has smelled a female in heat<br>• <b>Car sickness</b>: dogs not used to travel get sick too; ask the <b>vet</b> for a suitable tablet, or start with short drives"],["Why not a box on the back","<b>For the vehicle</b>: weight and length behind the axle overload the rear suspension, change the geometry, the steering loses precision and the <b>departure angle shrinks</b>. The vehicle's dimensions change too, check what applies legally.<br><b>For the dog</b>: it sits <b>right above the exhaust</b> breathing fumes, in a rear-end collision it's hit first, and cold, noise and loneliness in a tight box stress it."],["Mud and smell: solutions","Inside the car it rests better: warm, out of the wind and noise, next to you. If dirt worries you:<br>• <b>Pick-up</b> with a large box in the bed, away from exhaust, or<br>• Load space with a <b>net or dog guard</b><br>• <b>Cover</b> for seats and load space, or a large plastic sheet, also in case it has to ride in another car"],["One spot, always the same","Teach it to sit quietly where you decide, back seat or the passenger footwell. A dog <b>roaming freely</b> in the cabin can cause an accident."],["What you carry","• Food, <b>plenty of clean water</b>, bowl<br>• <b>Plenty of towels</b> or a zip-up towel bag<br>• Out of the water? <b>Give it two minutes to shake off</b> before it gets in"]],"q":[{"q":"You're considering a dog box on the tow bar. What happens to the 4×4?","a":["Nothing, it's light","The rear suspension is loaded and the departure angle shrinks","Rear traction improves"],"w":"Weight behind the axle changes geometry, steering and departure angle."},{"q":"The dog just came out of the river soaking wet. Before it gets in the car:","a":["Give it two minutes to shake off","Put it in straight away so it doesn't get cold","Dry it inside the cabin"],"w":"It will shake anyway, better outside."},{"q":"Why don't you let the dog roam freely in the cabin?","a":["Because it dirties the windows","It's fine as long as it's calm","It can distract or block the driver and cause an accident"],"w":"A set spot = a safe driver and dog."}]},{"id":"c57_games","n":"Games for the miles: less sleepiness, less moaning","src":"Overland’s Habit · Group, kids & pets","p":[["Stop first, then play","Rain on the highway, an endless dirt road, moaning in the back, heavy eyelids in front. The first answer is a <b>stop</b>: fresh air, water or coffee. The second: a game for <b>everyone</b>, not just the kids, it keeps the driver awake and the cabin calm. In a convoy they can be played over the <b>CB radio</b>."],["Guess what I'm thinking","• <b>Person, thing or animal</b>: one player thinks of a word and says only the category. The others have <b>10 questions in total</b> ('Is it wild?', 'Does it fit in a pocket?'). Agree beforehand whether 'this or that?' questions are allowed. With small children, give hints.<br>• <b>If they were…</b>: one player thinks of a person everyone knows. The others ask 'If they were an animal?', 'a colour?', 'a car?', 'a piece of furniture?', 'a season?' until they guess. The stranger the questions, the better the answers."],["Word games","• <b>Letter and category</b>: pick a letter and a topic (islands, mountains, towns, countries, singers). In turn, or in convoy order on the radio, each says one word. Whoever gets stuck says 'pass'; when everyone has passed, change the letter. For small children: names and animals.<br>• <b>Association chain</b>: each player quickly says the first word the previous one brings to mind. If the link isn't obvious, they explain it. No knowledge needed, lots of laughs."],["Song chain","Someone sings two lines. The next player must take <b>a word from those lines</b> and sing another song that contains it. Any kind of music. With kids and parents in the same car, hold genre 'duels' too."],["For the little ones","• <b>Rhymes</b> in pairs: one says a phrase, the other answers with a phrase that <b>rhymes</b> with the last word, and you keep going as long as you can<br>• <b>What will we see?</b> Guess the colour of the next car, the number plate, the next animal or sign. Keeps interest up and <b>sharpens observation</b>"]],"q":[{"q":"The driver is getting sleepy on the highway. What's the first step?","a":["A word game","A stop for air, water or coffee","Louder music and keep going"],"w":"The stop comes first; a game helps afterwards, it doesn't replace it."},{"q":"In 'Person, thing or animal', how many questions do the players get?","a":["Three each","As many as they like","Ten in total"],"w":"The 10-question limit is what makes the game interesting."},{"q":"In the song chain, what must the next song contain?","a":["A word from the previous lines","The same singer","The same rhythm"],"w":"The shared word is the link in the chain."}]},{"id":"c57_momtips","n":"A fresh driver: eyes, spacing, sun","src":"Overland’s Habit · Group, kids & pets","p":[["Before you start","• <b>Windows clean inside and out</b>, strong sun on a dirty screen tires the eyes fast<br>• Good, proven <b>driving glasses</b><br>• A plan with <b>breaks</b> and, if possible, a second driver<br>• A plan to arrive <b>before dark</b>: unknown or difficult terrain at night is a trap"],["At the wheel","Look <b>far ahead</b>, not just the few metres in front of the bonnet, you see the difficulty before you reach it. Keep a <b>comfortable gap</b> from the car in front so you can react to whatever it meets. On the highway, a guide is about <b>three broken white lines</b>. In mud and snow braking gets longer and stones fly far: open it up more."],["Fatigue: the silent danger","Driver fatigue is one of the main causes of accidents, by some estimates more than <b>4 in 10</b>. What helps:<br>• Share the driving<br>• Light food, <b>no alcohol</b><br>• Cold water, juice or coffee<br>• Headache or feeling unwell? <b>Hand the wheel to someone else</b>, your reaction time has already dropped"]],"q":[{"q":"Snowy dirt road behind another 4×4. What do you do with the gap?","a":["Increase it, longer braking, flying stones","Close it to follow their tracks","Keep it as on tarmac"],"w":"Mud and snow lengthen braking; you need room to react."},{"q":"It's 7 pm, getting dark, and ahead is an unknown difficult track. What do you do?","a":["Carry on with the spotlights","Camp or stay on a known road and do it in the morning","Go faster to make it"],"w":"In the dark, unknown terrain hides traps."},{"q":"The driver says they have a headache and feel heavy. What happens?","a":["They drink a coffee and carry on","They open the window and drive slower","Someone else drives"],"w":"Feeling unwell and headaches slow reactions; change drivers."}]},{"id":"c12_first","n":"112: when to call and what to say","src":"Overland’s Habit · Emergencies","p":[["Is it really an emergency?","A breakdown or an empty tank is not an emergency, roadside assistance or your group can handle it. It is an emergency when <b>someone's life or health is at risk</b>, or when only a rescue team can get you out of where you are. Then call <b>112</b>."],["Why 112 almost always gets through","• It is <b>free</b>, from mobile or landline.<br>• It works <b>without a SIM</b> and without credit.<br>• You don't need your own operator, <b>any</b> network will do.<br>• It also works from a payphone, with no coins or card."],["On the phone","The call helps the centre locate your rough area, but don't rely on that alone. Say clearly:<br>• who you are and which number reaches you<br>• exactly where you are (open “📍 My location” in SOS)<br>• what happened and how many are injured<br>Don't hang up yourself. Ask if they need anything else and let the centre end the call."],["Until help arrives","<b>You are not a doctor, don't act like one.</b> No medicines or home remedies unless you are completely sure. Breathe, assess, set priorities. <b>If you don't know what to do, do nothing</b> except call for help.<br>Talk to the casualty and keep their spirits up. Keep the group calm. Make sure nobody else ends up in danger, not you, and not the people coming to help."],["Learn it before you need it","First aid saves lives when it is done right; a mistake can leave permanent harm. Take a <b>first aid course</b> (Red Cross, hospitals, municipalities, clubs). What you have practised with your hands you remember in a panic; what you only read, usually not."]],"q":[{"q":"You ran out of fuel on a dirt road and everyone is fine. What do you do?","a":["Call 112","Call roadside assistance or ask your group for help","Leave the car and walk at night"],"w":"112 is for people in danger or a rescue need, not for a breakdown."},{"q":"Your phone has no SIM and your operator has no signal, but another network shows up. Can you call 112?","a":["No, not without a SIM","Only if you have credit","Yes, free, through any network"],"w":"112 works without a SIM or credit, over whatever network is available."},{"q":"The casualty complains of pain and someone offers a pill. You don't know what's wrong. What do you do?","a":["Give nothing and wait for instructions from 112","Give the pill, it can't hurt","Try something you saw online"],"w":"If you are not sure, any medicine or improvisation can do harm."}]},{"id":"c1214_scene","n":"Casualty in the wild: the first minutes","src":"Overland’s Habit · Emergencies","p":[["Before you approach","Look around before you bend over the casualty. Could the ground give way or rocks fall? Is the spot slippery? Is weather coming? Shield the casualty as best you can, but don't become the second person who needs rescuing."],["Don't move them","The casualty stays where they are. You move them <b>only</b> if their position threatens them directly, fire approaching or ground collapsing, and even then carefully, without risking yourself. If the neck or spine may be injured, moving them without immobilisation by someone who knows how can cause serious, permanent harm."],["Split the roles, the call comes first","<b>Calling for help comes before everything else.</b> Whoever has first aid training stays with the casualty and coordinates. Someone else takes the phone. Try 112 even if the phone shows “no service”.<br>Whether you are speaking to 112, the local police station or 166, don't hang up until you are sure they have understood: <b>where</b> exactly the casualty is, <b>what</b> happened, and that it is <b>urgent</b>."],["If someone must drive to find signal","• Check they actually took a phone, it gets forgotten in the confusion. Better still, phones from every operator the group has.<br>• Calm driving. A few minutes later is better than a second accident that also needs EMAK.<br>• Give them a map with the casualty's position and the route marked.<br>• Write the coordinates on paper for them. Under stress, memory goes blank."],["What to train for","On a first aid course, ask to practise:<br>• bites and stings (snake, spider, scorpion, bee, wasp, hornet)<br>• serious trauma, internal and external bleeding<br>• CPR<br>• fractures, joint injuries, minor wounds<br>• heat exhaustion, heatstroke, hypothermia, frostbite, burns<br>These are learned hands-on, from an instructor, not from a screen."]],"q":[{"q":"Your friend fell on a slope and has neck pain. The spot is stable. What do you do?","a":["Lift them and carry them to the car","Leave them as they are and call for help immediately","Roll them onto their back so they can rest"],"w":"With a possible neck injury, moving without immobilisation can cause permanent harm."},{"q":"The phone shows “no service”. What do you do first?","a":["Try calling 112 anyway","Wait for someone to pass","Set off on foot right away"],"w":"112 may get through on another network even when your own operator has no signal."},{"q":"Someone is driving off to find signal. What do you give them?","a":["Just your good wishes, so they hurry","The car's GPS is enough","A phone, a map with the position and written coordinates, and a reminder to drive calmly"],"w":"They must be able to give an exact position and must not cause a second accident."}]},{"id":"c1214_panic","n":"Panic in the group: how to put it out","src":"Overland’s Habit · Emergencies","p":[["What you may see","After something serious, people's emotional reaction is often harder to deal with than the injuries themselves, and it can put others at risk. In major disasters five types of reaction are recorded: <b>normal anxiety, panic, collapse (shutdown), overactivity, physical dysfunction</b>.<br>Signs of stress: shaking, weakness, sweating, fast breathing and pulse, nausea or vomiting, diarrhoea, frequent urination, confusion, freezing or restlessness, silence or non-stop talking. In most people they pass on their own.<br>A little anxiety keeps you alert. Too much, and panic above all, is dangerous, and contagious."],["You are the sedative","The others are watching you. Low, steady voice, slow movements, calm eyes. An “it's all fine” in a shaky voice does more harm than silence. Don't dismiss the other person's fear, but don't inflate it either."],["The anxious one who argues","Fear often comes out as argument: “this is madness”, while trying to win others over. Don't start a fight. Don't take it personally even if they insult you. Give them a job and tell them why it matters:<br>• look after someone “more badly hurt”<br>• fetch clothes to keep the casualty warm<br>• make food and coffee, or fetch water<br>• check the casualty's pulse and breathing and report to you<br>• keep the children busy away from the work<br>• photograph the accident scene<br>Always keep an eye on them."],["When stress shows in the body","Persistent vomiting, being unable to speak or move an arm or leg, blurred vision: they look like illness or injury. Treat them <b>as a real physical problem</b>, you cannot know the cause. Never say “they're putting it on”."],["Panic: act fast","A panicking person runs aimlessly, may head into traffic or over an edge ignoring their own injuries, screams, cries uncontrollably. Because it spreads:<br>• move them away from the others, calmly but firmly<br>• give them something hot to drink or eat; if they smoke and nothing flammable is near, a cigarette may help<br>• get them talking<br>• if they risk hurting themselves, get help to hold them<br><b>No</b> slapping, no water in the face, no pressure or force, they make it worse."],["The next day","If the trip goes on or you often travel together, sit down the next day for a short, calm talk. Not a trial for whoever panicked; a chance for everyone to say what they want from the trip. If it turns out others are uneasy too, change the route before the next difficulty finds you."]],"q":[{"q":"A group member shouts that “we'll all be killed” and stirs the others up. What do you do?","a":["Answer loudly to put them in their place","Stay calm and give them a useful job, explaining why it is needed","Ignore them completely"],"w":"Confrontation fuels anxiety; a task redirects their attention."},{"q":"Which of these do you NOT do to someone who has panicked?","a":["Move them away from the group","Give them a hot drink","Throw water in their face to snap them out of it"],"w":"Water, slaps and force make panic worse."},{"q":"After a fright, someone suddenly can't move their arm. How do you treat it?","a":["As a real physical problem","As an overreaction that will pass","Tell them to stop putting it on"],"w":"You don't know the cause, so the safe course is to treat it as physical."}]},{"id":"c1214_collapse","n":"The silent one, the overactive one, and keeping your head","src":"Overland’s Habit · Emergencies","p":[["Watch the one who doesn't speak","The quietest person in the group may be the one most at risk. <b>Collapse</b> (shutdown) looks like calm, but it needs care right away. Signs: standing still or wandering slowly with no purpose, not grasping what is going on, staring into space, answering in one word or not at all.<br>They can drift away unseen and get lost in the forest or snow. Children slip easily from anxiety into shutdown, keep small children in sight at all times."],["How you support them","• Never alone, not even briefly.<br>• Ask an anxious member to look after them, that keeps both busy.<br>• Talk to them about what happened and ask how they feel.<br>• Neither pity nor harshness: no “you poor thing”, no “snap out of it, you're fine”.<br>• Something hot to eat or drink, and a simple useful task."],["The overactive one","The opposite extreme. Often someone lightly hurt or unhurt who saw death up close: rushing everywhere to help, talking fast, arguing, fighting, even joking. Don't oppose them, let them let off steam. If they have no injury that movement would worsen, give them a job that is needed right then."],["Traps of the mind","Faced with something sudden and serious, some freeze, some see only disaster, some act as if it isn't happening or wait to “wake up”. None of this helps; it usually makes things worse. Danger calls for <b>realism, good sense, consistency and decisiveness</b>."],["Confidence is built","A healthy person who trusts themselves can endure far more than they think, even when injured. The hard part is getting past the first wave of fear and doubt. Fear, boredom, loneliness and anguish are held back with willpower and positive thinking. That confidence isn't a gift: you build it through training, clear thinking and experience."]],"q":[{"q":"After an accident, a group member sits quietly, stares into space and doesn't answer. What does it mean?","a":["They are calm and need nothing","Possible shutdown, they need care and constant watching","They are tired and want to sleep alone"],"w":"Shutdown looks like calm, but the person isn't taking in what is happening."},{"q":"Which line suits someone in shutdown?","a":["“Snap out of it, you're fine”","“You poor thing, what happened to you…”","“Tell me how you feel. Will you help me heat some water?”"],"w":"Neither harshness nor pity; talk, and a simple task."},{"q":"An unhurt group member is rushing around, talking non-stop and arguing. What do you do?","a":["Let them let off steam and give them a useful job","Restrain them by force","Send them off alone to find help"],"w":"Don't oppose them; channel their energy into something needed."}]},{"id":"c1214_survival","n":"Survival: put your needs in order","src":"Overland’s Habit · Emergencies","p":[["What “survival” means","It is the ability to stay alive and in decent shape when something goes badly wrong. In practice:<br>• you value every item of kit and take from nature what you need<br>• you make rescuers see you and find you fast<br>• you find the way back, even without map, compass or electronics<br>• you look after your body, even if injured or ill<br>• you keep your own morale and the group's high<br>No kit doesn't mean you have lost: will and decisiveness count for more."],["When it concerns you","A well-used dirt road between villages is not a survival scenario. But once you head where there are no marked roads and the map is no help, it takes little: a blizzard at night, a breakdown, a deep bog-down, sudden bad weather.<br>The most common mistake: <b>it gets dark and you carry on instead of turning back</b>. In the dark, melted snow refreezes into black ice, muddy gullies block the way, and on an icy downhill you can't even turn back."],["The four basics","<b>Shelter, water, fire, food.</b> Their order isn't fixed, place and season decide it:<br>• summer heatwave: water and shade first<br>• winter, mountains, frost: shelter and fire first<br>So the first step is always: <b>assess the situation and set priorities</b>."],["If you use someone else's roof","In need, an empty house or winter shepherd's hut with a fireplace can save you. Behave as a guest: when you leave, tidy up, repair anything you damaged to get in and leave something for what you used. Respect for nature and its people, and mature, responsible behaviour."],["A habit, not a movie","An emergency isn't only a shipwreck or a breakdown in the desert; it can happen on Parnitha, in the Pindus or abroad. When you buckle up, look before crossing the road or check the cooker before bed, you are already doing “survival”, it has just become automatic. City comfort makes us forget what we can do; these skills need practice."]],"q":[{"q":"Summer, heatwave, you are stranded in a dry area. What comes first?","a":["Water and protection from the sun","Looking for food","Fire"],"w":"The order of the four basics changes with place and season; in heat, water and shade come first."},{"q":"You are driving an unknown mountain road, it is getting dark and the temperature is falling. What is usually right?","a":["Carry on, the village is probably close","Stop in the middle of the road and wait","Turn back along the route you know while there is light"],"w":"In the dark, ice and mud turn an unknown road into a trap."},{"q":"What is the first step in any survival situation?","a":["Look for food","Assess the situation and set priorities","Set off on foot at once"],"w":"Without an assessment you don't know which of the four basics comes first."}]},{"id":"c1214_assess","n":"What really threatens you: cold, water, food","src":"Overland’s Habit · Emergencies","p":[["Stop and think","People don't die as easily as they fear. If you are stuck in a remote area or on a snowy mountain, sit down, breathe and weigh the situation with logic. Panic is a bigger danger than hunger."],["Number one: exposure","Wind, rain, snow and frost kill even in a mild climate like ours. If you are stranded in the mountains in winter, your main problem is <b>shelter</b>. You will need it after an accident, when the weather traps you, or when the vehicle stops at an obstacle it can't pass."],["Water","In Greece you will rarely run short, unless you end up on a dry island in summer. There are springs on almost every mountain and gully. Many now feed a tank, though: look for a tap or valve, and close it again.<br>Snow or icy water: <b>warm it before you drink it</b>. Ice-cold, it can cause diarrhoea and drop your body temperature, because your body now fights the cold from the inside too."],["Food: your last worry","A healthy person can go a very long time without food. On a trip of a few days, food is rarely a problem or a priority. You don't need rat traps like on TV reality shows."],["If you leave the vehicle","If you must leave it for good:<br>• take whatever might be useful<br>• note exactly where you leave it, so you or the rescuers can find it again"]],"q":[{"q":"Winter, you are stuck in the mountains with little food. What do you deal with first?","a":["Getting shelter from wind, damp and cold","Finding more food","Catching an animal"],"w":"Exposure kills much faster than hunger."},{"q":"You are thirsty and there is only snow around. What do you do?","a":["Eat the snow straight away","Melt it and warm it before drinking","Don't drink at all"],"w":"Ice-cold water lowers body temperature and can cause diarrhoea."},{"q":"You found a tap on a spring tank in the mountains. What do you do when you leave?","a":["Leave it open for the next person","Unscrew it and take it","Close it again"],"w":"Others need that water too, animals, shepherds, whoever comes next."}]},{"id":"c1214_lost","n":"Lost? Stop, think, move the right way","src":"Overland’s Habit · Emergencies","p":[["Calm down first","In Greece we have no large predators, swamps, quicksand or endless wilderness. Getting truly lost takes effort; however alone you feel, there is a shepherd or woodcutter somewhere near.<br>With GPS you only get lost if it fails, e.g. water in the electronics after a deep crossing, or if you don't know how to use it. So keep the classic methods alive too."],["Work out roughly where you are","• Look at peaks, rivers and other landmarks and match them to the map.<br>• Trust map and compass more than “instinct”.<br>• With no map, recall the big things: did you cross a river? Was the high peak left or right? Were you climbing or descending? What trees, what colour soil? You won't remember every bend, but you will remember these."],["Move with a plan","Make a simple plan and don't wander at random: you will burn fuel too. If you suspect you are going in circles, stop and leave your own mark with stones or branches. If you see it again, you know."],["Road rules","• On a road? <b>Stay on it.</b> Cutting through forest or fields loses all orientation.<br>• Avoid roads that look abandoned, narrow, tall grass. They are usually dead ends.<br>• Phone signal appeared? A good sign you are nearing inhabited areas, not proof."],["If you have nothing else","Then, and really only then, go <b>downhill</b>. Uphill leads to cold, empty peaks. Follow a stream, a river or the telephone poles, most of the time they end at a road or village."]],"q":[{"q":"You are lost on a forest road. Ahead is a narrow, overgrown branch and the wide road carrying on. Which do you take?","a":["The overgrown branch, it looks shorter","The wide, used road","Neither, you cut through the forest"],"w":"Abandoned roads are usually dead ends; leaving the road loses your orientation."},{"q":"You suspect you are passing the same spot again. What do you do?","a":["Stop and leave your own mark with stones or branches","Speed up to get out faster","Change direction at random"],"w":"A mark confirms whether you are circling, before you burn fuel."},{"q":"You have no map, GPS or other clue. Which way do you go?","a":["Toward the highest peak, to look around","Stay and drive in circles","Downhill, following a stream, river or poles"],"w":"Downhill usually leads to a road or village; uphill to cold, empty peaks."}]},{"id":"c1214_huts","n":"A roof for the night: hut, sheepfold, empty house","src":"Overland’s Habit · Emergencies","p":[["When to stop and look for a roof","Accident, a bad bog-down, weather closing in, darkness on ground you can't walk, exhaustion or injury: then the first job is a <b>safe, warm improvised shelter</b> for the night. The rest gets solved in daylight.<br>• Summer by the sea: you can sleep in the open.<br>• Summer in the mountains: the nights bite, the car is a good option.<br>• Winter, even in the lowlands: as soon as you know you'll be out overnight, find cover from wind, rain, snow and cold.<br>In the dark and rain, don't push on into unknown ground; better to go back over obstacles you already know. And don't dig through a landslide in the dark, it may come down again."],["Keep a map in your head","In Greece there are villages, sheepfolds, huts and shacks almost everywhere. When you travel off-road, <b>remember where you saw the last hut or sheepfold</b>. In daylight you spot it easily; in the dark, if it isn't on the road, you'll never find it."],["Make a rough shelter liveable","The first impression is usually bad: mud for a floor, holes in wall and roof. Stay, it's far better than outside.<br>• Dig a channel and slope the floor so water runs out.<br>• Light a small fire <b>under a hole in the roof</b> so the smoke escapes.<br>• Ration the food; keep some for tomorrow.<br>• Sleep in your jacket, sitting up if need be. Smoky but dry is a good night.<br>A cold metal car fills with damp; a poor shack is often warmer."],["In someone else's place: rules","• Before entering a building that looks empty, call out loudly several times. If someone is there and you ask, they will help; walk in uninvited and you may face a gun or be explaining yourself to the police.<br>• If you have a choice, take the house that opens easily and has a fireplace, not the prettiest.<br>• Look first at what each thing is for. Break nothing and don't burn objects or clothes, however old they look.<br>• Whatever you use goes back where it was.<br>• If you opened a door, secure it again when you leave.<br>You are a visitor, not an intruder."],["In mountain villages","Where snow cuts villages off, locals are used to unexpected visitors and usually help willingly. Be polite and careful, with sensible caution."]],"q":[{"q":"Rain, getting dark, 30 km of unknown ground ahead, 15 km of known ground behind. What is best?","a":["Go back over the known ground and look for shelter","Push on fast before it is fully dark","Stop wherever you are and wait for morning in the mud"],"w":"In the dark, unknown obstacles with water and mud are far more dangerous than ones you have already passed."},{"q":"Inside a shack you want to light a fire. Where?","a":["By the door, so it doesn't smell","Small, under a hole in the roof","As big as possible in the middle, to warm up fast"],"w":"Smoke needs a way out; a small fire is enough and safer."},{"q":"In an empty winter hut you find old chairs and rags. What do you do?","a":["Burn them, they are useless","Take them for the car","Burn nothing and take nothing; whatever you use goes back in place"],"w":"What looks old to you may be needed by the owner."}]},{"id":"c1214_carshelter","n":"A night in the car in the cold","src":"Overland’s Habit · Emergencies","p":[["Stay or go?","The car keeps off wind and rain, but without kit it is a metal box that gets cold and damp. Even so, <b>you stay with it</b>: going for help on foot in a blizzard, downpour, darkness or without knowing where you are is very dangerous. Stop at the roadside, not among the trees, on the road someone is more likely to pass.<br>You walk <b>only</b> if: it's daylight, the snow has stopped, you know where you are and where you're going, and you have basic kit. Before going even a short way:<br>• make big marks in the snow<br>• tie a bright cloth to a pole or the antenna<br>• leave a clear note saying where you went<br>Even if the sky is clear; in the mountains the weather turns fast. And if staff at a ski centre or on a road tell you to go down, fit chains and leave at once, however nice the day looks."],["Warmth without fuel","Above zero, everything is solvable. If it drops below:<br>• wear all your clothes and wrap up in whatever there is: blankets, covers, newspapers, bags<br>• don't pull clothes tight, the air under them insulates<br>• keep moving, as much as space allows<br>• with several cars, pile into one, close together; swap places so everyone gets a turn in the middle<br>• hold back the shivering as much as you can, as it makes you feel colder"],["The engine: little and smart","• If possible, turn the vehicle so the icy wind doesn't hit the grille, and cover the grille with cardboard or a mat.<br>• Wrap the lower part of the engine with spare rags or clothes, with no loose ends to catch in belts and fan.<br>• If you have fuel: engine and heater for <b>about 10 minutes</b>, switch off, and again when the cold wakes you. Turn the heater off once the damp drops.<br>You don't know how long this will last; save fuel and energy."],["Carbon monoxide: the silent danger","• Before starting, clear the exhaust of snow, branches, stones, so fumes go out the back and not into the cabin.<br>• Drowsy with the engine running? <b>Switch off at once</b> and get out to check the exhaust.<br>• With the engine running, <b>never all sleep</b>. One stays awake and watches the others for drowsiness."],["Damp and ice","Your breath fills the car with moisture, which freezes on windows and metal. Don't try to melt it, you only make more damp. Don't touch it. If you have spare clothes, hang them over the windows as curtains."],["When snow buries the car","Two dangers: getting trapped and suffocating. You stay inside only with <b>reliable, continuous ventilation</b>, and forget the engine and heater entirely: with a buried exhaust, fumes kill.<br>• As the snow rises, push the doors outward every so often. That keeps room to get out and more air.<br>• Fully buried, the car is out of the wind and the temperature settles around 0°C, but you face darkness, thirst, loneliness.<br>• With ventilation secured, a mountaineering candle gives light, a little warmth and morale.<br>Experts disagree on whether a buried car is safe; many prefer another shelter. Weigh it up, decide, and avoid extremes: neither passive nor a hero."]],"q":[{"q":"Which heating routine saves fuel?","a":["Engine idling all night","Never the engine, whatever happens","About 10 minutes of engine and heater, switch off, and again when the cold wakes you"],"w":"You don't know how many hours or days you'll be there, fuel is warmth for later."},{"q":"The engine is running for heat and you become very drowsy. What do you do?","a":["Switch off at once and check the exhaust","Turn on the radio to stay awake","Close your eyes for a bit, it's just tiredness"],"w":"Drowsiness with the engine running may be carbon monoxide."},{"q":"It is snowing hard and the car is getting buried. Which is right?","a":["Heater on full so you don't freeze","Secure ventilation, no engine, and push the doors outward now and then","Seal every opening to keep the warmth in"],"w":"With a buried exhaust, fumes are deadly; the doors keep an exit and air."},{"q":"When do you leave the car to find help on foot?","a":["When it gets dark, so as not to waste time","As soon as the blizzard starts","In daylight, with no snowfall, a known route and kit, after leaving marks and a note"],"w":"Otherwise the walker is at far greater risk than those who stay with the vehicle."}]},{"id":"c1214_wildshelter","n":"Build a shelter in the wild","src":"Overland’s Habit · Emergencies","p":[["Before you start","A shelter keeps off wind, rain and cold, and lets you sleep, which survival requires. The effort pays off, but <b>don't exhaust yourself</b> building it; exhaustion is a danger too. Work calmly, as if you were building a den as a kid."],["With a fallen trunk","• Clear the space underneath, cutting the branches that hang into it.<br>• Thicken the sides with branches and grass, to block wind and water.<br>• Dig a channel or raise a bank around it for running water.<br>• If you have a tarp, throw it over the “roof”."],["In a hollow, beside a rock","With no suitable trees, look for a hollow in the ground, ideally on the lee side of a rock. Check first that nothing can fall on you. Dig a little or build a low wall with stones, earth and branches, enough to sit inside. Cover with dense branches and, if you have one, a tarp."],["Under a fir in the snow","The low branches of a big conifer leave a space beneath, especially when buried in snow. Find the tip of a branch and dig carefully underneath from the <b>lee</b> side, without breaking the surface, that is your roof. Keep the entrance open at all times, for air and so you don't get trapped. It protects from wind and water, not from cold: inside it will be around or a bit below 0."],["Insulate from below","The ground steals your heat. Wear everything you have and bring mats, covers, bags from the vehicle, and the seats, if they come out easily. A rubbish bag big enough to get into works as a makeshift bivy bag. <b>Never put your head inside the bag</b>, risk of suffocation."],["Fire in a snow cave (advanced)","It is hard and risky without experience: it needs a chimney that doesn't melt the roof, dry wood and a fire that keeps going without choking you. The logic: warm air rises, cold air and carbon monoxide sink.<br>• Three levels: you sleep high, the fire in the middle, a low “sump” for cold air.<br>• Before lighting, open a hole in the roof directly above the fire.<br>• While it burns, leave a small opening at the entrance: so the smoke draws, fresh air comes in and carbon monoxide escapes.<br>Try it first on a sunny snowy day, safely, with the kids too, as an adventure."]],"q":[{"q":"You are digging a shelter under a fir's branches in the snow. Where do you go in?","a":["From the windward side","From the top, breaking the crust","From the lee side, without breaking the surface"],"w":"The snow surface becomes your roof and the lee side shields you from the wind."},{"q":"You get into a big rubbish bag for warmth. What do you watch out for?","a":["Your head always stays outside","Tie it tightly at the top","Have nothing underneath you"],"w":"Head inside the bag means risk of suffocation."},{"q":"Why leave a small opening at the entrance when a fire burns in a snow cave?","a":["To see if rescuers are coming","So the smoke draws and the heavy carbon monoxide escapes","So snow comes in to melt for water"],"w":"Carbon monoxide settles low and needs a way out."}]},{"id":"c1214_signals","n":"Make rescuers see you","src":"Overland’s Habit · Emergencies","p":[["Plan first, move later","The worst is over; now you need to be found. A plan keeps you busy and lifts morale. Before deciding whether to walk, answer:<br>• how far is the nearest village?<br>• how many hours can you last in your condition?<br>• what is the weather doing and what is coming?<br>• is it more likely you find help or they find you?<br>• can you see anyone, even a shepherd on the opposite ridge?<br>• can you be seen from the air?<br>• how much supply do you have and for how long?<br>• who knows your route and when will they start looking? (that's why you always leave a plan with someone before setting off)<br>Reckon that an average person walks about <b>5 km/h</b> on flat, good ground in good weather, with climbs, rough ground or bad weather, much less."],["The vehicle is a signal store","Even broken, it gives you material:<br>• fuel, oil, tyres, plastics, seats, for a fire with thick black smoke<br>• mirrors to flash the sun<br>• lights and horn at night, if the battery holds and you are in a visible spot"],["Make your signal stand out","A single fire just says someone lit a fire. You need something that shouts “trouble”:<br>• <b>rule of three</b>: almost any signal repeated three times reads as distress, three fires, three whistle blasts, three flashes<br>• a signal that stops and restarts at regular intervals is also clear<br>• make the smoke “odd” with a tyre or oil<br>• a simple laser pointer aimed at a team or a helicopter on the opposite ridge works very well"],["Don't burn it all at once","Haste makes you spend your means too early or at the wrong moment. Keep them for when you hear or see help. In fog, rain or snowfall visual signals can't be seen, wait for it to clear. Don't expect to be spotted at once: it may take hours or days."],["Be visible from above","A search usually starts from your last known position and likely route, and sweeps the area according to terrain and weather. Set up your scene before you hear a helicopter.<br>In dense forest you find shelter, but from the air you are invisible: smoke disperses and looks like cloud. Move early to a <b>clearing</b> and spread over a wide area the oddest things you have: bright clothes over bushes, seats, tyres, doors, metal, small fires, a big SOS in the snow."],["Tracks for ground teams","People on foot will search too, but often you won't hear each other, because of weather, terrain or the noise they make on purpose. If you move, leave signs behind: arrows showing your direction, piles of stones, ribbons on branches."]],"q":[{"q":"You want to show distress with fires. Which layout is clearest?","a":["One very big fire","Three fires","Many small fires at random spots in the forest"],"w":"The rule of three is recognised almost everywhere as a distress signal."},{"q":"You have a can of oil and two spare tyres for smoke. Visibility is poor. When do you burn them?","a":["Now, to be safe","Half now, half tomorrow, whatever happens","When the weather clears and you hear or see help"],"w":"A signal nobody can see is wasted material."},{"q":"You are in dense forest waiting for a helicopter. What do you do?","a":["Move early to a clearing and spread out visible objects","Stay under the trees for protection","Light a fire under the thick branches"],"w":"In forest, smoke disperses and objects are hidden from the pilot."},{"q":"The nearest village is 15 km away on flat ground in good weather. Roughly how long will an average person walk?","a":["About 1 hour","About 3 hours","About 8 hours"],"w":"About 5 km/h in good conditions, with climbs or bad weather, much longer."}]},{"id":"c1214_carfire","n":"Vehicle fire: step by step","src":"Overland’s Habit · Emergencies","p":[["Stop and get out the right way","A car is full of things that burn: fuel, oils, brake fluid, plastics, tyres, paint. On the move, a fire usually starts quietly under the bonnet and the airflow fans it. The first seconds decide:<br>• stop at once at the roadside, away from branches and low wires<br>• in gear, handbrake on<br>• pull the bonnet and tailgate release levers<br>• take the extinguisher and get out<br><b>Never</b> go back in for papers or belongings, the heat alone causes 2nd and 3rd degree burns."],["Get flammable cargo out","Jerrycans, gas bottles, camping cartridges: always load them at the back, where they come out easily. If the fire hasn't reached them, take them out calmly and carry them well away."],["The bonnet: never all at once","Gloves, or something covering your hands. <b>Don't open the bonnet the normal way</b>, even if you only see smoke: the fresh air can make the fire flare out at you.<br>• First spray the extinguisher from below, into the engine, angled 45° upwards.<br>• Then lift the bonnet bit by bit.<br>• If you already see flame, give short bursts into the engine bay as the bonnet opens gradually."],["Once it is open","• Disconnect the battery. Without a quick-disconnect switch, cut the cables with cutters.<br>• If you have enough, keep cooling the fuel tank with water or foam, so the fire doesn't spread there."],["Burning tyres","Don't stand in front of them: they will burst and the pressurised air injures. Once burnt, the vehicle may start rolling on its rims, put stones in front of the wheels."],["The limit","A vehicle fire is hard, because many different materials burn together. Often the right call is <b>to let it burn</b>. Your life is worth more than metal, for the metal there is fire insurance."]],"q":[{"q":"You stop with smoke coming from the bonnet. What is right before getting out?","a":["In gear, handbrake, release bonnet/tailgate, take the extinguisher","Switch off and run away with nothing","Grab your papers and belongings first"],"w":"A few moves before exiting let you intervene safely."},{"q":"Only smoke is coming from the engine. How do you open the bonnet?","a":["Wide and fast, to see","First spray the extinguisher from below at 45°, then lift it gradually","Spray nothing, just open it a bit to air it"],"w":"Sudden air can make the fire flare violently."},{"q":"The tyres are burning. Where do you stand?","a":["Right in front of them, to put them out","Leaning on the driver's door","To the side, at a distance"],"w":"Tyres burst and the pressurised air injures."}]},{"id":"c1214_forestfire","n":"You spotted a forest fire","src":"Overland’s Habit · Emergencies","p":[["Don't drive past","Make sure it is a fire and report it at once: <b>199</b> (Fire Service) or <b>112</b>. Every minute counts, don't assume someone else has already called."],["What to say on the phone","• Answer calmly and precisely whatever you are asked; don't hang up in a hurry.<br>• Describe where the seat of the fire is and which way it is heading.<br>• Give coordinates <b>and</b> a description anyone understands: “the third ridge on the right, past the sheepfold”. The person at the nearest station may not know GPS.<br>• Alert the nearby municipalities with exactly the same details."],["If you stay to help","• Until the Fire Service arrives, contain the fire with whatever you have, <b>always in groups</b>, never alone.<br>• Clothing: long trousers, long sleeves, gloves, leather boots, a wet cloth over your face. No plastic wellies, no canvas shoes.<br>• Once those in charge arrive, follow their instructions. Without experience, your place is behind the front line, you are needed there too.<br>• Bring plenty of drinking water for the firefighters. Everyone needs it and everyone forgets it."],["When to leave","• See an aircraft making water drops? Get out of the area immediately.<br>• Don't improvise, don't exhaust yourself, don't play the hero. The fire is stronger than all of you together, and to it you are just one more thing that burns."]],"q":[{"q":"You see smoke in a gully. Which numbers do you call?","a":["166 or 100","1018","199 or 112"],"w":"199 is the Fire Service and 112 the single emergency number."},{"q":"You are giving the fire's position on the phone. What is best?","a":["Coordinates plus a landmark description, e.g. “third ridge on the right, past the sheepfold”","Only coordinates, they are more precise","Only the name of the nearest village"],"w":"The person listening may not know GPS; landmarks everyone understands."},{"q":"You have no experience but want to help. Where are you most useful?","a":["Alone on the front line, to buy time","Behind the front, with the group and following those in charge","Under the aircraft drops, to cool off"],"w":"Inexperienced helpers work at the back line, always in a group, and keep away from water drops."}]},{"id":"c1214_crash","n":"Road accident: what to do on the spot","src":"Overland’s Habit · Emergencies","p":[["People first","Stay calm. Make sure the injured get medical help as soon as possible.<br>• <b>Don't move casualties.</b><br>• <b>Don't remove an injured motorcyclist's helmet.</b><br>• With serious injuries, and only then, the vehicles stay exactly as they are until the Traffic Police arrive."],["Secure the scene","Warning triangle <b>before and after</b> the spot, so both directions are warned. Stopped vehicles must not block traffic. The aim: no second collision and no further damage to your car."],["No serious injuries: record and clear","You don't need to wait for the police to settle where the vehicles were. Mark on the road (chalk, charcoal) the wheels and corners of each vehicle, or draw a sketch with positions, street names, plates, time and date, signed by everyone. Take photos. Move the broken pieces to the side and clear the cars."],["What you write down","• plate, colour, model, insurer and policy expiry of each vehicle<br>• name, address, phone of each driver and licence issue date<br>• owner's details, if they weren't driving<br>• foreign driver: ask for the green card and keep its tear-off slip<br>• foreign plates or uninsured vehicle: call an insurance agent or lawyer at once<br>• names and phones of witnesses and passengers"],["With the other driver","Exchange details calmly. With no injuries, fill in the accident report form (European accident statement), always keep one in the glovebox. <b>Don't pay anything on the spot</b>, even if it's your fault; that goes through an insurer or lawyer. If the other driver seems drunk, ask for a <b>breath test first</b> and don't agree, out of kindness, to anything untrue being written: if a drunk driver is involved, nobody may get compensated."],["With the Traffic Police","The officer didn't see the accident, don't ask their opinion, and whatever they say doesn't count in court. Describe calmly and in detail how the vehicles were moving. Don't argue with the other driver in front of them. Ask for witnesses to be recorded, note whether tickets were issued (and tell your insurer), and get the <b>incident report</b>, on the spot or from the station.<br>Rules change; for details ask the Traffic Police, your insurer and a lawyer. How the accident happened is for an expert assessor to judge."]],"q":[{"q":"Collision with no injuries; the cars are blocking the road. What do you do?","a":["Leave the vehicles as they are until the police arrive","Mark the positions or make a sketch and photos, then clear them","Leave without writing anything down"],"w":"Vehicles stay in place only when there are serious injuries."},{"q":"The other driver admits fault and offers cash to settle it. You are partly at fault too. What do you do?","a":["Accept, it's simpler","Give them something so you don't get involved","No money changes hands on the spot; accident form and insurer"],"w":"Payments go only through an insurer or lawyer."},{"q":"A motorcyclist is down and injured. Which do you NOT do?","a":["Take off their helmet so they can breathe","Call for help","Place triangles before and after"],"w":"The helmet stays on; removing it can worsen a neck or head injury."}]},{"id":"c1214_crashlegal","n":"After the crash: insurance, roads, off-road","src":"Overland’s Habit · Emergencies","p":[["Straight after","• Inform your insurer and speak to your agent.<br>• Once you are back, file an <b>accident report</b> with your insurer, as the at-fault or not-at-fault party, and ask the others to do the same.<br>• Filing doesn't mean the company will pay; assessors decide that and, if needed, the courts."],["Once you are compensated","If you were not at fault and the case closed with compensation in hand, ask your agent to <b>cancel your own report</b>. Some companies raise the premium simply because you were involved in an accident, not only when you were at fault."],["Collision off-road","If you hit another 4×4 while off the road network, in a river, gully, field, whether by bad luck or by “playing” with slides in snow and mud, <b>insurance doesn't cover it</b>. It only covers driving on the road network. Each pays for what they damaged; settle it between you, calmly."],["When the road is to blame","Each road has a different authority: national roads the State (the responsible ministry), provincial roads the Region, local roads the Municipality, forest roads the Forestry Service. None pays without a court ruling.<br>If you are damaged by a pothole, an unmaintained road or faulty works:<br>• call the Police at once to record the incident<br>• make sure the incident report records everything: signage, warnings, safety measures<br>• then see a lawyer, if your policy includes legal protection, the insurer covers it<br>On public works the contractor must carry civil liability insurance, which often makes things simpler."],["Abandoned forest road","On a neglected forest road, if a washout “swallows” you, don't expect compensation. There, in practice, the responsibility is yours, drive accordingly."]],"q":[{"q":"Two 4×4s in your group hit each other in a stream bed. What applies?","a":["The at-fault driver's insurer pays","Each driver pays for the damage they did, off the road network there is no cover","The Forestry Service compensates"],"w":"Insurance only covers driving within the road network."},{"q":"You damaged a rim in a deep, unmarked pothole on a provincial road. What is the first step?","a":["Email the Region and wait","Change the rim and forget it","Call the Police to record the incident in full detail"],"w":"Without an official record you can't claim anything in court."},{"q":"You weren't at fault and were compensated. Why ask for your report to be cancelled?","a":["Because some companies add a surcharge just for being involved","To get a second payout","Because the law requires it"],"w":"So you don't pay a higher premium for something you didn't cause."}]},{"id":"exo_threetrips","n":"The three-trip programme and the bridge log","src":"Outside the Walls, Appendices","p":[["Three weekends","From zero to your first night outside a campsite, ideally May–June or September–October.<br>• <b>Trip 1</b>: a campsite 1–2 hours from home, with friends or family. Goal: pitching, sleeping, cooking, one night without mains power. Read Ch. 3, 4, 6, 7, 8.<br>• <b>Trip 2</b>: a place you know, private land with permission or sleeping in the car, with one friend. Goal: water, toilet, evening watch. Ch. 2, 10, 11, 13.<br>• <b>Trip 3</b>: same type of spot, good weather, car next to you, alone. Goal: the three questions for every sound and a coffee at sunrise. Ch. 5, 12, 17."],["After each trip","Fill in the bridge log and buy <b>only what you genuinely missed</b>."],["The bridge log","One page per trip: date · spot and altitude · with whom · weather (forecast and actual) · minimum night temperature · how I slept (1–5) · what went well · what I missed · what I carried without needing it · what I'll change next time · one moment I want to remember. After ten pages it teaches you more than the book."],["Extra lists by trip type","Start from the base list and add:<br>• <b>Campsite</b>: 25 m extension reel, multi-socket, adapter, flip-flops and shower towel, earplugs.<br>• <b>Wild</b>: check the law and a plan-B spot, small trowel and toilet bags, wash bowl, biodegradable soap, solar shower, 12V compressor, jump starter, strap, shovel.<br>• <b>Summer</b>: fire-risk map for today/tomorrow, no-flame food, tarp, mosquito net/screens, hat, extra water.<br>• <b>Winter</b>: mat R 4+, sleeping bag comfort 5°C lower, jacket, gloves, thermals, thermos, propane cylinder, snow chains, antifreeze, tested battery, arrive two hours before sunset."],["Clothes for a weekend","In layers, each item in a zip bag or dry bag: thermal top and leggings (not cotton) · one set of thermals for sleeping only · a T-shirt per day · quick-dry trousers (no jeans in the mountains) · fleece · jacket/waterproof with hood · underwear, socks + one pair for sleeping only · beanie and gloves (even in summer in the mountains) · buff · boots or trainers with a good sole · slippers/flip-flops · winter: gaiters or wellies, waterproof trousers · summer: swimsuit, peaked cap, sunglasses, thin long-sleeve · quick-dry towel · one set of clean clothes in the car for the trip home."]],"q":[{"q":"Who do you go with on the third trip of the programme?","a":["Alone","With a big group","With family"],"w":"The third trip tests the three questions for every sound."},{"q":"What do you do after each trip?","a":["Fill in the log and buy only what you missed","Buy upgrades for everything","Nothing"],"w":"The bridge log keeps the memory and guides your purchases."},{"q":"Which clothes stay in the car for the trip home?","a":["One set of clean clothes","The mountain jacket","The sleeping thermals"],"w":"You go home clean without digging through bags."}]},{"id":"exo_why","n":"Why outside, and how to start","src":"Outside the Walls, Ch. 1: Why outside","p":[["The author's first night","A Suzuki Ignis, a new swag, a sleeping bag, two silver emergency blankets, a little water, a torch and lots of food. A clearing towards Pavliani, –3°C outside and 10°C inside. <b>The human body is the heater</b>; the tent, mat and sleeping bag simply stop it losing its heat."],["What he did right, what he got wrong","<b>Right:</b> he had checked the forecast, had water, a warm sleeping bag and the car next to him, someone knew where he was, he didn't pitch in a stream bed or under a dead tree.<br><b>Wrong:</b> he hadn't tried the tent beforehand, didn't know what ground insulation was, and picked winter for his first time, something nobody should do."],["What you gain","• <b>Rhythm:</b> you wake with the light, get sleepy with the dark.<br>• <b>Silence:</b> you hear the noise you carry inside you.<br>• <b>Self-reliance:</b> you look after yourself with what fits in a boot.<br>• <b>Simplicity:</b> a two-metre home.<br>• <b>People:</b> around a stove on the ground, things get said that don't get said in the city."],["What it isn't","It isn't a gear show, the sunrise is the same with a €50 tent and with a setup that costs as much as a flat. It isn't heroics: nature only asks for respect. It isn't therapy: it just stops the noise so you can listen. It isn't a rejection of civilisation: you'll go home and appreciate hot water."],["Where to start","• Never slept outside → Ch. 2, 6 and the three-trip programme.<br>• Want to buy gear → Ch. 3 and 4, then wait for one trip.<br>• Scared of sleeping alone → Ch. 10.<br>• Got cold/too hot → Ch. 8.<br>• Kids or a big group → Ch. 6 and 16.<br>• Want the next step → Ch. 2 and 11.<br>Never skip Ch. 2 (the law), most people learn it from a fine."],["Before you go & everyone's mistake","Do you know why you're going (quiet, company, kids, a test)? The answer picks the place. The first night: close to home, good weather, car next to you, and someone knows where you are and when you're back. The mistake: waiting until they're “ready”. The first night doesn't need you to be ready; it needs to be <b>close</b> and you need a <b>plan B</b>."]],"q":[{"q":"What actually keeps a person warm on a cold night outside?","a":["Their own body, the gear just holds the heat in","The tent","The car next to them"],"w":"The human body is the heater; the tent, mat and sleeping bag prevent heat loss."},{"q":"What does the first night outside need?","a":["To be close and to have a plan B","Full gear first","Winter, to learn fast"],"w":"The book says you don't need to be “ready”, just close and with a plan B."},{"q":"Which chapter should you never skip?","a":["Ch. 2 on the law","Ch. 19 on overlanding","Ch. 9 on cooking"],"w":"Most people learn the law from a fine."}]},{"id":"exo_law","n":"What the law says about camping","src":"Outside the Walls, Ch. 2: Campsite, car, wild","p":[["The three steps","1. <b>Campsite</b>, licensed site, legal. Next step when you can pitch and sleep without help from the neighbour.<br>2. <b>In the car</b>, sleeping inside a legally parked vehicle, nothing outside; grey zone. Next step when you've done nights with cold, wind, no power.<br>3. <b>Outside campsites</b>, tent on private land with permission or in an area designated by the municipality; conditional. When you're self-sufficient in water, food, safety, rubbish."],["The legislation","The basis is Article 10 of Law 392/1976 (amended by Laws 2160/1993 and 4055/2012). In 2025 Article 27 of Law 5170/2025 restated the ban for tents, caravans and motorhomes. The logic: camping happens at licensed campsites. The author isn't a lawyer; the picture was checked on 21/9/2026 and the law changes."],["Where it's banned","• Foreshore and beaches<br>• Forests, forest land and the edges of public forests<br>• Archaeological sites<br>• Parks, squares, public spaces<br>• Car parks, when use goes beyond simple parking<br>A beach doesn't become legal because it's deserted or has no sign."],["What counts as camping, and the penalties","What counts is <b>“setting up”</b>: tent, sleeping, cooking gear, table and chairs, large shade. Swimming and a day on the beach aren't camping.<br>• Administrative fine of <b>€300</b> per person, tent or vehicle (Police/Coast Guard).<br>• Criminal prosecution, possibly on-the-spot arrest; up to 6 months' detention or up to €3,000 (Law 4055/2012).<br>• Fire, rubbish, damage to vegetation, an archaeological site or Natura area weigh heavily."],["Sleeping in the car: the grey zone","Legal parking and sleeping inside the vehicle aren't automatically camping. But once you “spread out”, awning, table and chairs, barbecue, things on the ground, it can be treated as setting up. Signage, the Highway Code, municipal rules and protected areas also count. The author's reading (not legal advice): an opened <b>rooftop tent</b> with ladder, awning and table looks like setting up, don't assume it's legal outside a campsite."],["Private land, caravans, municipalities","The owner's permission counts, but a field can't operate as an informal campsite for lots of people. Caravans: on a private plot, free hosting of <b>up to one</b>; from two upwards €300 per vehicle (Article 27, Law 5170/2025). On forest/protected land the fire-protection rules also apply, however private it is. For municipal areas: don't assume, ask before you leave and keep the answer in writing."]],"q":[{"q":"How much is the administrative fine for illegal camping?","a":["€300 per person, tent or vehicle","€50 per group","€3,000 per tent"],"w":"The fine is €300 and is multiplied per person, tent or vehicle."},{"q":"When can sleeping in the car be treated as camping?","a":["When you put out an awning, table, chairs or barbecue","When it's night","Always, wherever you are"],"w":"“Spreading out” beyond the vehicle turns parking into setting up."},{"q":"How many caravans are allowed free on a private plot?","a":["Up to one","Up to five","Unlimited with permission"],"w":"From two upwards the fine is €300 per vehicle (Law 5170/2025)."}]},{"id":"exo_fireseason","n":"Fire season: fires and camping stoves","src":"Outside the Walls, Ch. 2: Campsite, car, wild","p":[["When and where","The fire season runs <b>1 May – 31 October</b> (Fire Service Ordinance 9/2024, Government Gazette B 2387/2024). It applies in forests, forest land, grassland and farmland, parks and groves, protected areas (national parks, Natura, archaeological sites) and any outdoor area <b>less than 300 m</b> from them. In practice: almost anywhere you'd want to sleep."],["The rules","• Fires for camping/recreation: <b>banned at every risk level</b>.<br>• Camping stove, barbecue, any device with gas, liquid or solid fuel: banned, except on your own property and licensed sites (campsites).<br>• Inside a campsite/property: gas or liquid fuel only at risk level <b>1–3</b>, charcoal/wood only at level <b>1–2</b>.<br>• Sky lanterns: banned 1 April – 30 November.<br>• Burning rubbish, cigarette ends, matches, charcoal, glass, rubbish in the forest: banned all year round."],["The risk level","It's published daily on Civil Protection's <b>Daily Fire Risk Forecast Map</b>, from 1 (low) to 5 (state of alert). Local authorities can extend the bans beyond October. In practice, May–October outside a campsite near forest or fields, the stove stays in its bag: eat cold or heat food with power from a power station inside the car."],["Penalties","• Administrative fine <b>up to €50,000</b> (Fire Service Ordinance 19/2024).<br>• Fire or sparks in forest/fields at risk level 4–5: imprisonment of at least 1 year and a fine of at least €10,000 (Article 265 Penal Code, Law 5090/2024).<br>• If a serious wildfire breaks out: a felony.<br>For fire or smoke: <b>199</b> (fire service) or <b>112</b>."],["What stays essentially legal","1. Campsites all year round, with a stove when the risk level allows.<br>2. Sleeping inside a legally parked car, nothing outside, arrive, sleep, leave.<br>3. Private land with permission: a friend's field, a farm, an estate that takes visitors.<br>4. Areas designated by the municipality, if they exist and you've confirmed it."],["Before you go & everyone's mistake","Is the place a campsite, private with permission or legal parking? Is it May–October? Then check the risk map and plan no-flame food. Is it Natura or an archaeological site? The mistake: thinking the ban only covers wood fires, it also covers <b>the stove for your coffee</b>. Fire isn't only your decision; it's everyone's."]],"q":[{"q":"Inside a campsite during the fire season, at which risk level is a gas stove allowed?","a":["1–3","1–5","Only 1"],"w":"Gas/liquid fuel only at level 1–3; charcoal/wood only at 1–2."},{"q":"Within what distance of forests/fields do the bans apply?","a":["Less than 300 m","Less than 30 m","Only inside the forest"],"w":"Ordinance 9/2024 covers any outdoor area less than 300 m away."},{"q":"When is burning rubbish banned?","a":["All year round","Only May–October","Only at risk level 4–5"],"w":"Burning rubbish and throwing cigarette ends in the forest are banned all year round."}]},{"id":"exo_sleepopts","n":"Where you sleep: the options","src":"Outside the Walls, Ch. 3: Where you sleep","p":[["The rule that matters most","The tent doesn't keep you warm; the <b>sleep system</b> does: mat, sleeping bag, clothes. The tent protects from wind, rain, insects. An average tent with a good mat sleeps better than an expensive tent with a bad mat. If the budget is tight, the money goes first on the mat and sleeping bag."],["The options at a glance","• <b>Campsite bungalow/caravan</b>: per night, no pitching, first taste, families.<br>• <b>Ground tent</b>: €60–350, 10–20 minutes, the default for beginners.<br>• <b>Sleeping in the car</b>: €0–150, 5 minutes, summer needs ventilation, winter insulation on the windows.<br>• <b>Swag</b>: €250–700, 3–5 minutes, solo or couples.<br>• <b>Hammock</b>: €30–250, 5–10 minutes, a supplement.<br>• <b>Rooftop tent</b>: €1,200–4,500, 2–10 minutes, frequent trips, overlanding.<br>• <b>Tarp & bivy</b>: €50–300, later, not at the start."],["Bungalow: a no-risk trial","Many campsites rent wooden cabins, caravans or ready-pitched tents. It isn't “camping for cowards”; it's a way to see if you like it before buying anything. Ideal for families with small children. One night there and a walk past the neighbours' tents, and you know what you want."],["Ground tent: the right start","The cheapest, most flexible, and the one that teaches you most; once you move on, it stays for guests. Two rules:<br>• <b>Size +1</b>: for one person a two-person tent, for two a three-person.<br>• <b>Three-season dome</b> to start: freestanding, pitches even on rock, covers almost the whole Greek year except snow."],["Sleeping inside the car","The most underrated option: if the rear seats fold flat and you fit lying down, you already have a shelter. Measure first, you need a flat length of <b>your height plus a few centimetres</b>. A rule with no exceptions: <b>never leave the engine running while you sleep</b>. Quick, safe, often warmer, but it needs organising and a window cracked open for condensation."],["The recommendation for beginners","1. Start with what you have: the car and a borrowed tent are enough.<br>2. First purchase: a good three-season dome tent, one size bigger, and a mat that suits you.<br>3. If after three trips pitching tires you, look at a swag.<br>4. Only if after ten nights you know you'll go out often, consider a rooftop tent."],["Before you go & everyone's mistake","Have you pitched the tent once in the garden or park and timed it? Are all the poles, pegs and guy lines in the bag? Do you fit lying down with your mat? The mistake: buying the Instagram tent and the cheapest mat on the shelf. It's not camping's fault, it's their back's."]],"q":[{"q":"For one person, what tent size is recommended?","a":["Two-person","One-person","Four-person"],"w":"The “size +1” rule: manufacturers' “2-person” is tight."},{"q":"If the budget is tight, where does the money go first?","a":["Mat and sleeping bag","Tent","Fridge"],"w":"The sleep system keeps you warm, not the tent."},{"q":"When does the book say to consider a rooftop tent?","a":["After ten nights, if you know you'll go out often","Before the first trip","After the first night"],"w":"A rooftop tent is your second or third way of sleeping out, not the first."}]},{"id":"exo_swag_rtt","n":"Swag, hammock, rooftop tent, tarp","src":"Outside the Walls, Ch. 3: Where you sleep (and Ch. 4 for rooftop tents)","p":[["Swag: the “coffin”","A long, narrow canvas shelter with a built-in mattress, mesh and waterproof floor. Arrive, unroll, sleep; in the morning roll it up with the sleeping bag inside. Underneath a simple groundsheet for dust; on folding camp beds, stones and slopes don't bother you.<br><b>Pros:</b> a few minutes to pitch, mattress ready, durable, warm in the cold, insects out, easy to move with the whole mattress.<br><b>Cons:</b> heavy/bulky, low (you can't change standing up), needs drying so it doesn't go mouldy, hard in continuous rain. If you travel solo, get a <b>single</b>."],["Hammock","Needs two strong trees about <b>4 m</b> apart and wide straps that don't damage the bark. You sleep <b>diagonally</b>, not down the middle. The air under your back chills you even at 15°C, for cold you need an underquilt. A mosquito net in the Greek summer isn't an extra. Ideal for an afternoon nap; as a beginner's only bed, no."],["Rooftop tent: hard or soft","<b>Soft shell:</b> unfolds like a book, more space often with an annex, more affordable, but noise/consumption and fabric flapping in the wind.<br><b>Hard shell:</b> opens in a minute on gas struts, aerodynamic, quieter and steadier in the wind, more expensive.<br><b>Pros:</b> fast to pitch, mattress inside, flat bed, away from damp, mud, animals. <b>Cons:</b> cost, 45–90 kg on the roof all year round, consumption, a ladder at night, and to go for bread you pack up the house."],["Checks before buying a rooftop tent","• The <b>dynamic roof load</b> (owner's manual) of the vehicle and bars must exceed the weight of tent + rack. When parked, the roof takes much more.<br>• The centre of gravity rises: in bends and on side slopes on dirt tracks it leans more.<br>• Consumption and noise on the motorway go up.<br>• If you stay several days in the same spot, “packing up for bread” matters.<br>• Open and close it <b>yourself in the shop</b> before you pay."],["The ladder at night","Climbing down half-asleep for the toilet at night isn't a simple matter, it's a dive into the void. It needs you fully awake and doesn't forgive mistakes. A rooftop tent is your second or third way of sleeping out, not the first."],["Tarp and bivy: for later","A stretched sheet and a waterproof bag around the sleeping bag. Light, beautiful, but it needs experience in pitching and reading the weather. Try it after you've done nights in a tent, in good weather and with the car next to you."],["Four ways, four characters","<b>Ground tent</b>: the purest way, cheap, goes anywhere, but damp and bugs. <b>Swag</b>: simplicity without bad sleep; for sleeping, not partying. <b>Rooftop tent</b>: up high, dry, with a view, what you leave inside stays, but cost, weight, height. <b>Car</b>: the “I'm not pitching anything” option, for when you're tired or the weather has turned. There's no best tent; there's the best one for the person, the vehicle, the weather and the way of travelling."]],"q":[{"q":"How do you lie properly in a hammock?","a":["Diagonally, not down the middle","Exactly in the middle","With your head low"],"w":"Diagonally, so you don't wake up “like a banana”."},{"q":"Which vehicle figure do you check before buying a rooftop tent?","a":["The dynamic roof load","The static towing load","The horsepower"],"w":"Tent and rack together must be under the dynamic roof load."},{"q":"Why does a hammock chill you even at 15°C?","a":["The air under your back steals heat","Because it's high up","Because it has no mesh"],"w":"Without insulation underneath (underquilt) you lose heat through your back."}]},{"id":"exo_gear","n":"Gear: essential, useful, extra and budget","src":"Outside the Walls, Ch. 4: Gear","p":[["The rule","Before every purchase, one question: <b>does it help you travel more or just buy more?</b> Do your first three trips with the essentials, borrowed where possible. After the third you know what you missed, buy only that. Three levels: essential (you don't leave without it), useful (after the 1st–2nd trip), extra (later, if you prove you need it)."],["The essentials","• <b>Shelter:</b> tent with all pegs and guy lines, rubber mallet, groundsheet (footprint).<br>• <b>Sleep:</b> mat, sleeping bag for the season, pillow.<br>• <b>Light:</b> head torch, spare batteries.<br>• <b>Cooking:</b> stove and cartridge, two lighters, small pot, mug, cutlery, sponge.<br>• <b>Water:</b> drinking water for the whole trip and a little extra.<br>• <b>Clothes:</b> layers, fleece, waterproof, beanie, spare socks.<br>• <b>Health:</b> first-aid kit, sunscreen, insect repellent, toilet paper, sanitiser.<br>• <b>Communication:</b> charged phone, powerbank, car charging cable.<br>• <b>Tools:</b> multitool, rope, repair tape. <b>Rubbish:</b> strong bags. <b>Vehicle:</b> spare wheel, jack, jump leads, full tank."],["The useful","• Chairs and a small table, the second evening on the ground is orthopaedic.<br>• Tarp for shade/rain, without shade, after 10 a.m. it's unbearable.<br>• Passive cool box with ice packs for 1–2 nights.<br>• Table lantern, so the head torch doesn't blind people.<br>• Wash bowl or collapsible bucket.<br>• Bags/boxes by use (kitchen, sleep, tools), ship logic: everything in its place.<br>• Small trowel for wild camping."],["The extras","Power station and solar panel, compressor fridge, awning, rooftop tent or swag, electric kettle, heated underlay, Starlink. And later: diesel heater, roof box (raises centre of gravity/consumption) or trailer (reversing on a narrow dirt track = patience), shower tent and chemical toilet, water tank with 12V pump, gazebo (in the meltemi it becomes a kite)."],["The basic kit at three budgets","For one person, three seasons (€ budget / mid / quality):<br>• Tent 70 / 180 / 400<br>• Mat 30 / 90 / 180<br>• Sleeping bag 40 / 120 / 300<br>• Kitchen 40 / 90 / 180<br>• Head torch 15 / 40 / 80<br>• Total ~195 / ~520 / ~1,140<br>On little money: a budget tent and a <b>mid-range mat and sleeping bag</b>, never the other way round."],["The whole kit under €250","2-person dome tent 40–60 · pegs/steel masonry nails 5–10 · 2×3 tarp as groundsheet (slightly smaller than the floor) 5 · 3×4 tarp for shade with ropes 12 · mallet 5 · foam mat 10 · sleeping bag comfort +10/+15°C 20–30 · pillow = bag with a fleece 0 · liner = old sewn sheet 0 · AAA torch 8–12 · LED lantern 10 · tabletop stove + 4 cartridges 35–40 · kitchenware from home 0 · soft cool bag/polystyrene box + 4 ice packs 10–15 · kitchen box 8 · 2 × 10 l water cans 10 · 5 l pump sprayer or solar shower bag 8–12 · chair 10 · first-aid kit 10. Total ~€210–255, under €150 with borrowed gear."],["Where to save & tricks","<b>Save on:</b> kitchenware, chairs, cool box, lantern, tarp, summer tent. <b>Don't save on:</b> mat and sleeping bag when the night drops below 10°C, and pegs.<br>• Borrow for the first three trips.<br>• Second-hand: pitch the tent, check zips, seams, poles, floor; smell the sleeping bag.<br>• In September shops clear their stock; check sports chains, supermarket “outdoor weeks”, hardware stores.<br>• Upgrade order: <b>mat, sleeping bag, tent, chair, cool box</b>."],["Before you go & everyone's mistake","Have you lit the stove once on the balcony? Does the cartridge have gas? Have you checked the night temperature at the altitude you're going to, not the city's? New torch batteries? Is there enough water if you stay an extra day? The mistake: buying the extras before the essentials, a compressor fridge and a supermarket sleeping bag. The beer is ice-cold, and so is the person."]],"q":[{"q":"When do you buy gear according to the practical rule?","a":["After the third trip, only what you missed","Before the first trip, all at once","Only on special offer"],"w":"The first three trips with the essentials, borrowed where possible."},{"q":"What's the right upgrade order?","a":["Mat, sleeping bag, tent, chair, cool box","Cool box, tent, chair, mat, sleeping bag","Tent, cool box, mat, sleeping bag, chair"],"w":"In this order every euro buys more sleep."},{"q":"On little money, which combination do you choose?","a":["Budget tent, mid-range mat and sleeping bag","Expensive tent, cheap mat","Expensive cool box, cheap sleeping bag"],"w":"Never the other way round: sleep decides the night."}]},{"id":"exo_tent","n":"The tent in depth, and pegs","src":"Outside the Walls, Ch. 4: Gear","p":[["Tent shapes","• <b>Dome (igloo)</b>: 2–3 crossed poles, almost freestanding, can be moved pitched, takes wind from all sides; little standing room at the edges.<br>• <b>Tunnel</b>: parallel arches, lots of space, big porch, families; won't stand without tensioned guy lines, pitch it with its back to the wind.<br>• <b>Pyramid</b>: 1–2 poles and lots of pegs, simple, light, good in snow; all its strength is in the pegs.<br>• <b>Pop-up</b>: pitches in seconds, but folding needs technique, takes space, doesn't like strong wind.<br>• <b>Inflatable</b>: air tubes instead of poles."],["Seasons, height, ventilation","<b>One-season:</b> thin fabric, lots of mesh, for summer at a campsite. <b>Three-season:</b> double wall, mesh that closes with fabric, the right first tent. <b>Four-season:</b> strong poles, fly to the ground, adjustable vents; for snow, but an oven in August. <b>Height:</b> if you can't sit up, you dress lying down. <b>Ventilation:</b> vents up high and a gap between inner tent and fly, where they touch, water gets through. Trick for multi-day rain: a tarp high above the tent (~€15)."],["Waterproofing in mm","Hydrostatic head: how tall a column of water the fabric withstands before water gets through. The floor always needs more.<br>• Summer/campsite: fly 1,500–2,000, floor 3,000+.<br>• Three seasons/mountains: fly 2,000–3,000, floor 5,000+.<br>• Exposed/winter: fly 3,000–5,000, floor 5,000–10,000.<br>What the label doesn't say: <b>seams</b> leak first (they need taping), bad pitching lets water in, and the <b>Greek sun</b> eats fabrics, look for a tall tent with ventilation, UV protection and shade."],["Why the tent's own pegs aren't enough","They're the only thing holding the tent to the ground. The ones in the box are <b>too few</b>, <b>thin and soft</b> (hooked wire that bends on the first stone) and made for <b>“one” type of ground</b>, while Greece gives you sand, soil and rock in the same weekend. The first purchase after the tent: a set of good, thick pegs."],["Peg types","• <b>Hooked wire</b>: firm soil without stones; bends, pulls out in soft ground.<br>• <b>V</b> (aluminium): soft–firm soil; the head bends on stone.<br>• <b>Y</b> (7000 aluminium): the best all-round type; struggles in stony ground.<br>• <b>Rock peg</b> (steel/titanium): hard, stony, gravel; poor in sand.<br>• <b>Screw</b>: loose soil, grass; heavy, slow.<br>• <b>Sand/snow</b>: wide, long, hollow; useless in hard ground.<br>• <b>Bag with sand/stones</b>: beach, concrete, platform.<br>Greek trick from the forum: steel <b>masonry nails</b> with a plastic head, they go into stone and come out with a twist."],["The recommended set","One bag with:<br>• 8–12 Y pegs in 7000 aluminium, 18–20 cm, for corners and main guy lines<br>• 4–6 steel rock pegs<br>• 4 sand pegs or 4 bags for the beach<br>• 4–6 spares<br>• rubber mallet and hook/peg extractor<br>Minimum lengths: soft soil Y/V 18 cm · hard soil steel peg 23 cm · sand wide peg or buried bag 25 cm · snow 30 cm · rock: peg or line to big stones."],["How to put them in","1. At ~45°, leaning away from the tent (a little more without a hook).<br>2. Guy lines long and low, the more horizontal the line, the harder it pulls out.<br>3. With your sole or the mallet, not big stones on aluminium.<br>4. On stone don't hit harder; pull it out and try next to it.<br>5. If nothing holds: line to a big stone or a <b>buried anchor</b> (peg or stick buried horizontally, tied at the middle)."],["Inflatable tents (air tents)","Air tubes (air beams) with a TPU bladder inside reinforced fabric. Pressures: Vango AirBeam 7 psi (~0.5 bar) · Heimplanet 6–8 or 4–6 psi depending on model · Zempire 7–12 psi (burst 35–50) · Outwell max 9 psi (0.6 bar). Inflate to what the manufacturer says.<br><b>Pros:</b> pitch in minutes, bend and spring back, no poles to lose, separate chambers.<br><b>Watch out:</b> often >20 kg, more expensive, <b>only the proper pump</b> with a gauge (a car compressor over-inflates), check pressure in the afternoon (temperature), always carry a repair kit, and pegs are doubly important."]],"q":[{"q":"Which peg type is considered the best all-rounder?","a":["Y in 7000 aluminium","Hooked wire","Screw"],"w":"The Y works in almost every soil."},{"q":"Why don't you inflate an air tent with the car compressor?","a":["It can over-inflate the tubes","It's too slow","It damages the battery"],"w":"Tents need only 4–12 psi; you want a pump with a gauge and a relief valve."},{"q":"What do you do if a peg hits stone?","a":["Pull it out and try a little to one side","Hit it harder with a rock","Leave it half in"],"w":"Hitting hard bends it without making it hold."},{"q":"Why does the floor need more mm than the fly?","a":["You kneel on it and put your full weight on it","Because it gets the sun","Because it's thinner"],"w":"Body pressure pushes water through the fabric."}]},{"id":"exo_sleepkit","n":"Sleeping bag and mat: the numbers","src":"Outside the Walls, Ch. 4: Gear","p":[["Comfort, Limit, Extreme","Serious sleeping bags are tested to <b>ISO 23537</b> (formerly EN 13537), with a heated mannequin in a cold chamber.<br>• <b>Comfort</b>: you sleep comfortably, relaxed (“standard woman”).<br>• <b>Limit</b>: curled up without shivering (“standard man”).<br>• <b>Extreme</b>: survival with risk of hypothermia, that's not sleep.<br>Manufacturers advertise the limit; you buy by <b>comfort, at least 5°C below</b> the lowest expected temperature. The test uses a very good mat and thermals, with a worse mat you'll be colder."],["Mat R-value","It measures insulation from the ground. Since 2020 the big brands use a common standard, <b>ASTM F3340</b>; without it, the R is an estimate.<br>• Summer 1–2 · Spring/autumn 2–4 · Winter 4–6 · Snow/altitude 6+<br>Two mats add up: foam 2 + inflatable 3 ≈ 5. A high R doesn't make you hot in summer, it just stops the ground stealing heat, so it's an all-season buy."],["Shape and fill","A sleeping bag doesn't produce heat; it holds the air you've warmed. <b>Mummy</b> with hood = warmer for its weight (winter/mountains). <b>Rectangular</b> = comfortable, opens like a blanket (summer/campsite).<br><b>Down:</b> best warmth/weight, small pack size, long life, expensive, but wet it loses almost all its insulation. <b>Synthetic:</b> affordable, keeps some insulation when wet, ideal for beginners, damp conditions, car camping."],["Fill power, ratio, stitching","• Fill power 550–650 good, 700–800 very good, over 800 top end.<br>• Down/feather ratio e.g. 90/10, the more down the better.<br>• If the stitching goes straight through from outside to inside, every seam is a cold line; good bags have internal baffles.<br>• Before you get in, shake it so the down spreads out."],["What you wear inside, and the liner","Thick clothes compress the bag and steal its air, and the day's clothes are sweaty. Wear <b>dry thermals, a beanie, sleeping socks</b>; the jacket goes on top. A <b>liner</b> keeps the bag clean and adds a few degrees (fleece more, cotton/silk less), the cheapest way to “upgrade” a sleeping bag."],["Sleeping bag care","• After every trip leave it open for a day, in shade and a breeze.<br>• At home, loose in a big cotton sack or hanging, <b>never compressed</b> in its stuff sack (it won't loft again).<br>• Wash rarely, with special detergent, on a gentle cycle. Down: tumble dry on low with 2–3 tennis balls. Read the label first."],["Mat types","• <b>Closed-cell foam</b>: cheap, can't puncture, insulates even wet; hard, bulky, beginners and as a second mat in winter.<br>• <b>Self-inflating</b>: comfortable, good insulation; heavier, can puncture, car camping.<br>• <b>Inflatable</b>: light, high R in good ones; expensive, noisy, a puncture finishes it.<br>• <b>Beach lilo/air bed</b>: the air inside gets cold, summer only, with something insulating on top.<br>• <b>Camp bed</b>: high, comfortable; cold air underneath, in winter it needs a mat.<br>Cheap tricks: a foil blanket/insulating sheet under the mat, and a blanket <b>under</b> the sleeping bag."],["Summer vs winter","• Sleeping bag comfort: summer +10/+15°C or just a liner at the beach · winter −5°C at low altitude, −10°C or lower in the mountains.<br>• Mat: 1–2 / 4+ or two mats.<br>• Tent: mesh, ventilation, morning shade / wind protection, porch for wet gear.<br>• Clothes: light long sleeves against mosquitoes, hat, light jacket / thermals, fleece, jacket, beanie, gloves, sleeping socks.<br>• Water: 6 l/person/day (4 drinking + 2 washing) / 3–4 l and a thermos.<br>• Vehicle: shade for the cool box / snow chains, antifreeze, full tank.<br>• No. 1 danger: heatstroke, wildfire / hypothermia, carbon monoxide."]],"q":[{"q":"Which label temperature do you buy a sleeping bag by?","a":["Comfort, at least 5°C below the expected minimum","Limit, equal to the minimum","Extreme"],"w":"Limit is curled-up sleep, extreme is survival."},{"q":"A foam mat R 2 under an inflatable R 3 give roughly?","a":["5","3","6.5"],"w":"Stacked mat R-values add up."},{"q":"How do you store the sleeping bag at home?","a":["Loose in a big cotton sack or hanging","Compressed in its stuff sack","In a vacuum bag"],"w":"Compressed for months, it won't loft again."}]},{"id":"exo_heatlight","n":"Stove, warmth, light, kitchen box, washing","src":"Outside the Walls, Ch. 4: Gear","p":[["Why the gas stove dies in the cold","Boiling points: <b>butane</b> ~−0.5°C (struggles below ~+5°C), <b>isobutane</b> ~−12°C (three seasons), <b>propane</b> ~−42°C (best in the cold). Tabletop stove cartridges are mostly butane. In mixes the propane burns off first, so a half-empty canister in the cold won't work. Fix: a fresh canister for winter, an isobutane/propane mix, inside your sleeping bag at night. <b>Never warm a canister over a flame.</b>"],["Warmth at night: safest first","• <b>Hot water bottle</b> in the sleeping bag: the best cheap trick.<br>• <b>12V electric blanket</b>: ~4–6 A, only with a second battery/power station; warm the bag and switch it off.<br>• <b>12V fan heater</b> 100–150 W: demists the windscreen, doesn't heat the cab.<br>• <b>220V fan heater</b> from an inverter: 400 W ≈ 35 A from 12V, useless without a huge battery (at a campsite with hook-up it's another story).<br>• <b>Diesel heater</b>: ~0.1–0.3 l/hour, the serious solution; a permanent one needs professional fitting, a portable “in a box” one stays outside. Always a CO detector and a second battery.<br>• <b>Candle lantern</b>: a few degrees, only while awake, hung away from fabric.<br>• <b>Gas heater, “catalytic” heater, brazier, running engine</b>: NO."],["The rule with no exceptions","No combustion inside the tent or the car while you sleep, no stove, no heater, no engine. The only burner in there is you. With any combustion near the sleeping area, have a <b>battery carbon monoxide detector</b> (it costs as much as two pizzas). The improvised internet contraptions with flower pots, candles and pipes have killed people."],["Small solar panel","Folding 20–100 W for phone, torches, camera, laptop. A little shade or cloud cuts output a lot, turn it to the sun every two hours. Charge an <b>intermediate battery</b> (powerbank/power station) and the phones from that. Test it on the balcony before you go."],["Light: which for which job","• Head torch: <b>one per person</b>, no exceptions; it blinds whoever you look at.<br>• Rechargeable LED lantern: table and inside the tent.<br>• Gas lantern: lots of light, hot glass, outside only, never in the tent.<br>• Paraffin lamp: for the photo.<br>• Candle lantern: for the company, with an eye on it.<br>• Solar fairy lights: markers around the tent and guy lines.<br>• 12V car LEDs: only with a second battery.<br>Red light doesn't spoil night vision; warm yellowish light attracts fewer insects."],["The kitchen in a box","Like a ship's galley: a 40–60 l plastic box with a lid, always packed. Fire (stove, fuel, 2 lighters, matches in a small bag) · cooking (pot with lid, frying pan, spatula, ladle, tongs, chopping board, knife, openers) · table (plate, bowl, mug, cutlery per person + one spare set) · washing up (bowl, sponge, biodegradable liquid, towel, paper) · order (bags, zip bags, foil, pegs, rubber bands, tablecloth) · flavour (oil in a screw-top bottle inside a bag) · cool box with ice packs from the freezer."],["Stoves","• Tabletop with cartridge: cheap, stable; struggles below +5°C.<br>• Small stove on a screw-thread canister: light, fast; unstable with a big pot.<br>• 2–5 kg LPG bottle with regulator: cheap per meal, for families; heavy, check the hose.<br>• Liquid fuel: works in cold and at altitude; noise, priming, not for beginners.<br>They all burn oxygen: cook outside, under a tarp if it's raining, never in the tent or porch."],["Washing, toilet, campsite vs wild","Shower: 10–20 l solar bag · <b>5–8 l pressure sprayer</b> (new, never one that's had pesticide in it) · 12V shower with pump · tank on the vehicle. Shower tent for privacy with kids/friends. Toilet: trowel and a hole · folding bucket toilet with bags and cat litter · chemical (empty it <b>only</b> at a disposal point) · urination funnel for women.<br>At a campsite you have hook-up (25 m extension reel, maybe a CEE adapter), taps, WC, bins, reception. In the wild you carry everything: whatever you forgot, you don't have."]],"q":[{"q":"Which gas works best in the cold?","a":["Propane","Butane","Both the same"],"w":"Propane vaporises down to ~−42°C, butane struggles below ~+5°C."},{"q":"Why don't you charge the phone straight from the solar panel?","a":["It cuts out every time a cloud passes","The battery burns out","There's no proper cable"],"w":"Charge an intermediate battery and the devices from that."},{"q":"Which of these heating options NEVER goes in the sleeping area?","a":["Catalytic gas heater","Hot water bottle","12V electric blanket"],"w":"All combustion, even “flameless”, burns oxygen and gives off carbon monoxide."}]},{"id":"exo_weather","n":"Read the forecast like a sailor","src":"Outside the Walls, Ch. 5: Weather and seasons","p":[["The four numbers","Forget the icons; open an hourly forecast <b>for the spot</b>, not the town:<br>1. <b>Minimum night temperature</b>, decides sleeping bag/mat; it comes just before dawn.<br>2. <b>Wind and gusts</b>, the average tells you how you'll sleep, the gusts whether you'll still have a tent in the morning.<br>3. <b>Rain</b>, in the surrounding mountains too, because it fills the streams next to you.<br>4. <b>Thunderstorms/lightning</b>, in summer often in the afternoon from a clear morning sky.<br>Fifth, in summer: <b>fire risk</b>, when it's high, you don't go into forest."],["Wind in tent language","• <b>0–3 Bf</b> (up to 19 km/h): ideal.<br>• <b>4–5 Bf</b> (20–38): manageable, all guy lines tied, kitchen behind a windbreak.<br>• <b>6 Bf</b> (39–49): not in exposed spots (summits, open beaches, saddles).<br>• <b>7+ Bf</b> (50+): for a beginner, change plan, campsite, car or another weekend.<br>Bridge logic: don't ask “will the tent hold?” but “if it holds, will I sleep?”."],["Feels-like temperature (wind chill)","Wind strips away the warm layer of air around you. Roughly: 0°C with 30 km/h ≈ −6, with 40 km/h ≈ −7, with 60 km/h ≈ −9 · +5°C with 40 km/h ≈ −1 · −5°C with 40 km/h ≈ −14 · −10°C with 60 km/h ≈ −23. A night at zero with a fresh north wind is a night at minus seven, that's why the tent goes behind something that breaks the wind."],["Dress like an onion","1. <b>Base layer</b> (thermals): wicks sweat, merino or synthetic, <b>not cotton</b>.<br>2. <b>Mid layer</b> (insulation): fleece or light down.<br>3. <b>Outer layer</b> (shell): breathable waterproof with a hood.<br>Mountain rule: start a little cold, take a layer off before you sweat, put it on as soon as you stop. The sweat of the climb is the cold of the summit."],["Mountain weather","• <b>~6°C colder per 1,000 m</b>: Athens 12 → at 1,500 m close to 3.<br>• Clear sky at night = colder; clouds are a blanket.<br>• Cold air flows down and pools in hollows and stream beds; a flat spot a few metres higher is warmer.<br>• Clouds building like cauliflowers at midday = a storm in the afternoon. Stay away from summits, lone trees, streams; the car is the best shelter from lightning."],["Sea weather","• <b>Meltemi</b>: north winds in the Aegean in summer, for days, on the islands choose a beach on the south side.<br>• <b>Sea breeze</b>: by day from the sea, at night from the land.<br>• <b>Humidity</b>: in the morning everything is soaked even without rain, keep clothes inside the car at night."],["Where to read the weather","• Always two sources: a Greek meteorological service and a wind app. If they disagree, prepare for the worse.<br>• Fire risk map every day in summer.<br>• Check again the morning you leave.<br>• Look up: the sky is the most reliable forecast for the next two hours.<br>The mistake: looking at the town's temperature and the sun icon."]],"q":[{"q":"Roughly how much does the temperature drop per 1,000 m of altitude?","a":["~6°C","~1°C","~15°C"],"w":"That's why 12 in Athens means about 3 at 1,500 m."},{"q":"With a 7 Bf forecast, what does a beginner with a ground tent do?","a":["Changes plan: campsite, car or another weekend","Ties more guy lines and stays on the summit","Pitches on the beach"],"w":"Above 50 km/h the question is whether you'll sleep, not whether the tent holds."},{"q":"Why no cotton for the base layer?","a":["It stays wet and chills you","It's too warm","It doesn't breathe at all"],"w":"The base layer must wick sweat; merino or synthetic."}]},{"id":"exo_daylight","n":"Seasons and sunset time: planning the day","src":"Outside the Walls, Ch. 5: Weather and seasons","p":[["The Greek year for a beginner","• <b>Dec–Feb</b>: cold, rain, snow in the mountains, dark early → only with experience or at low altitude.<br>• <b>Mar–Apr</b>: changeable, cold nights → fine with a warm sleeping bag.<br>• <b>May–Jun</b>: green, long days → the best season to start.<br>• <b>Jul–Aug</b>: heatwaves, meltemi, fire risk, full campsites → mountains or a beach with shade, never a fire.<br>• <b>Sep–Oct</b>: warm sea, empty beaches → just as good, maybe better.<br>• <b>Nov</b>: rain, storms → watch the stream beds."],["The two-hour rule","Arrive at the spot <b>two hours before sunset</b>: one hour to read it and pitch, one for the unexpected. Darkness hides holes, stream beds, drop-offs and tires the driver. In December that means around 15:00."],["Approximate sunset times (Athens)","1 Jan 17:15 · 1 Feb 17:46 · 1 Mar 18:17 · 1 Apr 19:47 · 1 May 20:14 · 1 Jun 20:41 · 15 Jun 20:48 · 1 Jul 20:51 · 1 Aug 20:35 · 1 Sep 19:56 · 1 Oct 19:09 · 15 Oct 18:48 · 1 Nov 17:27 · 1 Dec 17:06. Thessaloniki sets a little later in summer and a little earlier in winter (NOAA calculation, almost the same every year)."],["How to read sunset","• Sunset doesn't mean dark: ~half an hour of twilight. In dense forest, a gorge or on an east-facing slope it gets dark earlier.<br>• With cloud cover, reckon on dark <b>an hour earlier</b>.<br>• Clock change: last Sunday of March +1, last Sunday of October −1.<br>• Further west it gets dark later: Thesprotia +~10 minutes from Athens, Rhodes −~15."],["The rhythm of the day","Count backwards from sunset:<br>• Average speed on a mixed route <b>60–80 km/h at most</b>; on dirt much less.<br>• A 20-minute stop every two hours.<br>• A convoy goes at the speed of the slowest: add at least half an hour."],["Example","15 October, sunset 18:48 → aim to be at the spot by 16:45. 300 km at 70 km/h with two stops ≈ 5 hours → leave <b>by 11:45 at the latest</b>. Not “after work on Friday”."],["Before you go","Minimum temperature, gusts, rain in the surrounding mountains, storms, have you checked all four? In summer, the fire risk map? Do you have a plan B if the forecast turns bad: a campsite, a lower spot, a house? You don't beat the weather; you read it and choose when to go out."]],"q":[{"q":"How long before sunset do you arrive at the spot?","a":["Two hours","Ten minutes","After sunset, in twilight"],"w":"One hour to read it and pitch, one for the unexpected."},{"q":"With cloud cover, when do you reckon on darkness?","a":["An hour earlier than the table","Half an hour later","Exactly at sunset"],"w":"Clouds cut the light earlier."},{"q":"Which period is best to start in?","a":["May–June","July–August","December–February"],"w":"Green, long days and mild nights; Sep–Oct is just as good."}]},{"id":"exo_campsite","n":"Your first campsite","src":"Outside the Walls, Ch. 6: Your first campsite","p":[["School with showers","At a campsite you make all your mistakes with the shower next door: forgot the lighter, there's a shop; got cold, the car is ten metres away; a pole broke, the neighbour has spares. There you learn your gear and yourself without paying dearly for mistakes. It's the harbour where you train before heading out to open sea."],["How to choose your first","• <b>Close</b>: 1–2 hours from home.<br>• <b>Trees</b>: check there's shade on the pitches, not the sea in the photos.<br>• <b>Ground</b>: soil/grass take pegs; sand lets them pull out, gravel won't let them in.<br>• <b>Character</b>: family-oriented or with a bar, the reviews tell you.<br>• <b>Season</b>: June or September; August is a block of flats without walls.<br>• <b>Phone</b>: booking, what the price includes (per person, tent, car, electricity separately), what plug the hook-up needs."],["At reception: choose the pitch on foot","<b>You want:</b> shade that lasts into the morning, level ground, hook-up less than 25 m away, a few metres from water and showers.<br><b>You avoid:</b> next to toilets and bins, next to an internal road or gate, the bar and sports pitch if you want quiet, the lowest spot where water collects. The mistake: grabbing the first empty pitch, it's usually empty for a reason."],["Electricity: the beginner's trap","1. <b>25 m extension on a reel</b>, for outdoor use, unroll it fully, because coiled it heats up.<br>2. <b>The right adapter</b>: many have a blue industrial CEE socket, others Schuko, ask.<br>3. <b>Moderation</b>: lamp, fridge, chargers are fine; kettle plus heater trips the breaker.<br>Sockets and multi-sockets always off the ground, protected from rain and dew."],["The first hour","1. Walk round the pitch: ground, slope, shade, wind.<br>2. Tent, mat, sleeping bag.<br>3. Electricity.<br>4. Kitchen and chairs.<br>5. Walk round the campsite: toilets, water, bins, <b>emergency exit</b>, whatever you'll look for at two in the morning, find it now.<br>6. Then coffee."],["The unwritten laws","• Quiet hours (usually midday and night), ask at reception.<br>• A tent has no soundproofing.<br>• Don't cut across other people's pitches; make your guy lines visible at night.<br>• Fire only where allowed, usually at the shared barbecues.<br>• Leave shared facilities as you found them."],["The campsite as a school","• Sleep one night <b>without electricity</b>, as if you were in the mountains.<br>• <b>Measure the water</b> you use in 24 hours, that's what you'll carry in the wild.<br>• See how long your cool box lasts without power.<br>• Talk to the old hands; buy them a coffee and they'll tell you all the tricks."]],"q":[{"q":"Why do you unroll the extension reel fully?","a":["Coiled, it heats up","So it reaches further","So it doesn't tangle"],"w":"Cable on the reel overheats under load."},{"q":"Which pitch do you avoid at a campsite?","a":["The lowest, where water collects when it rains","The one with morning shade","The one near the hook-up"],"w":"Also next to toilets, bins, the gate and the bar."},{"q":"What test do you do at the campsite to prepare for wild camping?","a":["Measure the water you use in 24 hours","Light a fire on your pitch","Sleep with the engine running"],"w":"That's the amount you'll carry when there's no tap."}]},{"id":"exo_pitch","n":"Pitching camp: where, when, in what order","src":"Outside the Walls, Ch. 7: Pitching camp","p":[["Arrive in daylight","The novice chases a view, the experienced chases shade, the wise chase shade with a view. Target: on the spot <b>two hours before sunset</b>. Daylight isn't only for seeing what you're doing; it's for seeing the place, where the water runs, which tree is dead. Whatever you didn't see in daylight, your mind invents at two in the morning."],["The seven-question check","1. <b>Is it level?</b> Lie down for a minute; on a slope, head uphill.<br>2. <b>Where does the water go?</b> Never in a stream bed, not even a dry one, nor in a hollow.<br>3. <b>What's overhead?</b> Dead branches fall in wind, at night.<br>4. <b>Where's the wind from?</b> Narrow/low end into the wind, door on the opposite side, car as a windbreak.<br>5. <b>Where does the sun rise?</b> In summer, morning sun = oven before 8; in winter, a friend.<br>6. <b>Does anyone pass through?</b> Not on a dirt track, footpath or flock crossing.<br>7. <b>How far is the water?</b> At least <b>60 m</b> from a spring/lake/stream; on the beach, above the line of dried seaweed."],["The pitching order","1. Clear stones, pine cones, sticks, no digging.<br>2. Groundsheet <b>slightly smaller</b> than the tent (otherwise it collects rain under the floor).<br>3. Tent, windward corners first.<br>4. Pegs at 45°, leaning away from the tent.<br>5. Guylines now, Greek wind “pulls” them out at three in the morning.<br>6. Mat and sleeping bag inside straight away, so they loft.<br>7. Kitchen ≥3 m from the tent, on the downwind side.<br>8. Light: lantern on the table, head torch always in the same place.<br>9. Then, and only then, tsipouro."],["Pegging on the spot","Remember three things: angle ~45° away from the tent · windward corners first · on rock or soft sand, tie off to a big stone or a buried bag of sand. Always take <b>four extra pegs</b> and a rubber mallet, they're the things most often lost."],["The camp as a house with rooms","• <b>Bedroom</b>: the tent, sleep and clothes, never food.<br>• <b>Kitchen</b>: away from it, on the downwind side.<br>• <b>Storeroom</b>: the car, food and rubbish shut away at night (fox, sheepdog).<br>• <b>Toilet</b>: when wild camping, pick it in daylight.<br>• <b>Exit</b>: car nose pointing to the way out, keys always in the same place."],["Striking camp","1. Dry whatever you can in the morning sun; a wet tent gets spread out at home within one day (otherwise mould).<br>2. Count the pegs: as many out as went in.<br>3. Reverse order: sleeping bag into its bag first, tent last.<br>4. Final sweep bent over: bottle caps, cigarette butts, pegs."],["Everyone's mistake","They pitch at the prettiest spot on the beach, in the gully coming down from the mountain, the flattest and softest. It's also the water's road. Before you leave: do you know sunset time, do you have 4 extra pegs and rope, did you check for rain on the surrounding mountains?"]],"q":[{"q":"Why should the groundsheet be slightly smaller than the tent?","a":["Otherwise it collects rain and channels it under the floor","So it weighs less","So it's less visible"],"w":"The edge that sticks out becomes a water gutter."},{"q":"How far from a spring or stream do you pitch?","a":["At least 60 m","5 m","Right next to it for convenience"],"w":"The same 60 m rule applies to the toilet and washing."},{"q":"Where does the kitchen go relative to the tent?","a":["At least 3 m away, on the downwind side","In the porch","Between the tent and the car, where it's more sheltered"],"w":"Smoke, smells and flames away from the bed."}]},{"id":"exo_warmnight","n":"A warm night: heat leaks and the 11 rules","src":"Outside the Walls, Ch. 8: Sleep","p":[["Where you lose heat","• <b>From below (conduction)</b>: cold ground sucks heat → a mat with the right R-value.<br>• <b>To the air (convection)</b> → tent, a spot behind a windbreak.<br>• <b>To the sky (radiation)</b> → sleeping bag, beanie.<br>• <b>Through moisture (evaporation)</b> → dry clothes kept for sleeping only.<br>That's why people freeze in June in the mountains in a good sleeping bag on an inflatable beach mattress."],["The eleven rules","1. Eat well (fat and carbs).<br>2. Hot drink, no alcohol.<br>3. A little movement before getting in, without sweating; never get in already cold.<br>4. Toilet before bed.<br>5. Dry socks for sleeping only.<br>6. Beanie.<br>7. Don't bury your mouth inside, breath is moisture.<br>8. Hot-water bottle at your feet or on your thigh, inside a sock.<br>9. Next day's clothes inside the sleeping bag.<br>10. Not ten layers inside, better a blanket on top.<br>11. Leave some ventilation, even in the cold."],["Pitching tricks","• Not at the bottom of the ravine: cold air rolls down and pools; a few metres higher is warmer. If the frost is only down by the stream, you chose right.<br>• Windbreak (trees, hill, car) and door facing east.<br>• Boots inside the tent in a bag."],["Before and inside the sleeping bag","• Clean your skin with a wet wipe; take off whatever you wore during the day.<br>• Snack by the pillow (bar, halva).<br>• If the mat is short, an empty rucksack or foam pad under your feet; spare clothes at the foot end.<br>• Jacket on top like a duvet.<br>• <b>Pee bottle</b> with a wide mouth, a solid lid and a mark that tells it apart from the water bottle.<br>• Two sleeping bags that zip together share the warmth."],["The things that sleep with you","Phone, powerbank, torch batteries inside the sleeping bag (cold drains them). Water bottles <b>upside down</b>, water freezes from the top and the lid doesn't stick; below zero, one sleeps with you. The gas canister in the foot of the sleeping bag for the morning coffee."],["When you wake up cold at three","1. Eat the snack.<br>2. Tense all your muscles for a few seconds, relax, repeat.<br>3. If your bladder is pressing, the bottle, your body spends heat keeping it warm.<br>4. Refill the bottle with hot water from the thermos.<br>5. Pull the hood tight and close the draught collar.<br>If you're shivering non-stop, move into the car."],["When the cold stops being funny","First signs of hypothermia: shivering that won't stop, fingers that can't work a zip, clumsiness, confused thinking, easier to spot in someone else. 1. Dry clothes immediately. 2. Hot, sweet drink. 3. Into the car with the heater on, one person awake and one window open. 4. If it's getting worse, <b>112</b>. Everyone's mistake: they put on another jacket inside, the problem was <b>underneath</b> them."]],"q":[{"q":"Why no alcohol before sleeping in the cold?","a":["You feel warm while you're losing heat","It wakes you early","It freezes in the bottle"],"w":"Alcohol dilates blood vessels and drives heat away."},{"q":"Why do water bottles go upside down in the cold?","a":["Water freezes from the top and the lid doesn't stick","So they don't spill","So they warm up faster"],"w":"Ice forms at the surface, which is now the bottom."},{"q":"You're cold in the tent, where is the problem usually?","a":["Underneath: a mat with a low R-value","In the jacket you're wearing","In the colour of the tent"],"w":"The ground steals more heat than the sky."}]},{"id":"exo_summersleep","n":"Cool, damp, noise: summer sleep","src":"Outside the Walls, Ch. 8: Sleep","p":[["The moisture you think is a leak","One person breathes out about <b>a glass of water a night</b>; warm air on cold fabric turns to droplets (condensation). Open the vents and the porch zip a little, keep wet clothes and shoes out of the sleeping area, microfibre cloth in the morning. In the car: two windows slightly open, <b>diagonally</b>."],["Summer: the real enemy","Winter is fixed with gear; a 28°C night only with your head.<br>• Go high (~6°C per 1,000 m).<br>• Shade that holds in the morning.<br>• With no rain in the forecast, take the fly off, you're left with mesh and stars.<br>• Sleep on top of the sleeping bag, with a sheet or liner.<br>• On the beach, door facing the sea for the breeze.<br>• Wet towel on the back of your neck and a battery fan.<br>• Water all day, not all night.<br>• <b>Never sleep in a closed car in the sun</b>, not even “for a bit”."],["Summer is won with airflow","A black hard-shell rooftop tent, shut like a Tupperware box on a roof that baked all day, becomes an oven until 9 in the morning. A simple ground tent with mesh under a tree breathes. A swag ventilates perfectly. The −20 sleeping bag stays home; you want a summer one, a cotton liner or a sheet. A <b>USB fan</b> saves your sleep."],["Mosquitoes","Next to lakes, rivers and windless beaches, the evening turns into a survival film. You need <b>mesh everywhere</b>, a draught of air and a repeller like Thermacell. The classic ones run on a butane cartridge, on high fire-risk days prefer the <b>battery</b> version."],["Altitude: the big secret","The smart one heads for the mountains in summer: Pindos, Tzoumerka, Valia Kalda, Rhodope, Parnassos, 39 below, 27 up there, and a cardigan at night. Remember the fire bans in the forests. A little shade, a little wind, a little mountain."],["Noise and light","Two items costing a few euros: <b>earplugs and a sleep mask</b>, for snoring, moon, campsite lights, the six o'clock sun. But if you're sleeping alone in the wild, keep <b>one ear free</b>: you want to hear rain or a car approaching."],["When the sleeping bag is too warm","Lesson from Meteora: 10°C outside, sleeping bag comfort −4, cold at first, then so hot he ended up in his underwear. When the sleeping bag is much warmer than the night, <b>leave the zip half open</b>. Before you leave in summer: where will the shade be at seven in the morning?"]],"q":[{"q":"How much moisture does one person breathe out at night?","a":["About a glass of water","A few drops","About two litres"],"w":"That's why a tent without ventilation “drips” from the inside."},{"q":"Which repeller do you prefer on a high fire-risk day?","a":["Battery-powered","Butane cartridge","Burning coil"],"w":"Any flame or gas is a risk and a possible offence."},{"q":"Alone in the wild, what do you do with earplugs?","a":["Keep one ear free","Use both","Never take them"],"w":"You want to hear rain or a vehicle approaching."}]},{"id":"exo_cook","n":"Cooking and the cool box: simple, hot, safe","src":"Outside the Walls, Ch. 9: Cooking, food, cool box","p":[["The one-pot rule","The first night isn't a competition: one pot, one burner, under half an hour. Do half the work at home: chop veg, marinate, measure pasta into bags. If you freeze the second day's meal, it doubles as an ice pack."],["Two-day menu","<b>Day 1:</b> lunch, sandwiches from home and fruit · dinner, marinated chicken in the pan with pitta · night, tea or hot chocolate, nuts.<br><b>Day 2:</b> breakfast, eggs with tomato, bread, Greek coffee · lunch, salad, cheese, bread, olives · dinner, pasta with sauce from home."],["The beginner's stove","• <b>Tabletop cartridge stove</b>: stable, cheap, big surface, ideal for car camping, but doesn't like the cold.<br>• <b>Small canister-top stove</b>: light, quick for water/coffee, unstable with big pans.<br>• <b>Electric kettle off a power station</b>: silent, no flame (extra).<br>Double-burner stove with a lid-windshield: coffee on one burner, eggs on the other."],["Safety rules","• Flat, stable surface, not on dry grass or pine needles.<br>• Never inside the tent, not even in the porch “because it's raining”.<br>• Windshield around the flame, <b>not closed around the canister</b>, the canister must not heat up.<br>• 1/5–31/10 in forests, fields, protected areas and within 300 m of them: stove only on private property or a campsite, with fire-risk index 1–3.<br>• In wind, the pan goes behind the car."],["The cool box without power","A good passive one holds 1–2 days in summer:<br>1. Pre-chill it the night before with an ice pack.<br>2. Frozen water bottles instead of ice packs, when they melt, you drink them.<br>3. Meat at the bottom, veg on top.<br>4. Two cool boxes in a group: food (rarely opened) and drinks.<br>5. Shade and a blanket on top, not a boot in the sun.<br>6. If in doubt, throw it out.<br>First weekend: hard cheeses, cured meats, veg, pasta, tins, not mince and mayonnaise in August."],["After the meal","Wash up in a basin, away from streams and springs, with a little biodegradable soap. Scraps into the bag, not on the ground. Rubbish and food in the car at night, the smell is an invitation to fox and sheepdog. And a portion for the latecomers: they remember the plate that was waiting for them."],["Before you leave & everyone's mistake","Menu written down with its ingredients? Second lighter and spare canister? Pre-chilled cool box? One meal with no flame at all for a fire-ban day? The mistake: they plan like it's their home kitchen and end up washing dishes at eleven by torchlight, missing the sunset."]],"q":[{"q":"Where does the stove windshield go?","a":["Around the flame, not closed around the canister","Around the whole burner and the canister","It's never needed"],"w":"A canister that heats up is a hazard."},{"q":"What do you put in a passive cool box instead of ice packs?","a":["Frozen water bottles","Hot water","Nothing, the insulation is enough"],"w":"When they melt, it's drinking water."},{"q":"It's raining: can you cook in the porch?","a":["No, never, outside under a tarp","Yes, if the zip is open","Yes, for a few minutes"],"w":"All stoves burn oxygen and give off carbon monoxide."}]},{"id":"exo_foodsmart","n":"Eating smart and on a budget","src":"Outside the Walls, Ch. 9: Cooking, food, cool box","p":[["Plan and shop","• Write the menu and buy only that, the list is the cheapest gear you'll own.<br>• Shop near home, not at the fuel station (three times the price).<br>• Street market on Friday.<br>• Own-brand for pasta, rice, tins, pulses.<br>• Bread, eggs, cheese from the last village, you support the place.<br>• Portions in zip bags with a note of how much water they need.<br>• Shared kitty in a group: one pot for six."],["Work at home","• Frozen meals (stuffed veg, bean soup, mince, burgers): ice pack on day 1, ready meal on day 2.<br>• Marinate inside the bag.<br>• Sauce or pesto in a bottle.<br>• Breakfast mix: oats, milk powder, sugar, cinnamon, raisins, just add hot water.<br>• Spices in small pots."],["Less gas, fewer dishes","• Always with a lid · windshield around the flame.<br>• <b>Soak the pasta</b> 1–2 hours in cold water, it then needs 1–2 minutes' boiling instead of ten.<br>• Boil 2 minutes, turn off, cover, wrap in a towel/fleece and it finishes by itself.<br>• Quick ingredients: couscous, bulgur, cracked wheat, red lentils (~15 minutes), orzo, egg noodles (chylopites), trahanas.<br>• Heat only the water you need; the rest goes in the thermos.<br>• Prep first, light up after.<br>• Tortilla = an edible plate · eat from the pot · wipe with bread before washing · foil that comes back home with you."],["The Greek larder","Barley rusk (dakos) · trahanas (the best dinner for a cold night) · chylopites and orzo that “drink up” the water · tinned lentils/chickpeas or red lentils · graviera, kefalotyri, cheese in oil · village sausage, pastourma, air-dried salami · olives, sun-dried tomatoes, capers · tahini with honey/grape molasses, halva · nuts from the street market · apples, oranges, bananas on the first day."],["Ten one-pot meals under €2","Pasta with tuna and tomato (10′, 2′ if soaked) · lentils and rice with red lentils (20′) · trahanas with cheese (10′) · couscous with chickpeas (just boil water) · chylopites with egg (10′) · strapatsada, eggs scrambled with tomato (10′) · potatoes with eggs (20′) · quesadilla (5′) · tinned beans with sausage (10′) · “upgraded” noodles (5′). One freeze-dried meal costs as much as five of these portions, a taverna as much as ten."],["The emergency larder","For evenings with fatigue, wind or a fire ban: noodles (400–500 ml, half the seasoning sachet) · packet soup (~250 ml) · couscous · instant mash · ready rice/quinoa · tins (can be eaten cold) · freeze-dried (~€9–15, for the emergency pack) · instant oats. Measure the water: each pack of noodles takes half a litre, and salty food needs as much again for drinking. <b>One day's ration per person:</b> one oats, one noodles, one tin of pulses/fish, one couscous, rusks, nuts, two bars, replace before it expires."],["Drinks, coffee, zero waste","• Water in 5–10 l jerrycans instead of six-packs · a filter if the trip has springs.<br>• Greek coffee in the briki (the most economical), a shaker for frappé/freddo.<br>• Thermos filled the night before = coffee with no flame in the morning.<br>• Soft drinks/beers in moderation, they take up all the cold.<br>• Cook as much as you'll eat · leftovers = breakfast · what spoils first gets eaten first · whatever wasn't opened becomes the base of the next trip."]],"q":[{"q":"How long do you soak pasta so it cooks in 1–2 minutes?","a":["1–2 hours in cold water","5 minutes in hot water","All night in milk"],"w":"Soaking saves gas and water."},{"q":"What does the “one emergency day” ration include?","a":["Oats, noodles, a tin, couscous, rusks, nuts, two bars","Only freeze-dried meals","Bread and cheese"],"w":"It stays in the kitchen box and is replaced before it expires."},{"q":"Why do you watch the water with noodles?","a":["They need half a litre and they're salty, so you need as much again to drink","Because they don't boil","Because they ruin the pot"],"w":"Every emergency meal eats into the water supply."}]},{"id":"exo_fear","n":"The psychology of the first night","src":"Outside the Walls, Ch. 10: The psychology of the first night","p":[["It's not cowardice, it's biology","In an unfamiliar place part of the brain stays on watch, the “first-night effect”, which happens in hotels too. In nature, add darkness and sounds you don't recognise; if the mind doesn't know what something is, it picks the worst. The good news: the second night is always better, the fifth almost boring."],["The sounds of the Greek night","• Rustling in dry leaves → <b>hedgehog</b>.<br>• A harrowing, almost human scream → <b>fox</b>.<br>• A monotonous “gyoon” → <b>scops owl</b>.<br>• Howling in chorus → <b>jackals</b>.<br>• Heavy footsteps, grunting, digging → <b>wild boar</b> (looking for food).<br>• Bells → a flock, so sheepdogs soon.<br>• Barking that's getting closer → sheepdog: stay calm, don't run, don't look it in the eye.<br>• Tapping on the tent → pine cones, needles, dew.<br>• A metallic “tick” → engine/exhaust cooling down.<br>• Whistling and flapping fabric → wind; if it's picking up, check the guylines."],["Wolves, bears and the real dangers","They live in the big mountains, mainly in Northern Greece, they avoid people and attacks are extremely rare; protection: food and rubbish shut in the car. The real dangers are boring: <b>weather</b> (wind, storm, cold), <b>night driving</b> on an unknown dirt track, snakes → zip closed and shake out your shoes, “nobody will know” → make sure somebody knows, fire → your own flame and cigarette."],["The evening watch","Like an officer before the night watch, every evening in the same order:<br>1. A walk round in the last light.<br>2. Message to one person: position and time of next contact.<br>3. Food and rubbish in the car, locked.<br>4. Keys, torch, phone always in the same place.<br>5. Toilet.<br>6. Shoes in the porch, upside down.<br>7. Zip closed, the watch is over."],["The three questions for every sound","Instead of switching on the torch: <b>Is it getting closer? Is it persisting? Is it changing?</b> If all three answers are “no”, it's the forest living its life. If something is approaching and persisting, then you look. Everyone's mistake: a torch at every sound, it blinds you, the darkness gets blacker and the brain wakes up even more."],["The car is plan B","The biggest sedative is knowing you can leave: nose to the exit, keys beside you, fuel. If you don't like something, you leave, it's not defeat, it's good seamanship. Intuition isn't paranoia: a car park with music or cars passing again and again → change spot <b>while it's still light</b>."],["Step by step, and the noise inside you","First night: a campsite, with company or alone. Second: private land with permission or the car somewhere you know, with a friend. Third: same kind of place, car beside you, good weather, alone. When the sounds stop, the fears and guilt become audible; you don't need to do anything, write them in the bridge log. The mountain doesn't solve problems; it shows you how small they are."]],"q":[{"q":"What are the three questions for every sound?","a":["Is it getting closer? Is it persisting? Is it changing?","What is it? Where is it? How big is it?","Can I hear it? Can I see it? Can I smell it?"],"w":"If all three are “no”, you let the sound be."},{"q":"You hear a harrowing, almost human scream. What is it usually?","a":["A fox","A wolf","A wild boar"],"w":"The fox has the scariest voice for such a small animal."},{"q":"A sheepdog approaches barking. What do you do?","a":["Stay calm, don't run, don't look it in the eye","Run to the car","Stare it in the eye to scare it"],"w":"It's guarding its flock; you're the stranger."}]},{"id":"exo_carcamp","n":"Camping with a car","src":"Outside the Walls, Ch. 11: Camping with a car","p":[["The car as base","Sleeping in a legally parked car isn't camping in itself; an awning, table, chairs and a kitchen outside can bring a €300 fine. The car is storeroom, windbreak, lightning shelter, power source and plan B. <b>The car you have today is enough.</b>"],["Which vehicle stops where","• <b>Small city car</b>: tarmac, good dirt tracks, campsites · stops at mud, rocks, steep climbs.<br>• <b>SUV without low range</b>: better on dirt tracks, one person sleeps inside · stops at deep mud, mountain trails.<br>• <b>4×4 with low range</b>: almost everywhere · stops at the limits of the driver's head.<br>• <b>Van/camper</b>: space, sleep in any weather · narrow mountain roads, dirt after rain."],["Loading: ship's stowage","In a hard stop a loose water container becomes a missile.<br>1. Heavy items <b>low and forward</b>, close to the back of the rear seats: water, cool box, tools.<br>2. Everything strapped down or netted, not “wedged in”.<br>3. Boxes by use: kitchen, sleep, tools, hygiene.<br>4. Last in, first out: tent and torch on top.<br>5. Emergency bag <b>in the cab</b>: first-aid kit, torch, water, powerbank, cardigan."],["Sleeping inside","• Park with your head end higher; levelling ramps or two bits of wood.<br>• Mat and sleeping bag as in a tent, metal and glass get cold fast.<br>• Flat bed: folded seats with foam or a platform with drawers; length = your height + a few cm.<br>• Two windows slightly open, diagonally, even in winter.<br>• Sock-type mesh on the doors, dark curtains or reflective screens, in winter, window insulation makes the biggest difference.<br>• Keys in the same place, nose to the exit.<br>• <b>Never leave the engine running</b> for heat or air-con while you sleep."],["Dirt tracks for beginners","1. Anything you can't see, get out and walk it first (mud, water, rocks).<br>2. Turn back before it turns you back, if you can't turn around, think about a kilometre in reverse.<br>3. Never alone on anything difficult: two cars are a plan, one is a hope.<br>4. Slow, steady, no wheelspin.<br>5. After heavy rain it's a different road.<br>6. Tank above half.<br>At the start the best upgrade: good tyres and a checked spare."],["The four things that always stay in the car","• <b>12V compressor</b> for a tyre losing air.<br>• <b>Jump starter</b> for the day you forget the cabin light.<br>• <b>Shovel</b> for mud, sand and the toilet.<br>• <b>Tow strap and gloves</b>.<br>Before you leave: have you slept one night inside in the yard? Everything strapped down? Spare, oil, tyres checked? The mistake: loading to the roof in no order and spending half the trip searching."]],"q":[{"q":"Where do the heavy items go (water, cool box, tools)?","a":["Low and forward, close to the back of the rear seats","On the roof","Right at the back of the boot, loose"],"w":"Low centre of gravity and less risk of a missile under braking."},{"q":"Where do you keep the emergency bag?","a":["In the cab","Under everything in the boot","On the roof rack"],"w":"You must be able to reach it immediately, without unloading."},{"q":"At a difficult spot on your own, what does the rule say?","a":["Two cars are a plan, one is a hope, you don't go in alone","Go in fast for momentum","Air down and carry on"],"w":"Without a second vehicle there's no recovery."}]},{"id":"exo_findspot","n":"Finding a wild spot: mountain and beach","src":"Outside the Walls, Ch. 12: Finding a wild spot","p":[["The rule","A tent on a beach, in a forest or on public land is prohibited (€300 and possible prosecution); the decision and the responsibility are yours. Luck isn't a method. The right spot: <b>nobody sees you, but you can be found if needed</b>, isolated, not cut off."],["Before you leave: the spot on the map","1. <b>Satellite image</b>: clearings, flat ground, tree shade, and what's around (houses, pens, fields).<br>2. <b>Topo map</b>: contour lines show streams and where cold air collects.<br>3. <b>Access</b>: for your own car, and tomorrow, if it rains?<br>4. <b>Phone signal</b>: essential for a beginner.<br>5. <b>Spot B</b>: a second one nearby and the nearest campsite."],["The harbour check","The seven pitching questions all apply, plus three:<br>• <b>Does it belong to someone?</b> Fence, gate, crops, beehives = property. Ask or leave.<br>• <b>Can I leave at three in the morning?</b> In rain, in the dark, without manoeuvring.<br>• <b>Will I leave it as I found it?</b> If it'll leave a mark, it's not the right spot."],["Mountain","• A flat shelf, not a hollow (cold, water), not a summit (wind, lightning).<br>• Away from animal pens and flocks.<br>• The <b>village kafeneio</b> is the best information office: where there's water, which road is cut, whose field it is, sometimes even permission to sleep.<br>• Firs and pines in summer = forest = no flame, not even for coffee."],["Beach","• It's foreshore, the most frequently patrolled place, the Coast Guard gives no discount because it was deserted.<br>• Sand pegs or buried bags, zips closed, car as windbreak.<br>• Rinse zips and poles with fresh water at home, salt is a silent enemy.<br>• Distance from the waves: above the line of dried seaweed.<br>• Night visitors (groups, music, cars at two): if you see it building before dark, move."],["The legal wild spots","A friend's or relative's field · a farm or winery that welcomes visitors · the herder from the kafeneio who said “come to my meadow”. They aren't on maps; they're found through a chat. Everyone's mistake: they find the spot on Instagram, so do two hundred others, and the Coast Guard."]],"q":[{"q":"You see beehives and a fence at the spot. What does it mean?","a":["It's private property, ask or leave","It's public land","It's abandoned"],"w":"Fence, gate, crops, beehives point to an owner."},{"q":"Why not a summit in the mountains?","a":["It catches wind and lightning","It has a lot of pooled cold air","It has no view"],"w":"A hollow collects cold and water; a summit, wind and lightning, choose a flat shelf."},{"q":"What's the best “information office” for a spot?","a":["The village kafeneio","Instagram comments","The town police"],"w":"Locals know the water, the roads and who owns what."}]},{"id":"exo_stealth","n":"Stealth camping: you sleep, you don't camp","src":"Outside the Walls, Ch. 12: Finding a wild spot","p":[["The logic","In Greece sleeping isn't prohibited; what's prohibited is <b>looking like you've set up camp</b>. The law talks about installing tents, caravans and temporary structures, it's forbidden to set up, not to get sleepy."],["How the law reads you","• Tent on the ground → camping.<br>• Open rooftop tent → very probably camping (grey zone).<br>• Awning, table, chairs outside → possible “installation”.<br>• Lying inside a legally parked car → simply parked.<br>Same need, same sleep, completely different legal treatment."],["The big trap: the rooftop tent","Hard or soft, sleek, expensive, it doesn't matter. The moment it opens and goes up it <b>declares an intention to stay</b>. It isn't stealth; it's a neon sign inviting a check."],["The rules","1. Arrive late, leave early: park, lights off, doors shut, sleep.<br>2. Don't open or put out anything, no awning, no chair, no kitchen.<br>3. Don't light up like a harbour: red light and curtains.<br>4. No second night in the same spot, the second night is a stay.<br>5. Leave no trace, visual or ecological.<br>6. Don't give a lecture; if a police officer or owner asks, answer politely and leave if asked to."],["“Legally parked” matters","Not in a private field without permission, not where there's a no-parking sign, not on the sand, not in a forest closed to traffic on high-risk days. Municipalities may have their own parking rules. Real freedom: leaving in the morning without anyone realising you were there."],["Before you leave","Do you know the legal position for the spot? Do you have a plan B <b>and C</b>? Does someone know your position? The best spot isn't the one with the finest view; it's the one where you slept in peace."]],"q":[{"q":"Why isn't a rooftop tent stealth?","a":["Once open it declares an intention to stay","It's too noisy","It doesn't fit two"],"w":"As soon as it goes up, it looks like an installation."},{"q":"Can you stay a second night at the same stealth spot?","a":["No, the second night is a stay","Yes, if nobody sees you","Yes, if it's the weekend"],"w":"Rule 4 of stealth camping."},{"q":"The owner asks what you're doing there. What do you do?","a":["Answer politely and leave if asked","Explain the law to him in detail","Ignore him and sleep"],"w":"No lectures; show respect and move on."}]},{"id":"exo_waterpower","n":"Water and power when wild camping","src":"Outside the Walls, Ch. 13: Water, power, hygiene","p":[["The ship's reckoning","Before every trip: how much water, how much fuel, how many days, how much margin. Nobody sails on “we'll find something”. Burning rubbish is prohibited all year round; a power station isn't burning, it's the legal answer for hot water in summer."],["Water: how much and where","• Summer: at least <b>4 l/person/day</b> for drinking and cooking + <b>2 l</b> for washing, plus <b>one extra day</b>. Formula: people × days × 6 l + one day.<br>• 10 or 20 l containers with a tap, strapped down low, one drinking, one for everything else.<br>• Refill at village taps, square fountains, fuel stations, ask whether it's drinkable.<br>• The mistake: counting only drinking water and forgetting coffee, cooking, dishes, teeth."],["Water from a spring or stream","However clean it looks, you don't know what's upstream (a flock, a dead animal).<br>• <b>Boiling</b>: bacteria, viruses, parasites, 1 minute at a rolling boil, 3 above ~2,000 m.<br>• <b>0.1–0.2 µm filter</b>: bacteria, parasites, usually not viruses.<br>• <b>Tablets</b>: bacteria and viruses, they need time and leave a taste.<br>For a beginner: bring all your drinking water. The filter is for later."],["Power: what do you need running?","• 20,000 mAh powerbank: 3–4 phone charges, torches.<br>• Car charger: while you drive.<br>• ~500 Wh power station: a compressor fridge for one summer day or lots of briki coffees.<br>• 100 W solar panel: ~300–500 Wh a day in Greek summer, less in winter.<br><b>Golden rule:</b> never drain the starter battery, fridge, lights, chargers from another source."],["Power station with alternator charger or classic 12V","<b>Power station + alternator charger</b> (e.g. EcoFlow 800 W): mainly 230V (hob, kettle, blankets) · up to 800 W ≈ 1 kWh in a little over an hour of driving · few connections, certified · fewer failure points · needs brand-specific spares · for comfort without an electrical experiment.<br><b>Classic 12V</b> (second battery, DC-DC 30/60 A, inverter): mainly 12V (fridge, lights, pump, diesel heater) · fuses, relays, busbars, thick cables · only as good as the auto electrician · spares everywhere · for a permanent multi-day installation."],["What doesn't change with the brand","At 1,500–2,000 W from 12V the amps climb so high the cables “sweat”, many campers burn from bad installation, not from off-roading. A 1–2 kWh battery is enough for coffees, a little cooking and electric blankets, <b>not for electric heating all night</b>, that's why you want diesel. A real figure: a night at 7°C in Evia with two electric blankets in a rooftop tent = 25% of a power station."]],"q":[{"q":"How much water per person per day do you allow in summer?","a":["6 litres (4 drinking/cooking + 2 washing), plus one day","2 litres","15 litres"],"w":"Formula: people × days × 6 l + one day's margin."},{"q":"What does a 0.1–0.2 µm filter usually not remove?","a":["Viruses","Bacteria","Parasites"],"w":"For viruses you need boiling or tablets."},{"q":"What is the golden rule of power?","a":["Never drain the starter battery","Always a 2,000 W inverter","Charge only from the sun"],"w":"The worst moment is the “click-click” of the starter on a cold morning."}]},{"id":"exo_hygiene","n":"Hygiene and a toilet without a toilet","src":"Outside the Walls, Ch. 13: Water, power, hygiene","p":[["Hands first","Sanitiser before eating and after the toilet. Most ruined trips aren't ruined by the weather; they're ruined by <b>the stomach</b>."],["Shower and soap","Solar shower: black 20 l bag, a few hours in the sun, hot water in the afternoon. Wash yourself and the dishes <b>at least 60 m</b> from springs and streams, with a little biodegradable soap. Never in the spring itself. Wet wipes go in the rubbish bag, they don't break down in the soil, whatever the packet says."],["The toilet step by step","1. Trowel, toilet paper, a sealable bag, sanitiser.<br>2. At least 60 m from water, paths and your pitch.<br>3. A hole 15–20 cm deep.<br>4. The paper <b>in the bag</b>, not in the hole.<br>5. Cover, tread lightly, leave the spot as if nobody had been there."],["For groups and children","A folding camping toilet with bags solves a lot, and everything leaves with you. A chemical toilet is emptied only at a proper point (campsite, motorhome service point), never in nature."],["Before you leave","Water: people × days × 6 l plus one day? Everything charged and the starter battery left alone? Trowel, paper, bags, sanitiser? Self-sufficiency isn't needing nothing; it's having measured what you need."]],"q":[{"q":"How deep is the toilet hole?","a":["15–20 cm","2–3 cm","50 cm"],"w":"Deep enough to decompose, 60 m from water and paths."},{"q":"Where does the toilet paper go?","a":["In the bag that leaves with you","In the hole","You burn it"],"w":"Paper poking out of the soil is a camper's saddest signature; and burning is prohibited."},{"q":"According to the book, what ruins most trips?","a":["The stomach, hence sanitiser on your hands","The wind","The battery"],"w":"Hand hygiene before eating and after the toilet."}]},{"id":"exo_fire","n":"Fire: the finest and most dangerous habit","src":"Outside the Walls, Ch. 14: Fire","p":[["The rule","In Greece fire isn't a private matter; it's a public one. From 1/5 to 31/10 in forests, fields, protected areas and within 300 m of them, fire is prohibited at any risk index, with fines up to €50,000, and at index 4–5 imprisonment of at least one year. <b>If you're not sure it's allowed, it isn't. If you're sure but it's windy, still no.</b>"],["The circle without a flame","What we want from a fire is light, warmth and a centre:<br>• <b>Light</b>: a warm, low LED lantern in the middle, white light kills conversation.<br>• <b>Warmth</b>: a blanket over your legs, a thermos of tea from home or made on electric power.<br>• <b>Centre</b>: the table, the sky, without a fire you see more stars."],["When fire is allowed","At a campsite with a proper fire pit, on private land with permission and a low risk index, or outside the fire season with no local ban. If you don't know, ask the local Fire Service. Before you light it:<br>1. A metal fire bowl or an existing fire pit, not a new ring of stones.<br>2. <b>3 m</b> of clear ground, no dry grass, pine needles or branches overhead.<br>3. Water (a bucket, not a bottle) and a shovel beside you before the match.<br>4. Wind <b>below 4 Bf</b>.<br>5. Small, the size of a frying pan.<br>6. Never without someone beside it."],["How to put it out","Embers hold heat for hours; buried ones flare up again the next day.<br>1. Drown it with <b>water, not soil</b>.<br>2. Stir with the shovel.<br>3. Pour water again.<br>4. Test with the back of your hand above the ash, without touching.<br>5. Repeat until it's cold.<br>The mistake: burying it with soil, soil doesn't put fire out, it hides it."],["If you see smoke","A wildfire with wind runs faster uphill than a person.<br>1. Call <b>199 (fire service) or 112</b> immediately: where you are, what you see, which way the smoke is going.<br>2. Leave early, don't wait for the 112 alert message; things can be left behind.<br>3. Drive away from the smoke on a <b>road you know</b>, not an unknown dirt track.<br>4. That's why the car's nose points to the exit."],["Before you leave","Fire-risk map for today and tomorrow? A plan for hot food without a flame? Do you know which road you'd leave by? The finest fire is the one you remember without anyone else remembering it."]],"q":[{"q":"What do you put a fire out with?","a":["Water, stirring and pouring again","Soil on top","You let it go out by itself"],"w":"Soil hides the embers, it doesn't put them out."},{"q":"Up to what wind force may you light a fire (where it's legal)?","a":["Below 4 Bf","Up to 6 Bf","Regardless of the wind"],"w":"A spark flies further than you think."},{"q":"You see smoke: which way do you drive?","a":["Away from the smoke, on a road you know","Onto the first unknown dirt track","Towards the smoke to have a look"],"w":"An unknown road may be a dead end."}]},{"id":"exo_winter","n":"Winter camping: system and sequence","src":"Outside the Walls, Ch. 15: Winter camping","p":[["When you're ready","The ban on camping outside campsites applies in winter too; a gas stove is allowed outside the fire season, unless the authorities extend it. The right sequence:<br>1. At least three nights in spring/autumn with cold nights.<br>2. First winter night at a campsite that stays open or in the car at low altitude.<br>3. Then, with company, the mountain."],["The sleep system in the cold","• Mat <b>R 4+</b> or two mats (foam below, inflatable on top).<br>• Sleeping bag comfort rating at least 5°C below the expected minimum; for serious mountains −10 or lower.<br>• Liner and a blanket on top · beanie, sleeping socks, hot-water bottle.<br><b>You don't heat the space, you heat yourself</b>, the secret is keeping the sleeping bag warm. The mat is the underrated hero: the right mat is worth more than ten blankets. An electric blanket is a supplement for pre-warming, not the solution."],["Depending on where you sleep","• <b>Ground tent</b>: hard mode, cold from below, wind, damp → a serious mat and a winter tent.<br>• <b>Rooftop tent</b>: cold from everywhere → insulation liner and a good sleeping bag.<br>• <b>Car</b>: less wind → window insulation (silver, foam, bought or home-made); with a good sleeping bag and mat you sleep decently with no heating, even below zero.<br>Characters: the “Spartan” (zero power, needs discipline) · the best-value investment (sleeping bag and mat) · the most comfortable (diesel heater) · the most wrong (“we'll tough it out” with two blankets)."],["Winter specifics","• Dark early → arrive two hours before sunset with warm clothes to hand.<br>• 14 hours of darkness → book, company, lantern, early night.<br>• Condensation and ice → ventilation, microfibre cloth.<br>• Water freezes → bottles inside, upside down.<br>• Stove won't light → propane mix, canister in the sleeping bag.<br>• Batteries “die” → inside the sleeping bag.<br>• Snow on the tent → knock it off from inside; a three-season tent can't take heavy snow."],["The car in winter","• Snow chains tested, the first fitting in the garage, not in the snow.<br>• Full tank: if you get stuck, the heater is a lifeline.<br>• Battery and antifreeze checked.<br>• Park where you can get out even with snow, a dry downhill may not go back up when snowy."],["What the experienced really take","1. <b>Warmth</b>: sleeping bag, mat, blanket, thermals.<br>2. <b>Sleep</b>: they hunt for the perfect mat, not the perfect tent.<br>3. <b>Quick food</b>: stove, coffee, eggs, tins, pasta, noodles, nuts.<br>4. <b>Water</b>: ~10–15 l for two people on a winter weekend.<br>5. <b>A car that gets you out</b>: shovel, strap, traction boards, compressor, a good spare.<br>Everything else is the joy of gear. The mistake: sweating while pitching in a down jacket, pitch a little cold, put it on as soon as you stop."]],"q":[{"q":"What mat do you want in winter?","a":["R 4 and above, or two mats","R 1","An inflatable beach lilo"],"w":"Most of the cold comes from below."},{"q":"How much water do the experienced allow for two people on a winter weekend?","a":["About 10–15 litres","2 litres","50 litres"],"w":"Coffee, cooking, washing, drinking; much more in summer."},{"q":"Where do you spend your first winter night?","a":["At an open campsite or in the car at low altitude","Alone on a snowy summit","In a hammock in the forest"],"w":"The mountain comes later, with company."}]},{"id":"exo_heater","n":"Heating, carbon monoxide, hypothermia","src":"Outside the Walls, Ch. 15: Winter camping","p":[["The rule that saves lives","No combustion inside the tent or a closed car: no stove “for a minute”, no LPG heater, no engine running. Carbon monoxide <b>has no smell and can't be seen</b>. In winter the temptation is big, it's the one solution that isn't allowed. The only exception: a <b>diesel heater</b>, because it burns in a sealed chamber, the exhaust goes out through a pipe and only hot air comes inside."],["The test: battery or diesel","Pavliani and Vardousia, two systems: a battery portable air-conditioner/heater (EcoFlow Wave 3, ~€700 / ~€1,500 with battery) and a portable diesel heater in a box (Vevor 8 kW, ~€150–180). At ~5°C the battery unit heated quietly, but lasted <b>~3 hours</b> (4–5 in Eco). Below 5°C, in their test, <b>it didn't work</b>. The diesel one heated even below zero, so much that they often switched it off, it asked only for fuel and a little power."],["Comparison","• Cost: ~€700/1,500 vs ~€150–180.<br>• Run time: 3–5 hours vs as long as there's fuel.<br>• Below 5°C: didn't work vs heated even below zero.<br>• Ideal for: summer cooling, mild cold, campsites with a socket vs a winter mountain with no power.<br>The winner is whatever works when everything else stops."],["The conditions for a diesel heater","• Combustion and exhaust <b>outside</b>: the box stands outside the tent/car, exhaust away from doors and windows; only the hot-air hose comes inside (e.g. through a window panel).<br>• A <b>battery carbon monoxide detector</b> inside the sleeping area, always, cheap units vary a lot in hoses and connections.<br>• The <b>lowest setting</b>: 8 kW is a lot for a tent.<br>• A permanent one needs professional fitting and a second battery."],["Hypothermia","Shivering that won't stop, clumsiness, confusion. If the shivering <b>stops while it's still cold</b>, that's worse, not better. Dry clothes, a hot sweet drink, the car heater, 112."],["Frostbite","Fingers, ears, nose turning white and numb. Rewarm them <b>slowly</b>, with body heat or lukewarm water, <b>no rubbing</b>, never with fire or hot water. If it doesn't improve, 112. Before you leave: minimum temperature at that altitude and snow forecast, chains, full tank, battery checked, and somebody knows where you are."]],"q":[{"q":"Why is a diesel heater the only acceptable “combustion” near where you sleep?","a":["It burns in a sealed chamber and the exhaust goes outside","Because it doesn't burn oxygen","Because it's electric"],"w":"Only hot air comes inside, and still with a CO detector."},{"q":"The shivering has stopped but it's still cold. What does that mean?","a":["Worsening hypothermia, act immediately","They've warmed up","They're just tired"],"w":"The body stops reacting; it's a sign of deterioration."},{"q":"How do you rewarm frostbitten fingers?","a":["Slowly, with body heat or lukewarm water, no rubbing","Rub them hard with snow","Hold them near the fire"],"w":"Rubbing and strong heat destroy the tissue."}]},{"id":"exo_group","n":"With a group: building a village for one night","src":"Outside the Walls, Ch. 16: With groups and children","p":[["The law for groups","The fine is €300 <b>per person, tent or vehicle</b>, a group with many tents doesn't pay one fine. For big groups and families, a campsite or private land with permission is almost always the right choice. A group multiplies the fun, but also whatever goes wrong, the difference is the plan."],["The group list","One shared list on the phone with <b>a name next to every item</b>, otherwise three stoves and no lighter.<br>• Spot and booking: the most experienced.<br>• Kitchen and menu: one person with a helper.<br>• Water: each car its own.<br>• First aid: one big shared kit and a small one in every car.<br>• Lights, chairs, table: shared out.<br>• Rubbish and taking it home: one person, by name."],["Setting up a village","• <b>One</b> person decides the layout; five deciding together set up the kitchen in the dark.<br>• Kitchen in the middle, tents in a circle, cars as windbreaks.<br>• Keep walkways clear: guylines mustn't cross the path to the toilet."],["Who sleeps where","1. The tired one on the good mat.<br>2. Children close to the adults.<br>3. The champion snorer at the edge, door facing the other way.<br>4. Latecomers close to the cars.<br>5. The hammock goes to the volunteer."],["The latecomers","Position and a photo of the last junction, sent before the signal goes. A light left on where they'll park. A bed already set up and a plate in the cool box, that's family in the mountains."],["The striking-camp rule","Share out the jobs <b>in the evening, not the morning</b>: each family its own tent and sleeping bags, one person the kitchen, one the table and chairs, everyone together on the final rubbish sweep. The mistake: thinking more people need less planning, since “someone will bring it”, usually nobody does."]],"q":[{"q":"How does the group organise who brings what?","a":["A shared list with a name next to every item","Everyone brings whatever they like","Everyone shops on the way"],"w":"Otherwise three stoves and no lighter."},{"q":"When do you share out the striking-camp jobs?","a":["The evening before","The morning of departure","No need to share them out"],"w":"That way nobody “disappears for a bit of water”."},{"q":"Where does the kitchen go in a group camp?","a":["In the middle, with the tents in a circle around it","Inside a tent","At the edge next to the road"],"w":"The cars become windbreaks around the “village”."}]},{"id":"exo_kids","n":"With children and family","src":"Outside the Walls, Ch. 16: With groups and children","p":[["To get the child “hooked”","• <b>Their own head torch</b> and <b>a whistle round their neck</b> with one rule: blow it only if lost.<br>• <b>Visible limits</b>: “as far as that tree”.<br>• <b>A role</b>: the pegs, the lantern, the list, children love whatever they help to build."],["Night, comfort, safety","• A plan for the toilet at night: who they wake, where they go, with what light; a folding toilet next to the tent for the little ones.<br>• The favourite pillow or soft toy, the home that fits in their hands.<br>• Short first trips, good weather, warm sleep, one cold, wet first night can close the subject for years.<br>• Hat, sunscreen, water: children dehydrate and get cold faster, and say so later."],["When there's no room in the house","Easter in Theopetra: a house for eight, and there were nine, but the author already had his room on top of the car. Most Greeks don't not want it; they've just never seen it. And when they do, something wakes up."],["Take your home with you","At a family celebration, village festival or wedding with the hotels full: don't expect to squeeze into other people's houses. A cousin's garden or grandma's yard is <b>private land with permission</b>, the safest first step for camping with children."],["Before you leave","Is there a shared list with names? Does every child know the limits and have a torch and whistle? Are you at a campsite or on private land with permission? You won't remember whether you slept in a swag or a rooftop tent; you'll remember you were there with your people."]],"q":[{"q":"When does the child blow the whistle?","a":["Only if lost","Whenever they want to play","In the morning to wake everyone"],"w":"One clear rule makes it a safety tool."},{"q":"Why should the first trips with children be short and in good weather?","a":["One cold, wet first night can close the subject for years","Because the law requires it","So they cost less"],"w":"The first experience decides whether they'll want to go again."},{"q":"What's the safest first step for camping with children?","a":["A relative's yard, private land with permission","A free beach","A remote mountain clearing"],"w":"Legal, familiar, with a house next door."}]},{"id":"exo_planb","n":"Dangers and plan B: the voyage plan","src":"Outside the Walls, Ch. 17: Dangers and plan B","p":[["The plan in one message","Before you leave, to someone staying behind:<br>1. Where you're going, with position.<br>2. Who you are and what car (make, colour, plate).<br>3. When you're back.<br>4. When to worry (“if I haven't messaged you by 12 on Sunday”).<br>5. What to do then: 112 with all of the above.<br>Five lines that turn “nobody knows” into “somebody knows and knows what to do”."],["Water, lightning, wind, heat, cold","• <b>Rising stream</b>: never in a stream bed, forecast for the surrounding mountains → get high immediately, never cross running water, not even by car.<br>• <b>Lightning</b>: not summits/lone trees → inside the closed car.<br>• <b>Wind and branches</b>: look up → move, or sleep in the car.<br>• <b>Heatstroke</b>: shade, water, hat, no midday sun → shade, cooling, 112 if confused.<br>• <b>Hypothermia</b> → dry clothes, hot drink, car heater, 112.<br>• <b>Carbon monoxide</b>: no combustion in enclosed spaces → out into fresh air immediately, 112."],["Animals, accidents, getting lost","• <b>Viper</b>: closed shoes, torch, hands out of holes → calm, immobilise, remove rings, 112; no tourniquet, cutting or sucking.<br>• <b>Scorpion, bee, wasp</b>: shake out shoes/clothes → cold compress, watch; breathing trouble or facial swelling = 112.<br>• <b>Tick</b>: long clothes, body check → tweezers close to the skin; doctor if a red ring or fever.<br>• <b>Sheepdogs</b> → stop, don't run, don't look them in the eye, move away slowly.<br>• <b>Accident on a dirt track</b>: low speed, no night driving on unknown tracks → warning triangle, first aid, 112.<br>• <b>Lost</b>: offline maps → stop, go back the way you came, send your position."],["The numbers","<b>112</b> for everything · <b>166</b> EKAB (ambulance) · <b>199</b> Fire Service · <b>100</b> Police · <b>108</b> Coast Guard. Write them on paper inside the first-aid kit too, the phone may be dead."],["Before you leave & everyone's mistake","Did you send the five-line message? Did you download offline maps? Is the first-aid kit complete and somewhere everyone knows? The mistake: “we'll go somewhere around Zagori”. “Somewhere” isn't a plan; it's a search operation you don't want to need."]],"q":[{"q":"What does the five-line message contain?","a":["Where, who/which car, when you're back, when to worry, what to do","Just “off now, see you”","The gear list"],"w":"That way somebody knows where you are and what to do."},{"q":"With a viper bite, what do you NOT do?","a":["Tourniquet, cutting or sucking","Immobilise the limb","Remove rings"],"w":"Calm, immobilise, remove rings, call for help."},{"q":"What is the Coast Guard's number?","a":["108","199","166"],"w":"199 Fire Service, 166 EKAB (ambulance), 100 Police, 112 for everything."}]},{"id":"exo_firstaid","n":"The camper's first-aid kit","src":"Outside the Walls, Ch. 17: Dangers and plan B","p":[["The basics","Bandages and gauze, antiseptic, tweezers, scissors, painkillers, antihistamine, burn cream, elastic bandage, foil blanket, personal medication for <b>one extra day</b>, and a sheet with allergies and phone numbers for each person. One waterproof case in the car and a small one for the rucksack."],["By job (1)","• <b>Wounds</b>: sterile gauze, plasters in various sizes, medical tape, saline in ampoules, antiseptic (povidone-iodine or chlorhexidine), wound-closure strips.<br>• <b>Sprains</b>: elastic bandage in two widths, triangular bandage, SAM-type splint, instant ice pack.<br>• <b>Burns/skin</b>: burn gel or burn dressings, blister plasters, sunscreen, after-sun."],["By job (2)","• <b>Stings/allergies</b>: antihistamine tablets and cream, low-strength hydrocortisone, after-bite stick, insect repellent, tweezers or tick hook.<br>• <b>Pain/fever</b>: painkillers, fever reducers, thermometer.<br>• <b>Stomach</b>: anti-diarrhoeals, antispasmodics, travel-sickness tablets for the dirt tracks, rehydration sachets.<br>• <b>Tools</b>: disposable gloves, clothing-cutting shears, pointed tweezers, CPR mask, torch.<br>• <b>Cold/emergency</b>: a foil blanket per person, whistle."],["What still holds from the old lists","• <b>Venom suction pump</b>: not any more. For a snakebite: calm, immobilise, remove rings/watch, 166, no cutting, ice or tight binding.<br>• <b>Mercurochrome</b>: replaced by povidone-iodine or chlorhexidine.<br>• <b>Cervical collar</b>: if you suspect a neck injury, don't move them, keep the head still and call 112.<br>• <b>Injectable cortisone/adrenaline</b>: prescription only; anyone with a severe allergy keeps their medication and pen somewhere everyone knows."],["Two rules and the inspection","Show the list to the pharmacist and adapt it, especially for children. A <b>first-aid course</b> (Hellenic Red Cross and others) is worth more than any kit, the danger table doesn't replace it. Every spring, open the kit and check the expiry dates."]],"q":[{"q":"What replaced mercurochrome?","a":["Povidone-iodine or chlorhexidine","Surgical spirit","Hydrogen peroxide"],"w":"These are the modern antiseptics the book recommends."},{"q":"When do you check the first-aid kit's expiry dates?","a":["Every spring","Only when you need something","Every five years"],"w":"You don't want to find out on the mountain that the antihistamine has expired."},{"q":"Suspected neck injury. What do you do?","a":["Don't move them, keep the head still, 112","Fit a collar and carry them","Get them to walk"],"w":"A collar needs training so it doesn't do harm."}]},{"id":"exo_ethos","n":"The visitor's ethos in the Greek countryside","src":"Outside the Walls, Ch. 18: Leave no trace","p":[["How you judge a camper","You don't look at their gear; you look at the spot <b>an hour after they've left</b>. If you can't tell they were there, they were a good camper. The movement calls it Leave No Trace; in Greece we simply called it <b>shame</b>."],["The seven principles, Greek style","1. Plan: law, weather, fire-risk index, water, bags.<br>2. Durable ground: on already-used spots, not dunes, flower meadows, sown fields, a dirt track is a road, a meadow isn't.<br>3. Whatever you brought, take back, cherry stones and orange peel too.<br>4. Leave what you find: flowers, stones, shells; antiquities you don't even touch.<br>5. Fire: in Greece it usually means “no fire”.<br>6. Animals: don't feed wild boar and foxes, don't approach flocks.<br>7. Others: quiet, distance, good morning."],["The local people","• Gates and fences as you found them, an open gate costs the shepherd a day of searching.<br>• Village water in moderation; on the islands it's scarce in summer.<br>• Buy from the grocer and sit in the kafeneio, that's how you become a visitor, not an unwelcome guest.<br>• Say good morning to the shepherd: he has solved more than all the laws."],["The cleaner-spot rule","You don't just leave no trace; you leave the place <b>cleaner</b>. One extra bag for other people's rubbish and ten minutes before you leave, the cheapest way to thank a place."],["Before you leave the spot","Rubbish, bottle caps, cigarette butts, pegs all in? Gate or fence as you found them? If you had a fire where it was allowed, is it cold to the hand? The mistake: a tied bag left next to the full bin “for the council”. The council doesn't come; the foxes do."]],"q":[{"q":"What do you do with orange peel and fruit stones?","a":["Take them back with you","Throw them on the ground, they're biodegradable","Bury them under a stone"],"w":"They don't break down before the next visitor arrives."},{"q":"You found a pen gate closed. What do you do once through?","a":["Leave it as you found it, closed","Leave it open for the next person","Tie it with wire"],"w":"An open gate can cost the shepherd his animals."},{"q":"The full bin won't take your bag. What do you do?","a":["Take it with you","Leave it tied next to the bin","Hang it in a tree"],"w":"The council doesn't come; the foxes do."}]},{"id":"exo_overland","n":"From camping to overlanding","src":"Outside the Walls, Ch. 19: From camping to overlanding","p":[["What overlanding is","A multi-day journey by vehicle, where the road is the destination and the vehicle is home. You don't go somewhere to camp; you camp so you can keep going."],["The order of upgrades","1. <b>Tyres</b>: good all-terrains make the biggest difference.<br>2. <b>Off-road driving training</b>: a one-day course is worth more than a diff locker.<br>3. <b>Recovery gear</b>: strap, shovel, traction boards, compressor.<br>4. <b>Power and fridge</b>, once trips become multi-day.<br>5. <b>Rooftop tent or swag</b>, when pitching eats time from the road.<br>6. Anything else only once you've needed it <b>three times</b> and didn't have it."],["Pressure, lockers, winch","Tyre pressure, lockers and low range are learned through training and from old hands, not from a book. The pressure that gets you out of mud is dangerous on tarmac, if you air down, <b>air back up before the road</b>. On the winch: rope to a solid anchor point, helper at a safe distance from the line."],["The multi-day trip","• Fuel: fill up at the first fuel station you see once you're below half a tank.<br>• Water: refill at every village with a tap.<br>• Food: resupply every two days at small shops.<br>• Rest day: one in every three to four, with no driving.<br>• Fatigue: stop before your attention does.<br>• Plan: the five-line message and an update every evening."],["Lessons from a convoy in North Evia","Land Cruiser with 4 people, 2 dogs, a hard shell ~90 kg: the GPS sent them to the Arkitsa ferry instead of the Chalkida bridge, arrival 02:30. On a ~30% slope with mud and 50/50 tyres, almost three tonnes = an extra tonne of inertia; the lockers helped, but it only got out at the first tarmac.<br>1. Leave at the time you said.<br>2. Read the route, ferry and time of the last sailing.<br>3. Weight changes the car on a muddy climb.<br>4. Know when to leave the convoy, it's a seaman's judgement.<br>5. Each car keeps the next one in its mirror.<br>6. The guides are the local 4x4 club."],["The real cost","“As an accountant, no; as a person, I wouldn't change a single kilometre.” You don't remember what the winch cost; you remember the stranger you pulled out and the grandfather who bought you a coffee. The expensive part isn't the tyres; it's getting hooked on a life that can't be bought."]],"q":[{"q":"According to the book, what is the first upgrade?","a":["All-terrain tyres","Rooftop tent","Compressor fridge"],"w":"They make more difference than any other change."},{"q":"How often a rest day on a multi-day trip?","a":["One in every three to four","Never","Every other day"],"w":"No driving, so your attention doesn't give out."},{"q":"You aired down for mud. What do you do before the tarmac?","a":["Air back up","Nothing, carry on","Air down even more"],"w":"Low pressure is dangerous on tarmac."}]}],"coverage":[["Prologue","The first night at Pavliani / what went right and wrong"],["Appendices","A. Checklists (base, campsite, wild camping, summer, winter, kitchen, bathroom, emergency pantry, budget, clothes, food, first-aid kit)"],["Appendices","B. The three-trip programme / C. The bridge log"],["Appendices","D. Glossary / E. Emergency phone numbers"],["Ch. 1, Why go outside","What you gain / What it isn't / How to read this book / Before you go / The mistake everyone makes"],["Ch. 2, Campsite, car camping, wild camping","The three steps / The law on camping / Where it is banned / What counts as camping & penalties / Sleeping in the car: the grey zone / Private land / Caravans / Municipalities"],["Ch. 2, Campsite, car camping, wild camping","Fire and gas stoves: the fire season / Fire risk index / Penalties / What actually stays legal / Before you go / The mistake / Sources"],["Ch. 3, Where you sleep","The rule that matters more than the tent / The options at a glance / Bungalow and caravan / Ground tent / Sleeping inside the car / The recommendation for beginners / Before you go / The mistake"],["Ch. 3, Where you sleep","Swag / Hammock / Rooftop tent / Tarp and bivy / Four ways, four characters"],["Ch. 4, Equipment","The rule / Three levels / The essentials / The useful / The extras / The basic kit on three budgets / Budget kit under €250 / Budget tricks / Upgrade order / Before you go / The mistake"],["Ch. 4, Equipment","The tent in depth: shape and seasons / Height, ventilation, tarp / Waterproofing in mm / Tent pegs: why they're not enough, types, the Greek trick, the set, how to place them / Inflatable tents"],["Ch. 4, Equipment","Other beds: rooftop tent (hard/soft, before you buy) and sleeping in the car"],["Ch. 4, Equipment","The two numbers (comfort, R-value) / Shape / Down or synthetic / Fill power / What you wear inside / Care / Types of mattress / Summer and winter"],["Ch. 4, Equipment","Why the gas stove dies in the cold / No combustion / Warmth at night: what works / Portable solar panel / Campsite and wild camping / Light / The kitchen in a box / Stoves / Bathroom and toilet"],["Ch. 5, Weather and seasons","The four numbers / Wind in tent language / How cold you feel in the wind / Dress like an onion / The mountain has its own weather / So does the sea / Where to read the weather"],["Ch. 5, Weather and seasons","The Greek year for beginners / How much daylight you have (sunrise-sunset table) / How to read it / The rhythm of the day / Example / Before you go / The mistake"],["Ch. 6, The first campsite","How to choose your first / At reception: what to ask / Power: the beginner's trap / The first hour / The unwritten rules / The campsite as a school / Before you go / The mistake"],["Ch. 7, Pitching camp","The first rule: arrive in daylight / The seven-question check / The pitching order / Pegging in Greek ground / The space around the tent / Striking camp / Before you go / The mistake"],["Ch. 8, Sleep","Where you lose your warmth / The eleven rules / More tricks for cold nights / The things that sleep with you / When you wake up cold at three / When the cold stops being funny / The mistake"],["Ch. 8, Sleep","The damp you think is a leak / Summer: the real enemy / Summer is won with airflow / Noise and light / Before you go"],["Ch. 9, Cooking, food, cool box","The one-pot rule / Two-day menu / The gas stove / Safety rules / The cool box without power / After the meal / Before you go / The mistake"],["Ch. 9, Cooking, food, cool box","Smart, cheap solutions: Plan and shopping / Prep at home / Less gas / Fewer dishes / The Greek pantry / Ten meals under €2 / Ready meals: the emergency pantry / Drinks and coffee / Zero waste"],["Ch. 10, The psychology of the first night","Why you're afraid / The sounds of the Greek night / What you fear and what you should watch for / The night watch / The three questions / The car is plan B / The noise inside you / Step by step / The magic mountain / Before you go / The mistake"],["Ch. 11, Car camping","What the law says / The car as base / Loading: seaman's stowage / Sleeping inside / Dirt tracks for beginners / The four things that always stay in the car / Before you go / The mistake"],["Ch. 12, Finding a wild spot","What the law says / The rule / Before you go: the spot on the map / At the spot: the harbour check / Mountain / Beach / The legal wild spots / The mistake"],["Ch. 12, Finding a wild spot","Stealth camping / The big trap: the rooftop tent / The rules / Legally parked / Before you go"],["Ch. 13, Water, power, hygiene","What the law says / Water (how much, where, purifying) / Power / Power station charged from the alternator or a standard 12V"],["Ch. 13, Water, power, hygiene","Hygiene / The toilet without a toilet / Before you go / The mistake"],["Ch. 14, Fire","What the law says / The rule / The flameless circle / When fire is allowed / How to put it out / If you see smoke / Before you go / The mistake"],["Ch. 15, Winter camping","What the law says / When you're ready / The sleep system in the cold / Depending on where you sleep / Winter specifics / The car in winter / What the experienced really take / The mistake"],["Ch. 15, Winter camping","The rule that saves lives / The test: battery or diesel / Conditions for a diesel heater / Hypothermia and frostbite / Before you go"],["Ch. 16, With friends and children","What the law says / The group list / Setting up a village / Who sleeps where / The latecomers / The striking-camp rule / The mistake"],["Ch. 16, With friends and children","Children / When you don't fit in the house / Before you go"],["Ch. 17, Risks and plan B","The plan in one message / The risks that matter / The phone numbers / Before you go / The mistake"],["Ch. 17, Risks and plan B","The first-aid kit (base and per task) / What still holds from the old lists / Two rules and an expiry check"],["Ch. 18, Leave without a trace","The seven principles in Greek reality / The local people / The cleaner-spot rule / Before you leave the spot / The mistake"],["Ch. 19, From camping to overlanding","What overlanding is / The upgrade order / Pressure and lockers / The multi-day trip / The convoy: a night in North Evia / The real cost / After the book"]]};
const by=Object.fromEntries(E.lessons.map(l=>[l.id,l]));
(ACAD.lessons||[]).forEach(l=>{const e=by[l.id];if(!e)return;l.n=e.n;l.src=e.src;l.p=e.p;(l.q||[]).forEach((q,i)=>{const x=e.q[i];if(x){q.q=x.q;q.a=x.a;q.w=x.w}})});
if(E.coverage)ACAD.coverage=ACAD.coverage.map((c,i)=>E.coverage[i]?[E.coverage[i][0],E.coverage[i][1]].concat(c.slice(2)):c);})();
